const BANK = {"d-biz":[{"c":"price-elasticity","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Elasticity −2, gross margin 30%. You cut price 5% and revenue goes up. What happens to gross profit?","o":["Rises ~5%","Falls ~8%","Rises ~10%","Unchanged"],"a":1,"hint":"On a ₹100 item, unit margin drops from ₹30 to ₹25 while volume rises about 10%.","why":"Unit margin 30 → 25, volume ×1.10: 25 × 1.10 = 27.5 vs 30 → about −8%. Revenue up, profit down — the classic discount trap."},{"c":"price-elasticity","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Elasticity −0.5. You raise price 10%. Roughly, what happens to revenue?","o":["Falls ~5%","Rises ~10%","Rises ~5%","Unchanged"],"a":2,"hint":"Volume moves by elasticity × price change. Then multiply the price and volume factors.","why":"Volume ≈ −5%, so revenue ≈ 1.10 × 0.95 ≈ +4.5–5%. Inelastic demand: price up, revenue up. −5% is the volume change, not revenue."},{"c":"price-elasticity","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Which of these faces the MOST price-elastic demand?","o":["Table salt (the category)","Insulin","One cola brand among many","Petrol (the category)"],"a":2,"hint":"Elasticity rises with the number of close substitutes a shopper can switch to.","why":"A single brand has many near-perfect substitutes, so its demand is far more elastic than any category's. Salt, insulin and petrol overall have few substitutes."},{"c":"margin-vs-markup","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Cost ₹80, selling price ₹100. Gross margin and markup are…","o":["Margin 20%, markup 25%","Margin 25%, markup 20%","Both 20%","Both 25%"],"a":0,"hint":"Margin divides profit by price; markup divides profit by cost.","why":"Profit ₹20. Margin = 20/100 = 20%; markup = 20/80 = 25%. Mixing up the two bases is the most common pricing slip in retail."},{"c":"margin-vs-markup","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"You need a 40% gross margin on an item costing ₹120. What price?","o":["₹168","₹180","₹160","₹200"],"a":3,"hint":"Margin is a share of PRICE: price = cost ÷ (1 − margin).","why":"₹120 ÷ 0.60 = ₹200 (profit ₹80 = 40% of ₹200). ₹168 is a 40% markup — only a 28.6% margin."},{"c":"margin-vs-markup","t":"Two-tier","p":50,"q":"A supplier quotes a “50% markup”. What gross margin is that?","o":["50%","25%","66.7%","33.3%"],"a":3,"hint":"Take cost = 100. What's the price, and what share of it is profit?","why":"Cost 100 → price 150 → profit 50/150 = 33.3%. In general, margin = markup ÷ (1 + markup)."},{"c":"contribution-breakeven","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Fixed costs ₹6 lakh/month, price ₹500, variable cost ₹300 per unit. Break-even volume?","o":["1,200 units","2,000 units","1,500 units","3,000 units"],"a":3,"hint":"Each unit contributes price minus variable cost toward the fixed costs.","why":"Contribution = ₹200/unit; ₹6,00,000 ÷ 200 = 3,000. 1,200 divides by price and 2,000 by variable cost — both ignore contribution."},{"c":"contribution-breakeven","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Gross margin 30%. You run 10% off. How much must unit volume grow just to keep gross profit flat?","o":["+10%","+30%","+33%","+50%"],"a":3,"hint":"On a ₹100 item, profit per unit falls from ₹30 to ₹20.","why":"Profit per unit 30 → 20, so volume must rise 30/20 = 1.5× → +50%. Rule of thumb: discount ÷ (margin − discount) = 10 ÷ 20."},{"c":"contribution-breakeven","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Spare capacity. Full cost ₹120/unit (₹70 variable + ₹50 fixed). A one-off buyer offers ₹90, no cannibalisation. Accept?","o":["No — it loses ₹30/unit","Yes — ₹20/unit contribution","No — never sell below cost","Only at ₹120 or more"],"a":1,"hint":"Will the fixed ₹50 be spent whether or not you take the order?","why":"Fixed costs don't change, so each unit adds ₹90 − ₹70 = ₹20 to profit. The ‘₹30 loss’ comes from an allocated cost that isn't incremental."},{"c":"anchoring-decoy","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Popcorn: Small ₹150, Medium ₹280, Large ₹300. What is the Medium mainly there for?","o":["A decoy that pushes buyers to Large","The main profit driver","For price-sensitive buyers","To make Small look cheap"],"a":0,"hint":"Compare Medium with Large. Is Medium ever the good-value pick?","why":"Medium is poor value: just ₹20 more buys a Large. It is a decoy — it makes Large look like a steal and shifts choices up the ladder."},{"c":"anchoring-decoy","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"A wine list opens with a ₹25,000 bottle that rarely sells. What is its main job?","o":["Profit from the rare buyer","Clear old inventory","Anchor: ₹4,000 bottles feel reasonable","Meet a listing rule"],"a":2,"hint":"What does the first big number do to how later prices feel?","why":"It's an anchor. After ₹25,000, a ₹4,000 bottle feels moderate, lifting mid-tier sales. The occasional ₹25,000 sale is a bonus."},{"c":"anchoring-decoy","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"For a decoy to shift choice towards the target, the decoy should be…","o":["Dominated by the target, not by the rival","The cheapest option","Better than every option","Identical to the target"],"a":0,"hint":"The effect is called ‘asymmetric dominance’. Dominated by which option?","why":"The decoy must be dominated by the target but not by the competitor. That asymmetry makes the target look like the obvious winner."},{"c":"charm-pricing","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Why does ₹499 often sell more than ₹500 — beyond what ₹1 should explain?","o":["Buyers compute the exact saving","Left-digit bias: reads as ‘4-something’","It signals higher quality","Rounding rules at the till"],"a":1,"hint":"Shoppers read prices left to right and anchor on the first digit.","why":"Left-digit bias: ₹499 is encoded as ‘four hundred and something’, so it feels much cheaper than ₹500. The ₹1 itself barely matters."},{"c":"charm-pricing","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"A luxury watch brand re-prices from ₹2,00,000 to ₹1,99,999. What is the main risk?","o":["Margin loss of ₹1 per watch","It may cheapen the brand image","Competitors will match it","Legal issues with odd prices"],"a":1,"hint":"Do ‘.99’ endings say ‘premium’ or ‘deal’?","why":"Charm endings signal ‘deal’. For prestige, feelings-driven buys, round prices signal quality and fit better (Wadhwa & Zhang). The ₹1 is irrelevant."},{"c":"sunk-vs-opportunity-cost","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Your land could be leased out for ₹2 lakh/yr. Farming it yourself shows ₹3 lakh accounting profit (ignore your time). Economic profit?","o":["₹3 lakh","₹5 lakh","−₹2 lakh","₹1 lakh"],"a":3,"hint":"Economic profit subtracts the best alternative you gave up.","why":"₹3 lakh − ₹2 lakh forgone lease = ₹1 lakh. Accounting profit ignores the opportunity cost of using your own land."},{"c":"sunk-vs-opportunity-cost","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"You paid ₹3 lakh, non-refundable, for a trade-show booth. A more valuable use of that week now appears. Decision rule?","o":["Compare only future value; ignore ₹3 lakh","Go, or the ₹3 lakh is wasted","Split the week between both","Go only if it recoups the ₹3 lakh"],"a":0,"hint":"Will the ₹3 lakh come back under either choice?","why":"The ₹3 lakh is gone whatever you do — it's sunk. Pick the option with the higher value from here on. Trying to ‘recoup’ it is the sunk-cost fallacy."},{"c":"price-discrimination","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Student discounts, senior fares and regional pricing are which kind of price discrimination?","o":["First-degree (individual)","Second-degree (quantity/version)","Third-degree (by group)","Not discrimination at all"],"a":2,"hint":"The seller sorts buyers by an observable trait, not by how much they buy.","why":"Third-degree = different prices for identifiable groups. Second-degree lets buyers self-select (volume tiers, versions); first-degree prices each individual."},{"c":"price-discrimination","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Which condition is essential for price discrimination to hold?","o":["Segments differ in WTP and can't resell","Every segment has the same costs","The market is perfectly competitive","All buyers share one willingness to pay"],"a":0,"hint":"What happens if low-price buyers can sell on to the high-price ones?","why":"You need different willingness to pay, a way to tell segments apart, some pricing power and no arbitrage — else low-price buyers resell to the high-WTP segment."},{"c":"bundling","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Buyer A values X at ₹100, Y at ₹40; buyer B values X at ₹40, Y at ₹100. Revenue-maximising offer?","o":["Separate, ₹100 each → ₹200","Separate, ₹40 each → ₹160","Bundle at ₹140 → ₹280","Bundle at ₹200 → ₹400"],"a":2,"hint":"Add each buyer's values for X + Y. What's the most both would pay for the pair?","why":"Each values the pair at ₹140, so a ₹140 bundle sells twice = ₹280. Separately the best is ₹200. At ₹200 the bundle sells to no one."},{"c":"bundling","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Pure bundling lifts revenue most when customers' valuations of the parts are…","o":["Identical across buyers","Negatively correlated","Perfectly positively correlated","Zero for one of the parts"],"a":1,"hint":"Bundling evens out differences in which part each buyer values most.","why":"When one buyer loves X and another loves Y, their bundle values converge, so one price captures more from both. Positively correlated values gain little."}],"d-hindi":[{"c":"elegant-vocab","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"“कृपया थोड़ी ___ करें, मैं पुष्टि करके बताता हूँ।” (Please wait a little)","o":["परीक्षा","प्रतीक्षा","प्रतिज्ञा","प्रशंसा"],"a":1,"hint":"You want the word for ‘waiting’ — the other three are sound-alikes.","why":"प्रतीक्षा = waiting. परीक्षा = exam, प्रतिज्ञा = vow, प्रशंसा = praise. Bonus word: पुष्टि = confirmation."},{"c":"elegant-vocab","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"“इस हफ़्ते की प्राथमिकता: कटाई।” — “प्राथमिकता” का अर्थ?","o":["Proposal","Probability","Priority","Primary school"],"a":2,"hint":"It comes from प्रथम — first.","why":"प्राथमिकता = priority (from प्रथम, first). प्राथमिक विद्यालय is primary school — same root, different word."},{"c":"elegant-vocab","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"“Opportunity” के लिए सही हिंदी शब्द कौन-सा है?","o":["अवशेष","आवास","अवसाद","अवसर"],"a":3,"hint":"All four start with अव/आव — only one means a chance.","why":"अवसर = opportunity. अवशेष = remains, आवास = housing, अवसाद = depression. A classic sound-alike trap."},{"c":"elegant-vocab","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"“बैठक में सबकी ___ से निर्णय हुआ।” (by consensus)","o":["सहमति","सहायता","सहानुभूति","सहनशक्ति"],"a":0,"hint":"सह- means ‘together’. You want ‘agreeing together’.","why":"सहमति = agreement/consent. सहायता = help, सहानुभूति = sympathy, सहनशक्ति = endurance — all built on सह (with)."},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“बंदरों ने खेत में नाक में दम कर रखा है।” — मतलब?","o":["खेत की गंध बिगाड़ दी","बहुत परेशान कर रखा है","बंदर बीमार पड़ गए","बंदर हर चीज़ सूँघते हैं"],"a":1,"hint":"दम = breath. Imagine someone making it hard for you to breathe.","why":"नाक में दम करना = to harass, to make life miserable. Any farmer who has met a monkey troop knows this one."},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"वेंडर ने आख़िरी वक़्त पर “अंगूठा दिखा दिया।” — मतलब?","o":["सौदा पक्का कर दिया","तारीफ़ की (thumbs-up)","साफ़ मना कर दिया","हस्ताक्षर कर दिए"],"a":2,"hint":"Not the social-media thumbs-up — this is an old Hindi gesture.","why":"अंगूठा दिखाना = to refuse flatly or back out, often when you need them most. The thumbs-up reading is the trap — it means the opposite."},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"“इस डील में दाल में कुछ काला है।” — सबसे नज़दीकी English?","o":["Something is fishy","It's a piece of cake","Spill the beans","Too many cooks"],"a":0,"hint":"Something dark hiding in the lentils — what does that suggest?","why":"दाल में कुछ काला होना = something suspicious is going on. Its big brother, “पूरी दाल ही काली है”, means the whole thing is crooked."},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"“A hard nut to crack” के लिए हिंदी मुहावरा कौन-सा है?","o":["बाएँ हाथ का खेल","ईद का चाँद","टेढ़ी खीर","हवा से बातें करना"],"a":2,"hint":"Think of a dessert that's awkward to eat.","why":"टेढ़ी खीर = a tough task. बाएँ हाथ का खेल is the opposite (child's play), ईद का चाँद = rarely seen, हवा से बातें करना = to go very fast."},{"c":"lokoktiyan","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"“अब पछताए होत क्या, जब चिड़िया चुग गई खेत।” — English में?","o":["The early bird catches the worm","Birds of a feather flock together","A bird in hand is worth two in the bush","No use crying over spilt milk"],"a":3,"hint":"It's about regret after the damage is already done.","why":"Once the birds have eaten the crop, regret is useless — no use crying over spilt milk. The bird-themed options are decoys."},{"c":"lokoktiyan","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"प्रोजेक्ट बिगड़ा तो टीम-लीड बोला, “टूल ही बेकार था।” कौन-सी लोकोक्ति ठीक बैठती है?","o":["नाच न जाने आँगन टेढ़ा","जैसी करनी वैसी भरनी","दूर के ढोल सुहावने","अधजल गगरी छलकत जाय"],"a":0,"hint":"Someone who can't do the job blames something else for it.","why":"नाच न जाने आँगन टेढ़ा = can't dance, so blames the crooked courtyard — a bad workman blames his tools."},{"c":"lokoktiyan","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"किस स्थिति पर “जैसी करनी वैसी भरनी” सबसे ठीक बैठती है?","o":["मेहनत के बावजूद फ़सल ख़राब हुई","किसी ने मुफ़्त में मदद की","बारिश से फ़सल अच्छी हुई","धोखा देने वाले को ख़ुद धोखा मिला"],"a":3,"hint":"Think ‘as you sow, so shall you reap’ — a result that matches the person's own action.","why":"जैसी करनी वैसी भरनी = you reap what you sow. A cheat getting cheated fits exactly; bad luck despite hard work doesn't."},{"c":"lokoktiyan","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"“अधजल गगरी छलकत जाय” — इसका English जोड़ीदार?","o":["Still waters run deep","Half a loaf is better than none","Empty vessels make the most noise","Don't count your chickens"],"a":2,"hint":"A half-filled pot splashes about the most. Who makes the most noise?","why":"A half-full pot sloshes and spills — people with half-knowledge show off the most. ‘Still waters run deep’ is the flip side."},{"c":"paryayvachi","t":"पर्यायवाची","p":50,"q":"इनमें से कौन-सा शब्द “पानी” का पर्यायवाची नहीं है?","o":["जल","अनल","नीर","वारि"],"a":1,"hint":"One of these is the opposite element — it burns.","why":"जल, नीर, वारि (also अंबु, तोय) all mean water. अनल means fire, like अग्नि and पावक."},{"c":"paryayvachi","t":"पर्यायवाची","p":100,"q":"किस जोड़ी में दोनों शब्द “पेड़” के पर्यायवाची हैं?","o":["वृक्ष, तरु","वृक्ष, पर्ण","तरु, तरंग","विटप, वन"],"a":0,"hint":"Careful — leaf, wave and forest sit close to ‘tree’ but aren't the same thing.","why":"वृक्ष and तरु both mean tree (so does विटप). पर्ण = leaf, तरंग = wave, वन = forest."},{"c":"paryayvachi","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"{RH} के पाठ “हमारा आदित्य” में एक विद्यार्थी है — धरा। “धरा” किसका पर्यायवाची है?","o":["नदी","आकाश","हवा","पृथ्वी"],"a":3,"hint":"Don't confuse धरा with धारा (a stream).","why":"धरा = पृथ्वी (earth), like धरती, भू, वसुधा. धारा with a long आ is a stream — hence the नदी trap."},{"c":"boys-chapter-words","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"{RH} के पाठ में: सौर ऊर्जा “अक्षय” है। “अक्षय” का अर्थ?","o":["जो कभी ख़त्म न हो","जो बहुत महँगी हो","जो सिर्फ़ दिन में मिले","जो प्रदूषण करे"],"a":0,"hint":"अ (not) + क्षय (decay, loss).","why":"अक्षय = inexhaustible — the lesson says “जो समाप्त नहीं होती”. Same idea as अक्षय तृतीया, the day whose gains never diminish."},{"c":"boys-chapter-words","t":"Hindi","p":100,"q":"“सूर्य के रहस्यों का उद्घाटन” — यहाँ “उद्घाटन” का अर्थ?","o":["Inauguration","Hiding","Counting","Revealing / uncovering"],"a":3,"hint":"Mysteries don't get a ribbon-cutting. What do you DO to a mystery?","why":"उद्घाटन literally means ‘opening up’. For a building that's an inauguration; for रहस्य (mysteries) it means revealing them — Aditya-L1's purpose."},{"c":"boys-chapter-words","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"“मुझे बहुत कौतूहल हो रहा है।” — “कौतूहल” का अर्थ?","o":["Noise / commotion","Curiosity","Fear","Tiredness"],"a":1,"hint":"Don't mix it up with its sound-alike कोलाहल.","why":"कौतूहल = curiosity; कोलाहल = noise/commotion. Deepak says exactly this line in {R}'s Sun lesson."},{"c":"hindi-usage","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"मानक (standard) हिंदी में कौन-सा वाक्य सही है?","o":["मैंने कल फ़ार्म जाना है।","मैं कल फ़ार्म जाना है।","मुझे कल फ़ार्म जाना है।","मेरे को कल फ़ार्म जाना है।"],"a":2,"hint":"‘I have to…’ takes a dative pronoun plus the infinitive.","why":"“मुझे जाना है” is standard. “मैंने जाना है” is a Punjabi-influenced Delhi habit; “मेरे को” is colloquial; “मैं… जाना है” is ungrammatical."},{"c":"hindi-usage","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"मंच से संबोधन की सबसे शुद्ध शुरुआत कौन-सी है?","o":["देवियों और सज्जनों","देवियो और सज्जनो","देवियाँ और सज्जन","देवीयों और सज्जनों"],"a":1,"hint":"In direct address (संबोधन), the plural drops something you'd normally add.","why":"The vocative plural takes no nasal: भाइयो, बहनो, साथियो, देवियो और सज्जनो. “सज्जनों” with the bindu is common but technically incorrect."}],"d-maths":[{"c":"lcm-schedules","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Areca spraying every 12 days, pepper-vine check every 18 days. Both done today. Next time they fall on the same day?","o":["In 30 days","In 36 days","In 72 days","In 216 days"],"a":1,"hint":"Find the smallest number that both 12 and 18 divide into.","why":"LCM(12, 18) = 36. 30 just adds the gaps and 216 multiplies them — both common slips. 72 works, but it isn't the first."},{"c":"lcm-schedules","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Irrigation every 4 days, weeding every 10, manuring every 15. All three today. When do all three coincide again?","o":["In 30 days","In 120 days","In 600 days","In 60 days"],"a":3,"hint":"Prime-factorise: 4 = 2², 10 = 2·5, 15 = 3·5. Take the highest power of each prime.","why":"LCM = 2² × 3 × 5 = 60. 30 misses the 2² from the 4-day cycle; 600 is the plain product; 120 is a common multiple, not the least."},{"c":"lcm-schedules","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Investor call every 14 days, team review every 21. Both today. How many more times do they coincide in the next 365 days?","o":["8","10","17","26"],"a":0,"hint":"First find how often they coincide (the LCM), then count how many of those fit in 365.","why":"LCM(14, 21) = 42. 365 ÷ 42 ≈ 8.7, so 8 more times (day 336 is the last). 17 and 26 just divide 365 by one cycle."},{"c":"lcm-schedules","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Drip filter cleaned every 8 days, pump serviced every 12 — together every 24. Switch the filter to every 9 days. Now together every…","o":["24 days","27 days","36 days","108 days"],"a":2,"hint":"9 = 3², 12 = 2² × 3. Rebuild the LCM from the prime factors.","why":"LCM(9, 12) = 2² × 3² = 36. Stretching one cycle by a single day moved the overlap from 24 to 36 days; 108 is just 9 × 12."},{"c":"hcf-packing","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"84 kg black pepper and 126 kg white pepper go into equal-weight bags, no mixing, nothing left. Largest bag size?","o":["14 kg","21 kg","63 kg","42 kg"],"a":3,"hint":"You need the largest number that divides both 84 and 126 exactly.","why":"HCF(84, 126) = 42 → 2 bags of black, 3 of white. 14 and 21 work but aren't the largest; 63 doesn't divide 84."},{"c":"hcf-packing","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"A 240 ft × 180 ft plot is split into the largest equal square beds possible. How many beds?","o":["7","12","48","108"],"a":1,"hint":"First find the side (the HCF of 240 and 180), then count beds along each side.","why":"HCF(240, 180) = 60 ft, so 4 × 3 = 12 beds. 7 adds 4 + 3 instead of multiplying; 48 and 108 come from 30 ft or 20 ft squares."},{"c":"hcf-packing","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"48 premium and 64 standard samples go into identical retail kits, nothing left over. Most kits you can make?","o":["8","12","16","32"],"a":2,"hint":"Every kit holds the same mix, so the kit count must divide both 48 and 64.","why":"HCF(48, 64) = 16 kits of 3 premium + 4 standard. 32 doesn't divide 48; 12 doesn't divide 64; 8 works but isn't the most."},{"c":"hcf-packing","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"A manager says: “HCF of 36 and 48 is 144, so we can make 144 equal packs.” What's wrong?","o":["144 is the LCM; the HCF is 12","Nothing — 144 is right","The HCF is 6","The HCF is 24"],"a":0,"hint":"Can the HCF ever be bigger than the smaller number?","why":"The HCF can't exceed 36; 144 is the LCM. HCF(36, 48) = 12 → 12 packs of 3 + 4. 6 is a common factor but not the highest; 24 doesn't divide 36."},{"c":"factors","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"A 60-person pricing workshop must split into equal teams of 2 to 30 people. How many team sizes are possible?","o":["6","8","12","10"],"a":3,"hint":"List the factors of 60, then drop the ones outside 2–30.","why":"Factors of 60: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 12, 15, 20, 30, 60. Dropping 1 and 60 leaves 10. 12 forgets to drop them."},{"c":"factors","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"420 areca saplings are planted in a rectangle with equal rows. Which row count is NOT possible?","o":["12 rows","14 rows","15 rows","16 rows"],"a":3,"hint":"420 = 2² × 3 × 5 × 7. Which option needs more 2s than that?","why":"16 = 2⁴, but 420 has only 2². 12 rows of 35, 14 of 30 and 15 of 28 all work."},{"c":"multiples","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Harvest goes into 12-kg sacks with nothing left over. Which lot can't be packed that way?","o":["144 kg","156 kg","186 kg","204 kg"],"a":2,"hint":"A multiple of 12 must be divisible by both 3 and 4.","why":"186 ÷ 12 = 15.5 — its last two digits, 86, aren't divisible by 4. 144 = 12 × 12, 156 = 12 × 13, 204 = 12 × 17."},{"c":"multiples","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Pepper ships only in full 25-kg bags. A buyer needs at least 340 kg. Smallest shipment you can send?","o":["325 kg","350 kg","340 kg","375 kg"],"a":1,"hint":"Find the first multiple of 25 that is 340 or more.","why":"340 ÷ 25 = 13.6 → 14 bags = 350 kg. 325 falls short, and 340 isn't a multiple of 25."},{"c":"multiples","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Can 7,425 areca nuts be packed into bags of 45 with none left? Quick test?","o":["Yes — ends in 5 and digits sum to 18","No — 45 can't divide an odd number","Yes — anything ending in 5 works","No — the digit sum isn't 45"],"a":0,"hint":"45 = 9 × 5. Test for 9 and for 5 separately.","why":"Ends in 5 → divisible by 5; 7+4+2+5 = 18 → divisible by 9. So 45 divides it: 7,425 ÷ 45 = 165. ‘Ends in 5’ alone only proves 5."},{"c":"lcm-hcf-relation","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Two delivery cycles: HCF 6 days, LCM 180 days. One cycle is 36 days. The other?","o":["5 days","30 days","60 days","144 days"],"a":1,"hint":"For two numbers, HCF × LCM = the product of the two numbers.","why":"6 × 180 = 1,080 and 1,080 ÷ 36 = 30. Check: HCF(36, 30) = 6, LCM = 180. 60 fails — HCF(36, 60) is 12."},{"c":"lcm-hcf-relation","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"An analyst reports two cycles with HCF 8 days and LCM 60 days. Why is that impossible?","o":["An LCM must be odd","An HCF can't be even","HCF must divide LCM; 60 ÷ 8 isn't whole","It's possible: 8 and 60"],"a":2,"hint":"Both numbers are multiples of the HCF, and the LCM is a multiple of both numbers.","why":"Each number is a multiple of the HCF and the LCM is a multiple of each number — so the HCF must divide the LCM. 60 ÷ 8 = 7.5."}],"r-air":[{"c":"air-all-around","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Where can we find air?","o":["Only outdoors, in the open","Only where the wind blows","All around us, even in “empty” jars","Only high up in the sky"],"a":2,"hint":"Is there air inside your room right now, even with the windows shut?","why":"Air is everywhere around us — indoors, outdoors, and even inside a jar that looks empty. We can’t see it, but it’s always there."},{"c":"air-all-around","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"We can’t see air. Which of these shows that air is really there?","o":["A stone lying still","Leaves rustling on a tree","A shadow under a tree","Sunlight on a wall"],"a":1,"hint":"Look for something that moves even though nobody touched it.","why":"Leaves rustle because moving air pushes them. We can’t see air, but we can see and feel what it does!"},{"c":"air-all-around","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"{R} says, “This empty bottle has nothing inside it.” What’s his mistake?","o":["It is full of air","It has water hidden in it","It is full of dust only","Nothing — he is right"],"a":0,"hint":"Push the “empty” bottle under water. What bubbles out?","why":"An “empty” bottle is really full of air! Push it under water and bubbles of that air come rushing out."},{"c":"air-takes-space","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"A tissue is stuck inside a glass. The glass goes upside down, straight into water. Does the tissue get wet? Answer + reason.","o":["Yes — water rushes in at once","Yes — the tissue soaks up water","No — tissue paper is waterproof","No — trapped air fills the glass"],"a":3,"hint":"The glass isn’t really empty. What is already inside it?","why":"Air trapped in the glass takes up the space, so water can’t rise up to the tissue and it stays dry. Air takes up space, even though we can’t see it!"},{"c":"air-takes-space","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Under water, you tilt an upside-down glass. Big bubbles pop out. What are they?","o":["Steam from the water","Soap left in the glass","Gas made by the water","Air escaping from the glass"],"a":3,"hint":"What was inside the glass before it went into the water?","why":"The “empty” glass was full of air. When you tilt it, the air escapes as bubbles, and water moves in to take its place."},{"c":"air-takes-space","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Why does a balloon get bigger when you blow into it?","o":["The air you blow in takes up space","Your breath makes rubber grow","Air makes the rubber lighter","Rubber stretches when it’s cold"],"a":0,"hint":"What are you putting inside, and does it need room?","why":"The air you blow in needs room, so it pushes the rubber outwards. More air needs more space — so the balloon gets bigger!"},{"c":"air-has-weight","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Two blown-up balloons hang from a balanced stick. One balloon bursts. Which side goes down?","o":["The side with the burst balloon","The side with the full balloon","Neither — air weighs nothing","Both sides go up together"],"a":1,"hint":"Which side still has air in its balloon?","why":"The full balloon still holds air, and air has weight. So that side is heavier and dips down. The burst balloon has lost its air."},{"c":"air-has-weight","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which sentence about air is TRUE?","o":["Air has no weight","Air takes up no space","Air is only found outdoors","Air has weight"],"a":3,"hint":"Remember the balloon-on-a-stick experiment.","why":"Air has weight! It is very light, but a ball or balloon full of air weighs a little more than the same one when empty."},{"c":"air-has-weight","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A football pumped full of air weighs a little MORE than the same ball when flat. Why?","o":["The air pushed in has weight","Pumping makes the leather thicker","The pump adds a little water","Flat balls lose their stitches"],"a":0,"hint":"What did the pump add to the ball?","why":"Air has weight. When you pump air into a ball, you add weight — only a tiny bit, but a good scale can show it!"},{"c":"breathing-air","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which part of the air do we need to stay alive?","o":["Carbon dioxide","Oxygen","Smoke","Dust"],"a":1,"hint":"Hospitals give this gas to patients from a cylinder.","why":"We need oxygen from the air to stay alive. Our body uses it, and we breathe out carbon dioxide."},{"c":"breathing-air","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A shark has no lungs, but it needs oxygen too. How does it get it?","o":["It swims up and gulps air","It doesn’t need oxygen at all","Its gills take oxygen from water","It gets it from the fish it eats"],"a":2,"hint":"Look at the slits on the side of a shark’s head.","why":"Water has a little air dissolved in it. As water flows over a shark’s gills, they take out the oxygen. Whales must come up to breathe — sharks don’t!"},{"c":"breathing-air","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Climbers on Mount Everest often carry oxygen cylinders. Why?","o":["High up, there is less air to breathe","Oxygen keeps their toes warm","Snow blocks their noses","Cylinders help them climb faster"],"a":0,"hint":"Does the air get thicker or thinner as you go higher?","why":"The higher you go, the thinner the air — each breath has less oxygen. So climbers carry oxygen cylinders to breathe easily."},{"c":"air-for-burning","t":"Predict","p":50,"q":"A lit candle is covered with a glass jar. What happens after a little while?","o":["It burns even brighter","It keeps burning for ever","The flame goes out","The jar catches fire"],"a":2,"hint":"What does a fire need from the air to keep burning?","why":"A flame needs oxygen from the air. Inside the jar, the oxygen gets used up and no fresh air can come in, so the candle goes out."},{"c":"air-for-burning","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Two candles of the same size: one under a small glass, one under a big jar. Which goes out first?","o":["The one under the big jar","The one under the small glass","Both at exactly the same time","Neither — candles don’t need air"],"a":1,"hint":"Which one has more air trapped with it?","why":"The small glass holds less air, so its oxygen runs out sooner. The big jar has more air, so that candle keeps burning a bit longer."},{"c":"air-for-burning","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Why do people throw sand or a thick blanket over a small fire?","o":["To give the fire more air","To make the fire wet","To stop air reaching the fire","To hide the flames"],"a":2,"hint":"Remember the candle under the jar.","why":"Fire needs air to burn. Sand or a thick blanket covers the fire and cuts off its air, so it dies down. (Always call a grown-up for any fire!)"},{"c":"whats-in-air","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A glass of icy water gets tiny drops on its OUTSIDE. Where do they come from?","o":["Water leaking through the glass","Ice melting through the glass","Water vapour in the air","Sweat from your fingers"],"a":2,"hint":"Glass doesn’t let water through. So what touched the outside of the glass?","why":"Air has water vapour in it. When it touches the cold glass, it cools and turns into tiny drops. Water can’t leak through glass!"},{"c":"whats-in-air","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"A sunbeam shines into a dark room. You see tiny specks floating in it. What are they?","o":["Bits of oxygen","Dust in the air","Water drops","Tiny insects"],"a":1,"hint":"Think of what collects on a shelf if nobody wipes it.","why":"Air carries tiny bits of dust. They’re always there, but a bright sunbeam in a dark room lets you see them dancing! Oxygen is invisible."},{"c":"whats-in-air","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which fact about air is WRONG?","o":["Air has oxygen in it","Air has carbon dioxide in it","Air is made of only oxygen","Air has water vapour in it"],"a":2,"hint":"Is air one single gas, or a mixture?","why":"Air is a mixture: oxygen, carbon dioxide and other gases, plus water vapour and dust. Most of it is actually a gas called nitrogen!"},{"c":"plants-and-air","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"When green plants make food in sunlight, which gas do they give out?","o":["Carbon dioxide","Smoke","Steam","Oxygen"],"a":3,"hint":"It’s the gas we need to breathe.","why":"Green plants take in carbon dioxide and give out oxygen while making food in sunlight. That oxygen is what we breathe!"},{"c":"plants-and-air","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"We breathe out carbon dioxide. What do green plants do with it in sunlight?","o":["Take it in and give out oxygen","Give out even more of it","Turn it into dust","They can’t use it at all"],"a":0,"hint":"Plants and animals make a perfect swap of gases.","why":"Plants use carbon dioxide to make food and give out oxygen. We use oxygen and give out carbon dioxide — a perfect swap!"},{"c":"plants-and-air","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if all the trees in a city were cut down?","o":["The air would get cleaner","The air would get dirtier","Nothing would change","There’d be more oxygen"],"a":1,"hint":"Trees are like a city’s lungs. What would go missing?","why":"Trees give out oxygen and trap dust and smoke on their leaves. Without them, a city’s air gets dirtier and less healthy."},{"c":"wind-moving-air","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A windmill’s blades spin round and round. What turns them?","o":["A motor hidden inside","Water pushing them","Moving air pushing them","The Sun pulling them"],"a":2,"hint":"Think of what makes a paper pinwheel spin.","why":"Moving air is called wind, and wind can push things. It pushes the blades round. Big windmills use this push to make electricity!"},{"c":"wind-moving-air","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Odd one out — which one does NOT need wind to move?","o":["A kite","A sailboat","A windmill","A motorboat"],"a":3,"hint":"One of these has an engine.","why":"Kites, sailboats and windmills all need moving air to push them. A motorboat has an engine, so it can move even on a still day."},{"c":"wind-moving-air","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"On which day will wet clothes on a line dry the FASTEST?","o":["A still, cloudy day","A sunny, windy day","A damp, rainy day","A cold, calm evening"],"a":1,"hint":"Think of two helpers: one warms, one blows.","why":"The Sun warms the clothes, and the wind carries the water vapour away. So sunny + windy dries clothes fastest!"},{"c":"air-pollution","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which of these makes the air dirty?","o":["Smoke from cars and buses","Plants in a park","Rain washing the streets","Clothes drying in the sun"],"a":0,"hint":"Look for something that puffs out of an exhaust pipe.","why":"Smoke from vehicles puts harmful gases and soot into the air — that’s air pollution. Plants actually help clean the air!"},{"c":"air-pollution","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"On smoggy winter mornings in Delhi-NCR, many people cough and their eyes sting. Why?","o":["There is too much oxygen","Cold air is poisonous","The air is full of smoke and dust","The Sun is too bright"],"a":2,"hint":"Smog = smoke + fog. What’s in it?","why":"Smog is smoke and dust mixed with foggy air. Breathing it makes us cough and stings our eyes. That’s why people wear masks on bad-air days."},{"c":"air-pollution","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Odd one out — which one does NOT pollute the air?","o":["Bursting crackers","Burning garbage","Factory smoke","Riding a bicycle"],"a":3,"hint":"Which one makes no smoke at all?","why":"A bicycle runs on leg power — no smoke at all! Crackers, burning garbage and factory chimneys all fill the air with smoke."},{"c":"cleaner-air","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Which habit helps keep the air clean?","o":["Burning dry leaves in the park","Bursting more crackers","Taking the car for very short trips","Switching off the engine at red lights"],"a":3,"hint":"Which one stops smoke from coming out?","why":"An engine running at a red light burns fuel and puffs out smoke for nothing. Switching it off keeps the air cleaner. Walking short trips helps too!"},{"c":"cleaner-air","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Why do towns plant trees along busy roads?","o":["Leaves catch dust; trees give oxygen","Trees make cars use less petrol","Trees turn smoke into rain","Trees stop cars from honking"],"a":0,"hint":"What do trees give out, and what sticks to their leaves?","why":"Tree leaves trap dust and soot, and trees give out oxygen. So rows of trees help clean the air along smoky roads."},{"c":"cleaner-air","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Many buses in Delhi run on CNG instead of diesel. Why is that better?","o":["CNG makes much less smoke","CNG makes buses go faster","CNG is made from plants","CNG never runs out"],"a":0,"hint":"Think about what comes out of a bus’s exhaust pipe.","why":"CNG is a cleaner fuel — it burns with much less smoke than diesel, so the air stays cleaner. It still comes from under the ground, so it can run out."},{"c":"nb-what-is-air","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: what is air?","o":["A single gas called oxygen","A clear liquid that we cannot see","Tiny bits of dust floating around","A mixture of gases that surrounds the Earth"],"a":3,"hint":"Air is made of many gases, not just one.","why":"Air is a mixture of gases (mainly nitrogen and oxygen, with carbon dioxide, water vapour and dust) that surrounds the Earth."},{"c":"nb-what-is-air","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: why do green plants need air?","o":["They need dust from the air to grow roots","They need water vapour to make seeds","They need smoke to make their leaves green","They take in carbon dioxide to make their food"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of how plants make food in sunlight.","why":"Plants take in carbon dioxide from the air to make their food. Humans and animals need the oxygen in air to breathe."},{"c":"nb-see-air","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: can we see air?","o":["Yes, but only at night, when it is cold","No, but we can feel it when it moves, like the wind","No, and we can never feel it either","Yes, air is pale blue, like the sky above"],"a":1,"hint":"What do you feel on a windy day?","why":"Air is invisible, but we feel it when it moves: that's the wind."},{"c":"nb-properties","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: which is NOT a property of air?","o":["Air occupies space","Air expands when heated","Air can be compressed","Air has no weight"],"a":3,"hint":"Remember the balloon balance.","why":"Air flows around objects, occupies space, has weight, can be compressed, exerts pressure and expands when heated."},{"c":"nb-properties","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: ‘air can be compressed’ means…","o":["air turns into ice when you press it hard","air can be squeezed into a smaller space, as in a pump","air can be cut into small pieces with scissors","air becomes visible when it is squeezed"],"a":1,"hint":"Think of pumping air into a bicycle tyre.","why":"Compressed = squeezed. A pump squeezes lots of air into a tyre or a football."},{"c":"nb-properties","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: what happens to air when it is heated?","o":["It expands and takes up more space","It shrinks and takes up less space","It disappears completely","It turns into water"],"a":0,"hint":"A balloon left in the hot sun gets a little bigger…","why":"Air expands when heated, which is why a balloon in the hot sun swells."},{"c":"nb-wind","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: air moving from one place to another is called…","o":["pressure","wind","weight","water vapour"],"a":1,"hint":"It makes kites fly.","why":"Moving air is wind."},{"c":"nb-wind","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: which is NOT a use of wind?","o":["Drying clothes","Moving sailboats and kites","Freezing water into ice","Running windmills to make electricity"],"a":2,"hint":"Three of these need moving air.","why":"Wind dries clothes, moves sailboats and kites, and turns windmills that make electricity. It doesn't freeze water."},{"c":"nb-air-space","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class activity: you push an empty glass, upside down, straight into a bowl of water. What happens?","o":["Water rushes in and fills the whole glass","Water doesn't go in, because the glass is full of air","The water in the bowl turns very cold","The glass floats away and then breaks"],"a":1,"hint":"Is the ‘empty’ glass really empty?","why":"The glass is full of air, and air occupies space, so the water can't get in. Tilt it, and bubbles of air escape."},{"c":"bw-air-flows","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Book work: a candle burns behind a glass jar. You blow hard on the front of the jar. What happens?","o":["Nothing: the jar blocks all of the air","The flame grows, because the jar heats the air","The flame flickers or goes out, because air flows around the jar","The jar fills up with smoke"],"a":2,"hint":"Does moving air stop at the jar?","why":"Air flows around objects, so your breath curls around the jar and reaches the flame."},{"c":"bw-air-weight","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Book work: two balloons balance on a stick. One is pricked and goes flat. Why does the side with the full balloon go down?","o":["The needle made the stick heavier","Air has no weight, so the flat balloon floats up","The full balloon became cold and heavy","Air has weight, and the pricked balloon has lost its air"],"a":3,"hint":"What did the pricked balloon lose?","why":"The full balloon still holds air, and air has weight, so that side is heavier and tilts down."}],"r-animal":[{"c":"shark-cartilage","t":"Animal fact","p":50,"q":"What is a shark’s skeleton made of?","o":["Hard bone, just like ours","Cartilage, like the tip of your nose","A hard shell, like a crab’s","Nothing — sharks have no skeleton"],"a":1,"hint":"Squeeze the tip of your nose. It’s firm but bendy — that’s the clue!","why":"Sharks have no bones at all! Their skeleton is cartilage, the light, bendy stuff in your nose tip and ears. It’s lighter than bone and helps them twist and turn fast."},{"c":"shark-cartilage","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if you dug up a shark that lived millions of years ago? What would you most likely find?","o":["Its whole bony skeleton","Only its fins","Mostly its teeth","Its bones, but no teeth"],"a":2,"hint":"Which part of a shark is the hardest? Remember, its skeleton is soft cartilage.","why":"Cartilage usually rots away before it can become a fossil, but hard teeth last. That’s why fossil shark teeth are so common — even megalodon is known mostly from its teeth!"},{"c":"shark-cartilage","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out: which of these has a skeleton made of BONE?","o":["Great white shark","Sawfish","Manta ray","Tuna"],"a":3,"hint":"Sharks and rays belong to one big cartilage family. Which one isn’t in it?","why":"Sharks, sawfish and manta rays all have cartilage skeletons. The tuna is a bony fish, like most fish you see at a fish market."},{"c":"shark-senses","t":"Animal fact","p":50,"q":"A shark can find a fish hiding under the sand without seeing it. What does the shark sense?","o":["Tiny electric signals from its muscles","The fish’s shadow","Heat from the fish’s body","The fish’s bright colours"],"a":0,"hint":"Every moving muscle makes something tiny that sharks can feel. It isn’t light or heat.","why":"Moving muscles give off tiny electric signals. Sharks feel them with jelly-filled pores on their snouts, called ampullae of Lorenzini — a superpower we don’t have!"},{"c":"shark-senses","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Does a hammerhead’s wide, flat head help it find food? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — its sensors spread wide to scan more","No — it is only for show","Yes — it hammers crab shells open","No — it makes hunting harder"],"a":0,"hint":"Think of a metal detector. A wider one sweeps more ground at once.","why":"The hammerhead’s wide head spreads its electric-sensing pores far apart, so it can sweep the sea floor and find stingrays hiding in the sand."},{"c":"shark-senses","t":"Animal fact","p":50,"q":"Sharks have a ‘lateral line’ along each side of the body. What does it sense?","o":["Light and colours","Heat from other animals","Movements and vibrations in water","Tastes in the water"],"a":2,"hint":"When a fish splashes or wriggles nearby, the water around it moves.","why":"The lateral line is a row of tiny sensors that feel water moving. A wriggling fish sends out ripples, and the shark feels them even in murky water."},{"c":"shark-teeth","t":"Animal fact","p":50,"q":"What happens when a shark loses a tooth?","o":["It has a gap there forever","A new tooth moves forward to replace it","Only baby sharks can regrow teeth","It grows back only once"],"a":1,"hint":"Peek inside a shark’s mouth: there’s more than one row of teeth!","why":"Sharks have rows of spare teeth lined up behind the front ones. When one falls out, a new tooth moves forward, like a conveyor belt — all their lives!"},{"c":"shark-teeth","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"A shark has flat, rounded teeth, like tiny pebbles. What does it most likely eat?","o":["Big seals","Tiny plankton only","Seaweed","Crabs and shellfish"],"a":3,"hint":"Flat teeth are good for crushing, not cutting. Which food has a hard shell?","why":"Flat teeth work like nutcrackers, crushing hard shells. The horn shark eats crabs and sea urchins this way. Seal-hunters like the great white have sharp, jagged teeth instead."},{"c":"sawfish-ray","t":"Animal fact","p":50,"q":"It looks a lot like a shark, but a sawfish is actually a kind of…","o":["Swordfish","Ray","Dolphin","Eel"],"a":1,"hint":"Its cousins are flat, with wing-like fins, like stingrays and mantas.","why":"Sawfish are rays — cousins of stingrays and manta rays! A swordfish has a long bill too, but it’s a bony fish with no teeth on its sword."},{"c":"sawfish-ray","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Is a sawfish a shark or a ray? Pick the answer AND the clue that proves it.","o":["Shark — it has a long saw","Shark — it has a shark-like tail","Ray — its gill slits are underneath","Ray — it lives in the sea"],"a":2,"hint":"Sharks and rays look different underneath. Where are the gill slits?","why":"Rays have gill slits on their underside; sharks have them on the sides of the head. A sawfish’s gills are underneath, so it’s a ray — even with that sharky tail!"},{"c":"sawfish-ray","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out: three of these are rays. Which one is a true shark?","o":["Sawshark","Sawfish","Stingray","Manta ray"],"a":0,"hint":"Two of them have saws. Check which one has its gill slits on its sides.","why":"The sawshark is a true shark: gill slits on its sides and two long whiskers on its saw. Sawfish, stingrays and manta rays are all rays."},{"c":"sawfish-ray","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Sawfish are now endangered. What is one main reason?","o":["Their saws get tangled in fishing nets","Their saws stop growing","Sharks have eaten them all","The sea has become too salty"],"a":0,"hint":"Think about what happens when a long, toothy saw meets a net.","why":"A sawfish’s toothy saw easily gets caught in fishing nets. That’s why many countries now protect sawfish by law, and scientists are working to help them recover."},{"c":"ocean-mammals","t":"Animal fact","p":50,"q":"A whale’s blowhole is actually its…","o":["Mouth, for eating","Ear, for hearing","Gill, for breathing water","Nostril, for breathing air"],"a":3,"hint":"Whales breathe air, just like you. What do you breathe through?","why":"The blowhole is the whale’s nose, on top of its head! The big ‘spout’ is the whale breathing out warm, wet air. Whales have no gills — they’re mammals."},{"c":"ocean-mammals","t":"Animal fact","p":100,"q":"Dolphins must swim up to breathe air, even when resting. How do they sleep?","o":["They never sleep at all","On the sea floor, breathing water","Half of the brain sleeps at a time","By holding their breath for a week"],"a":2,"hint":"They need to stay a little bit awake to keep breathing.","why":"Dolphins rest one half of their brain at a time. The awake half keeps them breathing and watching for danger. Then the halves swap!"},{"c":"ocean-mammals","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out: dolphin, blue whale, orca, whale shark. Which one is NOT a mammal?","o":["Dolphin","Blue whale","Orca","Whale shark"],"a":3,"hint":"Don’t be fooled by the names! Which one breathes with gills?","why":"The whale shark is a fish with gills, even with ‘whale’ in its name! Dolphins, blue whales and orcas breathe air. The orca is actually the biggest dolphin."},{"c":"filter-feeders","t":"Animal fact","p":50,"q":"Which is the largest FISH in the world?","o":["Blue whale","Whale shark","Great white shark","Basking shark"],"a":1,"hint":"Careful — one of these isn’t a fish at all!","why":"The whale shark is the biggest fish alive and can be as long as a bus! The blue whale is bigger, but it’s a mammal. The basking shark comes second among fish."},{"c":"filter-feeders","t":"Animal fact","p":100,"q":"The blue whale, the largest animal ever known, eats tiny krill. How does it catch enough?","o":["Chases each krill one by one","Bites them with huge, sharp teeth","Gulps water, strains krill through baleen","Sucks them in through its blowhole"],"a":2,"hint":"Blue whales have no teeth. Think of how a tea strainer works.","why":"A blue whale takes a giant gulp of krill-filled water, then pushes the water out through bristly baleen plates. The krill stay trapped inside — dinner is served!"},{"c":"filter-feeders","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out: which does NOT eat by filtering tiny food from the water?","o":["Whale shark","Manta ray","Blue whale","Great white shark"],"a":3,"hint":"Three are gentle giants that sieve up tiny food. One hunts big prey.","why":"Whale sharks, manta rays and blue whales all filter tiny food like plankton and krill. The great white is a hunter that catches big prey, such as seals."},{"c":"octopus","t":"Animal fact","p":50,"q":"How many hearts does an octopus have?","o":["One","Two","Three","Eight"],"a":2,"hint":"More than you have, but fewer than its arms.","why":"An octopus has three hearts! Two pump blood through its gills, and one pumps it around the body. Its blood is even blue."},{"c":"octopus","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if a big octopus wants to escape through a hole much smaller than its body?","o":["It squeezes through — it has no bones","It’s stuck — it’s far too big","It breaks the hole wider with its arms","It shrinks by squirting out ink"],"a":0,"hint":"Squeeze your arm. What hard thing inside stops it from squishing flat?","why":"An octopus has no bones, so its soft body can squeeze through tiny gaps. The only hard part is its parrot-like beak — if the beak fits, the octopus fits!"},{"c":"octopus","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out: which of these can NOT squirt ink to escape danger?","o":["Starfish","Octopus","Squid","Cuttlefish"],"a":0,"hint":"Three are close cousins with big eyes and soft arms. Which one isn’t?","why":"Octopuses, squids and cuttlefish squirt dark ink clouds to confuse attackers, then zoom away. A starfish has no ink — it relies on its tough, spiny skin."},{"c":"sea-turtles","t":"Animal fact","p":50,"q":"For sea turtles, the warmth of the sand decides if babies are boys or girls. Warmer nests make more…","o":["Boys","Girls","Exactly half and half","Bigger babies"],"a":1,"hint":"Warm sand and cool sand give different results — so it can’t be half and half!","why":"Warm sand makes more girl turtles; cooler sand makes more boys. Scientists sum it up as “hot chicks, cool dudes”! That’s why a warming planet worries turtle experts."},{"c":"sea-turtles","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Baby sea turtles find the sea by heading for the brighter horizon over the water. What if a beach hotel has bright lights?","o":["They may crawl toward the hotel instead","They swim even faster","Nothing — they follow the smell of the sea","They go back inside their eggs"],"a":0,"hint":"If the hotel lights are brighter than the sea, which way would the babies head?","why":"Bright lights can trick hatchlings into crawling away from the sea. That’s why many turtle beaches ask people to switch off bright lights at night."},{"c":"sea-turtles","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Is a floating plastic bag dangerous for sea turtles? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["No — turtles only eat seaweed","No — turtles can digest plastic","Yes — it makes the water too hot","Yes — it looks like a jellyfish to eat"],"a":3,"hint":"Picture a soft, see-through bag drifting in the water. What sea animal does it look like?","why":"Many sea turtles eat jellyfish, and a floating bag looks just like one! Swallowed plastic can make turtles very sick. Picking up beach litter really helps."},{"c":"jellyfish","t":"Animal fact","p":50,"q":"Which of these does a jellyfish NOT have?","o":["Tentacles","A mouth","A brain","Stinging cells"],"a":2,"hint":"It eats, it stings and it drifts. But does it think the way you do?","why":"A jellyfish has no brain, no heart and no bones! A simple net of nerves lets it sense the world, and it catches food with its stinging tentacles."},{"c":"jellyfish","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Is a jellyfish really a fish? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — it lives in the sea","No — it has no backbone or gills","Yes — ‘fish’ is in its name","No — it is too small to be a fish"],"a":1,"hint":"Think about what real fish, from sardines to sharks, have inside their bodies.","why":"Fish have a backbone and breathe with gills. A jellyfish has neither — it’s a soft, jelly animal with no bones at all. Some scientists call them ‘sea jellies’!"},{"c":"jellyfish","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"You spot a jellyfish washed up on the beach. Is it safe to touch?","o":["No — its tentacles may still sting","Yes — out of water it can’t sting","Yes — dead jellyfish are harmless","Yes — only big ones can sting"],"a":0,"hint":"Its stinging cells are tiny traps. Do traps stop working the moment the animal stops moving?","why":"A jellyfish’s stinging cells can still fire even when it’s washed up or dead. Look and take a photo, but don’t touch — and tell a lifeguard if you see lots."},{"c":"tigers","t":"Animal fact","p":50,"q":"Unlike most cats, tigers…","o":["Hate getting wet","Love water and are strong swimmers","Live in big groups called prides","Hunt in packs, like wolves"],"a":1,"hint":"Think about how a tiger might cool down on a hot Rajasthan afternoon.","why":"Tigers love water! They cool off in lakes and pools on hot days and are strong swimmers. Lions live in prides; tigers mostly live and hunt alone."},{"c":"tigers","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"In Ranthambore, experts tell tigers apart in camera-trap photos. How?","o":["By the colour of their eyes","By the length of their tails","Each tiger wears a name tag","Each tiger has its own stripe pattern"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of your fingerprints. Is anyone else’s exactly the same as yours?","why":"No two tigers have the same stripes — they’re like fingerprints! Hidden cameras photograph tigers, and experts match the stripes to know exactly which tiger walked by."},{"c":"tigers","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out: three of these big cats usually live alone. Which one lives in a group?","o":["Tiger","Leopard","Lion","Snow leopard"],"a":2,"hint":"One of these cats is famous for sharing meals and hunting with its family.","why":"Lions live in groups called prides and often hunt together. Tigers, leopards and snow leopards mostly live alone. India’s wild lions live only in and around Gir, Gujarat!"}],"r-eng":[{"c":"prep-in-on","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"The crab is ___ the rock, warming its shell in the sun. Pick the word AND the reason.","o":["in — it is inside the rock","on — it is inside the rock","on — it rests on the rock’s top","in — it rests on the rock’s top"],"a":2,"hint":"First think: can a crab get inside solid rock? Then pick the reason that matches.","why":"‘On’ is for something resting on a surface. A crab can’t get inside solid rock, so it sits ON it. The right word needs the right reason too!"},{"c":"prep-in-on","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{R} wants his shark book to stay dry in the rain, so he puts it ___ his school bag.","o":["in","on","over","across"],"a":0,"hint":"To stay dry, should the book be inside the bag or on top of it?","why":"‘In’ means inside something. A book ON the bag would get soaked, but a book IN the bag stays safe and dry."},{"c":"prep-in-on","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which sentence has a mistake?","o":["The parrot sits on the branch.","The jellyfish floats in the sea.","The kitten sleeps on the mat.","The goldfish swims on its bowl."],"a":3,"hint":"Think about where each animal really is: inside something, or on top of it?","why":"A goldfish swims IN the water inside its bowl. ‘On its bowl’ would mean swimming on top of the glass — impossible! The other three are correct."},{"c":"prep-under-over","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The flatfish hides ___ the sand, with only its two eyes peeping out.","o":["over","under","on","across"],"a":1,"hint":"If only the eyes peep out, where is the rest of the fish?","why":"The flatfish covers itself with sand, so it is UNDER the sand. Only its eyes stay out to watch for food — a clever hiding trick!"},{"c":"prep-under-over","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The seagull glided high ___ the sea, looking down for fish.","o":["under","into","between","over"],"a":3,"hint":"The bird is high up, looking down. Is it above the water or below it?","why":"‘Over’ means above something. The seagull is high in the air, above the sea, so it glides OVER the sea."},{"c":"prep-under-over","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A bridge goes ___ the river, and boats sail ___ it.","o":["over / under","under / over","into / on","between / in"],"a":0,"hint":"The bridge is up high. The boats are down on the water.","why":"The bridge is above the river, so it goes OVER it. The boats pass below the bridge, so they sail UNDER it."},{"c":"prep-between-among","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The baby octopus hid ___ the two big rocks.","o":["among","across","between","into"],"a":2,"hint":"How many rocks are there? Which word is used for just two things?","why":"‘Between’ is for two things. There are TWO rocks, so the octopus hid between them. ‘Among’ is for a group of three or more."},{"c":"prep-between-among","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"One little sardine got lost ___ a huge school of sardines.","o":["among","between","on","at"],"a":0,"hint":"Is it two fish, or a huge crowd of fish?","why":"A school of sardines can have thousands of fish! For a big group of three or more, we use ‘among’. ‘Between’ is only for two."},{"c":"prep-between-among","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Count carefully! Spot the sentence with the wrong word.","o":["The crab hid between two shells.","The turtle swam among the fish.","The dolphin swam among the two boats.","The bee buzzed among the flowers."],"a":2,"hint":"Count the things in each sentence. Which word should go with just two?","why":"With just TWO boats, we must say ‘between the two boats’. ‘Among’ is for groups of three or more, like the fish and the flowers."},{"c":"prep-behind-in-front-of","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The tiger crouched ___ the bushes, so the deer could not see it.","o":["in front of","behind","over","across"],"a":1,"hint":"If the deer can’t see the tiger, the bushes must be blocking the view. Where is the tiger?","why":"The tiger hid BEHIND the bushes, so the leaves blocked the deer’s view. In front of the bushes, the deer would spot it at once!"},{"c":"prep-behind-in-front-of","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"If a shark is swimming behind a diver, then the diver is ___ the shark.","o":["behind","beside","under","in front of"],"a":3,"hint":"Picture it: the shark is following the diver. Who is ahead?","why":"If the shark is behind the diver, the diver is ahead — IN FRONT OF the shark. ‘Behind’ and ‘in front of’ are opposite sides."},{"c":"prep-behind-in-front-of","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{R} is first in the lunch queue and {Z} is second. {Z} stands ___ {R}.","o":["in front of","beside","behind","across"],"a":2,"hint":"In a queue, the second person stands at whose back?","why":"The first person is at the front. The second person stands at their back, so {Z} is BEHIND {R}, and {R} is in front of {Z}."},{"c":"prep-into-in","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The diver jumped off the boat ___ the sea with a big splash.","o":["on","into","under","between"],"a":1,"hint":"The diver moves from outside the water to inside it.","why":"‘Into’ shows movement from outside to inside. The diver goes from the boat INTO the sea — splash!"},{"c":"prep-into-in","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"A: “The fish is in the net.” B: “The fish swam into the net.” Which one tells you the fish MOVED inside?","o":["A — ‘in’ shows movement","B — ‘into’ shows where it is","A — ‘in’ shows where it is","B — ‘into’ shows movement"],"a":3,"hint":"One word tells you WHERE something is. The other tells you it went from outside to inside.","why":"‘In’ tells us where something is. ‘Into’ shows movement from outside to inside. So B tells us the fish swam INTO the net. Poor fish!"},{"c":"prep-into-in","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which sentence is NOT correct?","o":["My shark toy is into the box.","The keys are in my pocket.","She poured milk into the glass.","The bird flew into its cage."],"a":0,"hint":"‘Into’ needs something to move from outside to inside. Which thing is just sitting still?","why":"The toy is just sitting there, so it is IN the box. The milk and the bird really moved from outside to inside, so ‘into’ fits them."},{"c":"prep-across-through","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The train went ___ the long, dark tunnel and came out the other side.","o":["across","among","through","between"],"a":2,"hint":"The train goes inside the tunnel and comes out at the far end.","why":"‘Through’ means going in one side and out the other, with something all around you, like a train in a tunnel. ‘Across’ is for flat surfaces like roads."},{"c":"prep-across-through","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"At the zebra crossing, {R} looks both ways and walks ___ the road.","o":["through","into","among","across"],"a":3,"hint":"A road is a flat surface. You go from one side of it to the other.","why":"‘Across’ means from one side of a flat surface to the other, like crossing a road. You can’t walk THROUGH a road — you’d need a tunnel!"},{"c":"prep-across-through","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"The tiger walked ___ the thick forest. Pick the word AND the reason.","o":["across — a forest is a flat surface","through — trees are all around it","through — a forest is a flat surface","across — trees are all around it"],"a":1,"hint":"Is the tiger moving over a flat surface, or with trees on every side?","why":"With trees all around it, the tiger goes THROUGH the forest. We use ‘across’ for flat things, like walking across a field or a road."},{"c":"prep-near-beside","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Our hotel is a two-minute walk from the beach. Which sentence describes it?","o":["The hotel is in the sea.","The hotel is near the sea.","The hotel is under the sea.","The hotel is across the sea."],"a":1,"hint":"A two-minute walk means close by, but not touching the water.","why":"‘Near’ means close to something. The hotel is close to the sea but not inside it — and certainly not under it, unless the sharks are running it!"},{"c":"prep-near-beside","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In class, {R} sits ___ his best friend, side by side.","o":["between","among","beside","behind"],"a":2,"hint":"Side by side means right next to each other.","why":"‘Beside’ means right next to. Side by side = beside! ‘Behind’ would put him at his friend’s back."},{"c":"prep-near-beside","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which of these sentences is WRONG?","o":["The well is near the farm.","Sit beside me, {Z}!","The pond is near our school.","The dog sleeps besides my bed."],"a":3,"hint":"Look closely at the spelling. One extra letter changes the meaning!","why":"‘Beside’ means next to. ‘Besides’ (with an s) means ‘as well as’. So the dog sleeps BESIDE my bed."},{"c":"prep-at-place","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Let’s meet ___ the main gate of the zoo.","o":["on","into","at","among"],"a":2,"hint":"Which little word do we use for a meeting point, like a gate or a bus stop?","why":"We use ‘at’ for a particular point or spot: at the gate, at the bus stop, at the door. ‘On the gate’ would mean climbing on top of it!"},{"c":"prep-at-place","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Someone is knocking ___ the door — go and see who it is!","o":["at","in","under","across"],"a":0,"hint":"The visitor is outside, tapping one spot of the door.","why":"We knock AT the door (‘on the door’ is also fine, but it’s not here). ‘In the door’ would mean being inside the wood!"},{"c":"prep-at-place","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Only ONE of these sentences is correct. Which one?","o":["Dad is at home.","The fish is at the tank.","The bird is at the sky.","My pencil is at the bag."],"a":0,"hint":"Think of phrases we always say with ‘at’, like ‘at school’ or ‘at the bus stop’.","why":"We say ‘at home’, ‘at school’, ‘at the gate’. But fish are IN a tank, birds are IN the sky, and pencils are IN a bag."},{"c":"prep-time-in-on-at","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Most sea turtles crawl onto the beach to lay their eggs ___ night.","o":["on","at","in","between"],"a":1,"hint":"The same word fits “___ noon” and “___ 6 o’clock”.","why":"We say ‘at night’, ‘at noon’ and ‘at 6 o’clock’. Many sea turtles nest in the dark, when the sand is cooler."},{"c":"prep-time-in-on-at","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The monsoon rains usually reach Kerala ___ June.","o":["on","at","across","in"],"a":3,"hint":"Which word do we use with months, like ___ May or ___ December?","why":"We use ‘in’ with months, years and seasons: in June, in 2026, in winter. ‘On’ is for days and dates, like on Monday."},{"c":"prep-time-in-on-at","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Three of these have a time mistake. Which one is correct?","o":["My party is in Sunday.","School reopens on April.","We wake up at 6 o’clock.","We swim on the evening."],"a":2,"hint":"Remember: at + clock time, on + days, in + months.","why":"‘At’ goes with clock times: at 6 o’clock. The others should be ON Sunday, IN April and IN the evening."},{"c":"prep-opposites","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What is the opposite of ‘over’?","o":["near","across","beside","under"],"a":3,"hint":"If ‘over’ means above, which word means below?","why":"‘Over’ means above and ‘under’ means below. A seagull flies OVER the sea; a sawfish swims UNDER the waves."},{"c":"prep-opposites","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out: which pair is NOT a pair of opposites?","o":["over – under","near – beside","in front of – behind","into – out of"],"a":1,"hint":"Opposite pairs point in opposite directions. Which two words mean almost the same?","why":"‘Near’ and ‘beside’ both mean close by — they’re partners, not opposites! The opposite of ‘near’ is ‘far’."},{"c":"prep-opposites","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"‘Into’ is to ‘out of’ as ‘in front of’ is to ___.","o":["behind","near","between","across"],"a":0,"hint":"‘Out of’ is the opposite of ‘into’. Now find the opposite of ‘in front of’.","why":"Each pair is a set of opposites: into ↔ out of, and in front of ↔ behind."},{"c":"prep-for-since","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: My father has been serving in the army ___ 20 years.","o":["from","among","for","since"],"a":2,"hint":"Is ‘20 years’ HOW LONG, or a starting point?","why":"For tells how long: for 20 years. Since needs a starting point, like since 2006."},{"c":"prep-for-since","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: He has not been attending school ___ last Monday.","o":["among","for","into","since"],"a":3,"hint":"‘Last Monday’ is a starting point.","why":"Since + a starting point: since last Monday, since July. For + a length of time: for two days."},{"c":"prep-for-since","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: We have been waiting for her ___ fifteen minutes.","o":["since","at","into","for"],"a":3,"hint":"Fifteen minutes is an amount of time.","why":"Fifteen minutes tells how long, so we use for."},{"c":"prep-for-since","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: which one needs SINCE, not FOR?","o":["___ two hours","___ ten years","___ a long time","___ last Monday"],"a":3,"hint":"Since = a starting point. For = how long.","why":"‘Last Monday’ is a point in time, so since. The others are lengths of time, so for."},{"c":"prep-verb-pairs","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: The boy wanted to catch the dog, so he ran ___ the dog.","o":["between","among","after","since"],"a":2,"hint":"To chase something is to run ___ it.","why":"Run after = chase. ‘The boy ran after the dog’ means he was chasing it."},{"c":"prep-into-in","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: The frogs jumped ___ the well.","o":["into","among","since","for"],"a":0,"hint":"The frogs moved from outside to inside.","why":"Into shows movement from outside to inside: jumped into the well."},{"c":"prep-between-among","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: The Principal distributed the prizes ___ the students.","o":["since","between","among","into"],"a":2,"hint":"How many students: two, or a whole group?","why":"Among is for a group of more than two. Between is for two."},{"c":"prep-between-among","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: The two children were not ready to share the money ___ them.","o":["among","since","between","into"],"a":2,"hint":"Count the children.","why":"Two children → between. A bigger group → among."},{"c":"prep-between-among","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: I placed the table lamp ___ the table, which was ___ the two beds.","o":["between / beside","beside / among","beside / between","among / beside"],"a":2,"hint":"The lamp is next to the table. The table has a bed on each side.","why":"Beside = next to. Between = in the middle of two things: between the two beds."},{"c":"prep-verb-pairs","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: We should not compare one person ___ another.","o":["among","into","with","since"],"a":2,"hint":"compare ___ = put side by side to see the difference","why":"We compare one thing with another. (Some books say ‘compare to’; both are fine.)"},{"c":"prep-verb-pairs","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: The bus collided ___ a train yesterday.","o":["on","to","with","at"],"a":2,"hint":"Collide means crash. Two things crash ___ each other.","why":"We say collided with: the bus and the train hit each other."},{"c":"prep-verb-pairs","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: Ishita was congratulated ___ her remarkable achievement.","o":["into","in","at","on"],"a":3,"hint":"We congratulate someone ___ their success.","why":"We congratulate someone on something: ‘Congratulations on your prize!’"},{"c":"prep-verb-pairs","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: Take the knife away ___ the baby.","o":["into","among","to","from"],"a":3,"hint":"The knife moves away. Away ___ the baby?","why":"Away from shows separation: take the knife away from the baby."},{"c":"prep-verb-pairs","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: Ice cream is made ___ milk and cream.","o":["into","at","by","from"],"a":3,"hint":"What is ice cream made out of?","why":"Made from tells what something is made out of: ice cream is made from milk and cream."},{"c":"prep-in-on","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: The baby is sleeping ___ the bed.","o":["into","of","at","on"],"a":3,"hint":"The baby is lying on the bed's top surface.","why":"On is for a surface: on the bed, on the table."},{"c":"prep-near-beside","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: Rohan is sitting ___ his mother at the party.","o":["into","between","beside","among"],"a":2,"hint":"Side by side…","why":"Beside means right next to. Between needs two people; among needs a group."},{"c":"prep-across-through","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: She was waiting for me ___ the road, under a tree.","o":["since","into","about","across"],"a":3,"hint":"She was on the other side of the road.","why":"Across the road = on the other side of it."},{"c":"prep-behind-in-front-of","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: The police caught the thief ___ the whole crowd.","o":["about","across","over","in front of"],"a":3,"hint":"Everyone in the crowd could see it happen.","why":"In front of = where others can see. It is a three-word (compound) preposition."},{"c":"prep-time-in-on-at","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: The plane arrives ___ 7 o'clock.","o":["in","on","at","since"],"a":2,"hint":"An exact time on the clock…","why":"At for a clock time: at 7 o'clock. On for days, in for months and years."},{"c":"prep-time-in-on-at","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: Riya will submit her project ___ Monday.","o":["since","at","in","on"],"a":3,"hint":"Days of the week take…","why":"On for days and dates: on Monday, on 14 June."},{"c":"compound-prep","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: I finished my project several days ___ the deadline.","o":["in spite of","ahead of","instead of","in case of"],"a":1,"hint":"Before the deadline = ___ it.","why":"Ahead of = before. Finishing ahead of the deadline means finishing early!"},{"c":"compound-prep","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: ___ fire, ring the alarm bell.","o":["Instead of","In spite of","Ahead of","In case of"],"a":3,"hint":"If there is a fire…","why":"In case of = if something happens. In case of fire, ring the alarm bell."},{"c":"compound-prep","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: ___ his poverty, he completed his studies.","o":["In case of","Due to","In place of","In spite of"],"a":3,"hint":"Being poor made it hard, but he still did it.","why":"In spite of = even though there was a problem. ‘Due to his poverty, he completed his studies’ makes no sense."},{"c":"compound-prep","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: She was unable to attend the party ___ her engagement.","o":["instead of","in case of","in spite of","due to"],"a":3,"hint":"Her engagement was the reason she couldn't come.","why":"Due to = because of. It gives the reason."},{"c":"compound-prep","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: Sravani goes to school ___ Yamuni every day.","o":["due to","in case of","in spite of","along with"],"a":3,"hint":"The two girls go together.","why":"Along with = together with."},{"c":"compound-prep","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: Madhu got a good job ___ her own abilities and skills.","o":["in front of","by means of","in case of","instead of"],"a":1,"hint":"Her skills were HOW she got the job.","why":"By means of = with the help of. It tells how something was done."},{"c":"jumbled-sentences","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet, make a sentence: playing / children / the / park / are / in / the","o":["The children are playing in the park.","Children the are playing park in the.","The park are playing in the children.","The children are park in the playing."],"a":0,"hint":"Find the doer first, then the action, then the place.","why":"Who? The children. Doing what? are playing. Where? in the park."},{"c":"jumbled-sentences","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet, make a sentence: rises / east / the / sun / in / the","o":["The sun rises in the east.","Sun the rises in east the.","The east rises in the sun.","The sun rises the in east."],"a":0,"hint":"What rises every morning?","why":"The sun (doer) rises (action) in the east (place)."},{"c":"jumbled-sentences","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet, make a sentence: garden / flowers / beautiful / the / has","o":["The flowers has beautiful garden.","The garden beautiful has flowers.","The garden has beautiful flowers.","Beautiful the garden has flowers."],"a":2,"hint":"A describing word comes just before the thing it describes.","why":"‘Beautiful’ describes the flowers, so it comes right before them: The garden has beautiful flowers."},{"c":"jumbled-sentences","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet, make a sentence: should / elders / respect / we / our","o":["Our elders should respect we.","Respect we should our elders.","We should respect our elders.","We our elders should respect."],"a":2,"hint":"Who should do it? Start with that word.","why":"We (doer) should respect (action) our elders."},{"c":"jumbled-sentences","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet, make a sentence: every / school / day / I / go / to","o":["I go every to school day.","I to school go every day.","I go to school every day.","School go I to every day."],"a":2,"hint":"Time words like ‘every day’ usually come at the end.","why":"I (doer) go (action) to school (place) every day (when)."},{"c":"exp-what","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes, experience writing: a personal experience tells about…","o":["an event, memory or trip that happened, and how you felt","a made-up story about a dragon who lives in a castle","facts about a famous place, with numbers and dates","how to make or do something, step by step, with a list of things you need"],"a":0,"hint":"It is about something that really happened to YOU.","why":"Experience writing = a real event, memory or trip, plus your feelings about it."},{"c":"exp-parts","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: the three parts of experience writing, in order, are…","o":["Title → Date and place → Signature at the end","Introduction → Body (middle) → Conclusion (ending)","Conclusion (ending) → Body (middle) → Introduction","Body (middle) → Introduction → Conclusion (ending)"],"a":1,"hint":"Beginning, middle, end.","why":"Start with an introduction, tell what happened in the body, and finish with a conclusion."},{"c":"exp-pronouns","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: which pronouns do we mostly use in experience writing?","o":["you, your","it, its","he, she, they","I, me, we"],"a":3,"hint":"You are writing about yourself.","why":"It is your own experience, so we use first-person pronouns: I, me, we."},{"c":"exp-tense","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: experience writing is mostly written in the…","o":["simple present tense","future tense","past tense","present continuous tense"],"a":2,"hint":"The event has already happened.","why":"The event already happened, so we use the past tense: I went, we played. The future tense can share future plans at the end."},{"c":"exp-ending","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Topic: ‘A surprise party for Mom's birthday’. Which line makes the best CONCLUSION?","o":["First, we bought balloons, streamers and a chocolate cake from the bakery.","It was the happiest evening, and I can't wait to plan the next surprise!","Then Dad hid the gifts under the bed so that Mom wouldn't find them.","Last Sunday was Mom's birthday, so Dad and I planned a big surprise."],"a":1,"hint":"A conclusion ends the piece and shares feelings.","why":"The conclusion closes the story with a feeling or a final thought. The other lines belong in the introduction or the body."},{"c":"exp-feelings","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"In experience writing we also say how we felt. Which sentence does that?","o":["We sat in rows in the hall.","The exam had ten questions.","I felt nervous before my first exam.","My first exam was on Monday."],"a":2,"hint":"Look for a feeling word.","why":"‘I felt nervous’ shares a feeling. The other sentences only give facts."}],"r-frac":[{"c":"equal-parts","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A fraction names a part of a whole. First, the whole must be cut into…","o":["Equal parts","Big and small parts","Exactly 2 parts","Exactly 10 parts"],"a":0,"hint":"Think of cutting a cake so that everyone gets a fair share.","why":"A fraction counts EQUAL parts: 1/4 means 1 of 4 equal parts. A whole can be cut into 2, 3, 8 or 100 parts — but they must all be the same size!"},{"c":"equal-parts","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"{R} cuts a roti into 4 pieces: 2 big, 2 small. He says each piece is 1/4. Is he right?","o":["Yes — there are 4 pieces","Yes — 1/4 means any 1 of 4 pieces","No — a roti can only be cut in half","No — the 4 pieces are not equal"],"a":3,"hint":"Count the pieces, then check their sizes. What must be true before we can say 1/4?","why":"1/4 means one of 4 EQUAL parts. A big piece is more than 1/4 and a small piece is less. Counting 4 pieces isn't enough — they must be the same size."},{"c":"equal-parts","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Three sharks share one big fish so that each gets exactly 1/3 of it. How must the fish be cut?","o":["Into 3 pieces of any size","Into 2 equal halves","Into 3 equal pieces","Into 4 equal pieces"],"a":2,"hint":"1/3 means one of how many parts? And what must be true about those parts?","why":"1/3 = one of 3 EQUAL parts. With pieces of any size, the hungriest shark would grab the biggest piece — and that is more than 1/3!"},{"c":"equal-parts","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Which of these does NOT show quarters (fourths)?","o":["A pizza cut into 4 same-size slices","A square folded into 4 equal squares","A rope cut into 4 equal lengths","A cake cut into 4 pieces of mixed sizes"],"a":3,"hint":"Quarters means 4 parts — but what else must be true about those parts?","why":"Quarters are 4 EQUAL parts. The pizza, the square and the rope are all split equally. The cake has 4 pieces too, but of mixed sizes — so they are not quarters."},{"c":"numerator-denominator","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In the fraction 3/8, which number is the denominator?","o":["3","Both 3 and 8","The line between them","8"],"a":3,"hint":"The denominator is the number Down below the line. D for Down!","why":"8 is the denominator: the whole is cut into 8 equal parts. 3 is the numerator: it counts the parts we are talking about."},{"c":"numerator-denominator","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What does the numerator (the top number) of a fraction tell us?","o":["How many equal parts the whole has","How big the whole is","How many equal parts we are counting","How many wholes there are"],"a":2,"hint":"The bottom number tells how many equal parts there are in all. So what does the top one count?","why":"The numerator counts the parts we're talking about — eaten, coloured or taken. The denominator tells how many equal parts the whole was cut into."},{"c":"numerator-denominator","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Tara ate 2 of the 5 equal slices of a cake. She wrote 5/2 for the part she ate. What went wrong?","o":["It should be 2/7","It should be 3/5","She swapped numerator and denominator","Nothing — 5/2 is right"],"a":2,"hint":"Which number counts ALL the slices? Does it go on top or at the bottom?","why":"She ate 2 of 5 equal slices → 2/5. The parts eaten (2) go on top; the total parts (5) go at the bottom. She wrote it upside down! (3/5 is the part left.)"},{"c":"numerator-denominator","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"A chocolate bar has 12 equal squares. {R} eats 5. In the fraction for the part he ate, what is the denominator?","o":["5","7","12","17"],"a":2,"hint":"The denominator counts ALL the equal squares in the bar — not just the eaten ones.","why":"The bar has 12 equal squares, so the denominator is 12. He ate 5, so the fraction is 5/12. (The 7 squares left make 7/12.)"},{"c":"read-write-fractions","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"How do we write “three-fifths” as a fraction?","o":["5/3","1/3","3/5","1/5"],"a":2,"hint":"“Fifths” tells how many equal parts the whole has. Where does that number go?","why":"Three-fifths = 3 of 5 equal parts → 3/5. The “fifths” (5) is the denominator at the bottom; the “three” (3) is the numerator on top."},{"c":"read-write-fractions","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"How do we read the fraction 2/7?","o":["Seven-halves","Twenty-seven","Two-sevenths","Two and seven"],"a":2,"hint":"Say the top number first, then the bottom one as a “th” word, like fourths or fifths.","why":"We read the numerator first, then the denominator as a “th” word: 2/7 = two-sevenths. “Twenty-seven” is a whole number, not a fraction!"},{"c":"read-write-fractions","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"{R} filled “three-quarters” of a fish tank with water. Which fraction is that?","o":["1/4","3/4","4/3","1/3"],"a":1,"hint":"A quarter is one of 4 equal parts. How many quarters did he fill?","why":"A quarter = 1/4, one of 4 equal parts. Three-quarters = 3 of those parts = 3/4. It's the same as “three-fourths”."},{"c":"read-write-fractions","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which fraction is written in words WRONGLY?","o":["2/5 — five-halves","1/2 — one-half","3/4 — three-fourths","4/9 — four-ninths"],"a":0,"hint":"Read each fraction top number first. Does its word name match?","why":"2/5 is “two-fifths”. “Five-halves” would be 5/2 — top and bottom swapped! The others are right: one-half, three-fourths, four-ninths."},{"c":"part-of-a-whole","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A pizza is cut into 8 equal slices and 3 are eaten. What fraction of the pizza is LEFT?","o":["3/8","3/5","8/5","5/8"],"a":3,"hint":"First count the slices left. The whole pizza still has 8 equal slices.","why":"8 − 3 = 5 slices left, out of 8 equal slices → 5/8. 3/8 is the part eaten, not the part left. 3/5 compares eaten with left slices."},{"c":"part-of-a-whole","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A window has 9 equal glass panes. 4 panes are blue and the rest are clear. What fraction of the window is blue?","o":["4/5","5/9","4/9","9/4"],"a":2,"hint":"Count ALL the panes for the bottom number.","why":"4 blue panes out of 9 equal panes → 4/9. 4/5 compares blue with clear panes, but the denominator must count all 9. 5/9 is the clear part."},{"c":"part-of-a-whole","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"A chocolate bar has 10 equal pieces. What if {R} eats ALL 10 pieces? What fraction did he eat?","o":["1/10","10/10 — the whole bar","0/10","10/1"],"a":1,"hint":"He ate every one of the 10 equal pieces.","why":"10 out of 10 pieces = 10/10 — the whole bar! When the top and bottom numbers are the same, the fraction is one whole."},{"c":"part-of-a-whole","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"A ribbon is cut into 7 equal pieces and 3 are used for a gift. What fraction is used, and why?","o":["3/7 — 3 of the 7 equal pieces","3/4 — 3 used, 4 not used","4/7 — 4 pieces are still left","7/3 — 7 pieces, 3 used"],"a":0,"hint":"The bottom number counts ALL the equal pieces of the ribbon.","why":"3 of 7 equal pieces are used → 3/7. 3/4 compares used with unused pieces, but a fraction compares the part with the WHOLE. 4/7 is the part left."},{"c":"unit-fractions","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which of these is a unit fraction?","o":["9/1","1/9","2/9","9/9"],"a":1,"hint":"“Unit” means one. Check the top number.","why":"A unit fraction has 1 as its numerator: 1/2, 1/5, 1/9… It means just ONE of the equal parts. 9/9 is a whole, and 9/1 is nine wholes."},{"c":"unit-fractions","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"One pizza is shared equally by 3 friends. A same-size pizza is shared equally by 6 friends. Who gets a bigger slice?","o":["Each of the 3 friends","Each of the 6 friends","Both slices are the same size","Whoever cuts the pizza"],"a":0,"hint":"More people sharing the same pizza means… bigger or smaller slices?","why":"Each of 3 friends gets 1/3; each of 6 friends gets 1/6. The same pizza cut into MORE parts makes SMALLER parts, so 1/3 is bigger than 1/6."},{"c":"unit-fractions","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Kabir says, “1/8 is bigger than 1/4, because 8 is bigger than 4.” What is the truth?","o":["Right — the bigger number wins","Right — 1/8 has more parts","Wrong — both are equal","Wrong — 1/4 is bigger"],"a":3,"hint":"Picture one roti cut into 4 equal parts and another into 8. Which piece is bigger?","why":"Cutting into 8 parts makes each part smaller than cutting into 4, so 1/8 < 1/4. For unit fractions, a bigger denominator means a SMALLER piece!"},{"c":"unit-fractions","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"A watermelon is cut into equal slices for 7 people, one slice each. What fraction does each person get?","o":["7/1","1/7","7/7","1/6"],"a":1,"hint":"How many equal slices is the watermelon cut into? Each person gets ONE of them.","why":"7 equal slices, one for each person → 1/7. That's a unit fraction: 1 on top. All 7 slices together make 7/7, the whole watermelon."},{"c":"fractions-on-number-line","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"On a number line, 0 to 1 is split into 5 equal parts. A turtle starts at 0 and swims 3 parts. Where is it?","o":["3/5","3/6","5/3","2/5"],"a":0,"hint":"Count the equal PARTS (the gaps) between 0 and 1, not the marks.","why":"0 to 1 has 5 equal parts, so each part is 1/5. Three parts from 0 → 3/5. There are 6 marks, but we count the gaps — so it's not 3/6."},{"c":"fractions-on-number-line","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A number line from 0 to 1 is cut into 4 equal parts. Which fraction is at the FIRST mark after 0?","o":["1/3","4/1","1/5","1/4"],"a":3,"hint":"Each equal part between 0 and 1 is one ___ of the whole. How many parts are there?","why":"4 equal parts → each part is 1/4. The marks after 0 are 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, and then 4/4 = 1."},{"c":"fractions-on-number-line","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"On a number line from 0 to 1, which of these fractions sits CLOSEST to 1?","o":["2/9","7/9","5/9","4/9"],"a":1,"hint":"All are ninths. Which one needs the fewest jumps to reach 9/9 = 1?","why":"Ninths go 1/9, 2/9 … up to 9/9 = 1. 7/9 is just 2 small jumps from 1. 2/9 is close to 0, far from 1."},{"c":"fractions-on-number-line","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Dev splits 0 to 1 on a number line into 3 equal parts. Where should he mark 2/3?","o":["At the 2nd mark after 0","At the 3rd mark after 0","Just after 1","Exactly halfway"],"a":0,"hint":"Each equal part is 1/3. How many of those parts make 2/3?","why":"The marks after 0 are 1/3, 2/3 and 3/3 = 1. So 2/3 is the 2nd mark — a bit past halfway, and before 1."},{"c":"like-unlike-fractions","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Like fractions are fractions that have the same…","o":["Numerator","Value","Number of digits","Denominator"],"a":3,"hint":"Like fractions come from wholes cut into the same number of equal parts.","why":"Like fractions share the same denominator, like 2/9, 5/9 and 8/9. They needn't be equal — they're just cut into the same number of parts."},{"c":"like-unlike-fractions","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Three of these are like fractions. Which one is NOT?","o":["3/7","5/7","1/7","7/8"],"a":3,"hint":"Check the bottom number of each fraction.","why":"3/7, 5/7 and 1/7 all have 7 at the bottom — like fractions. 7/8 has 8 at the bottom, so it's the odd one out. The 7 on top is a trap!"},{"c":"like-unlike-fractions","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Change ONE number in 4/9 so that it becomes a like fraction with 2/7. What could it become?","o":["4/7","2/9","7/9","4/2"],"a":0,"hint":"Like fractions share the same bottom number. What is the bottom number of 2/7?","why":"To be like 2/7, it needs 7 at the bottom. Swap the 9 for a 7: 4/9 → 4/7. Changing the top number doesn't help — 2/9 is still ninths."},{"c":"like-unlike-fractions","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Meera says 3/4 and 3/5 are like fractions because both have 3 on top. Is she right?","o":["No — like fractions need the same denominator","Yes — the same numerator is enough","Yes — both are less than 1","No — like fractions must be equal"],"a":0,"hint":"Which number decides whether two fractions are “like” — the top or the bottom?","why":"Like fractions share the same DENOMINATOR. 3/4 and 3/5 have 4 and 5 at the bottom, so they're unlike. Like fractions needn't be equal: 1/7 and 6/7 are like."},{"c":"compare-like-fractions","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which is bigger: 5/8 or 3/8?","o":["3/8","They are equal","5/8","Can't tell"],"a":2,"hint":"Both are eighths — same-size parts. Which fraction has more of them?","why":"Same denominator means same-size parts. 5 parts are more than 3 parts, so 5/8 > 3/8."},{"c":"compare-like-fractions","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"A sawfish swam 4/10 of the way across a lagoon. A turtle swam 7/10 of the way. Who swam farther, and why?","o":["Sawfish — 4/10 has a smaller top","Turtle — 7 tenths beat 4 tenths","Same — both have 10 at the bottom","Sawfish — sawfish swim faster"],"a":1,"hint":"Both are tenths of the same trip. Compare how many tenths each one swam.","why":"Same lagoon, same-size tenths: 7 tenths is more than 4 tenths, so the turtle went farther. When the bottom numbers are the same, the bigger top number wins!"},{"c":"compare-like-fractions","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which comparison is WRONG?","o":["2/6 > 5/6","7/9 > 4/9","1/5 < 3/5","6/11 < 8/11"],"a":0,"hint":"> means “is bigger than” and < means “is smaller than”. Test each one.","why":"2/6 is LESS than 5/6, because 2 parts < 5 parts of the same size. It should be 2/6 < 5/6. The other three are correct."},{"c":"compare-like-fractions","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Which fraction is MORE than 4/9 but LESS than 8/9?","o":["3/9","6/9","9/9","2/9"],"a":1,"hint":"All are ninths, so the parts are the same size. Compare how many ninths each one has.","why":"With ninths, just compare the tops: 4 < 6 < 8, so 4/9 < 6/9 < 8/9. 9/9 is a whole — too big. 2/9 and 3/9 are too small."},{"c":"order-like-fractions","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"Put in order from SMALLEST to BIGGEST: 5/12, 1/12, 11/12, 7/12","o":["1/12, 5/12, 7/12, 11/12","11/12, 7/12, 5/12, 1/12","1/12, 7/12, 5/12, 11/12","5/12, 1/12, 11/12, 7/12"],"a":0,"hint":"Same denominator — so just put the top numbers in order.","why":"All are twelfths, so compare the tops: 1 < 5 < 7 < 11. Smallest to biggest: 1/12, 5/12, 7/12, 11/12. This is called ascending order."},{"c":"order-like-fractions","t":"Order","p":50,"q":"Arrange in DESCENDING order (biggest first): 3/10, 9/10, 6/10","o":["3/10, 6/10, 9/10","9/10, 6/10, 3/10","6/10, 9/10, 3/10","9/10, 3/10, 6/10"],"a":1,"hint":"Descending means going down, like stairs — start with the biggest.","why":"All are tenths, so the biggest top number gives the biggest fraction: 9/10 > 6/10 > 3/10. Descending = biggest to smallest."},{"c":"order-like-fractions","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Three same-size fish tanks are 2/6, 5/6 and 4/6 full. Which tank needs the MOST water to be full?","o":["The 5/6 full tank","The 2/6 full tank","The 4/6 full tank","All need the same"],"a":1,"hint":"The emptiest tank needs the most water. Which fraction is the smallest?","why":"2/6 is the smallest, so that tank is the emptiest — it needs 4/6 more. The 5/6 tank needs only 1/6 more, and the 4/6 tank needs 2/6."},{"c":"fraction-of-collection","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"There are 12 fish in a tank. 1/4 of them are clownfish. How many clownfish are there?","o":["4","8","3","48"],"a":2,"hint":"Share the 12 fish into 4 equal groups. How many fish are in one group?","why":"1/4 of 12 means 12 ÷ 4 = 3. Four equal groups of 3 fish — one group is clownfish. (4 is the denominator, not the answer!)"},{"c":"fraction-of-collection","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A bay has 20 sharks, and 3/5 of them are hammerheads. How many hammerheads are there?","o":["4","12","15","8"],"a":1,"hint":"First find 1/5 of 20. Then you need 3 of those parts.","why":"1/5 of 20 = 20 ÷ 5 = 4. So 3/5 = 3 × 4 = 12 hammerheads. (4 is only ONE fifth; the other 2/5 are 8 sharks.)"},{"c":"fraction-of-collection","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{R} has 18 shark cards. He gives 1/3 of them to {Z}. How many cards does {R} have LEFT?","o":["6","15","12","3"],"a":2,"hint":"First find 1/3 of 18 — that's what he gives away. Then subtract.","why":"1/3 of 18 = 18 ÷ 3 = 6 cards given away. 18 − 6 = 12 cards left — that's 2/3 of his cards!"},{"c":"fraction-of-collection","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Ira says 1/2 of 14 ladoos is 12, because 14 − 2 = 12. What is the right answer?","o":["12 — Ira is right","2 — the bottom number","28 — double 14","7 — share 14 into 2 equal groups"],"a":3,"hint":"Try Ira's way on 10 ladoos: 10 − 2 = 8. Is 8 really half of 10?","why":"1/2 of 14 = 14 ÷ 2 = 7. To find a fraction of a group, we SHARE it into equal groups — we don't subtract the denominator."},{"c":"fraction-of-collection","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"1/5 of the starfish in a rock pool is 4 starfish. How many starfish are in the pool altogether?","o":["9","16","5","20"],"a":3,"hint":"If ONE-fifth is 4, there are 5 equal groups of 4. How many in all?","why":"1/5 is one of 5 equal groups, and each group has 4 starfish. So 5 × 4 = 20. Check: 1/5 of 20 = 20 ÷ 5 = 4."}],"r-hindi":[{"c":"find-the-real-cause","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"खरगोश को क्यों लगा कि आसमान गिर रहा है?","o":["उसने आसमान टूटते देखा","लोमड़ी ने उसे बताया","“धम्म” सुना, पर कुछ दिखा नहीं","तेज़ बारिश हो रही थी"],"a":2,"hint":"He was asleep under a tree. What did he HEAR, and what did he SEE when he looked around?","why":"He heard a loud “धम्म!”, looked इधर-उधर (here and there) and saw nothing — so he only guessed the sky fell. He never actually saw it!"},{"c":"find-the-real-cause","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"सब जानवर भागे, पर शेर ने ऐसा क्या किया जिससे सच पता चला?","o":["सबको रोककर पूछा, फिर जाकर देखा","वह भी सबके साथ भागा","पेड़ पर चढ़कर छिप गया","खरगोश की बात मान ली"],"a":0,"hint":"The lion roared “रुको!” (stop!). Did he want to run, or to check?","why":"शेर asked “किसने कहा?” and “कहाँ गिर रहा है?”, then said “चलो, चलकर देखें” — let's go and see. Checking for yourself beats running with the crowd!"},{"c":"find-the-real-cause","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"अगर शेर भी डरकर भाग जाता और कुछ न पूछता, तो क्या होता?","o":["सबको सच तुरंत पता चल जाता","आसमान सच में गिर जाता","खरगोश सबसे पहले रुकता","सब डरकर भागते ही रहते"],"a":3,"hint":"Who was the only animal that stopped to ask questions?","why":"Only the lion stopped to check. Without him, nobody would find out it was just a fruit — everyone would keep running scared!"},{"c":"find-the-real-cause","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"“चेरापूँजी के मेहमान” में घर पानी से कैसे भर गया?","o":["कोई नल खुला रह गया","खुली खिड़की से बादल आ गए","पानी की टंकी लीक हो गई","छत में बड़ा छेद था"],"a":1,"hint":"All the taps were closed. Who are the “मेहमान” (guests) in the title?","why":"In Cherrapunji's rainy days clouds float very low. The window was open, so the clouds came in like guests! Like the rabbit, he first guessed wrong: a tap."},{"c":"asmaan-gira-story","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"पेड़ से “धम्म” से गिरने वाला बड़ा-सा फल कौन-सा हो सकता है?","o":["कद्दू","तरबूज","अंगूर","कटहल"],"a":3,"hint":"It grows high up on a big tree, and it is big and heavy.","why":"कटहल (jackfruit) grows on tall trees and is heavy — धम्म! कद्दू (pumpkin) and तरबूज (watermelon) grow on creepers on the ground; अंगूर (grapes) are tiny."},{"c":"asmaan-gira-story","t":"Order","p":50,"q":"भागने वालों की लाइन में सबसे पीछे कौन था?","o":["लोमड़ी","हाथी","भालू","शेर"],"a":1,"hint":"शेर never ran at all — he stopped everyone. Who joined the running last?","why":"The line: खरगोश → लोमड़ी (fox) → भालू (bear) → सबसे पीछे हाथी (elephant). शेर (lion) never ran — he stopped them!"},{"c":"asmaan-gira-story","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"शेर ने पूछा, “किसने कहा?” हाथी ने किसका नाम लिया?","o":["खरगोश","लोमड़ी","भालू","किसी का नहीं"],"a":2,"hint":"हाथी only knew what the animal just ahead of him in the line had told him.","why":"It was a chain! हाथी: “भालू ने कहा।” भालू: “लोमड़ी ने कहा।” लोमड़ी: “खरगोश ने कहा।” Everyone just passed on what they heard."},{"c":"asmaan-gira-story","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"“खरगोश चौंककर उठ गया।” — “चौंककर” का मतलब?","o":["Startled, with a jump","Slowly and sleepily","Happily","Hungrily"],"a":0,"hint":"A sudden loud “धम्म!” woke him up. How would YOU wake up?","why":"चौंकना = to be startled by something sudden. The loud धम्म made the rabbit jump awake!"},{"c":"sun-and-its-names","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"सुमन: “सूरज एक ग्रह है।” रवि: “यह एक तारा है।” किसकी बात ग़लत थी?","o":["रवि की","सुमन की","दोनों की","किसी की नहीं"],"a":1,"hint":"ग्रह = planet, तारा = star. Which one makes its own light?","why":"सुमन was wrong. The teacher said “उत्तम! सूर्य एक तारा है।” The Sun is a star (तारा) — it shines by itself. Planets (ग्रह) like Earth don't."},{"c":"sun-and-its-names","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"अध्यापक ने भास्कर से कहा, “आदित्य तुम ही तो हो।” क्यों?","o":["भास्कर का मतलब भी सूर्य है","भास्कर सबसे होशियार था","भास्कर ने यान बनाया था","भास्कर का घर सूरज के पास है"],"a":0,"hint":"आदित्य means सूर्य (Sun). So what could the name भास्कर mean?","why":"आदित्य = सूर्य, and another name for the Sun is भास्कर. So Bhaskar is an ‘Aditya’ too — and so are दिनेश and रवि!"},{"c":"sun-and-its-names","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"इनमें से कौन-सा नाम सूर्य (Sun) का नहीं है?","o":["रवि","दिनेश","चंद्र","भास्कर"],"a":2,"hint":"One of these shines at night, not in the day.","why":"रवि, दिनेश and भास्कर all mean Sun — the teacher said the class had two more ‘Adityas’: दिनेश and रवि. चंद्र means Moon!"},{"c":"sun-and-its-names","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"दिनेश के पिताजी ने सूर्य की ओर देखने से मना किया। अध्यापक ने क्या कहा?","o":["धूप का चश्मा पहनकर देखो","दोपहर में देखना ठीक है","पिताजी ग़लत कह रहे हैं","पिताजी सही हैं, आँखों के लिए ठीक नहीं"],"a":3,"hint":"Think about your eyes. Is staring at something so bright ever safe?","why":"“सही कहा आपके पिताजी ने।” Never look straight at the Sun — it can hurt your eyes. That's why Aditya-L1's pictures are such a treat!"},{"c":"aditya-l1","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"आदित्य-एल 1 का मुख्य काम क्या है?","o":["चाँद पर उतरना","मंगल ग्रह पर जाना","सूर्य की जानकारी जुटाना","बारिश लाना"],"a":2,"hint":"आदित्य = सूर्य. The name gives you a clue!","why":"Aditya-L1 is like Chandrayaan, but for the Sun (सूर्य): how it changes, how hot it is, and whether its heat affects our Earth."},{"c":"aditya-l1","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"आदित्य-एल 1 सूर्य की इतनी गरमी में जलता क्यों नहीं?","o":["वह बर्फ़ से बना है","वह सुरक्षित दूरी पर रुका है","वह सिर्फ़ रात में काम करता है","सूरज उसे पहचानता है"],"a":1,"hint":"Did Aditya-L1 land ON the Sun? Remember what the teacher told पूर्वा.","why":"It did NOT go onto the Sun. It stays at a safe distance (सुरक्षित दूरी) — the L1 point — and takes pictures of the Sun from there."},{"c":"aditya-l1","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"आदित्य-एल 1 ने सूर्य के कितने रंगों के चित्र भेजे?","o":["ग्यारह","सात","आठ","दस"],"a":0,"hint":"More than the 7 colours of a rainbow!","why":"It sent pictures of the Sun in ग्यारह (11) colours. सात (7) is tempting — that's the rainbow, and the horses in Vani's story!"},{"c":"viram-chinh","t":"विराम चिह्न","p":50,"q":"“ये पुस्तकें किसकी हैं” — अंत में कौन-सा चिह्न लगेगा?","o":["। पूर्ण विराम","? प्रश्नवाचक","! विस्मयादिबोधक",", अल्प विराम"],"a":1,"hint":"“किसकी” means “whose”. Is the sentence telling something, or asking?","why":"किसकी (whose) asks something, so the sentence ends with ? — the question mark (प्रश्नवाचक चिह्न)."},{"c":"viram-chinh","t":"विराम चिह्न","p":100,"q":"“अरे इतना बड़ा हाथी” — वाक्य के अंत में कौन-सा चिह्न लगेगा?","o":[", अल्प विराम","। पूर्ण विराम","? प्रश्नवाचक","! विस्मयादिबोधक"],"a":3,"hint":"“अरे” shows surprise. Which mark shows a big feeling?","why":"“अरे! इतना बड़ा हाथी!” — surprise needs ! (विस्मयादिबोधक चिह्न), the exclamation mark. It goes at the end, and can follow अरे too."},{"c":"viram-chinh","t":"विराम चिह्न","p":100,"q":"“मैंने खरगोश लोमड़ी भालू और हाथी देखे।” — नामों के बीच क्या लगेगा?","o":[", अल्प विराम","। पूर्ण विराम","! विस्मयादिबोधक","? प्रश्नवाचक"],"a":0,"hint":"In a list of names, which small mark gives a tiny pause?","why":"A list needs , (अल्प विराम, comma) for a short pause: “खरगोश, लोमड़ी, भालू और हाथी”."},{"c":"viram-chinh","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"किस वाक्य में विराम चिह्न ग़लत है?","o":["सूरज एक तारा है।","क्या तुम मेरे साथ चलोगे।","वाह! कितना बड़ा फल है!","तुम कहाँ जा रहे हो?"],"a":1,"hint":"Find a sentence that ASKS something but doesn't end like a question.","why":"“क्या तुम मेरे साथ चलोगे?” is asking, so it needs ? not ।. The other three are correct."},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"मुहावरा “आसमान छूना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["बहुत ऊँचाई पर पहुँचना","हाथ ऊपर उठाना","पेड़ पर चढ़ना","बादल पकड़ना"],"a":0,"hint":"Nobody really touches the sky! Think of a rocket going very, very high.","why":"आसमान छूना = to reach great heights, like huge success or very high prices: “दाम आसमान छू रहे हैं।” No real touching needed!"},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"खोई गेंद ढूँढ़ने के लिए {RH} ने “ज़मीन-आसमान एक कर दिया।” मतलब?","o":["उसने ज़मीन खोद दी","वह आसमान में उड़ा","उसने बहुत कोशिश की","उसने ढूँढ़ना छोड़ दिया"],"a":2,"hint":"Joining the earth and the sky would take a HUGE effort…","why":"ज़मीन-आसमान एक करना = to try your very hardest, searching everywhere — under, over and behind everything!"},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"डरकर बहुत तेज़ भाग जाने के लिए कौन-सा मुहावरा है?","o":["आँखों का तारा होना","आसमान छूना","ईद का चाँद होना","नौ दो ग्यारह होना"],"a":3,"hint":"Picture the animals in आसमान गिरा running off. Which phrase sounds like a quick escape?","why":"नौ दो ग्यारह होना = to run away fast. In आसमान गिरा, all the animals were नौ दो ग्यारह — until the lion said “रुको!”"},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“{RH} अपने पापा की आँखों का तारा है।” — इसका मतलब?","o":["उसे तारे देखना पसंद है","वह पापा को बहुत प्यारा है","उसकी आँखें चमकती हैं","उसकी आँख में कुछ गिरा"],"a":1,"hint":"This is a मुहावरा — it isn't about real stars or real eyes.","why":"आँखों का तारा = someone very dear and loved. There's no real star in anyone's eye!"},{"c":"vilom","t":"विलोम","p":50,"q":"“दूर” का विलोम (opposite) क्या है?","o":["पास","दूरी","ऊपर","बाहर"],"a":0,"hint":"The Sun is दूर (far) from us. Your school bag is right next to you — it is…?","why":"दूर = far, पास = near. दूरी only means distance — it's not the opposite."},{"c":"vilom","t":"विलोम","p":50,"q":"“प्रकाश” का विलोम क्या है?","o":["धूप","चमक","अंधकार","सूरज"],"a":2,"hint":"प्रकाश = light. What is there when all the lights go off?","why":"प्रकाश (light) ↔ अंधकार (darkness). धूप, चमक and सूरज all go WITH light, so none of them can be its opposite."},{"c":"vilom","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"किस जोड़ी में विलोम (opposite) ग़लत है?","o":["आगे — पीछे","पुराना — नया","ऊपर — ऊँचा","बड़ा — छोटा"],"a":2,"hint":"In an opposite pair, the two words mean opposite things. Which pair means almost the same?","why":"ऊपर (up) and ऊँचा (high) mean almost the SAME. ऊपर का विलोम is नीचे. The other pairs are true opposites."},{"c":"ling","t":"लिंग","p":50,"q":"“हाथी” का स्त्रीलिंग (female) क्या है?","o":["हाथिन","हथिनी","हाथा","हथौड़ी"],"a":1,"hint":"Like शेर → शेरनी. But the हा gets shorter: ह…","why":"हाथी → हथिनी (female elephant), like शेर → शेरनी and मोर → मोरनी. And हथौड़ी? That's a hammer!"},{"c":"ling","t":"लिंग","p":100,"q":"दीपक ने कहा, “मैं बड़ा होकर अंतरिक्ष वैज्ञानिक ___।”","o":["बनूँगी","बनेगी","बनूँगा","बनोगे"],"a":2,"hint":"दीपक is a boy talking about himself. A boy says “मैं जाता हूँ”, not “जाती हूँ”.","why":"A boy speaking about himself uses -गा: “मैं बनूँगा।” सुमन, a girl, said “मैं भी बड़ी होकर… बनूँगी।”"},{"c":"ling","t":"लिंग","p":100,"q":"कहानी “आसमान गिरा” में कौन-सा जानवर स्त्रीलिंग (feminine) है?","o":["भालू","खरगोश","हाथी","लोमड़ी"],"a":3,"hint":"Check the verbs in the story: लगा is masculine, लगी is feminine.","why":"“लोमड़ी भी भागने लगी” — लगी shows लोमड़ी is feminine. But “भालू भी… भागने लगा” and “हाथी भी भागने लगा” are masculine."},{"c":"vachan","t":"वचन","p":50,"q":"“मछली” का बहुवचन (plural) क्या है?","o":["मछलियाँ","मछलीयाँ","मछलें","मछलीओं"],"a":0,"hint":"Like नदी → नदियाँ: the long ई becomes a short इ.","why":"मछली → मछलियाँ. The ई turns into इ + याँ, just like नदी → नदियाँ and तितली → तितलियाँ."},{"c":"vachan","t":"वचन","p":100,"q":"कौन-सा शब्द बहुवचन में भी वैसा ही रहता है?","o":["कहानी","चिड़िया","भालू","बिल्ली"],"a":2,"hint":"Try saying “चार ___”. Which word doesn't change at all?","why":"एक भालू, चार भालू — no change! But कहानी → कहानियाँ, चिड़िया → चिड़ियाँ, बिल्ली → बिल्लियाँ."},{"c":"vachan","t":"वचन","p":100,"q":"“पेड़ से एक फल गिरा।” — इसे बहुवचन में कैसे लिखेंगे?","o":["पेड़ से कई फल गिरा।","पेड़ से कई फलें गिरीं।","पेड़ से कई फलों गिरे।","पेड़ से कई फल गिरे।"],"a":3,"hint":"फल stays फल, but the verb गिरा must change too.","why":"फल doesn't change (एक फल, कई फल), but the verb does: गिरा → गिरे. So: “पेड़ से कई फल गिरे।”"},{"c":"sangya","t":"संज्ञा","p":50,"q":"शब्द-पिटारे से संज्ञा (naming word) चुनो।","o":["गरम","मैं","सूर्य","सात"],"a":2,"hint":"A संज्ञा is the name of a person, place, animal or thing.","why":"सूर्य names a thing → संज्ञा. गरम describes (विशेषण), मैं stands in for a name (सर्वनाम), सात is a number word."},{"c":"sangya","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"कौन-सा शब्द संज्ञा नहीं है?","o":["खरगोश","जंगल","पेड़","भागा"],"a":3,"hint":"Can you point at it? Or is it something you DO?","why":"खरगोश, जंगल and पेड़ are names → संज्ञा. भागा (ran) is an action — a क्रिया (verb)."},{"c":"animal-names","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"Crab को हिंदी में क्या कहते हैं?","o":["कछुआ","केकड़ा","मेंढक","घोंघा"],"a":1,"hint":"It walks sideways on the beach and has two big pincers.","why":"केकड़ा = crab. कछुआ = turtle, मेंढक = frog, घोंघा = snail."},{"c":"animal-names","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"“मगरमच्छ” कौन-सा जानवर है?","o":["Lizard","Snake","Chameleon","Crocodile"],"a":3,"hint":"A huge reptile with a long snout and lots of teeth that lies in rivers.","why":"मगरमच्छ = crocodile. छिपकली = lizard, साँप = snake, गिरगिट = chameleon (the colour-changer!)."},{"c":"animal-names","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"“कछुआ” को English में क्या कहते हैं?","o":["Turtle / tortoise","Starfish","Octopus","Seal"],"a":0,"hint":"It carries its house — a hard shell — on its back.","why":"कछुआ = turtle (in water) or tortoise (on land). Sea turtles swim thousands of kilometres across oceans!"},{"c":"animal-names","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"कौन-सा जानवर पानी में नहीं रहता?","o":["मछली","शार्क","ऊँट","डॉल्फ़िन"],"a":2,"hint":"One of these is called the ‘ship of the desert’.","why":"ऊँट (camel) lives in the desert — रेगिस्तान का जहाज़! मछली (fish), शार्क (shark) and डॉल्फ़िन (dolphin) live in water."},{"c":"anekarthi-shabd","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"कविता “हम सब सुमन एक उपवन के” में “सुमन” का मतलब?","o":["कक्षा की लड़की सुमन","फूल","सूरज","माली"],"a":1,"hint":"उपवन means a garden. What grows in a garden?","why":"Here सुमन = फूल (flower) and उपवन = बगीचा (garden): we are all flowers of one garden. In the lesson, सुमन was a girl's name — same word, two uses!"},{"c":"anekarthi-shabd","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"“जल ही जीवन है।” और “दीया जल रहा है।” — दूसरे वाक्य में “जल” का अर्थ?","o":["पानी (water)","जलना (burning)","जीवन (life)","दीया (lamp)"],"a":1,"hint":"In the second sentence the lamp is giving light. What is its flame doing?","why":"One word, two meanings! In “जल ही जीवन है”, जल = water. In “दीया जल रहा है”, it means burning (from जलना)."},{"c":"asmaan-gira-patra","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा के प्रश्न-उत्तर: “आसमान गिरा” में पात्र किस क्रम में आते हैं?","o":["खरगोश, लोमड़ी, भालू, हाथी, शेर","शेर, हाथी, भालू, लोमड़ी, खरगोश","लोमड़ी, खरगोश, हाथी, भालू, शेर","खरगोश, भालू, लोमड़ी, शेर, हाथी"],"a":0,"hint":"डर की ख़बर सबसे पहले किसने फैलाई, और आख़िर में किसने सबको रोका?","why":"कहानी में पाँच पात्र हैं, इसी क्रम में: खरगोश, लोमड़ी, भालू, हाथी और शेर।"},{"c":"asmaan-gira-samajh","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"कक्षा के प्रश्न-उत्तर: खरगोश की बात सुनकर पशुओं को क्या करना चाहिए था?","o":["रुककर पूछना और आवाज़ का कारण ढूँढ़ना","तुरंत जंगल छोड़कर भाग जाना","खरगोश को जंगल से निकाल देना","पेड़ों पर चढ़कर छिप जाना"],"a":0,"hint":"बिना जाँचे डर जाना ठीक है क्या?","why":"उन्हें पहले खरगोश से पूछना और आवाज़ का कारण ढूँढ़ना चाहिए था। बिना जाँच किए किसी बात से डरना ठीक नहीं होता।"},{"c":"asmaan-gira-samajh","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा के उत्तर के अनुसार, कहानी में किस पात्र ने सबसे समझदारी से काम लिया?","o":["लोमड़ी","हाथी","शेर","खरगोश"],"a":2,"hint":"किसने सबको रोककर सवाल पूछे?","why":"शेर ने घबराकर भागने के बजाय सबको रोका, प्रश्न पूछे और जाकर सच्चाई का पता लगाया।"},{"c":"asmaan-gira-shiksha","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कहानी “आसमान गिरा” से हमें क्या शिक्षा मिलती है?","o":["पेड़ के नीचे कभी नहीं सोना चाहिए","जो सबसे पहले बोले, वही सही होता है","बड़े जानवरों की हर बात मान लेनी चाहिए","डरने से पहले सच्चाई का पता लगाना चाहिए"],"a":3,"hint":"शेर ने क्या किया था?","why":"सच्चाई जाने बिना किसी बात पर विश्वास नहीं करना चाहिए, और जल्दबाज़ी में ग़लत नतीजा नहीं निकालना चाहिए।"},{"c":"asmaan-gira-shiksha","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कहानी की एक और शिक्षा: अपनी ग़लती समझ आने पर क्या करना चाहिए?","o":["हँसते हुए ग़लती सुधार लेनी चाहिए","दिन भर उदास रहना चाहिए","ग़लती छिपा लेनी चाहिए","दूसरों पर दोष डाल देना चाहिए"],"a":0,"hint":"सच पता चलने पर सब जानवर क्या करते हैं?","why":"ग़लती समझ आने पर उसे हँसते हुए सुधार लेना चाहिए।"},{"c":"asmaan-gira-shabdarth","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"शब्दार्थ: “धम्म” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["बारिश की बूँदों की हल्की आवाज़","किसी भारी चीज़ के गिरने की ज़ोर की आवाज़","शेर की ज़ोरदार और डरावनी आवाज़","चिड़िया के चहचहाने की मीठी आवाज़"],"a":1,"hint":"खरगोश ने पेड़ के पास क्या सुना था?","why":"धम्म = ज़ोर की आवाज़, जैसे किसी भारी चीज़ के गिरने पर होती है।"},{"c":"asmaan-gira-shabdarth","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"शब्दार्थ: “दहाड़” किसे कहते हैं?","o":["किसी भारी चीज़ के गिरने की आवाज़","शेर की ज़ोरदार आवाज़","हाथी की चिंघाड़","कुत्ते का भौंकना"],"a":1,"hint":"जंगल के राजा की आवाज़…","why":"दहाड़ = शेर की ज़ोरदार आवाज़।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-a","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: मुहावरा “चार चाँद लगाना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["पछताना","शोभा बढ़ाना","राहत मिलना","तैयार होना"],"a":1,"hint":"चाँद कितना सुंदर लगता है!","why":"चार चाँद लगाना = शोभा बढ़ाना। बच्चों की सुंदर प्रस्तुति ने कार्यक्रम में चार चाँद लगा दिए।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-a","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “हाथ बँटाना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["सच्चाई सामने आना","बहुत आदर करना","पछताना","सहायता करना"],"a":3,"hint":"घर के कामों में माँ का हाथ बँटाना…","why":"हाथ बँटाना = सहायता करना।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-a","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “जान में जान आना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["बहुत उन्नति करना","राहत मिलना","शोभा बढ़ाना","पूरी मेहनत करना"],"a":1,"hint":"खोया हुआ बच्चा मिल जाए तो माँ को कैसा लगता है?","why":"जान में जान आना = राहत मिलना।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-a","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “अंधे की लकड़ी” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["बहुत पुरानी बात","टूटी हुई छड़ी","बहुत कमज़ोर चीज़","एकमात्र सहारा"],"a":3,"hint":"श्रवण कुमार अपने माता-पिता के लिए क्या थे?","why":"अंधे की लकड़ी = एकमात्र सहारा। श्रवण कुमार अपने माता-पिता के लिए अंधे की लकड़ी थे।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-b","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “कमर कसना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["पछताना","तैयार होना","बहुत प्रसन्न होना","प्रशंसा पाना"],"a":1,"hint":"परीक्षा से पहले विद्यार्थी क्या करते हैं?","why":"कमर कसना = तैयार होना। विद्यार्थियों ने परीक्षा के लिए कमर कस ली।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-b","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “हाथ मलना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["सहायता करना","शोभा बढ़ाना","तैयार होना","पछताना"],"a":3,"hint":"समय पर काम न किया तो बाद में…","why":"हाथ मलना = पछताना। (सहायता करना = हाथ बँटाना।)"},{"c":"class-muhavare-b","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “ईद का चाँद होना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["बहुत सुंदर और चमकदार दिखना","बहुत दिनों बाद दिखाई देना","रात में देर तक घूमना","सबको बहुत प्यारा होना"],"a":1,"hint":"ईद का चाँद कितने दिनों बाद दिखता है?","why":"ईद का चाँद होना = बहुत दिनों बाद दिखाई देना। “तुम तो आजकल ईद का चाँद हो गए हो।”"},{"c":"class-muhavare-b","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “फूले न समाना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["बहुत प्रसन्न होना","बहुत मोटा होना","बहुत डर जाना","बहुत गुस्सा होना"],"a":0,"hint":"पुरस्कार पाकर कैसा लगता है?","why":"फूले न समाना = बहुत प्रसन्न होना।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-b","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “दिल जीतना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["सबको प्रसन्न कर देना","दिल का इलाज करना","बहुत डर जाना","लड़ाई में जीत जाना"],"a":0,"hint":"अच्छे व्यवहार से…","why":"दिल जीतना = प्रसन्न कर देना। अच्छे व्यवहार से सबका दिल जीता जा सकता है।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-vakya","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"सही मुहावरा चुनिए: बच्चों की सुंदर प्रस्तुति ने कार्यक्रम में ___।","o":["छक्के छुड़ा दिए","चार चाँद लगा दिए","नाक-भौंह सिकोड़ ली","हाथ मल लिए"],"a":1,"hint":"प्रस्तुति ने कार्यक्रम की शोभा…","why":"चार चाँद लगाना = शोभा बढ़ाना। सुंदर प्रस्तुति से कार्यक्रम और सुंदर हो गया।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-vakya","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"सही मुहावरा चुनिए: खोया हुआ बच्चा मिलते ही माँ की ___।","o":["नाक-भौंह सिकुड़ गई","वाहवाही लुट गई","कलई खुल गई","जान में जान आई"],"a":3,"hint":"माँ को बहुत राहत मिली।","why":"जान में जान आना = राहत मिलना।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-vakya","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"सही मुहावरा चुनिए: विद्यार्थियों ने परीक्षा के लिए ___।","o":["घी के दिए जलाए","हाथ मल लिए","नाक-भौंह सिकोड़ ली","कमर कस ली"],"a":3,"hint":"वे परीक्षा के लिए तैयार हो गए।","why":"कमर कसना = तैयार होना।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-vakya","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"शरारत पकड़े जाने पर वह डर के मारे चुप और दब्बू बन गया। कौन-सा मुहावरा ठीक है?","o":["चार चाँद लगाना","आसमान छूना","फूले न समाना","भीगी बिल्ली बनना"],"a":3,"hint":"डरी हुई, भीगी हुई बिल्ली कैसी दिखती है?","why":"भीगी बिल्ली बनना = डर के मारे चुप या दब्बू बन जाना।"},{"c":"anekarthi-class","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"अनेकार्थी शब्द (कक्षा की कॉपी): “कर” के दो अर्थ हैं…","o":["हाथ और टैक्स","पैर और पैसा","घर और बाहर","काम और आराम"],"a":0,"hint":"“मैंने अपने कर से उसे पानी पिलाया।” “पापा ने अपने कर जमा कर दिए।”","why":"कर = हाथ, और कर = टैक्स (सरकार को दिया जाने वाला पैसा)।"},{"c":"anekarthi-class","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"अनेकार्थी शब्द: “पत्र” के दो अर्थ हैं…","o":["पत्ता और चिट्ठी","फूल और फल","किताब और कलम","पत्थर और पानी"],"a":0,"hint":"डाकिया क्या लाता है? और पेड़ पर क्या उगता है?","why":"पत्र = पत्ता, और पत्र = चिट्ठी।"},{"c":"anekarthi-class","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"अनेकार्थी शब्द: “अंबर” के दो अर्थ हैं…","o":["आग और धुआँ","धरती और पानी","आकाश और कपड़ा","बादल और बारिश"],"a":2,"hint":"एक अर्थ ऊपर देखने पर मिलता है।","why":"अंबर = आकाश, और अंबर = कपड़ा।"},{"c":"anekarthi-class","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"अनेकार्थी शब्द: “नव” के दो अर्थ हैं…","o":["नीला और हरा","नाव और नदी","पुराना और दस","नया और नौ"],"a":3,"hint":"“नव वर्ष” और “नवरात्रि” याद करो।","why":"नव = नया (नव वर्ष), और नव = नौ (नवरात्रि: नौ रातें)।"},{"c":"anekarthi-vakya","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"“भारत मैच में हार गया।” यहाँ “हार” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["एक दिशा","गहना (माला)","पराजय","समय"],"a":2,"hint":"मैच में जीत का उल्टा…","why":"हार के दो अर्थ हैं: गहना और पराजय। मैच हारने का मतलब है पराजय।"}],"r-lit":[{"c":"poem-plant-chores","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Dad says, “Make your bed!” In the poem, which plants never have to do this?","o":["Mushrooms","Flowers","Trees","Stalks of corn"],"a":1,"hint":"It’s the plant in the poem’s very first line.","why":"The poem begins with flowers waking up each morning — and they never have to make their beds! Mushrooms skip cleaning rooms, and trees skip geometry."},{"c":"poem-plant-chores","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In the poem, who never has to study geometry?","o":["A mushroom","A stalk of corn","A tree","An asparagus"],"a":2,"hint":"Mushrooms skip cleaning and corn skips washing. Which plant gets to skip maths?","why":"A tree never has to study geometry — no shapes and angles for a tree! Say the word slowly: geome-TREE. Can you hear the tree hiding in it?"},{"c":"poem-plant-chores","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which sentence does NOT match the poem?","o":["Flowers don’t make their beds","Mushrooms don’t clean their rooms","Corn doesn’t wash behind its ears","Trees must study geometry hard"],"a":3,"hint":"In the poem, every plant gets OUT of a job. Which sentence gives a plant a job?","why":"The poet says a tree NEVER has to study geometry. In the poem, plants escape all our jobs — that’s why the poet envies them!"},{"c":"poem-wordplay","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Which plant in the poem never has to clean its room?","o":["Tree","Mushroom","Flower","Corn"],"a":1,"hint":"Say each name slowly and listen for a part of a house.","why":"Mush-ROOM! The poet has fun with the word: a mushroom has a ‘room’ in its name, yet it never has to tidy it."},{"c":"poem-wordplay","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"The poem says corn never washes behind its “ears”. What are a corn plant’s ears?","o":["Its roots under the soil","Its long green leaves","The cobs that hold its grains","Holes that let it hear the wind"],"a":2,"hint":"Which part of the corn plant do we eat?","why":"A corn ‘ear’ is the cob that holds the grains — the bhutta we roast! The poet jokes that corn has ears but never has to wash behind them."},{"c":"poem-wordplay","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"No one tells asparagus not to “play with spears”. What are asparagus spears?","o":["Sharp thorns on its stem","Its tiny white flowers","Its round red seeds","Its pointed green shoots"],"a":3,"hint":"A spear is long and pointy. Which part of asparagus looks like that?","why":"Asparagus shoots grow straight up with pointy tips, just like little spears — and that’s the part we eat. The poet pretends they are toys!"},{"c":"poem-poets-wish","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"At the end of the poem, what does the poet wish to be?","o":["A plant","A bird","A gardener","A grown-up"],"a":0,"hint":"The whole poem is about who gets out of doing jobs.","why":"The poet is tired of being a person and wishes to be a plant — no beds to make, no messy rooms, no geometry!"},{"c":"poem-poets-wish","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Is the poet happy being a person? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — people get to play all day","No — people have chores and lessons","Yes — plants work much harder","No — people can’t grow flowers"],"a":1,"hint":"Look at all the jobs the poet lists that plants never have to do.","why":"The poet is tired of being a person, because people must make beds, clean rooms and study. Plants skip all that — so the poet would rather be one!"},{"c":"poem-poets-wish","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"The poet calls the poem a “rant” — a long, grumbly speech. How does the poet feel?","o":["Scared","Sleepy","Fed up","Proud"],"a":2,"hint":"Think how you feel when you’re told to tidy up for the tenth time!","why":"A rant is a long complaint. Being tired of something means being fed up with it — not sleepy! The poet is fed up with a person’s jobs and wishes to be a plant."},{"c":"poem-rhyme","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which word rhymes with “spears”?","o":["Spoons","Sparks","Ears","Spills"],"a":2,"hint":"Rhyming words share their END sound, not their first sound.","why":"Sp-EARS and EARS end with the same sound — and the poet rhymes them in the poem! Spoons, sparks and spills only share the start."},{"c":"poem-rhyme","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The poem’s last line ends with “plant”. Which word rhymes with it?","o":["Rant","Plate","Planet","Paint"],"a":0,"hint":"Say each word aloud. Which one ends with the same sound as plant?","why":"Pl-ANT and R-ANT end with the same sound, and the poet rhymes them in the last verse. Plate, planet and paint end with different sounds: -ate, -et and -aint."},{"c":"poem-rhyme","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out — which word does NOT rhyme with “corn”?","o":["Born","Torn","Horn","Cord"],"a":3,"hint":"Say each word next to ‘corn’. One ends with a different sound.","why":"Born, torn and horn all end in ‘-orn’, just like corn. Cord starts the same way but ends with a ‘d’ sound — so it doesn’t rhyme."},{"c":"pushmi-pullyu-rare","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What was special about the pushmi-pullyu’s body?","o":["It had two humps","It had a head at each end","It had wings and a tail","It had stripes like a zebra"],"a":1,"hint":"Its name is a clue: one end could “push” while the other “pulls”.","why":"The pushmi-pullyu had no tail — instead it had a head at each end, with sharp horns on each head. No wonder it was so amazing!"},{"c":"pushmi-pullyu-rare","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which fact about the pushmi-pullyu is WRONG?","o":["It was very shy","Each head had sharp horns","It had a long, bushy tail","It lived in African jungles"],"a":2,"hint":"Think about what was at the back end of this animal.","why":"It had no tail at all — there was a second head where the tail should be! It was shy, lived in African jungles and had sharp horns on each head."},{"c":"pushmi-pullyu-rare","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Why is the story called “The Rarest Animal of All”?","o":["Very few lived, and none was ever caught","It was the largest jungle animal","It lived deep in the ocean","It was the Doctor’s very first pet"],"a":0,"hint":"“Rare” means very hard to find.","why":"“Rarest” means the most uncommon. Only a few pushmi-pullyus were left, no hunter had ever caught one, and no other animal had two heads!"},{"c":"pushmi-never-caught","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Could a hunter catch a pushmi-pullyu while it slept? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — both heads slept at night","No — it never slept at all","Yes — it slept very deeply","No — one head stayed awake"],"a":3,"hint":"It had two heads. Did they both sleep at the same time?","why":"Only one head slept at a time — the other stayed awake and kept watch. So no one could catch it napping! It did sleep, just half at a time."},{"c":"pushmi-never-caught","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Why had no pushmi-pullyu ever been seen in a zoo?","o":["Zoos thought it was ugly","It was too big for any cage","No one had ever caught one","It was against jungle law"],"a":2,"hint":"Before an animal can be shown in a zoo, what must happen first?","why":"No hunter or zoo-keeper had ever managed to catch a pushmi-pullyu. It was shy, rare and always had one head awake and watching."},{"c":"pushmi-never-caught","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"This animal had a head at each end. What if a hunter sneaked up on it from behind?","o":["He’d grab it by the tail","A head would be facing him","It would think he was a friend","It would fall fast asleep"],"a":1,"hint":"For a pushmi-pullyu, which end is the “behind”?","why":"A pushmi-pullyu has no real ‘behind’ — there’s a head at each end and no tail! So whichever way a hunter came, a head was facing him."},{"c":"persuading-pushmi","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"How did the monkeys try to persuade the shy pushmi-pullyu to go?","o":["They said he’d be looked at, not caged","They offered him sacks of honey","They tied him up with vines","They said he’d be king of a zoo"],"a":0,"hint":"He was afraid of zoos and cages. What would calm that fear?","why":"The monkeys promised he would only be looked at, not locked up like in a zoo. They also told him how kind the Doctor was to animals."},{"c":"persuading-pushmi","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"At first the pushmi-pullyu almost cried and said no. Why didn’t he want to go?","o":["He was scared of the sea","He wanted to stay with the monkeys","He didn’t like the Doctor","He was too shy to be stared at"],"a":3,"hint":"Think about the kind of animal he was — bold or bashful?","why":"He was very shy and hated being stared at. Crowds looking at him was his worst fear! He hadn’t even met the Doctor yet."},{"c":"persuading-pushmi","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What was Chee-Chee the monkey’s big idea?","o":["Sell the pushmi-pullyu to a zoo","Ride on him across the sea","Hide him deep in the jungle","Take him home — people would pay to see him"],"a":3,"hint":"Chee-Chee was thinking about how to get money for the Doctor.","why":"Chee-Chee said people would pay any money to see this two-headed animal. That money could help the kind but poor Doctor."},{"c":"money-and-promise","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"The Doctor said he didn’t want money. Why did Dab-Dab the duck say they needed it?","o":["To pay for a new boat for the sailor","To buy food for the monkeys","To build a zoo in Africa","To buy medicine for the jungle"],"a":0,"hint":"To sail to Africa, the Doctor had borrowed something. What did he still owe?","why":"They had borrowed a sailor’s boat and owed him a new one. The Doctor planned to build it himself, but sensible Dab-Dab knew even that needed money."},{"c":"money-and-promise","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Dab-Dab reminds the Doctor how they had to “pinch and scrape”. This means…","o":["Live carefully on very little money","Scratch and pinch each other","Spend money on treats","Clean the house very hard"],"a":0,"hint":"Think about a family that has to save every rupee.","why":"To pinch and scrape is to live carefully on very little money, saving every coin. Back home, money had been very tight for the Doctor."},{"c":"money-and-promise","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Before agreeing to go, what did the pushmi-pullyu make the Doctor promise?","o":["To feed him only mangoes","To send him back if he didn’t like it","To never let anyone see him","To take all the monkeys too"],"a":1,"hint":"He was nervous about a strange new land. What would make him feel safe?","why":"He agreed to go only if the Doctor promised to send him back if he didn’t like it there. The kind Doctor gave his promise."},{"c":"story-words-1","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes (The Rarest Animal of All): ‘rarest’ means…","o":["biggest","oldest","most uncommon","most common"],"a":2,"hint":"Something rare is not seen very often.","why":"Rarest = most uncommon. Hardly any pushmi-pullyus existed."},{"c":"story-words-1","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: ‘extinct’ means…","o":["a kind of animal that lives only in a zoo","a kind of animal that runs faster than all others","a kind of animal that is very angry and fierce","a kind of animal that does not exist anymore"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of the dinosaurs.","why":"Extinct = no longer exists anywhere, like the dinosaurs."},{"c":"story-words-1","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: ‘encounter’ means…","o":["come across (meet)","run away from","count again","hide behind"],"a":0,"hint":"To meet someone or something by chance…","why":"Encounter = come across or meet."},{"c":"story-words-1","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: ‘persuade’ means…","o":["forget","convince","punish","chase"],"a":1,"hint":"It's what the monkeys tried to do with their words.","why":"Persuade = convince someone to agree with you."},{"c":"story-words-2","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: ‘murmured’ means…","o":["shouted loudly in an angry voice","said quietly in a low voice","sang a song in a high voice","wrote something down quickly"],"a":1,"hint":"Shh… almost a whisper.","why":"Murmured = said something quietly, in a low voice."},{"c":"story-words-2","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: ‘fortune’ means…","o":["a strong fort with high walls","a kind of sweet fruit from Africa","a large amount of money or property","a small coin that is worth very little"],"a":2,"hint":"Something people hope to make by working hard or doing business.","why":"Fortune = a large amount of money or property."},{"c":"story-words-2","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: ‘make up one's mind’ means…","o":["decide","change the subject","put on make-up","forget something"],"a":0,"hint":"Before you choose, you have to…","why":"Make up your mind = decide. The pushmi-pullyu had to make up his mind about going."},{"c":"story-words-2","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: ‘huntsmen’ are…","o":["people who hunt animals","zoo keepers","people who build huts","animal doctors"],"a":0,"hint":"Look inside the word.","why":"Huntsmen = hunters. Even the greatest huntsmen could not catch a pushmi-pullyu."},{"c":"who-said","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Book work: “No… You know how shy I am. I hate being stared at.” Who said this, and to whom?","o":["Chee-Chee, to the pushmi-pullyu","The pushmi-pullyu, to the monkeys","Dab-Dab, to Doctor Dolittle","Doctor Dolittle, to the monkeys"],"a":1,"hint":"Who was the shy one?","why":"The shy pushmi-pullyu said it to the monkeys, who wanted him to go with the Doctor."},{"c":"who-said","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Book work: “Oh, do be sensible!” Who said this, and to whom?","o":["The pushmi-pullyu, to the monkeys","Doctor Dolittle, to Dab-Dab","Dab-Dab the duck, to Doctor Dolittle","Chee-Chee the monkey, to the pushmi-pullyu"],"a":2,"hint":"Who looked after the Doctor's money worries?","why":"Dab-Dab the duck said it to the Doctor, who had said he didn't want money."},{"c":"speaker-did","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Book work: what did the pushmi-pullyu do right after saying he hated being stared at?","o":["He agreed at once","He ran into the jungle","He almost began to cry","He laughed loudly"],"a":2,"hint":"He was very shy, and the idea upset him.","why":"He almost began to cry: he was too shy to be put on show."},{"c":"money-and-promise","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Book work: Dab-Dab wanted the Doctor to be sensible about…","o":["the weather: it was too stormy to sail home","his manners: he was too rude to the animals","his health: he was not eating or sleeping well","his need for money: he had many bills to pay"],"a":3,"hint":"The Doctor had just said he didn't want money.","why":"The Doctor was poor and owed money, so Dab-Dab told him to be sensible and earn some."},{"c":"persuading-pushmi","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Book work: for how many days did the monkeys try to persuade the pushmi-pullyu?","o":["One day","Ten days","Seven days","Three days"],"a":3,"hint":"They didn't give up after the first day.","why":"The monkeys tried for three days, telling him the Doctor was a kind man who needed money."},{"c":"capture","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: how did the monkeys catch the pushmi-pullyu?","o":["They tracked its footprints, found it in tall grass and made a ring around it","They left a trail of bananas that led it straight into a cage","They dug a deep pit near the river and covered it with leaves and grass","They waited quietly in the trees until both of its heads fell asleep"],"a":0,"hint":"Remember: one head was always awake, so a sleeping trick could never work.","why":"They tracked its footprints along the river, found it hiding in the high grass and made a circle around it. It couldn't break through the ring."},{"c":"why-agree","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: why did the pushmi-pullyu agree to go with the Doctor?","o":["He wanted to become rich and famous in the Land of Men","The monkeys tied him up with vines and carried him all the way to the ship","The Doctor looked kind and trustworthy, and promised to send him back if needed","He had always dreamed of sailing across the sea in a ship"],"a":2,"hint":"He looked carefully at the Doctor's face…","why":"He trusted the Doctor's kind face, and the Doctor promised to send him back if he didn't like the Land of Men."},{"c":"dolittle","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: which word does NOT describe Doctor Dolittle?","o":["Greedy","Trustworthy","Kind-hearted","Poor"],"a":0,"hint":"He said he didn't even want money.","why":"Doctor Dolittle was kind-hearted, trustworthy, helpful and poor. He was not greedy."},{"c":"dolittle","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: what extraordinary gift did Doctor Dolittle have?","o":["He could see in the dark","He could become invisible","He could fly","He could talk to animals"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of how he worked with Dab-Dab and Chee-Chee.","why":"He had the gift of talking to animals, so he could work with Dab-Dab and Chee-Chee."},{"c":"dabdab","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: which best describes Dab-Dab?","o":["A smart, sensible duck who gave the Doctor good advice","A shy animal with two heads who hated being stared at","A lazy dog who slept all day and never helped anyone","A hunter who wanted to catch the rarest animals of all"],"a":0,"hint":"She convinced the Doctor to take the pushmi-pullyu.","why":"Dab-Dab was a smart, intelligent duck who advised the Doctor well: she convinced him to earn some money."},{"c":"pushmi-pullyu-rare","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: where did the pushmi-pullyu live?","o":["In the deepest jungles of Africa","In a big city zoo in the Land of Men","Under the sea, near the coral reefs","In the snowy mountains of the Arctic"],"a":0,"hint":"Far away, where hunters could hardly reach.","why":"Pushmi-pullyus lived in the deepest jungles of Africa."},{"c":"poem-why","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Book work (When Flowers Wake Each Morning): why does nobody tell asparagus not to play with spears?","o":["Asparagus has no parent to guide it","Asparagus spears are soft and safe","Asparagus is too old to listen","Asparagus lives inside a house"],"a":0,"hint":"Who usually tells children what not to do?","why":"Your book work says it: asparagus has no parent to guide it. Plants don't get told what to do!"},{"c":"poem-mushroom","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Book work: what does the poet mean by ‘A mushroom doesn't have to clean its room’?","o":["Mushrooms live in tiny rooms that never get dirty","Mushrooms clean their rooms at night when no one is looking","A mushroom has no house, and nobody tells it what to do","Mushrooms are very tidy and clean their rooms every day"],"a":2,"hint":"Does a mushroom even have a room?","why":"A mushroom doesn't live in a house, and nobody tells it to do anything."},{"c":"poem-theme","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Book work: what is the poem about?","o":["A farmer who grows corn, asparagus and mushrooms","A child who wishes to be a plant and live a carefree life","A child who loves cleaning, bathing and learning geometry","A plant that wishes to become a child and go to school"],"a":1,"hint":"Who is speaking, and what does the speaker wish for?","why":"The poet, a child, is tired of chores and wishes to be a plant with a carefree life."},{"c":"poem-chores","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Book work: which is NOT one of the chores the poet dislikes?","o":["Learning geometry","Bathing","Cleaning the room","Eating ice cream"],"a":3,"hint":"Which one isn't a chore at all?","why":"The poet dislikes cleaning the room, bathing, learning geometry, washing ears and combing hair. Eating ice cream isn't a chore!"},{"c":"unseen-detail","t":"Exam practice","p":50,"q":"Unseen passage: “Every morning, Aman feeds the stray dog outside his gate. He saves half a chapati from his breakfast for it. Now the dog waits for him at seven o'clock sharp.” What does Aman save for the dog?","o":["A glass of milk from his lunch","A packet of biscuits from the shop","Half a chapati from his breakfast","Some rice and dal from his dinner"],"a":2,"hint":"Find the sentence with the word ‘saves’.","why":"The passage says he saves half a chapati from his breakfast. In an unseen passage the answer is always in the text."},{"c":"unseen-word","t":"Exam practice","p":50,"q":"Unseen passage: “The pond was so murky that we could not see a single fish in it.” ‘Murky’ most likely means…","o":["dark and dirty","clean and clear","small and round","deep and cold"],"a":0,"hint":"Why couldn't they see the fish?","why":"If you can't see the fish, the water must be dark and dirty. Use the clues around a new word to work out its meaning."},{"c":"unseen-feelings","t":"Exam practice","p":100,"q":"Unseen passage: “Riya practised the piano every evening. On the day of the concert her hands shook at first, but soon she was playing happily.” How did Riya feel at the START of the concert?","o":["Bored","Nervous","Sleepy","Angry"],"a":1,"hint":"What can happen to your hands when you are scared?","why":"Shaking hands show she was nervous; later she felt happy. Look for clues that show feelings."},{"c":"unseen-title","t":"Exam practice","p":100,"q":"Unseen passage: “Bees visit flowers to drink nectar. As they fly from flower to flower, they carry pollen, which helps plants make seeds and fruit.” Which is the best title?","o":["Making Honey at Home","My Favourite Flower","Bees Sting People","How Bees Help Plants"],"a":3,"hint":"A good title fits the WHOLE passage.","why":"The whole passage is about how bees help plants make seeds and fruit. A title should match the main idea."},{"c":"unseen-word","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's practice passage: which word means ‘a period of ten years’?","o":["forefathers","century","civilized","decade"],"a":3,"hint":"Dec- means ten, as in decimal.","why":"A decade is 10 years. A century is 100 years."},{"c":"unseen-word-2","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's practice passage: ‘forefathers’ means…","o":["people who live in the house next door to us","fathers who are very strong and brave","four fathers who all live together in one house","ancestors: people of our family who lived long ago"],"a":3,"hint":"Fore- means before.","why":"Forefathers = ancestors, the people who lived long before us."}],"r-maths":[{"c":"multiples","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which list shows the first four multiples of 7?","o":["7, 14, 21, 28","1, 7, 14, 21","7, 17, 27, 37","0, 7, 70, 77"],"a":0,"hint":"Multiples come from the 7 times table: 7 × 1, 7 × 2, 7 × 3…","why":"7 × 1 = 7, 7 × 2 = 14, 7 × 3 = 21, 7 × 4 = 28. The list starts at 7 itself — 1 is a factor of 7, not a multiple."},{"c":"multiples","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which number is NOT a multiple of 6?","o":["46","42","36","54"],"a":0,"hint":"Say the 6 times table: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36…","why":"36 = 6 × 6, 42 = 6 × 7 and 54 = 6 × 9. But 6 × 7 = 42 and 6 × 8 = 48, so 46 is skipped — it's not a multiple of 6."},{"c":"multiples","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Tia writes the multiples of 5: 5, 10, 15, 21, 25. Which number is the mistake?","o":["10","21","15","25"],"a":1,"hint":"Multiples of 5 always end in 0 or 5.","why":"Counting in 5s goes 5, 10, 15, 20, 25. Tia wrote 21 instead of 20 — and 21 ends in 1, so it can't be a multiple of 5."},{"c":"multiples","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"A spider has 8 legs. Can a group of spiders have exactly 60 legs?","o":["Yes — 60 is an even number","No — 60 legs is too many","Yes — because 6 + 0 = 6","No — 60 is not a multiple of 8"],"a":3,"hint":"Count in 8s: 8, 16, 24… does 60 show up?","why":"7 spiders have 56 legs and 8 spiders have 64. 60 sits in between, so it isn't a multiple of 8. Being even isn't enough!"},{"c":"factors","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which of these is a factor of 18?","o":["4","5","8","6"],"a":3,"hint":"A factor divides 18 exactly, with nothing left over.","why":"18 ÷ 6 = 3 exactly, so 6 is a factor. 18 ÷ 4, 18 ÷ 5 and 18 ÷ 8 all leave something left over."},{"c":"factors","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which list shows ALL the factors of 12?","o":["2, 3, 4, 6","1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12","1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 12","12, 24, 36, 48"],"a":1,"hint":"Don't forget 1 and the number itself — they are factors too.","why":"1 × 12, 2 × 6 and 3 × 4 all make 12, so the factors are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12. 5 doesn't divide 12, and 24, 36, 48 are multiples."},{"c":"factors","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Arjun lists the factors of 20 as 1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20. Which one does NOT belong?","o":["2","5","10","8"],"a":3,"hint":"Try dividing 20 by each one. Which leaves a remainder?","why":"20 ÷ 8 = 2 with 4 left over, so 8 is not a factor. The factor pairs of 20 are 1 × 20, 2 × 10 and 4 × 5."},{"c":"factors","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Why is 4 a factor of 28?","o":["Because 28 is bigger than 4","4 is a factor of every even number","28 ÷ 4 = 7 with nothing left over","Because 4 + 24 = 28"],"a":2,"hint":"A factor must divide the number exactly.","why":"4 × 7 = 28, so 28 ÷ 4 leaves no remainder. Careful: 4 is NOT a factor of every even number — 6 ÷ 4 leaves 2 over!"},{"c":"factor-pairs","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which pair of numbers multiplies to make 36?","o":["4 and 8","6 and 7","5 and 7","4 and 9"],"a":3,"hint":"Check each pair with your times tables.","why":"4 × 9 = 36, so 4 and 9 are a factor pair of 36. 4 × 8 = 32, 5 × 7 = 35 and 6 × 7 = 42 — close, but not 36."},{"c":"factor-pairs","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"An aquarium floor has 24 tiles laid in equal rows. Which layout is NOT possible?","o":["3 rows of 8","4 rows of 6","5 rows of 5","2 rows of 12"],"a":2,"hint":"Multiply rows × tiles in each row. Which one doesn't make 24?","why":"3 × 8, 4 × 6 and 2 × 12 all make 24. But 5 × 5 = 25 — one tile too many. 5 isn't a factor of 24."},{"c":"factor-pairs","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"How many factor pairs does 18 have? (1 × 18 and 18 × 1 are the same pair.)","o":["2","6","4","3"],"a":3,"hint":"Try 1, 2, 3, 4, 5… which ones divide 18 exactly?","why":"1 × 18, 2 × 9 and 3 × 6 — that's 3 pairs. 18 has 6 factors in all, but they team up in twos, so 6 is the factor count, not the pair count."},{"c":"factor-pairs","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"{R} wants to lay 13 shells in equal rows with none left over. Which layout works?","o":["2 rows of 6","1 row of 13","3 rows of 4","4 rows of 3"],"a":1,"hint":"Multiply each layout. Does it use all 13 shells?","why":"2 × 6, 3 × 4 and 4 × 3 all make 12 — one shell is left out! 13 has only one factor pair, 1 × 13, so it's one long row."},{"c":"factor-or-multiple","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"7 × 8 = 56. Which sentence is TRUE?","o":["56 is a factor of 7","7 is a multiple of 56","7 and 8 are factors of 56","8 is a multiple of 56"],"a":2,"hint":"The small numbers you multiply are factors. The answer you get is the multiple.","why":"7 and 8 are factors of 56, and 56 is a multiple of both 7 and 8. The other sentences mix up the two words."},{"c":"factor-or-multiple","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which sentence is WRONG?","o":["1 is a factor of every number","A factor can be bigger than its number","Every number is a factor of itself","Every number is a multiple of itself"],"a":1,"hint":"Can a number bigger than 10 divide 10 exactly?","why":"A factor divides a number exactly, so it can never be bigger than the number. The biggest factor of 10 is 10 itself."},{"c":"factor-or-multiple","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"How many multiples does 3 have?","o":["Only 3","Only 10","Endless — they never stop","Only the ones up to 30"],"a":2,"hint":"Can you keep counting 3, 6, 9, 12… forever?","why":"You can always add 3 more, so multiples never run out. Factors are different — 3 has only two factors: 1 and 3."},{"c":"factor-or-multiple","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Is 9 a factor or a multiple of 3? Pick the answer with the right reason.","o":["Factor — because 9 is bigger","Multiple — because 3 × 3 = 9","Factor — because 9 ÷ 3 = 3","Neither — because 9 is odd"],"a":1,"hint":"Is 9 in the 3 times table?","why":"9 is in the 3 times table (3 × 3 = 9), so 9 is a multiple of 3. It works the other way too: 3 is a factor of 9."},{"c":"common-multiples","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which number is a multiple of BOTH 4 and 6?","o":["10","16","18","36"],"a":3,"hint":"Check each number in the 4 times table AND the 6 times table.","why":"36 = 4 × 9 and 36 = 6 × 6, so it's a common multiple. 16 is only in the 4 table, 18 only in the 6 table, and 10 is just 4 + 6."},{"c":"common-multiples","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What is the smallest common multiple of 3 and 5?","o":["8","30","15","35"],"a":2,"hint":"List multiples of 5: 5, 10, 15… which is the first one in the 3 table too?","why":"5, 10, 15 — and 15 is in the 3 table (3 × 5). 30 is a common multiple too, but bigger. 8 is just 3 + 5."},{"c":"common-multiples","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"What is the smallest common multiple of 4 and 8?","o":["4","8","12","32"],"a":1,"hint":"Is 8 itself already in the 4 times table?","why":"8 = 4 × 2, so 8 is a multiple of both. No need to multiply to 32! When one number fits into the other, the bigger one is the answer."},{"c":"common-multiple-stories","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A dolphin comes up for air every 4 minutes, a sea turtle every 10 minutes. They surface together now. When next?","o":["After 20 minutes","After 14 minutes","After 40 minutes","After 10 minutes"],"a":0,"hint":"List the turtle's times: 10, 20, 30… which is also in the 4 table?","why":"Turtle: 10, 20… Dolphin: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20. 20 is the first time on both lists! 40 works too, but it comes later."},{"c":"common-multiple-stories","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"One light blinks every 3 seconds, another every 7 seconds. They blink together now. When do they blink together next?","o":["After 10 seconds","After 14 seconds","After 4 seconds","After 21 seconds"],"a":3,"hint":"Find the first number that is in BOTH the 3 and 7 times tables.","why":"7, 14, 21 — and 21 = 3 × 7 is in the 3 table too. 14 is only in the 7 table, and 10 is just 3 + 7."},{"c":"common-multiple-stories","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Sharks in tank A are fed every 2 days, sharks in tank B every 5 days. Both were fed today. Both get fed on the same day again in…","o":["7 days","10 days","5 days","3 days"],"a":1,"hint":"Count in 5s and look for a number that is also even.","why":"Day 5 is odd, so it isn't tank A's day. Day 10 is in both the 2 and 5 tables — the first shared feeding day. 7 is just 2 + 5."},{"c":"common-multiple-stories","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Pencils come in packs of 6, erasers in packs of 9. You want the SAME number of each. What is the smallest number you can buy?","o":["18 of each","15 of each","36 of each","54 of each"],"a":0,"hint":"Count in 9s: 9, 18… is any of these also a multiple of 6?","why":"18 = 6 × 3 = 9 × 2: three packs of pencils, two packs of erasers. 36 works too but isn't the smallest, and 54 is just 6 × 9."},{"c":"common-multiple-stories","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Alarm A beeps every 5 minutes, alarm B every 15 minutes. Both beeped at 9:00. When do they next beep together?","o":["9:05","9:20","9:15","10:15"],"a":2,"hint":"Is 15 already in the 5 times table?","why":"15 = 5 × 3, so alarm A also beeps at 9:15 — the same moment as alarm B. 9:20 adds 5 + 15, and 10:15 is 5 × 15 = 75 minutes later."},{"c":"common-factors","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which number is a factor of BOTH 12 and 18?","o":["4","9","8","6"],"a":3,"hint":"Check: does it divide 12 exactly? Does it divide 18 exactly?","why":"12 ÷ 6 = 2 and 18 ÷ 6 = 3, so 6 is a common factor. 4 divides 12 but not 18; 9 divides 18 but not 12; 8 divides neither."},{"c":"common-factors","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What are the common factors of 8 and 12?","o":["2, 4, 8","1, 2, 4","1, 2, 3, 4","24, 48"],"a":1,"hint":"List the factors of 8, then of 12, and circle the ones in both.","why":"Factors of 8: 1, 2, 4, 8. Factors of 12: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12. Both lists share 1, 2 and 4. 24 and 48 are common multiples."},{"c":"common-factors","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"12 shark stickers and 16 fish stickers are shared equally among friends, with none left over. What is the most friends?","o":["4 friends","3 friends","2 friends","8 friends"],"a":0,"hint":"The number of friends must divide BOTH 12 and 16 exactly.","why":"4 friends get 3 shark and 4 fish stickers each. 3 works for 12 but not 16, and 8 works for 16 but not 12. 2 works, but 4 is more!"},{"c":"common-factors","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Two numbers have only 1 as a common factor. Which pair could they be?","o":["4 and 6","6 and 9","4 and 9","10 and 15"],"a":2,"hint":"For each pair, look for any number bigger than 1 that divides both.","why":"4 has factors 1, 2, 4 and 9 has 1, 3, 9 — they share only 1. 4 and 6 share 2, 6 and 9 share 3, and 10 and 15 share 5."},{"c":"even-odd","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which of these numbers is odd?","o":["58","70","126","93"],"a":3,"hint":"Look only at the last digit.","why":"93 ends in 3, so it's odd — it can't be split into two equal groups. 58, 70 and 126 end in 8, 0 and 6, so they're even."},{"c":"even-odd","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Add any two odd numbers, like 7 + 9 or 15 + 3. The answer is always…","o":["Even","Odd","More than 20","A multiple of 5"],"a":0,"hint":"Try a few: 1 + 3, 5 + 7, 7 + 9…","why":"Each odd number has one ‘left-over’. Two left-overs make a pair, so odd + odd is always even: 7 + 9 = 16, 15 + 3 = 18."},{"c":"even-odd","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"An octopus has 8 arms. Is the total number of arms in ANY group of octopuses odd or even?","o":["You can't tell","Always odd","Odd if the group is odd","Always even"],"a":3,"hint":"Try 3 octopuses, then 5. Count in 8s.","why":"8 is even, and every multiple of an even number is even: 3 octopuses have 24 arms, 5 octopuses have 40. Always even!"},{"c":"even-odd","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Find the FALSE statement about even and odd numbers.","o":["Odd numbers end in 1, 3, 5, 7 or 9","Even + even can be odd","Odd + even is always odd","Even numbers end in 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8"],"a":1,"hint":"Test each statement with small numbers, like 2 + 4 or 3 + 4.","why":"Even + even is always even (2 + 4 = 6, 6 + 8 = 14). Odd + even really is always odd: 3 + 4 = 7."},{"c":"divisible-by-2","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A number is divisible by 2 if its LAST digit is…","o":["0, 2, 4, 6 or 8","1, 3, 5, 7 or 9","Only 2","0 or 5"],"a":0,"hint":"Divisible by 2 means the number is even.","why":"Every even number ends in 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8, and splits into 2 equal groups. ‘0 or 5’ is the rule for 5!"},{"c":"divisible-by-2","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Is 4,316 divisible by 2? Pick the answer with the right reason.","o":["Yes — its first digit, 4, is even","No — it has an odd digit, 3","Yes — its last digit, 6, is even","No — its digits add up to 14"],"a":2,"hint":"Only one digit decides if a number is divisible by 2.","why":"Only the LAST digit matters. 4,316 ends in 6, so it's even: 4,316 ÷ 2 = 2,158. The first digit being 4 is true, but it's not the reason."},{"c":"divisible-by-2","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Odd one out: which number is NOT divisible by 2?","o":["310","572","845","998"],"a":2,"hint":"Check the last digit of each.","why":"845 ends in 5, an odd digit, so it isn't divisible by 2. 310, 572 and 998 end in 0, 2 and 8 — all even."},{"c":"divisible-by-5-and-10","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which number is divisible by 5 but NOT by 10?","o":["75","50","100","120"],"a":0,"hint":"Divisible by 10 means it ends in 0. Divisible by 5 means it ends in 0 or 5.","why":"75 ends in 5, so 5 divides it (75 ÷ 5 = 15) but 10 doesn't. 50, 100 and 120 end in 0, so they work for both."},{"c":"divisible-by-5-and-10","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"A number is divisible by 10. Will it also be divisible by 2 and by 5?","o":["Only by 2","Only by 5","Yes, always — because 10 = 2 × 5","Never"],"a":2,"hint":"What two numbers multiply to make 10?","why":"It ends in 0, which passes the test for 2 (even) and the test for 5 (0 or 5). Example: 70 ÷ 2 = 35 and 70 ÷ 5 = 14."},{"c":"divisible-by-5-and-10","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"You only have ₹5 coins. Which amount can you pay exactly, with no change?","o":["₹85","₹63","₹42","₹98"],"a":0,"hint":"The amount must be a multiple of 5.","why":"₹85 ends in 5, so it's a multiple of 5: seventeen ₹5 coins. ₹42, ₹63 and ₹98 don't end in 0 or 5."},{"c":"divisible-by-5-and-10","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Ravi says, “255 is divisible by 10 because it ends in 5.” What is the truth?","o":["Wrong — it's divisible by 5, not 10","He's right — 5 is a factor of 10","Wrong — it isn't divisible by 5 either","He's right — every odd number is"],"a":0,"hint":"What must the last digit be for a number to be divisible by 10?","why":"Ending in 5 passes the test for 5 (255 ÷ 5 = 51), but a number divisible by 10 must end in 0. Ravi mixed up the two rules."},{"c":"divisible-by-5-and-10","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Shark badges are sold in boxes of 10. Which order can be packed in full boxes, with no badge left over?","o":["345 badges","340 badges","304 badges","355 badges"],"a":1,"hint":"Full boxes of 10 means the total must be divisible by 10.","why":"340 ends in 0, so it fills exactly 34 boxes. 345 and 355 end in 5 (divisible by 5 only), and 304 leaves 4 badges over."},{"c":"ws-factor-lists","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which list shows ALL the factors of 24?","o":["2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12","1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12, 24","1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24","1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24"],"a":3,"hint":"Find the pairs: 1 × 24, 2 × 12, 3 × 8, 4 × 6.","why":"The factor pairs of 24 are 1 × 24, 2 × 12, 3 × 8 and 4 × 6, so its factors are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12 and 24."},{"c":"ws-factor-lists","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which list shows ALL the factors of 36?","o":["1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18, 36","1, 2, 3, 4, 9, 12, 18, 36","1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12, 18, 36","1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 12, 18, 36"],"a":0,"hint":"Pairs: 1 × 36, 2 × 18, 3 × 12, 4 × 9, 6 × 6.","why":"36 = 1 × 36 = 2 × 18 = 3 × 12 = 4 × 9 = 6 × 6, so its factors are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18 and 36. 8 is not a factor: 36 ÷ 8 leaves 4."},{"c":"ws-is-it-a-factor","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: is 7 a factor of 749?","o":["No: 749 ÷ 7 leaves 2 left over","No: 7 can't be a factor of an odd number","Yes: 749 ÷ 7 = 107 with nothing left over","Yes: because 749 ends in a 9"],"a":2,"hint":"Divide 749 by 7 using long division.","why":"749 ÷ 7 = 107 exactly (7 × 107 = 749), so 7 is a factor of 749."},{"c":"ws-is-it-a-factor","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: is 9 a factor of 726?","o":["No: 726 ÷ 9 leaves 3 left over","No: 726 ÷ 9 leaves 6 left over","Yes: because 7 + 2 + 6 = 15","Yes: 726 ÷ 9 = 80 exactly"],"a":1,"hint":"Divide 726 by 9. Is anything left over?","why":"9 × 80 = 720, and 726 − 720 = 6. There is a remainder of 6, so 9 is not a factor of 726."},{"c":"ws-primes","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which list shows all the prime numbers between 1 and 15?","o":["3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13","2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13","2, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15","1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13"],"a":1,"hint":"A prime number has exactly two factors: 1 and itself.","why":"2, 3, 5, 7, 11 and 13 have exactly two factors each. 1 has only one factor, and 9 and 15 have more than two."},{"c":"ws-primes","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: circle the prime number.","o":["91","63","49","59"],"a":3,"hint":"Try dividing each one by 3 and by 7.","why":"59 has only the factors 1 and 59. 49 = 7 × 7, 91 = 7 × 13 and 63 = 7 × 9 are composite."},{"c":"ws-prime-or-composite","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"A composite number is a number that has…","o":["only one factor","more than two factors","no factors at all","exactly two factors: 1 and itself"],"a":1,"hint":"Think of 4: its factors are 1, 2 and 4.","why":"Composite numbers, like 4, 6 and 9, have more than two factors. Prime numbers have exactly two."},{"c":"ws-prime-or-composite","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Why is 1 NOT a prime number?","o":["It has only one factor: itself","It has too many factors","It is a multiple of every number","It is an even number"],"a":0,"hint":"How many factors does a prime number need?","why":"A prime number has exactly two factors. 1 has only one, so it is neither prime nor composite."},{"c":"ws-missing-factor","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 7 × ___ = 56","o":["6","8","9","7"],"a":1,"hint":"Count in sevens: 7, 14, 21…","why":"7 × 8 = 56."},{"c":"ws-missing-factor","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: ___ × 3 = 24","o":["6","9","8","7"],"a":2,"hint":"24 ÷ 3 = ?","why":"8 × 3 = 24."},{"c":"ws-factor-pairs","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which is a factor pair of 72?","o":["12 and 7","9 and 7","36 and 36","8 and 9"],"a":3,"hint":"Multiply each pair. Which makes exactly 72?","why":"8 × 9 = 72. (12 × 7 = 84, 36 × 36 = 1,296 and 9 × 7 = 63.)"},{"c":"ws-prime-factors","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: the prime factors of 36 are…","o":["3 × 3 × 4","2 × 2 × 3 × 6","1 × 2 × 2 × 9","2 × 2 × 3 × 3"],"a":3,"hint":"Keep breaking the numbers down until every one is prime.","why":"36 = 2 × 18 = 2 × 2 × 9 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 3. The other answers use 1, 4, 6 or 9, which are not prime (and 2 × 2 × 3 × 6 makes 72)."},{"c":"ws-prime-factors","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: the prime factors of 42 are…","o":["6 × 7","2 × 21","2 × 3 × 7","3 × 14"],"a":2,"hint":"Use a factor tree: 42 = 2 × 21, and 21 = ?","why":"42 = 2 × 21 = 2 × 3 × 7. Every factor is now prime."},{"c":"ws-prime-factors","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: the prime factors of 26 are…","o":["2 × 3 × 4","2 × 2 × 13","2 × 13","1 × 26"],"a":2,"hint":"26 is even, so start with 2.","why":"26 = 2 × 13, and both 2 and 13 are prime."},{"c":"ws-hcf","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: the HCF of 12 and 18 is…","o":["2","36","6","3"],"a":2,"hint":"HCF = Highest Common Factor: the biggest number that divides both.","why":"Factors of 12: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12. Factors of 18: 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18. The highest common one is 6. (36 is their LCM.)"},{"c":"ws-hcf","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: the HCF of 24, 36 and 96 is…","o":["12","4","6","24"],"a":0,"hint":"Which is the biggest number that divides all three exactly?","why":"24 = 12 × 2, 36 = 12 × 3 and 96 = 12 × 8, and no bigger number divides all three. HCF = 12."},{"c":"ws-hcf","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: the HCF of 9 and 72 is…","o":["9","8","3","72"],"a":0,"hint":"Does 9 divide 72 exactly?","why":"72 = 9 × 8, so 9 itself divides both numbers. HCF = 9."},{"c":"ws-multiples","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 40 is a multiple of which number?","o":["15","6","8","11"],"a":2,"hint":"Which number's times table reaches 40?","why":"8 × 5 = 40, so 40 is a multiple of 8."},{"c":"ws-multiples","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: what is the tenth multiple of 9?","o":["99","90","19","81"],"a":1,"hint":"Tenth multiple = 9 × 10.","why":"9 × 10 = 90."},{"c":"ws-multiples","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 56 is the ___ multiple of 7.","o":["sixth","seventh","ninth","eighth"],"a":3,"hint":"7 × ? = 56","why":"7 × 8 = 56, so 56 is the eighth multiple of 7."},{"c":"ws-multiples","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which multiples of 6 are greater than 20 and less than 50?","o":["24, 30, 36, 42, 48","20, 26, 32, 38, 44","24, 30, 36, 42","18, 24, 30, 36, 42"],"a":0,"hint":"Start at the first multiple of 6 after 20, and stop before 50.","why":"6 × 4 = 24, then 30, 36, 42 and 48 (6 × 8). 54 is too big."},{"c":"ws-lcm","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: the least common multiple (LCM) of 6 and 9 is…","o":["36","3","18","54"],"a":2,"hint":"Count in nines: 9, 18… Is 18 a multiple of 6 too?","why":"Multiples of 9: 9, 18… and 18 = 6 × 3. So the LCM is 18. (54 and 36 are common multiples, but not the least.)"},{"c":"ws-lcm","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: three common multiples of 5 and 7 are…","o":["35, 45 and 55","12, 24 and 36","35, 70 and 105","5, 7 and 35"],"a":2,"hint":"The first one is 5 × 7.","why":"The LCM of 5 and 7 is 35, so 35, 70 and 105 are common multiples."},{"c":"ws-lcm","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: the LCM of 3, 9 and 12 is…","o":["108","72","12","36"],"a":3,"hint":"Count in twelves: 12, 24, 36… Which one is also a multiple of 9?","why":"36 = 3 × 12 = 9 × 4 = 12 × 3. It is the smallest number all three divide. (72 and 108 are common multiples, but bigger.)"},{"c":"ws-lcm","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: three common multiples of 2, 3 and 4 are…","o":["12, 24 and 36","8, 16 and 24","6, 12 and 18","24, 30 and 36"],"a":0,"hint":"The first common multiple is the LCM of 2, 3 and 4.","why":"The LCM of 2, 3 and 4 is 12, so 12, 24 and 36 are common multiples. 6, 8, 16, 18 and 30 are not multiples of all three."}],"r-nakli":[{"c":"raja-ki-chinta","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"राजा ने सब दरबारियों को दरबार में क्यों बुलवाया?","o":["हीरे बेचने के लिए","नया राजा चुनने के लिए","अपना जन्मदिन मनाने के लिए","बेटे का सलाहकार ढूँढ़ने के लिए"],"a":3,"hint":"Think about the old king's worry for his only son.","why":"राजा ने सोचा — “मुझे अपने पुत्र के लिए कोई सलाहकार ढूँढ़ना चाहिए।” So he called the court and asked a question. It was really a test to find a good advisor!"},{"c":"raja-ki-chinta","t":"Hindi","p":100,"q":"राजा का “एक ही” बेटा था। यहाँ “ही” से क्या पता चलता है?","o":["बेटा बहुत छोटा था","राजा का बस एक बेटा था, दूसरा नहीं","बेटा बिल्कुल राजा जैसा था","राजा का कोई बेटा नहीं था"],"a":1,"hint":"Say both aloud: “एक बेटा” and “एक ही बेटा”. What extra feeling does ही add?","why":"ही adds the meaning “only”: एक ही बेटा = only one son, no other. So finding a good advisor for this one son mattered a lot to the king."},{"c":"raja-ki-chinta","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"राजा “वृद्ध” था। इसलिए उसे किस बात की चिंता थी?","o":["उसके हीरे चोरी हो जाएँगे","वह अधिक दिन जीवित नहीं रहेगा","दरबारी उससे नाराज़ हैं","उसका बेटा उसकी बात नहीं मानता"],"a":1,"hint":"वृद्ध means old. Look at what the king was thinking in the very first picture.","why":"वृद्ध = बूढ़ा (old). The king thought, “मैं अधिक दिन जीवित नहीं रहूँगा” — I won't live much longer. So his son would need a good advisor."},{"c":"darbari-chaplusi","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"राजा के प्रश्न पर सब दरबारियों ने बारी-बारी से क्या कहा?","o":["आप-सा बुद्धिमान राजा कोई नहीं है","आपसे भी श्रेष्ठ राजा हो चुके हैं","हमें नहीं पता, महाराज","आप ईमानदार राजा नहीं हैं"],"a":0,"hint":"They all said the same thing, one by one. Was it big praise or careful truth?","why":"Each one said: “संसार में आप-सा बुद्धिमान और ईमानदार राजा और कोई नहीं है।” — no king in the world is like you! The “श्रेष्ठ राजा” line was the young courtier's."},{"c":"darbari-chaplusi","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"दरबारियों ने राजा की इतनी तारीफ़ क्यों की?","o":["क्योंकि वही पूरा सच था","क्योंकि प्रश्न समझ नहीं आया","राजा को ख़ुश करने के लिए","क्योंकि राजा ने ऐसा कहने को कहा था"],"a":2,"hint":"Later the king called their answer “झूठा” (false). So why would they say it?","why":"The young courtier said, “दूसरों ने तो आपको प्रसन्न करने वाले उत्तर दिए।” प्रसन्न = ख़ुश (happy). They said sweet words to please the king, not the full truth."},{"c":"darbari-chaplusi","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"दरबारियों ने तुरंत हीरों की जाँच जौहरी से करवाई। इससे क्या पता चलता है?","o":["वे राजा पर पूरा भरोसा करते थे","वे हीरे राजा को लौटाना चाहते थे","वे जौहरी को हीरे तोहफ़े में देना चाहते थे","उन्हें हीरों की क़ीमत की बहुत परवाह थी"],"a":3,"hint":"Would you rush to check a gift's price if you fully trusted the giver?","why":"जौहरी = jeweller. Rushing to check showed they cared a lot about the diamonds' value — and didn't fully trust the king. That's how they found out the diamonds were नकली!"},{"c":"imaandar-darbari","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"नौजवान दरबारी पहले चुपचाप कोने में क्यों खड़ा था?","o":["उसका उत्तर दूसरों जैसा नहीं था","उसे प्रश्न सुनाई नहीं दिया","उसके पास कोई उत्तर नहीं था","वह राजा से नाराज़ था"],"a":0,"hint":"He did have an answer. What was different about it?","why":"He said, “मेरा उत्तर वैसा नहीं है” — my answer is not like theirs. The others gave sweet praise; his honest answer was different, so he waited quietly."},{"c":"imaandar-darbari","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"नौजवान दरबारी ने राजा को क्या उत्तर दिया?","o":["संसार में आप-सा राजा कोई नहीं है","आप बिल्कुल बुद्धिमान नहीं हैं","मुझे भी एक हीरा चाहिए","आप बुद्धिमान हैं, पर आपसे श्रेष्ठ राजा भी हुए"],"a":3,"hint":"He was honest but kind. Did he say the king was NOT wise?","why":"He said the king IS wise and honest — “पर आपसे भी श्रेष्ठ राजा हो चुके हैं” — but there have been even better kings. Honest, without being rude!"},{"c":"imaandar-darbari","t":"Hindi","p":100,"q":"दरबारी ने कहा, “क्षमा कीजिएगा, मेरा उत्तर वैसा नहीं है।” इससे पता चलता है कि वह…","o":["घमंडी था (proud)","विनम्र था (polite)","डरपोक था (timid)","झूठा था (a liar)"],"a":1,"hint":"“क्षमा कीजिएगा” means “please excuse me”. What kind of person talks like that?","why":"क्षमा कीजिएगा = excuse me, forgive me. He told the truth politely — a विनम्र (polite, humble) young man. Polite AND honest: the best mix!"},{"c":"nakli-asli-heere","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"राजा ने बाक़ी दरबारियों को नकली हीरे क्यों दिए?","o":["उन्होंने उसके प्रश्न का झूठा उत्तर दिया","राजा के असली हीरे ख़त्म हो गए थे","राजा उनसे बस मज़ाक कर रहा था","उन्होंने बहुत देर से उत्तर दिया"],"a":0,"hint":"Think of the king's rule: which kind of answer earned which kind of diamond?","why":"राजा: “आप सबने मेरे प्रश्न का झूठा उत्तर दिया… इसलिए हीरे भी आपको नकली दिए गए।” A fake answer earned a fake (नकली) diamond — fair and clever!"},{"c":"nakli-asli-heere","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"अगर नौजवान दरबारी भी राजा की झूठी तारीफ़ करता, तो क्या होता?","o":["उसे दो असली हीरे मिलते","राजा उसे तुरंत सलाहकार बना देता","उसे भी नकली हीरा मिलता","जौहरी हीरे को असली बताता"],"a":2,"hint":"He would be just like the other courtiers. What did THEY get?","why":"The king's rule: झूठा उत्तर → नकली हीरा. If he had flattered too, he'd get a fake diamond — and the king would still be searching for an honest advisor."},{"c":"nakli-asli-heere","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"इस चित्रकथा का नाम “नकली हीरे” क्यों रखा गया होगा?","o":["झूठे उत्तर पर नकली हीरे मिले","राजा को हीरे पसंद नहीं थे","जौहरी नकली हीरे बनाता था","राजा के मुकुट के हीरे नकली थे"],"a":0,"hint":"What did the fake diamonds have to do with the courtiers' answers?","why":"The नकली हीरे are the heart of the story: a false answer got a fake diamond. चित्रकथा = a story told in pictures and speech bubbles, like a comic."},{"c":"kya-hua","t":"Order","p":50,"q":"इनमें से सबसे पहले क्या हुआ?","o":["दरबारियों ने हीरों की जाँच करवाई","राजा ने दरबारियों से प्रश्न पूछा","राजा ने बेटे का सलाहकार चुना","दरबारी शिकायत करने वापस आए"],"a":1,"hint":"Picture the first morning in the दरबार. What started everything?","why":"Story order: राजा ने प्रश्न पूछा → सबको हीरे मिले → हीरों की जाँच हुई → दरबारी शिकायत करने लौटे → राजा ने सलाहकार चुना."},{"c":"kya-hua","t":"Order","p":50,"q":"सब दरबारी हीरे लेकर ख़ुशी-ख़ुशी चले गए। उसके ठीक बाद क्या हुआ?","o":["दरबारी जौहरी के पास से लौट आए","राजा ने सलाहकार की घोषणा की","राजा ने चुप खड़े दरबारी से उत्तर पूछा","राजा ने सबको दरबार में बुलवाया"],"a":2,"hint":"Not everyone left the दरबार. Who was still standing in a corner?","why":"One courtier stayed quietly in a corner. The king asked him, “आप चुप क्यों हैं? आपका उत्तर क्या है?” The jeweller news and the big announcement came the next morning."},{"c":"kya-hua","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"कहानी के बारे में कौन-सी बात ग़लत है?","o":["नौजवान दरबारी को नकली हीरा मिला","राजा बूढ़ा और बुद्धिमान था","दरबारियों ने हीरे जौहरी को दिखाए","राजा का एक ही बेटा था"],"a":0,"hint":"Three sentences match the story exactly. Check each one against what you read.","why":"The young courtier got the ONLY असली (real) diamond: “इसलिए इसे असली हीरा दिया गया।” All the others got नकली (fake) ones."},{"c":"vishwast-salahkar","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"राजा ने नौजवान को ही बेटे का सलाहकार क्यों चुना?","o":["वह चुप रहना अच्छी तरह जानता है","उसने सबसे लंबा उत्तर दिया","वह सच बोलता है, झूठी तारीफ़ नहीं करता","उसे असली हीरों की पहचान थी"],"a":2,"hint":"What did his answer show — that he flatters, or that he tells the truth?","why":"He gave a सीधा-सादा (simple, honest) answer, not flattery. An advisor who tells the truth helps a ruler make good choices. Staying quiet wasn't the point — honesty was!"},{"c":"vishwast-salahkar","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"एक दोस्त हर ड्रॉइंग को “सबसे अच्छी” कहता है। दूसरा बताता है कि क्या सुधारें। राजा किसे सलाहकार चुनता?","o":["हर बार तारीफ़ करने वाले को","दोनों दोस्तों को","किसी को भी नहीं","सच बताने वाले दोस्त को"],"a":3,"hint":"Which friend is like the young courtier, and which is like the other courtiers?","why":"The friend who honestly says what to improve is like the young courtier — a real helper. Praise for everything is nice to hear, but it doesn't help you get better."},{"c":"vishwast-salahkar","t":"Hindi","p":100,"q":"“…मेरे बाद यही मेरे बेटे का विश्वस्त सलाहकार रहेगा।” — “मेरे बाद” का मतलब?","o":["जब राजा नहीं रहेगा, तब","राजा के पीछे खड़े होकर","राजा के बेटे के बाद","अगली सुबह से"],"a":0,"hint":"Remember why the old king was looking for an advisor in the first place.","why":"मेरे बाद = after me — when the old king is no longer there. Then this honest young man will guide the king's son."},{"c":"vishwast-salahkar","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"“विश्वस्त सलाहकार” — कैसा सलाहकार?","o":["जो पूरा विश्व घूम चुका हो","जो बहुत बूढ़ा हो","जो बहुत अमीर हो","जिस पर भरोसा किया जा सके"],"a":3,"hint":"It's linked to the word विश्वास, as in “मुझे तुम पर विश्वास है”.","why":"विश्वस्त = trusted, someone you can rely on (from विश्वास = trust). It has nothing to do with विश्व (world)! An honest person is easy to trust."},{"c":"shabd-arth","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"कौन-सा शब्द “बुद्धिमान” जैसा नहीं है?","o":["विवेकवान","समझदार","होशियार","धनवान"],"a":3,"hint":"बुद्धि = intelligence. Which word is about something other than the mind?","why":"विवेकवान, समझदार and होशियार all mean wise or clever — like बुद्धिमान. धनवान means rich (धन = money). Being rich doesn't make you wise!"},{"c":"shabd-arth","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"“निस्संदेह महाराज…” — “निस्संदेह” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["शायद","बिना किसी संदेह के","बिल्कुल नहीं","धीरे-धीरे"],"a":1,"hint":"संदेह means doubt. Did the courtiers sound sure or unsure?","why":"संदेह = doubt, so निस्संदेह = without any doubt, surely. The courtiers sounded very sure — even though they weren't telling the truth!"},{"c":"shabd-arth","t":"पर्यायवाची","p":50,"q":"“…आपसे भी श्रेष्ठ राजा हो चुके हैं…” — “श्रेष्ठ” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["बहुत बूढ़ा","बहुत अमीर","सर्वोत्तम, बहुत अच्छा","बहुत ताक़तवर"],"a":2,"hint":"The courtier is comparing how good kings were at being wise and honest.","why":"श्रेष्ठ = सर्वोत्तम (excellent, the best). He meant there were kings even better than this one in wisdom and honesty."},{"c":"shabd-arth","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"राजा बोला, “ठहरिए… मैं समझाता हूँ।” — “ठहरिए” का मतलब?","o":["चले जाइए","जल्दी कीजिए","रुकिए","बैठ जाइए"],"a":2,"hint":"The courtiers were upset and wanted answers. What did the king want them to do first?","why":"ठहरना = रुकना (to stop, to wait), so ठहरिए = please wait. The king was saying, “Wait — let me explain.”"},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"मुहावरा “आँखों में धूल झोंकना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["धोखा देना","आँखें साफ़ करना","धूल उड़ाना","रास्ता दिखाना"],"a":0,"hint":"It's not about real dust! With dust in your eyes, can you see what is really happening?","why":"आँखों में धूल झोंकना = to trick someone (धोखा देना). In the poem “हवा और धूल”, हवा does exactly this: “धूल की आँख में धूल झोंककर” — and then it's gone!"},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"शार्क को देखते ही छोटी मछलियाँ “हवा हो गईं”। इसका मतलब?","o":["हवा में उड़ने लगीं","तुरंत ग़ायब हो गईं","बहुत ठंडी हो गईं","गहरी नींद में सो गईं"],"a":1,"hint":"Fish can't fly! What do little fish do when a shark swims near?","why":"हवा हो जाना = to vanish in a flash. The little fish zipped out of sight! The poem ends the same way: “हवा हो गई हवा-हवाई!”"},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"राजा को ख़ुश करने के लिए दरबारियों ने उसकी “हाँ में हाँ मिलाई”। इसका मतलब?","o":["ज़ोर-ज़ोर से गाना गाया","बिना सोचे बस सहमति जताई","सच-सच बता दिया","राजा से बहस की"],"a":1,"hint":"Picture someone saying “हाँ जी, हाँ जी!” to everything the boss says.","why":"हाँ में हाँ मिलाना = to agree with everything just to please someone. That's what the courtiers did — only the young man spoke his own mind."},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"राजा ने सच्चे और झूठे दरबारियों को अलग-अलग पहचान लिया। कौन-सा मुहावरा ठीक है?","o":["दूध का दूध, पानी का पानी करना","हवा से बातें करना","आग बबूला होना","मुँह में पानी आना"],"a":0,"hint":"Which idiom is about separating two things that were mixed together?","why":"दूध का दूध, पानी का पानी करना = to separate truth from lies and judge fairly. The king's diamond test did just that! (हवा से बातें करना = to go super fast.)"},{"c":"anekarthi-shabd","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"राजा ने पूछा, “आपका उत्तर क्या है?” — यहाँ “उत्तर” का अर्थ?","o":["उत्तर दिशा (north)","जवाब (answer)","प्रश्न (question)","इनाम (reward)"],"a":1,"hint":"उत्तर has two meanings. Which one fits a king who has just asked a question?","why":"उत्तर is an अनेकार्थी शब्द (one word, many meanings): जवाब (answer) or a direction (north). Here it's the answer. “हिमालय भारत के उत्तर में है” uses the other one!"},{"c":"anekarthi-shabd","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"झूठे दरबारियों को अपने झूठ का “फल” मिला। यहाँ “फल” का अर्थ?","o":["खाने वाला फल (fruit)","पेड़ (tree)","बीज (seed)","नतीजा (result)"],"a":3,"hint":"Nobody got mangoes here! What did the courtiers get because of their lie?","why":"फल means fruit — and also नतीजा (result). Here the result of their lie was fake diamonds. Like “मेहनत का फल मीठा होता है” — hard work brings sweet results."},{"c":"anekarthi-shabd","t":"Hindi","p":100,"q":"किस वाक्य में “सोना” का मतलब “नींद लेना” (to sleep) है?","o":["जौहरी ने सोना तौला।","सोना एक क़ीमती धातु है।","रात को जल्दी सोना चाहिए।","राजा ने सोना दान किया।"],"a":2,"hint":"In three sentences, सोना is something you can weigh, give away or call a metal. In one, it's something you do.","why":"सोना = gold, and सोना = to sleep. “रात को जल्दी सोना चाहिए” = we should sleep early. In the poem, “सोती धूल अचानक जागी” — the sleeping dust woke up!"},{"c":"class-muhavare-c","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “सोने पर सुहागा” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["अच्छी चीज़ में और अच्छाई जुड़ना","घर से सोने की चोरी हो जाना","अच्छी चीज़ का अचानक खो जाना","बहुत आलसी और सुस्त होना"],"a":0,"hint":"अच्छे शिक्षक के साथ अच्छा विद्यालय भी मिल जाए तो…","why":"सोने पर सुहागा = अच्छी चीज़ में और अच्छाई जुड़ना।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-c","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “कलई खुलना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["राहत मिलना","दरवाज़ा खुल जाना","बहुत ख़ुश होना","सच्चाई सामने आना"],"a":3,"hint":"झूठ ज़्यादा दिन छिपता नहीं…","why":"कलई खुलना = सच्चाई सामने आना। नकली हीरों की तरह, झूठी तारीफ़ की भी कलई खुल जाती है।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-c","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “वाहवाही लूटना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["पछताना","डर जाना","चोरी करना","प्रशंसा पाना"],"a":3,"hint":"सुंदर नृत्य देखकर सब “वाह-वाह” करते हैं।","why":"वाहवाही लूटना = प्रशंसा पाना। यहाँ “लूटना” का मतलब चोरी नहीं है!"},{"c":"class-muhavare-c","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “सिर आँखों पर बिठाना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["बहुत डाँटना","पछताना","सिर पर बोझ रखना","बहुत आदर करना"],"a":3,"hint":"हम अपने अतिथियों के साथ ऐसा करते हैं।","why":"सिर आँखों पर बिठाना = बहुत आदर करना।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-c","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “सिर ऊँचा करना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["शर्म से झुक जाना","गर्व महसूस कराना","ऊपर की ओर देखना","बहुत गुस्सा होना"],"a":1,"hint":"अच्छे अंक लाकर परिवार का…","why":"सिर ऊँचा करना = गर्व महसूस कराना।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-d","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “नाक-भौंह सिकोड़ना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["ज़ोर-ज़ोर से हँसना","नापसंदगी या नाराज़गी दिखाना","किसी की सहायता करना","बहुत ख़ुश होकर नाचना-गाना"],"a":1,"hint":"कड़वी दवा देखकर चेहरा कैसा बनता है?","why":"नाक-भौंह सिकोड़ना = नापसंदगी या नाराज़गी प्रकट करना।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-d","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “छक्के छुड़ा देना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["किसी की बहुत तारीफ़ करना","किसी को राहत दे देना","बुरी तरह हरा देना या परेशान कर देना","क्रिकेट में एक के बाद एक छक्का मारना"],"a":2,"hint":"हमारी टीम ने विरोधी टीम के साथ क्या किया?","why":"छक्के छुड़ा देना = बुरी तरह परेशान कर देना या हरा देना। इसका क्रिकेट के छक्के से कोई लेना-देना नहीं!"},{"c":"class-muhavare-d","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “मन मोह लेना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["डराना","नाराज़ करना","भूल जाना","आकर्षित करना"],"a":3,"hint":"बगीचे के सुंदर फूलों ने सबका…","why":"मन मोह लेना = आकर्षित करना।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-d","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “जी-जान लगाना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["पूरी मेहनत करना","जान बचाकर भागना","आराम करना","हार मान लेना"],"a":0,"hint":"प्रतियोगिता जीतने के लिए…","why":"जी-जान लगाना = पूरी मेहनत करना।"},{"c":"class-muhavare-d","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"कक्षा की सूची: “घी के दिए जलाना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["अँधेरा कर देना","ख़ुशियाँ मनाना","पैसे बर्बाद करना","खाना पकाना"],"a":1,"hint":"दिवाली पर…","why":"घी के दिए जलाना = ख़ुशियाँ मनाना।"},{"c":"anekarthi-class-2","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"अनेकार्थी शब्द (कक्षा की कॉपी): “बाल” के दो अर्थ हैं…","o":["हाथ और पैर","पेड़ और पत्ता","केश और बालक","दूध और दही"],"a":2,"hint":"एक अर्थ सिर पर उगता है।","why":"बाल = केश (hair), और बाल = बालक (बच्चा)।"},{"c":"anekarthi-class-2","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"अनेकार्थी शब्द: “अंक” के दो अर्थ हैं…","o":["पैर और हाथ","गोद और संख्या","आँख और कान","घर और गाँव"],"a":1,"hint":"परीक्षा में मिलते हैं, और माँ बच्चे को इसमें बिठाती है।","why":"अंक = गोद, और अंक = संख्या (परीक्षा के अंक)।"},{"c":"anekarthi-class-2","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"अनेकार्थी शब्द: “वार” के दो अर्थ हैं…","o":["पानी और हवा","दिन और हमला","दवा और डॉक्टर","रात और नींद"],"a":1,"hint":"सोमवार, मंगलवार… और तलवार का…","why":"वार = दिन (सोमवार), और वार = हमला।"},{"c":"anekarthi-class-2","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"अनेकार्थी शब्द: “काल” के दो अर्थ हैं…","o":["खेत और खलिहान","मृत्यु और समय","काला और नीला","आवाज़ और शोर"],"a":1,"hint":"भूतकाल, वर्तमान काल…","why":"काल = समय (भूतकाल), और काल = मृत्यु।"},{"c":"anekarthi-vakya-2","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"“किसान खेत में हल चलाता है।” यहाँ “हल” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["रास्ता","समाधान","पानी","एक औज़ार"],"a":3,"hint":"किसान इससे खेत जोतता है।","why":"हल के दो अर्थ हैं: समाधान (सवाल का हल) और खेत जोतने का औज़ार। यहाँ औज़ार।"}],"r-raj":[{"c":"perennial-seasonal","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A river that has water all year round is called a…","o":["Perennial river","Seasonal river","Desert river","Salty river"],"a":0,"hint":"A seasonal river flows only in one season. Which word means the opposite?","why":"Perennial rivers flow all year. Himalayan rivers like the Ganga and Yamuna are perennial because melting snow and rain both feed them."},{"c":"perennial-seasonal","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Is the Luni a perennial river? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — melting snow feeds it all year","No — it is fed by rain and dries up","Yes — desert sand keeps it full","No — it flows only in winter"],"a":1,"hint":"Does any snowy mountain feed the Luni?","why":"The Luni is a seasonal river: it fills up with monsoon rain and stays dry for much of the year. No snowy mountains feed it."},{"c":"perennial-seasonal","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Odd one out — which river is SEASONAL, not perennial?","o":["Ganga","Yamuna","Brahmaputra","Luni"],"a":3,"hint":"Three of these begin in the snowy Himalayas.","why":"The Ganga, Yamuna and Brahmaputra start in the snowy Himalayas and flow all year. The Luni of Rajasthan is rain-fed and runs dry for much of the year."},{"c":"luni-river","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which river flows through the dry western part of Rajasthan?","o":["Kaveri","Brahmaputra","Ganga","Luni"],"a":3,"hint":"It’s the river that turns salty as it flows.","why":"The Luni is the main river of the dry Thar region. It starts in the Aravalli hills and flows south-west across Rajasthan."},{"c":"luni-river","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"The Luni’s water is fresh at first but turns salty as it flows on. Why?","o":["Sea water rushes up into it","Too much rain falls on it","It picks up salt from salty land","Sharks swim up and bring salt"],"a":2,"hint":"Think about what the river touches as it flows along the ground.","why":"The Luni flows over land with lots of salt in the soil. The water picks up this salt, so it turns salty downstream. (No sharks either — the Luni is dry for much of the year!)"},{"c":"luni-river","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which fact about the Luni river is WRONG?","o":["It flows through Rajasthan","It gets its water from rain","Its water turns salty downstream","It flows into the Bay of Bengal"],"a":3,"hint":"Is the Bay of Bengal to the east or the west of India?","why":"The Luni never reaches the Bay of Bengal, far away in the east. It ends in the salty marshes of the Rann of Kutch in Gujarat."},{"c":"thar-desert","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The Thar Desert is also called the…","o":["Cold Desert","Sahara of Asia","Great Indian Desert","Salt Desert"],"a":2,"hint":"Think big — it’s named after our whole country.","why":"The Thar is called the Great Indian Desert, and most of it lies in Rajasthan. India’s cold desert is far away in Ladakh."},{"c":"thar-desert","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Desert sand gets very hot by day. What happens to it at night?","o":["It gets even hotter","It turns into mud","It stays just as hot","It cools down quickly"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of a beach: burning hot at noon, but at night?","why":"Sand heats up fast in the Sun and loses its heat fast at night. That’s why deserts have hot days and much cooler nights."},{"c":"thar-desert","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Why is every drop of water so precious in the Thar Desert?","o":["Very little rain falls there","Desert people don’t like rain","Its rivers flow all year","Sand turns water into salt"],"a":0,"hint":"What makes a place a desert?","why":"The Thar gets very little rain, and its rivers are seasonal. So people there have clever ways to catch and store every drop."},{"c":"rajasthan-map","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which country lies along Rajasthan’s western border?","o":["Nepal","Bangladesh","Pakistan","China"],"a":2,"hint":"Rajasthan is in India’s north-west. Which neighbour is on that side?","why":"Pakistan lies to the west of Rajasthan. The Thar Desert even stretches across the border into Pakistan."},{"c":"rajasthan-map","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{R} flies from Noida to Jaisalmer, deep in the Thar Desert. Which way does his plane mostly head?","o":["East","West","North","South"],"a":1,"hint":"On a map with north at the top, is Rajasthan to the left or right of Delhi?","why":"Jaisalmer lies far to the west of Noida. On a map with north at the top, west is on the left — and that’s where Rajasthan and the Thar are."},{"c":"desert-water-saving","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Chand Baori, a stepwell in Rajasthan, has thousands of steps. Why so many?","o":["To reach water even when it drops low","To keep camels fit","To store grain safely","To keep snakes away"],"a":0,"hint":"In summer, the water in a well sinks lower and lower.","why":"In dry months the water in a stepwell drops deep down. Many steps let people walk down and reach it all year round."},{"c":"desert-water-saving","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In Rajasthan, a johad is…","o":["A desert plant","A small mud dam that holds rainwater","A kind of camel race","A river from the mountains"],"a":1,"hint":"It’s one of the old ways people in Rajasthan save rain.","why":"A johad is a small mud wall that stops rainwater so it collects in a pond. The water slowly soaks into the ground and fills up nearby wells."},{"c":"desert-water-saving","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Rainwater is kept in covered underground tanks called kunds or tankas. Why keep them covered?","o":["To keep the water warm","So camels can stand on them","To hide water from neighbours","To keep water clean and stop it drying up"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of two things the hot, dusty desert could do to open water.","why":"A cover keeps out dust, leaves and animals, and stops the hot desert Sun from drying the water up. So the water stays clean and lasts longer."},{"c":"indira-gandhi-canal","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The Indira Gandhi Canal carries river water into the Thar Desert from which state?","o":["Punjab","Kerala","Assam","Goa"],"a":0,"hint":"It’s the state just north of Rajasthan.","why":"The Indira Gandhi Canal starts in Punjab, where the Sutlej and Beas rivers meet, and carries their water far into the Thar."},{"c":"indira-gandhi-canal","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out — which one brings water from far-away rivers instead of storing rain?","o":["Johad","Kund","Indira Gandhi Canal","Baori"],"a":2,"hint":"Three of these hold rain that falls nearby.","why":"Johads, kunds and baoris catch and store rain close to where it falls. The Indira Gandhi Canal is different: it carries river water all the way from Punjab."},{"c":"indira-gandhi-canal","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"The Indira Gandhi Canal reached dry parts of the Thar. What changed there?","o":["The sand dunes all vanished","Farmers could grow crops","The Luni became salty","Summers stopped being hot"],"a":1,"hint":"What do farmers need most to water their fields?","why":"Canal water lets farmers water their fields, so crops now grow where there was mostly sand. The desert is still hot, though!"},{"c":"camels","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which camel fact is WRONG?","o":["Its hump stores water","Long eyelashes keep out sand","Wide feet stop it sinking in sand","It can go days without drinking"],"a":0,"hint":"Lots of people get this one wrong! What is the hump really made of?","why":"A camel’s hump stores FAT, not water! The fat is its food store for hard times. The other three are true — that’s why camels suit the desert."},{"c":"camels","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Why is the camel called the “ship of the desert”?","o":["It can float on water","It has sails on its back","It carries people and loads across sand","It lives near the sea"],"a":2,"hint":"What does a ship do for people at sea?","why":"Just as ships carry people and goods across the sea, camels carry them across the ‘sea’ of sand — for days, without needing much water."},{"c":"camels","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A sandstorm blows across the Thar. How does a camel protect itself?","o":["It digs a deep hole and hides","Shuts its nostrils; lashes guard its eyes","It runs to the nearest river","It climbs up a tall tree"],"a":1,"hint":"Think about the parts of its face that sand could get into.","why":"A camel can shut its nostrils to keep sand out, and two rows of long eyelashes guard its eyes. Built for sandstorms!"},{"c":"sheet-raj-rivers","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: the perennial river of Rajasthan is the…","o":["Chambal","Luni","Sahibi","Ganga"],"a":0,"hint":"It's the deep one, not the one that dries up.","why":"The Chambal is Rajasthan's perennial river; the Luni is seasonal."},{"c":"sheet-raj-rivers","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: the two main rivers of Rajasthan are the…","o":["Luni and Chambal","Ganga and Yamuna","Godavari and Krishna","Brahmaputra and Meghna"],"a":0,"hint":"One is seasonal, one is perennial.","why":"The Luni and the Chambal are the two main rivers of Rajasthan."},{"c":"sheet-raj-thar","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: which desert covers most of Rajasthan?","o":["Thar","Sahara","Gobi","Kalahari"],"a":0,"hint":"It's also called the Great Indian Desert.","why":"The Thar Desert covers most of Rajasthan."},{"c":"sheet-raj-sources","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: where does the Chambal begin?","o":["Vindhya range, Indore district (MP)","Aravali mountains","Gangotri glacier","Thar desert"],"a":0,"hint":"It starts in Madhya Pradesh.","why":"The Chambal starts in the Vindhya range in Indore district, Madhya Pradesh."},{"c":"sheet-raj-sources","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: where does the Luni begin?","o":["Aravali mountains","Vindhya range","Himalayas","Bay of Bengal"],"a":0,"hint":"The old hills that cross Rajasthan.","why":"The Luni starts in the Aravali mountains."},{"c":"sheet-raj-compare","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: which is TRUE about the Luni and the Chambal?","o":["Luni is seasonal and shallow; Chambal is perennial and deep","Luni is perennial and deep; Chambal is seasonal and shallow","Both are perennial","Both dry up in summer"],"a":0,"hint":"Which one dries up in summer?","why":"The Luni is seasonal, rain-fed and shallow; the Chambal flows all year and is deep."},{"c":"sheet-raj-pollution","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Practice sheet: how do the textile factories harm the Luni?","o":["They dump waste and dyes; a bluish streak shows when it dries","They take out all its salt","They build stepwells in it","They plant trees along it"],"a":0,"hint":"What colourful waste does a cloth factory make?","why":"Textile factories dump waste and dyes into the Luni; in summer a bluish streak is left instead of water."},{"c":"sheet-raj-rivers","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: per your sheet, the only perennial river of Madhya Pradesh is the…","o":["Chambal","Luni","Sahibi","Ganga"],"a":0,"hint":"It starts in Indore district.","why":"Your sheet's answer: the Chambal."}],"r-sci":[{"c":"fibre-basics","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What is a fibre?","o":["A button on a shirt","A piece of finished cloth","A thick twisted rope","A very thin, thread-like strand"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of the tiny hair-like bits you can pull out of a cotton ball.","why":"Fibres are thin, thread-like strands. Many fibres twisted together make yarn, and yarn is woven or knitted into cloth."},{"c":"fibre-basics","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Fibres we get from plants and animals are called…","o":["Synthetic fibres","Natural fibres","Plastic fibres","Factory fibres"],"a":1,"hint":"They come straight from nature.","why":"Cotton and jute (from plants) and wool and silk (from animals) all come from nature, so they are natural fibres. Synthetic fibres are made in factories."},{"c":"fibre-basics","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{R} reads the labels on his clothes. Which label shows a NATURAL fibre?","o":["100% polyester","100% cotton","100% acrylic","100% nylon"],"a":1,"hint":"Which one grows on a plant?","why":"Cotton grows on the cotton plant, so it is natural. Polyester, nylon and acrylic are all made in factories from chemicals."},{"c":"natural-or-synthetic","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Spot the WRONG fact.","o":["Nylon is a natural fibre","Silk comes from an insect","Jute comes from a plant","Wool comes from animals"],"a":0,"hint":"Which fibre is made in a factory?","why":"Nylon is synthetic — it's made in factories from chemicals. Silk comes from silkworms, jute from a plant and wool from sheep."},{"c":"natural-or-synthetic","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Odd one out — which fibre is NOT man-made?","o":["Nylon","Polyester","Silk","Acrylic"],"a":2,"hint":"Three are made in factories. One is spun by a tiny caterpillar.","why":"Silk is a natural fibre spun by silkworms. Nylon, polyester and acrylic are all synthetic (man-made) fibres."},{"c":"natural-or-synthetic","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which pair are BOTH plant fibres?","o":["Cotton and jute","Wool and silk","Nylon and cotton","Silk and jute"],"a":0,"hint":"Think of fibres that grow in fields.","why":"Cotton comes from cotton bolls and jute from the stem of the jute plant. Wool and silk come from animals; nylon is man-made."},{"c":"cotton","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which part of the cotton plant gives us cotton fibre?","o":["The roots","The fluffy bolls around its seeds","The leaves","The stem"],"a":1,"hint":"Picture the soft white balls on a cotton plant.","why":"Cotton fibres grow around the seeds inside the plant's fruit, called the boll. When the boll bursts open, the fluffy white cotton is picked."},{"c":"cotton","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Why are cotton clothes best for a hot summer day?","o":["Cotton keeps heat trapped in","Cotton soaks up sweat and lets air pass","Cotton is waterproof","Cotton is shiny"],"a":1,"hint":"On a hot day you sweat a lot. What should your shirt do with that sweat?","why":"Cotton soaks up sweat and lets air move through, so the sweat dries off and cools you. Wool traps heat — great for winter, not summer."},{"c":"cotton","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which sentence about cotton is WRONG?","o":["It grows on a plant","It keeps us cool in summer","It's made in factories from chemicals","It soaks up water well"],"a":2,"hint":"Is cotton natural or man-made?","why":"Cotton is a natural plant fibre. Fibres made from chemicals in factories, like nylon and polyester, are called synthetic."},{"c":"jute","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Jute fibre comes from which part of the jute plant?","o":["Seeds","Flowers","Stem","Roots"],"a":2,"hint":"It's the tall, long part of the plant.","why":"Jute fibres come from the long stem of the jute plant. The stems are soaked in water so the fibres loosen and can be pulled out."},{"c":"jute","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A farmer needs strong sacks to carry potatoes. Which fibre is the best pick?","o":["Thin cotton","Silk","Wool","Jute"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of rough, strong, brown gunny bags.","why":"Jute is rough and very strong, so it makes gunny bags, sacks and ropes. Silk is too soft and costly, and wool is for warmth."},{"c":"jute","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Why is a jute bag kinder to nature than a plastic bag?","o":["Jute is natural and rots away safely","Jute bags melt in the Sun","Jute is made from petrol","Jute bags can't hold anything"],"a":0,"hint":"What happens to each bag after it is thrown away?","why":"Jute comes from a plant, so an old jute bag rots back into the soil. Plastic can stay around for hundreds of years and harm animals."},{"c":"wool","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Odd one out — which animal does NOT give us wool?","o":["Sheep","Yak","Camel","Cow"],"a":3,"hint":"Three of these have thick hairy coats that people use for warm clothes.","why":"Wool comes from the hair of sheep, goats, yaks and camels. We get milk from cows, not wool."},{"c":"wool","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Cutting the woolly coat (fleece) off a sheep is called…","o":["Spinning","Weaving","Ginning","Shearing"],"a":3,"hint":"It's like a haircut for a sheep.","why":"Shearing takes off the fleece with big clippers. The wool is then washed, sorted, spun into yarn, and knitted or woven."},{"c":"wool","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Does shearing hurt the sheep? Pick the right answer AND reason.","o":["No — it's like a haircut; it grows back","Yes — the wool is cut from its skin","Yes — sheep never grow wool again","No — sheep can't feel anything"],"a":0,"hint":"Does a haircut hurt you?","why":"The fleece is hair, so cutting it doesn't hurt — just like your haircut. It grows back, and shearing in summer keeps the sheep cool."},{"c":"wool","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Why does a woollen sweater keep you warm in winter?","o":["Wool makes its own heat","Wool is very heavy","Its fibres trap air that holds in body heat","Wool blocks the cold sunlight"],"a":2,"hint":"A sweater lying on a chair doesn't feel warm. So where does the warmth come from?","why":"Wool doesn't make heat. Its fluffy fibres trap lots of air, which stops your own body heat from escaping — like a cosy blanket."},{"c":"silk","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Silk thread comes from…","o":["The seeds of a plant","A sheep's coat","The skin of a snake","The cocoon of a silkworm"],"a":3,"hint":"A tiny caterpillar wraps itself up in it.","why":"A silkworm spins a cocoon around itself from one long silk thread. People unwind the cocoon to get silk."},{"c":"silk","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Silkworms munch on the leaves of which tree?","o":["Mango","Mulberry","Neem","Peepal"],"a":1,"hint":"This tree also gives small, dark-purple berries.","why":"Silkworms eat mulberry leaves. Silk farmers grow mulberry trees to keep their silkworms well fed."},{"c":"silk","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Rearing silkworms to get silk is called…","o":["Shearing","Ginning","Weaving","Sericulture"],"a":3,"hint":"Shearing is for wool, ginning is for cotton, and weaving makes cloth.","why":"Sericulture means raising silkworms for silk. India is one of the biggest silk makers in the world!"},{"c":"silk","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"In a silk moth's life, at which stage do we get silk?","o":["When the eggs hatch","When the moth lays eggs","When the moth flies off","When the silkworm spins its cocoon"],"a":3,"hint":"Silk is the thread the caterpillar wraps around itself.","why":"Egg → silkworm (caterpillar) → cocoon → moth. The silkworm spins its cocoon out of silk thread, so that's the stage that gives us silk."},{"c":"synthetic-fibres","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which is a synthetic (man-made) fibre?","o":["Polyester","Silk","Cotton","Jute"],"a":0,"hint":"Three come from plants or animals. One is made in a factory.","why":"Polyester is made in factories from chemicals that come from petroleum. Cotton and jute are plant fibres; silk is from silkworms."},{"c":"synthetic-fibres","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Sawfish often get tangled in strong fishing nets that don't rot in water. These nets are mostly made of…","o":["Cotton","Wool","Nylon","Silk"],"a":2,"hint":"It's a strong, man-made fibre that also makes ropes and toothbrush bristles.","why":"Nylon is very strong and doesn't rot in water, which makes it perfect for fishing nets — and sadly very hard for a tangled sawfish to escape."},{"c":"synthetic-fibres","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"A nylon swimsuit dries faster than a cotton T-shirt. Why?","o":["Nylon soaks up very little water","Nylon is heavier","Cotton is man-made","Nylon comes from plants"],"a":0,"hint":"Which fabric drinks up more water?","why":"Nylon absorbs very little water, so it dries quickly. Cotton soaks up lots of water — great for sweat, but slow to dry."},{"c":"synthetic-fibres","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if a nylon fishing net gets lost at sea?","o":["It rots away in a few days","It turns into sand","It dissolves like sugar","It lasts for years and can trap sea animals"],"a":3,"hint":"Does nylon rot away like plant fibres do?","why":"Nylon doesn't rot easily, so a lost ‘ghost net’ can drift for years, trapping sharks, turtles and fish. That's why fishers try hard not to lose nets."},{"c":"spinning","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Twisting fibres together to make yarn is called…","o":["Weaving","Knitting","Spinning","Shearing"],"a":2,"hint":"It happens BEFORE weaving or knitting.","why":"Spinning twists short fibres into a long, strong yarn. Then the yarn is woven or knitted into fabric."},{"c":"spinning","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Gandhiji spun his own yarn every day on a…","o":["Loom","Sewing machine","Charkha","Knitting needle"],"a":2,"hint":"It's a wooden spinning wheel turned by hand.","why":"A charkha is a hand spinning wheel. Gandhiji spun cotton yarn on it to make khadi cloth. A loom weaves cloth — it doesn't spin yarn."},{"c":"spinning","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Why are fibres twisted together when we make yarn?","o":["Twisting makes the thread long and strong","Twisting changes cotton into silk","Twisting adds colour","Twisting melts fibres together"],"a":0,"hint":"Try pulling apart loose cotton wool, then a piece of thread.","why":"Single fibres are short and weak. Twisted together, they grip each other and make a long, strong yarn."},{"c":"weaving-knitting","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Your woollen sweater was most likely made by…","o":["Ginning","Weaving","Knitting","Shearing"],"a":2,"hint":"Think of needles and loops.","why":"Sweaters, socks and caps are usually knitted — one long yarn looped again and again, by hand with needles or by machine."},{"c":"weaving-knitting","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"How is knitting different from weaving?","o":["Knitting uses a loom; weaving uses needles","They are exactly the same","Knitting is only for silk","Knitting loops 1 yarn; weaving crosses 2 sets"],"a":3,"hint":"A sweater can unravel into one long thread. A hanky has threads going two ways.","why":"Weaving crosses two sets of yarn over and under each other on a loom. Knitting makes loops from a single yarn using needles."},{"c":"weaving-knitting","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Look at a cotton hanky with a magnifier: threads go over and under, up-down and side-to-side. How was it made?","o":["Weaving","Knitting","Spinning","Shearing"],"a":0,"hint":"Two sets of threads crossing each other — which method makes that?","why":"Weaving crosses two sets of yarn at right angles, over and under. Knitted cloth looks like rows of tiny loops instead."},{"c":"fibre-to-fabric-order","t":"Order","p":50,"q":"Which order is right for making cloth?","o":["Fabric → fibre → yarn","Fibre → yarn → fabric","Yarn → fibre → fabric","Fibre → fabric → yarn"],"a":1,"hint":"Start with the thinnest, smallest strands.","why":"Fibres are spun into yarn, and yarn is woven or knitted into fabric. Then the fabric is stitched into clothes."},{"c":"fibre-to-fabric-order","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"Making wool: what comes right after shearing the sheep?","o":["Knitting a sweater","Weaving on a loom","Washing the fleece","Wearing it"],"a":2,"hint":"Fresh fleece is greasy and dusty. What should happen to it first?","why":"The fleece is first washed to get rid of grease and dirt. Then it's sorted, dyed, spun into yarn, and finally knitted or woven."},{"c":"fibre-to-fabric-order","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"Which step comes LAST when making a cotton shirt?","o":["Picking cotton bolls","Stitching the fabric into a shirt","Weaving fabric","Spinning yarn"],"a":1,"hint":"You can't stitch a shirt until you have cloth.","why":"Pick cotton → spin yarn → weave fabric → stitch the shirt. Stitching needs ready cloth, so it comes last."},{"c":"fabric-for-season","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"What should you wear on a snowy day in Manali?","o":["Thin cotton","Woollen clothes","A silk shirt","A jute sack"],"a":1,"hint":"Choose what traps air and keeps your body heat in.","why":"Woollen clothes trap air and keep your body heat in, so they're perfect for snow. Thin cotton is for hot days."},{"c":"fabric-for-season","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Monsoon is here! Which is best for a raincoat or an umbrella?","o":["Cotton — it soaks up water","Nylon or polyester — water runs off","Wool — it is warm","Jute — it is strong"],"a":1,"hint":"You want a fabric that does NOT soak up water.","why":"Nylon and polyester soak up very little water, so rain slides off and they dry fast. Cotton would get soggy and heavy."},{"c":"fabric-for-season","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if you wore a thick woollen sweater on a hot May afternoon in Delhi?","o":["You'd feel cool and fresh","The sweater would melt","You'd feel too hot and sweaty","You'd stop sweating"],"a":2,"hint":"Wool traps body heat. Do you want that in summer?","why":"Wool traps air and holds in body heat — lovely in January, terrible in May! In summer, loose cotton lets heat and sweat escape."},{"c":"fabric-for-season","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Two kids play in the hot sun — one in a cotton shirt, one in a polyester shirt. Whose shirt feels stickier with sweat?","o":["The polyester one","The cotton one","Both the same","Neither — shirts don't touch sweat"],"a":0,"hint":"Which fabric soaks up sweat, and which one doesn't?","why":"Polyester absorbs very little sweat, so the sweat stays on your skin and feels sticky. Cotton soaks it up, so it feels cooler in summer."},{"c":"fabric-for-use","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Why should you NOT wear a nylon or polyester apron while cooking near a flame?","o":["It soaks up too much oil","It can melt and stick to the skin","It's too heavy","It changes colour"],"a":1,"hint":"What happens to plastic-like things when they get very hot?","why":"Synthetic fibres melt when they get very hot and can stick to skin, causing bad burns. A cotton apron is safer in the kitchen."},{"c":"fabric-for-use","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Parachutes need a fabric that is light but very strong. Most are made of…","o":["Cotton","Wool","Jute","Nylon"],"a":3,"hint":"It's man-made, and fishing nets are made of it too.","why":"Nylon is light, very strong and dries fast, so it's used for parachutes, ropes and tents. Jute is strong too, but far too heavy and rough."},{"c":"fabric-for-use","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Which fabric-and-use pair is a good match?","o":["Silk — festive sari","Nylon — kitchen apron","Wool — summer shorts","Jute — warm sweater"],"a":0,"hint":"Think: soft and shiny, worn on special days.","why":"Silk is soft, smooth and shiny — perfect for a festive sari. Wool shorts would be too hot, a nylon apron can melt, and jute is rough."},{"c":"ws-fibre-sources","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: wool is obtained from…","o":["plants","silkworms","sheep","earthworms"],"a":2,"hint":"Which animal gets a haircut called shearing?","why":"Wool comes from the fleece of sheep (and some other animals, like goats and yaks)."},{"c":"ws-fibre-sources","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which of these is a plant fibre?","o":["Wool","Silk","Nylon","Cotton"],"a":3,"hint":"It grows in fluffy bolls.","why":"Cotton comes from the cotton plant. Silk and wool come from animals, and nylon is made in factories."},{"c":"ws-fibre-sources","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: silk is made by…","o":["factories, from plastic","silkworms","sheep","plants"],"a":1,"hint":"It spins a cocoon.","why":"Silk comes from the cocoon that a silkworm spins."},{"c":"ws-fibre-processes","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: the process of making yarn from fibres is called…","o":["knitting","cutting","spinning","weaving"],"a":2,"hint":"Gandhiji did this on a charkha.","why":"Spinning twists fibres together into yarn. Weaving and knitting then turn yarn into fabric."},{"c":"ws-fibre-processes","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: knitting is done using…","o":["a comb","scissors","needles","a loom"],"a":2,"hint":"Think of how a sweater is made by hand.","why":"Knitting makes loops with one yarn and a pair of needles. A loom is used for weaving."},{"c":"ws-fibre-processes","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet, match: weaving is…","o":["cutting the wool off a sheep","making loops with one yarn and needles","making yarn by twisting fibres","making fabric using two sets of yarn"],"a":3,"hint":"Threads go over and under each other.","why":"Weaving criss-crosses two sets of yarn on a loom. Twisting fibres into yarn is spinning."},{"c":"ws-fibre-types","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: polyester is a…","o":["animal fibre","synthetic fibre","plant fibre","natural fibre"],"a":1,"hint":"Where is polyester made?","why":"Polyester is a synthetic (man-made) fibre, made in factories from chemicals."},{"c":"ws-fibre-types","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet, match: fibres made by humans in factories are called…","o":["natural fibres","plant fibres","synthetic fibres","animal fibres"],"a":2,"hint":"Nylon and polyester are examples.","why":"Synthetic fibres, like nylon and polyester, are made by humans. Natural fibres come from plants and animals."},{"c":"ws-fibre-types","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet, match: a shiny and smooth fibre is…","o":["cotton","wool","silk","jute"],"a":2,"hint":"It's used for festive saris.","why":"Silk is shiny and smooth. Jute is rough, wool is fluffy and cotton is soft but not shiny."},{"c":"ws-fibre-uses","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which fibre is strong, long-lasting and dries quickly?","o":["Wool","Nylon","Silk","Cotton"],"a":1,"hint":"It's used for raincoats and sportswear.","why":"Nylon is strong and long-lasting, and it dries quickly because it soaks up very little water."},{"c":"ws-fibre-uses","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: jute is mainly used for making…","o":["sweaters","bags and ropes","umbrellas","ties and sarees"],"a":1,"hint":"Jute is strong but rough.","why":"Jute is a strong, rough fibre from the stem of the jute plant, so it's used for sacks, bags and ropes."},{"c":"ws-fibre-uses","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which fabric keeps us warm in winter?","o":["Silk","Cotton","Nylon","Wool"],"a":3,"hint":"Sheep wear it all year!","why":"Wool traps air between its fibres, and that air holds in our body heat."},{"c":"ws-which-fibre","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: for a waterproof jacket in the monsoon, nylon or cotton?","o":["Cotton: it soaks up the rain well","Cotton: it is the warmest fibre of all","Nylon: it lets the rain soak through","Nylon: it doesn't soak up water and dries quickly"],"a":3,"hint":"Which one keeps you dry?","why":"Nylon soaks up very little water, so rain runs off and it dries quickly. Cotton soaks up water."},{"c":"ws-which-fibre","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: for a dress for a fancy party, silk or jute?","o":["Silk: it is rough and good for sacks","Jute: it is shiny, smooth and soft","Silk: it is shiny, smooth and soft","Jute: it is the softest fibre of all"],"a":2,"hint":"Which one is used for festive clothes?","why":"Silk is shiny, smooth and soft, so it suits a party. Jute is rough and is used for sacks and ropes."},{"c":"ws-which-fibre","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: to make a mop for cleaning floors, cotton or polyester threads?","o":["Cotton: it soaks up water well","Cotton: it melts when it gets wet","Polyester: it soaks up water best","Polyester: it is a plant fibre"],"a":0,"hint":"A mop has to soak up water.","why":"Cotton soaks up water well, so it is good for a mop. Polyester soaks up very little."},{"c":"ws-which-fibre-2","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: for a rock-climbing rope, cotton or nylon?","o":["Nylon: it is very strong","Cotton: it is the strongest fibre","Cotton: it dries the fastest","Nylon: it breaks very easily"],"a":0,"hint":"The rope must hold a climber's weight.","why":"Nylon is very strong and long-lasting, so climbing ropes are made of nylon."},{"c":"ws-which-fibre-2","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: a chef needs an apron that does not catch fire easily. Cotton or polyester?","o":["Cotton: polyester can melt and catch fire near a flame","Cotton: it is completely waterproof in rain","Polyester: it never burns, even in a flame","Polyester: it keeps the chef warm all day"],"a":0,"hint":"What happens to synthetic fabric near heat?","why":"Polyester and nylon can melt and stick to the skin near a flame, so a cotton apron is safer in the kitchen."}],"r-sst":[{"c":"sources-of-water","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What is the main source of water for our rivers, lakes and wells?","o":["Taps","Rain","Water tankers","Bottled water"],"a":1,"hint":"Think about what falls from the sky in the monsoon.","why":"Rain fills rivers, lakes and ponds, and soaks into the ground to fill wells. Taps and tankers only carry water — they don't make it."},{"c":"sources-of-water","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out — which is NOT a natural source of water?","o":["River","Lake","Spring","Tap"],"a":3,"hint":"Three of these exist in nature without anyone building them.","why":"Rivers, lakes and springs are natural sources. A tap is just the end of a pipe that brings water to your home from a source."},{"c":"sources-of-water","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"A handpump in a village brings up water from…","o":["The clouds","The sea","Under the ground","A river, through pipes"],"a":2,"hint":"Where does a handpump's pipe go — up or down?","why":"A handpump pulls up groundwater — rainwater that soaked into the soil and collected between rocks deep below."},{"c":"sources-of-water","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Where does the water in a deep well come from in the end?","o":["Sea water pushed up from below","Rain that soaked into the ground","Water made by the rocks","Pipes laid by the city"],"a":1,"hint":"Follow a raindrop after it lands on soil.","why":"Rainwater seeps down through soil and cracks in rocks and collects as groundwater. Wells and tube wells dig down to reach it."},{"c":"ganga","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Where does the river Ganga begin?","o":["Gangotri glacier","Yamunotri glacier","Bay of Bengal","Thar Desert"],"a":0,"hint":"It starts high up in the Himalayas, in Uttarakhand.","why":"The Ganga starts at the Gangotri glacier, at a spot called Gaumukh, in Uttarakhand. Yamunotri is where the Yamuna begins."},{"c":"ganga","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"India's national aquatic animal lives in the Ganga. What is it?","o":["Blue whale","Ganges river dolphin","Great white shark","Sawfish"],"a":1,"hint":"It's a mammal that breathes air and finds food using echoes.","why":"The Ganges river dolphin is India's national aquatic animal. It's almost blind and ‘sees’ with sound echoes in the muddy river!"},{"c":"ganga","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"At Prayagraj, the Ganga is joined by which river?","o":["Kaveri","Godavari","Narmada","Yamuna"],"a":3,"hint":"This river also starts from a glacier in Uttarakhand.","why":"The Yamuna meets the Ganga at Prayagraj, at a place called the Sangam. Kaveri, Narmada and Godavari flow far away in central and south India."},{"c":"ganga","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The Ganga's LAST Indian state before it reaches the sea is…","o":["Gujarat","West Bengal","Punjab","Kerala"],"a":1,"hint":"The Ganga flows east, towards the Bay of Bengal.","why":"The Ganga flows east through Uttarakhand, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Jharkhand and West Bengal, then into the Bay of Bengal."},{"c":"ganga","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which fact about the Ganga is WRONG?","o":["It flows into the Arabian Sea","It flows through Uttar Pradesh","It starts at the Gangotri glacier","The Yamuna joins it"],"a":0,"hint":"Does the Ganga flow east or west?","why":"The Ganga flows east and empties into the Bay of Bengal. Rivers like the Narmada flow west into the Arabian Sea."},{"c":"rivers-of-india","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which river flows through Delhi?","o":["Ganga","Brahmaputra","Yamuna","Kaveri"],"a":2,"hint":"It meets the Ganga later, at Prayagraj.","why":"The Yamuna flows past Delhi, then joins the Ganga at Prayagraj. The Ganga itself doesn't pass through Delhi."},{"c":"rivers-of-india","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which mighty river flows through Assam?","o":["Narmada","Brahmaputra","Kaveri","Yamuna"],"a":1,"hint":"It comes from Tibet and flows through India's north-east.","why":"The Brahmaputra comes from Tibet, flows through Assam, and joins the Ganga's waters in Bangladesh before reaching the sea."},{"c":"rivers-of-india","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Why do rivers like the Ganga have water even in the hot, dry summer?","o":["Melting Himalayan snow keeps feeding them","Sea water flows back into them","Water tankers fill them up","The Sun adds water to them"],"a":0,"hint":"Where do these rivers start? What happens there when it gets hot?","why":"Himalayan rivers start from glaciers. In summer the snow and ice melt, so these rivers keep flowing even when there's no rain."},{"c":"rivers-of-india","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Why did many big cities in India grow up next to rivers?","o":["Rivers kept the cities cold","Kings liked boat races","Rivers stopped the rain","Rivers gave water for drinking and farming"],"a":3,"hint":"What do people need every single day?","why":"Rivers gave people water to drink, cook and grow crops, plus a way to travel by boat. Delhi, Varanasi and Kolkata all sit on rivers."},{"c":"water-cycle","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The Sun heats water and it turns into water vapour. This is called…","o":["Condensation","Melting","Evaporation","Freezing"],"a":2,"hint":"It's the step where water rises up into the air.","why":"Evaporation turns liquid water into an invisible gas called water vapour, which rises into the air."},{"c":"water-cycle","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"High in the sky, water vapour cools and forms clouds. This step is called…","o":["Evaporation","Boiling","Condensation","Harvesting"],"a":2,"hint":"It's the opposite of evaporation.","why":"Condensation turns water vapour back into tiny water droplets. Millions of them together make a cloud."},{"c":"water-cycle","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"A drop of sea water goes on a trip. Which order is right?","o":["Rain → clouds → evaporation","Evaporation → rain → clouds","Clouds → evaporation → rain","Evaporation → clouds → rain"],"a":3,"hint":"First the drop has to leave the sea. How does it go up?","why":"The Sun evaporates the drop, the vapour cools into clouds (condensation), and it falls as rain. Then the trip starts all over again!"},{"c":"water-cycle","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Water falling from clouds as rain, snow or hail is called…","o":["Condensation","Evaporation","Precipitation","Collection"],"a":2,"hint":"It's the step where water comes back DOWN.","why":"Precipitation is any water falling from clouds — rain, snow or hail. It fills up rivers, lakes and the ground again."},{"c":"water-cycle","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if the Sun stopped heating the Earth's water?","o":["We'd get more rain","Seas would turn sweet","There'd be no evaporation, so no new rain","Clouds would get bigger"],"a":2,"hint":"Which step of the water cycle needs the Sun's heat?","why":"The Sun's heat drives evaporation. Without it, water couldn't rise to make new clouds — so the rain would stop."},{"c":"water-cycle-around-us","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Wet clothes on the line dry in the sun. Where did the water go?","o":["It vanished from Earth","Deep into the clothes","Down the drain","Into the air as water vapour"],"a":3,"hint":"You can't see it, but it went up.","why":"The Sun evaporated the water into water vapour, which mixed into the air. One day it will be part of a cloud!"},{"c":"water-cycle-around-us","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Will a puddle dry faster on a hot sunny day or a cool cloudy day?","o":["Cool cloudy day","Hot sunny day","Same speed","Puddles never dry"],"a":1,"hint":"Which day gives more heat for evaporation?","why":"More heat means faster evaporation, so the puddle disappears quicker on a hot sunny day."},{"c":"water-cycle-around-us","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Sharks swam in Earth's seas millions of years ago. Could today's rain hold some of the same water?","o":["No — new water is made every day","Only if it rains on the sea","No — old water is all used up","Yes — Earth keeps reusing its water"],"a":3,"hint":"The water cycle goes round and round…","why":"Earth's water keeps cycling — sea, sky, rain, rivers, sea again. So your raindrop may once have splashed around an ancient shark!"},{"c":"fresh-and-salt-water","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Most of the water on Earth is…","o":["Fresh water in rivers","Water under the ground","Water in clouds","Salty water in seas and oceans"],"a":3,"hint":"Look at a globe — what colour covers most of it?","why":"About 97 out of every 100 parts of Earth's water is salty sea water. That's why fresh water is so precious!"},{"c":"fresh-and-salt-water","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"If all of Earth's water filled 100 buckets, about how many would be fresh water?","o":["About 3","About 30","About 50","About 97"],"a":0,"hint":"Seas and oceans hold almost all the water, and they are salty.","why":"Only about 3 buckets would be fresh — and most of that is frozen as ice! The other 97 or so would be salty sea water."},{"c":"fresh-and-salt-water","t":"Connect","p":50,"q":"Some sawfish swim from the sea up into rivers. What kind of water are they moving into?","o":["Saltier water","Boiling water","Frozen water","Fresh water"],"a":3,"hint":"Is river water salty like the sea?","why":"River water is fresh, not salty. Most sea animals can't handle the change, but some sawfish and bull sharks can swim between the two!"},{"c":"fresh-and-salt-water","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"How do salt makers get common salt from sea water?","o":["Letting sea water dry up in the sun","Freezing the sea water","Boiling river water","Squeezing it from fish"],"a":0,"hint":"The water goes off into the air. What stays behind?","why":"Sea water is let into shallow salt pans. The sun evaporates the water, and the salt is left behind as white crystals."},{"c":"fresh-and-salt-water","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Sea water is salty, but rain is not. Why?","o":["Clouds filter out salt like a sieve","Rain falls only on rivers","Only water evaporates; salt stays in the sea","Salt melts in the sky"],"a":2,"hint":"Think of salt pans: when sea water dries up, what is left behind?","why":"When the Sun evaporates sea water, only the water rises. The salt stays behind in the sea, so clouds and rain are fresh."},{"c":"saving-water","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Which saves the MOST water while brushing your teeth?","o":["Brushing faster with the tap on","Turning the tap off while brushing","Using cold water","Using a smaller toothbrush"],"a":1,"hint":"Where does the water go when nobody is using it?","why":"A running tap can waste many litres in just two minutes. Turn it off while you brush, and use a mug to rinse."},{"c":"saving-water","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which habit WASTES water?","o":["Watering plants with leftover water","Fixing a dripping tap","Bathing with a bucket","Washing a car with a running hose"],"a":3,"hint":"Which one lets water run the whole time?","why":"A hose left running pours out water nonstop. A bucket and a cloth can wash a car with far less."},{"c":"saving-water","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"A tap drips all day and all night. Does it matter?","o":["No — the drops are too tiny","Yes — drips add up to many litres","No — the water comes back by evening","Only if the tap is gold"],"a":1,"hint":"One drop every second — how many in a whole day?","why":"One drip a second makes 86,400 drops in a day — they really add up! Fixing a leaky tap saves lots of water."},{"c":"saving-water","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Which uses LESS water: a bucket bath or a long shower?","o":["A long shower","A bucket bath","Both use the same","A shower with hot water"],"a":1,"hint":"A bucket holds a fixed amount. A shower keeps running.","why":"A bucket holds a set amount of water, while a long shower keeps pouring. A bucket bath usually uses much less."},{"c":"water-pollution","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which of these makes river water dirty?","o":["Fish swimming in it","A sailboat on it","Rain falling on it","Factory waste poured into it"],"a":3,"hint":"Look for something people put into the river.","why":"Some factories dump chemicals into rivers, which poisons the water for fish, plants and people."},{"c":"water-pollution","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Sea turtles sometimes eat plastic bags floating in the sea. Why?","o":["The bags look like jellyfish","Plastic tastes sweet","Turtles need plastic to grow","They want to clean the sea"],"a":0,"hint":"Turtles love a squishy, see-through sea snack.","why":"A floating plastic bag looks just like a jellyfish, a favourite turtle food. Eating plastic can make turtles very sick."},{"c":"water-pollution","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What may happen if people drink polluted, dirty water?","o":["They grow stronger","Nothing — all water is safe","They may fall ill with cholera","They stop feeling thirsty"],"a":2,"hint":"Dirty water can carry tiny germs you cannot see.","why":"Polluted water can carry germs that cause cholera, typhoid and stomach upsets. That's why we drink clean, boiled or filtered water."},{"c":"water-pollution","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which is a safe way to make water fit to drink at home?","o":["Adding sugar","Putting it in the fridge","Boiling it","Stirring it fast"],"a":2,"hint":"Strong heat kills germs.","why":"Boiling water for a few minutes kills most germs. Filters and purifiers help too. Cooling or stirring doesn't clean it."},{"c":"water-pollution","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Which of these does NOT pollute a river?","o":["Planting trees along its banks","Bathing animals in it","Throwing garbage into it","Letting sewage flow into it"],"a":0,"hint":"One of these actually helps the river.","why":"Trees along the banks hold the soil and keep the river healthy. Garbage, sewage and washing animals in it all make the water dirty."},{"c":"rainwater-harvesting","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Collecting and storing rainwater to use later is called…","o":["Evaporation","Rainwater harvesting","Irrigation","Flooding"],"a":1,"hint":"Irrigation brings water TO fields. This one SAVES rain for later.","why":"Rainwater harvesting means catching rain — from rooftops or the ground — and storing it, instead of letting it run away."},{"c":"rainwater-harvesting","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"In a city building, where is rainwater usually collected for harvesting?","o":["From the rooftop, into a tank or pit","From the kitchen sink","From the washing machine","From the road into the drain"],"a":0,"hint":"Rain lands first on the highest part of the building.","why":"Rain on the roof flows down pipes into a tank to store, or into a pit to soak into the ground. Sink water is used water, not rain."},{"c":"rainwater-harvesting","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Rainwater is sent into a pit to soak into the ground. How does this help?","o":["It makes the soil salty","It stops all future rain","It refills groundwater for wells and pumps","It turns into sea water"],"a":2,"hint":"Where does a handpump get its water from?","why":"Water soaking down refills the groundwater, so wells, handpumps and tube wells don't run dry in summer."},{"c":"rainwater-harvesting","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Agrasen ki Baoli in Delhi is an old stepwell. Why were stepwells like it built?","o":["To store water for the dry months","To grow fish for the market","To store the city's grain","As a swimming pool for kings"],"a":0,"hint":"Delhi gets lots of rain in the monsoon but little in other months.","why":"Stepwells collected rain and groundwater so people had water in the dry months. The steps let people walk down as the water level fell."},{"c":"rainwater-harvesting","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Odd one out — which is NOT a way of harvesting rainwater?","o":["Water tanker","Recharge pit","Stepwell","Rooftop tank"],"a":0,"hint":"Three of these catch rain where it falls.","why":"A water tanker just carries water from somewhere else. Rooftop tanks, recharge pits and stepwells all catch and save rain."},{"c":"class-terms-1","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: a large natural or artificial lake or tank used for collecting and storing water is a…","o":["glacier","barrage","reservoir","rapids"],"a":2,"hint":"It is a store of water, like a giant tank.","why":"A reservoir is a large lake or tank, natural or made by people, that collects and stores water for different uses."},{"c":"class-terms-1","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: potable water is water that is…","o":["frozen hard into ice on the mountains","too salty to drink or to cook with","used only for watering plants and fields","safe enough for drinking and preparing food"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of the water you can drink straight from a clean bottle.","why":"Potable water is safe to drink and to cook with. Water can be naturally potable, or made potable by treating it."},{"c":"class-terms-1","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: saline water is water that…","o":["is safe and clean to drink","falls from the clouds as rain","has a lot of salt dissolved in it","comes from melting glaciers"],"a":2,"hint":"Sea water tastes like this.","why":"Saline water contains a lot of dissolved salt, like the water in the seas and oceans."},{"c":"class-terms-1","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: about how much of the water on Earth is saline (salty)?","o":["About 97%","About 3%","About 10%","About 50%"],"a":0,"hint":"Almost all of it!","why":"About 97% of Earth's water is saline; only about 3% is fresh water."},{"c":"class-terms-2","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: a barrage is…","o":["a dam with large gates that controls how much water flows in the river","a stretch of fast water tumbling over rocks","a slow-moving mass of ice high up in the mountains","a lake that stores rainwater for a village"],"a":0,"hint":"It is built across a river by people.","why":"A barrage is a type of dam with large gates. Opening or closing the gates controls how much water flows on."},{"c":"class-terms-2","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: a glacier is…","o":["a dam with large gates","a fast-flowing river","a lake made by people","a slow-moving mass of ice"],"a":3,"hint":"It forms where snow piles up and never melts.","why":"A glacier is a huge mass of ice formed as snow and ice pile up. It moves very slowly."},{"c":"class-terms-2","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: rapids are…","o":["slow rivers of ice high up in the mountains","the places where a river finally meets the sea","fast-flowing water tumbling over a rocky, shallow river bed","deep, still parts of a lake where boats can rest"],"a":2,"hint":"Rafting boats bounce through them!","why":"Rapids are stretches of fast water tumbling over a rocky, shallow river bed."},{"c":"our-responsibility","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: how are WE responsible for our water problems?","o":["Rain falls on the rivers far too often, flooding them","Fish, frogs and turtles make the river water dirty","The Sun dries up the rivers in the long, hot summer months","People dump household and untreated factory waste into the rivers"],"a":3,"hint":"Who throws waste into the Ganga and other rivers?","why":"People dump household and untreated industrial waste into rivers. Cities pour huge amounts of waste water into the Ganga, and pilgrims and tourists add to the pollution too."},{"c":"ocean-and-sea","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: which is TRUE about an ocean and a sea?","o":["Oceans and seas are both completely surrounded by land","An ocean is bigger and deeper; a sea is smaller and partly enclosed by land","A sea is bigger and deeper; an ocean is partly enclosed by land","There are many oceans in the world, but only five seas"],"a":1,"hint":"Which one covers most of the Earth?","why":"An ocean is enormous, deeper and covers most of the Earth. A sea is smaller, less deep and partly enclosed by land."},{"c":"ocean-and-sea","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: the largest ocean is the ___, and the largest sea is the ___.","o":["Pacific Ocean / Dead Sea","Pacific Ocean / Philippine Sea","Indian Ocean / Red Sea","Atlantic Ocean / Black Sea"],"a":1,"hint":"The biggest ocean lies between Asia and the Americas.","why":"The Pacific is the largest ocean. Your class notes give the Philippine Sea as the largest sea."},{"c":"ocean-and-sea","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: how many oceans are there in the world?","o":["5","3","12","7"],"a":0,"hint":"Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Arctic and…","why":"There are only 5 oceans: the Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Arctic and Southern Oceans. There are many seas."},{"c":"glaciers","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: the two most important conditions for glaciers to form are…","o":["Heavy rain and long, hot summers every year","Flat land close to the warm, salty sea","Low temperature all year round and high altitude","Strong winds, sandy soil and very little snow"],"a":2,"hint":"The snow must never melt.","why":"Glaciers form where it stays cold all year, so the snow doesn't melt, and high up in the mountains (high altitude)."},{"c":"fresh-water-scarce","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: why is fresh water scarce on Earth?","o":["Fresh water is found only deep inside the glaciers","Only about 3% of Earth's water is fresh, and pollution spoils some of it","Most fresh water floats away into space every single year","Rain keeps turning the fresh water in rivers into salt water"],"a":1,"hint":"Remember the 97% and the 3%.","why":"About 97% of Earth's water is saline. Only about 3% is fresh water for our homes and industries, and water pollution makes even less of it usable."},{"c":"water-scarcity","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: which is NOT one of the ways water scarcity affects people?","o":["Children sometimes miss school","People walk miles to fetch water","People get free bottled water delivered to their homes","People stand in long queues for their turn"],"a":2,"hint":"Three of these are real hardships. One is the opposite.","why":"Water scarcity makes people walk miles, wait in long queues and carry heavy pots; children sometimes miss school, and the water they fetch may not be potable."},{"c":"water-scarcity","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: drinking contaminated water can cause diseases like…","o":["chickenpox, measles and mumps","asthma, allergies and hay fever","polio, typhoid and jaundice","broken bones and sprains"],"a":2,"hint":"These diseases spread through dirty water.","why":"Contaminated water can cause polio, typhoid and jaundice, as well as stomach trouble, kidney damage and skin diseases."},{"c":"ganga-course","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: how does the Ganga change from Gangotri to Gangasagar?","o":["It starts wide and dirty, and becomes narrow and clean","It starts polluted and dark, and becomes crystal clear","It starts fresh, clear and narrow, and grows wider and polluted","It stays exactly the same from start to finish"],"a":2,"hint":"Think of all the waste poured in along the way.","why":"At Gangotri the Ganga is fresh, cool and crystal clear. As it flows through Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Jharkhand and West Bengal, waste makes it polluted and dark. It is narrow at first and grows wider."},{"c":"ganga-course","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: which state is NOT on the Ganga's path in your notes?","o":["Uttar Pradesh","West Bengal","Bihar","Rajasthan"],"a":3,"hint":"The Ganga flows east towards the Bay of Bengal.","why":"Your notes name Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Jharkhand and West Bengal. Rajasthan is in the dry west."},{"c":"ganga-course","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: why is the water at Gangasagar not potable?","o":["It is polluted, and its fresh water mixes with salty sea water","Too many fish and dolphins live in the water there","The river flows far too fast there to collect any water","It is frozen solid for most of the year, like a glacier"],"a":0,"hint":"Gangasagar is where the river meets the sea.","why":"At Gangasagar the river is polluted by human activities, and its fresh water mixes with the saline water of the sea."},{"c":"river-threat","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: why did the river become a threat to the people, even though it is their lifeline?","o":["Waste, detergents, dead animals and oil were all dumped into it","Its water became much too cold for anyone to use","It turned into a glacier during the cold winter months","It suddenly started to flow uphill, away from the sea"],"a":0,"hint":"Look at what people and industries put into it.","why":"Cattle wallowing, bathing and washing with soap, untreated factory and household waste, dead cattle, pilgrims' offerings and oil from ships all polluted the river."},{"c":"class-perennial-seasonal","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: a seasonal river is one that…","o":["is fed by rain and dries up in summer","always has salty water","is fed by Himalayan snow and flows all year","is dug by people for farming"],"a":0,"hint":"Its water depends on the rainy season.","why":"Seasonal rivers are fed by rainfall, so they dry up completely or partly in summer. Perennial rivers flow all year."},{"c":"class-perennial-seasonal","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: perennial rivers like the Ganga and the Yamuna are fed by…","o":["rain water only, during the monsoon","Himalayan snow and glaciers, as well as rain","salty sea water flowing upstream","underground water from deep wells only"],"a":1,"hint":"Why do they still flow in the hot summer?","why":"Melting Himalayan snow and glaciers keep perennial rivers flowing all year, along with the rain."},{"c":"fresh-water-sources","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: which is NOT a source of fresh water?","o":["Glaciers","Wells","The sea","Springs"],"a":2,"hint":"Which one is salty?","why":"Rivers, lakes, ponds, wells, springs, groundwater, ice and glaciers are sources of fresh water. The sea is saline."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-source","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: the glacier behind Gaumukh is known as the…","o":["Gangotri glacier","Siachen glacier","Zemu glacier","Pindari glacier"],"a":0,"hint":"It shares its name with the place the Ganga starts.","why":"The glacier behind Gaumukh is the Gangotri glacier, where the Ganga begins."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-source","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: in Uttarakhand, the Ganga is called the…","o":["Bhagirathi","Hooghly","Meghna","Luni"],"a":0,"hint":"It meets the Alaknanda at Devprayag.","why":"In Uttarakhand the Ganga is called the Bhagirathi."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-source","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: the Alaknanda and the Bhagirathi meet at…","o":["Devprayag","Prayagraj","Gangasagar","Kanpur"],"a":0,"hint":"Its name ends in 'prayag', but it's in Uttarakhand.","why":"The Alaknanda and the Bhagirathi meet at Devprayag. From there the river is called the Ganga."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-meet","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: the place where the Yamuna and the Ganga meet is called the…","o":["Sangam","Delta","Barrage","Glacier"],"a":0,"hint":"A word that means 'meeting point'.","why":"The Yamuna and the Ganga meet at the Sangam in Prayagraj."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-source","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: at Suryakund in Uttarakhand, the Ganga falls from a height of about…","o":["60 feet","6 feet","600 feet","6,000 feet"],"a":0,"hint":"Taller than a house, but not a mountain.","why":"At Suryakund the Ganga falls from a height of about 60 feet."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-places","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: which barrage is the northernmost point of the Ganga delta?","o":["Farakka Barrage","Luv Kush Barrage","Tehri Dam","Bhakra Dam"],"a":0,"hint":"It's in West Bengal, near where the delta begins.","why":"Farakka Barrage is the northernmost point of the Ganga delta."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-places","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: Luv Kush Barrage is located in…","o":["Uttar Pradesh","Rajasthan","West Bengal","Kerala"],"a":0,"hint":"The state with Kanpur and Prayagraj.","why":"Luv Kush Barrage is in Uttar Pradesh."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-places","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: Gangasagar Island is also known as…","o":["Sagar Island","Majuli","Sunderban","Sangam Island"],"a":0,"hint":"Drop the first word of its name.","why":"Gangasagar Island in West Bengal is also called Sagar Island."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-places","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Why is the water at Gangasagar neither too salty nor too fresh?","o":["Fresh river water mixes with salty sea water there","It rains there every single day","A factory takes the salt out","Glaciers melt right there"],"a":0,"hint":"Think about where the river meets the sea.","why":"At Gangasagar the Ganga breaks into many streams and meets the sea, so fresh river water mixes with saline sea water."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-meet","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: in which country does the Ganga join the Brahmaputra?","o":["Bangladesh","Nepal","Bhutan","Myanmar"],"a":0,"hint":"India's neighbour to the east of West Bengal.","why":"The Ganga flows into Bangladesh and joins the Brahmaputra there."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-meet","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: the Ganga finally falls into the…","o":["Bay of Bengal","Arabian Sea","Red Sea","Dead Sea"],"a":0,"hint":"It's on India's east side.","why":"The Ganga falls into the Bay of Bengal."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-meet","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: which rivers form the Sunderban Delta?","o":["Ganga, Brahmaputra, Hooghly and Meghna","Luni and Chambal","Yamuna and Sahibi","Godavari and Krishna"],"a":0,"hint":"Four rivers that meet near the Bay of Bengal.","why":"The Ganga, Brahmaputra, Hooghly and Meghna together make the Sunderban Delta."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-meet","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class Q&A: name two states the Ganga does NOT flow through.","o":["Madhya Pradesh and Rajasthan","Uttarakhand and Uttar Pradesh","Bihar and West Bengal","Uttar Pradesh and Bihar"],"a":0,"hint":"Two states further west and south, away from its path.","why":"The Ganga does not flow through Madhya Pradesh or Rajasthan."},{"c":"sheet-ganga-places","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class Q&A: Brahmpuri on the Ganga is famous for which water sport?","o":["River rafting","Surfing","Water polo","Swimming races"],"a":0,"hint":"Boats, paddles and rapids!","why":"Brahmpuri is famous for river rafting on the Ganga's rapids."},{"c":"sheet-rivers-people","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: the 'Leather City' of India is…","o":["Kanpur","Jaipur","Kolkata","Patna"],"a":0,"hint":"A big city on the Ganga in Uttar Pradesh.","why":"Kanpur is known as the Leather City of India."},{"c":"sheet-rivers-people","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: the major occupation of people living along the river is…","o":["Fishing","Mining","Pottery","Camel riding"],"a":0,"hint":"What can you catch in a river?","why":"Fishing is the major occupation of communities living along the river."},{"c":"sheet-rivers-people","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: which of these POLLUTES river water?","o":["Cattle wallowing in it","Rafting on it","Bird-watching on its banks","Planting trees along it"],"a":0,"hint":"Which one puts dirt and waste into the water?","why":"Cattle wallowing, bathing, washing clothes, dumping waste and untreated factory waste all pollute rivers."},{"c":"sheet-rivers-people","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: sluice gates are used to control the amount of water in a…","o":["dam","well","glacier","ocean"],"a":0,"hint":"Something built across a river to hold water back.","why":"Sluice gates open and close to control the water in a dam."},{"c":"sheet-water-facts","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: which city in India gets very heavy rainfall every year?","o":["Cherrapunji","Jaisalmer","Delhi","Jaipur"],"a":0,"hint":"It's in the north-east, not the desert.","why":"Cherrapunji receives very heavy rainfall every year."},{"c":"sheet-water-facts","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class Q&A: Cherrapunji is in which state?","o":["Meghalaya","Rajasthan","Kerala","Punjab"],"a":0,"hint":"A north-eastern state whose name means 'abode of clouds'.","why":"Cherrapunji is in Meghalaya."},{"c":"sheet-water-facts","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class Q&A: about how much salt does one litre of ocean water contain?","o":["About 30 g","About 3 kg","No salt at all","About 300 g"],"a":0,"hint":"Roughly two tablespoons.","why":"Ocean water contains about 30 g of salt in every litre."},{"c":"sheet-water-facts","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class Q&A: in the chapter, Afiya lives in ___, and her name means ___.","o":["Africa — health","India — water","Africa — river","Japan — health"],"a":0,"hint":"Her name is about staying well.","why":"Afiya lives in Africa, and her name means 'health'."},{"c":"sheet-water-facts","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: most rivers in northern India get their water from…","o":["Glaciers","The sea","Wells","Factories"],"a":0,"hint":"Think of the snowy Himalayas.","why":"Most rivers of northern India are fed by Himalayan glaciers."},{"c":"sheet-water-facts","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"True or false: 'A river is the part of an ocean enclosed by land.'","o":["False — that describes a sea","True","False — that describes a glacier","True — but only in summer"],"a":0,"hint":"Which water body is a smaller part of an ocean?","why":"False. A sea is a part of the ocean partly enclosed by land; a river flows over land."},{"c":"sheet-perennial","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: which is a SEASONAL river?","o":["Sahibi","Ganga","Brahmaputra","Indus"],"a":0,"hint":"It's the one that flows through Delhi as a drain.","why":"The Sahibi is a seasonal, rain-fed river. The Ganga, Brahmaputra and Indus are perennial."},{"c":"sheet-perennial","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: which three are perennial rivers?","o":["Ganga, Brahmaputra and Indus","Luni, Sahibi and Ganga","Luni, Godavari and Sahibi","Sahibi, Luni and Indus"],"a":0,"hint":"All three start in the Himalayas.","why":"The Ganga, Brahmaputra and Indus are perennial: they flow all year."},{"c":"sheet-najafgarh","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: the Najafgarh drain in Delhi is really a river called the…","o":["Sahibi (Sabi)","Yamuna","Luni","Chambal"],"a":0,"hint":"It starts in the Aravali hills near Jaipur.","why":"The Najafgarh drain is the Sahibi (or Sabi) river, the only rain-fed river in Delhi."},{"c":"sheet-najafgarh","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Why is the Najafgarh drain Delhi's most polluted water body?","o":["Untreated sewage flows straight into it","It carries glacier ice","It is too salty","Camels bathe in it"],"a":0,"hint":"Think about the crowded areas it passes through.","why":"Untreated sewage from the thickly populated areas it passes through flows straight into it."},{"c":"sheet-najafgarh","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class notes: the Najafgarh drain (Sahibi) later joins which river?","o":["Yamuna","Ganga","Luni","Chambal"],"a":0,"hint":"The river that flows through Delhi.","why":"The Sahibi (Najafgarh drain) joins the Yamuna."},{"c":"sheet-oceans","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Practice sheet: which list names the five oceans?","o":["Arctic, Atlantic, Pacific, Indian, Southern","Arctic, Atlantic, Pacific, Indian, Red","Pacific, Indian, Atlantic, Bengal, Southern","Arctic, Atlantic, Dead, Indian, Pacific"],"a":0,"hint":"Red, Dead and Bengal are seas or a bay, not oceans.","why":"The five oceans are the Arctic, Atlantic, Pacific, Indian and Southern Oceans."}],"r-tense":[{"c":"past-regular","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Yesterday, the dolphin ___ out of the water again and again.","o":["jumps","jumping","jumpt","jumped"],"a":3,"hint":"‘Yesterday’ tells you it already happened. How do most verbs show the past?","why":"Most verbs make their past form by adding -ed: jump → jumped. It may sound like ‘jumpt’, but we always write -ed. Dolphins love to leap!"},{"c":"past-regular","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"One past form is spelt wrong. Which sentence is it in?","o":["The crab stoped under a stone.","The frog hopped onto a rock.","The otter carried a shell.","The seals played in the waves."],"a":0,"hint":"Check each -ed word. Short words like ‘hop’ double their last letter before -ed: hop → hopped.","why":"Stop → stopped. Short words ending in one vowel + one consonant double the last letter, like hop → hopped. Carried (carry) and played (play) are spelt right."},{"c":"past-regular","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Last winter, the penguin dad ___ the egg on his feet for weeks.","o":["carryed","carried","carrid","carries"],"a":1,"hint":"‘Last winter’ means the past. When a verb ends in a consonant + y, what happens to the y?","why":"Carry → carried: when a word ends in a consonant + y, change the y to i and add -ed. Emperor penguin dads really do keep the egg warm on their feet all winter!"},{"c":"past-irregular","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Last week, a whale shark ___ right under our boat!","o":["swimmed","swum","swam","swims"],"a":2,"hint":"‘Last week’ means the past. Think of ‘sing → sang’. ‘Swim’ changes in the same way.","why":"Swim → swam. ‘Swimmed’ is a common mistake, and ‘swum’ needs a helper (has swum). Whale sharks are the biggest fish, yet they eat mostly tiny plankton!"},{"c":"past-irregular","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The octopus ___ inside a shell when the shark came near.","o":["hided","hides","hidden","hid"],"a":3,"hint":"‘Came’ shows this happened in the past. Think of ‘bite → bit’. ‘Hide’ changes the same way.","why":"Hide → hid. Irregular verbs don’t take -ed, so ‘hided’ is wrong, and ‘hidden’ needs a helper (has hidden). Clever octopuses squeeze into shells to stay safe."},{"c":"past-irregular","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"After the aquarium trip, {R} says: “I ___ a sawfish with a long saw!”","o":["seed","seen","saw","sees"],"a":2,"hint":"The trip is over, so we need the past form of ‘see’. It is irregular and changes its vowels.","why":"See → saw: “I saw a sawfish!” ‘Seen’ needs a helper (I have seen), and a ‘seed’ grows into a plant. Fun fact: a sawfish’s saw is called a rostrum."},{"c":"past-irregular","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"All of these happened in the past. Which sentence has a WRONG past form?","o":["The shark eated a squid.","The seal caught a fish.","The crab ran sideways.","The gull flew over the waves."],"a":0,"hint":"One irregular verb was given -ed by mistake. Find the base form of each verb first.","why":"Eat → ate, so it’s ‘The shark ate a squid.’ Caught (catch), ran (run) and flew (fly) are all correct irregular past forms."},{"c":"past-irregular","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"‘Grow’ becomes ‘grew’. So, yesterday the whale ___ a spout of water from its blowhole.","o":["blowed","blown","blew","blows"],"a":2,"hint":"‘Grow’ and ‘blow’ rhyme. Make the same change to ‘blow’ that ‘grow’ made.","why":"Grow → grew, know → knew, blow → blew: the ‘ow’ changes to ‘ew’. ‘Blown’ needs a helper (has blown). Whales breathe out through a blowhole on top of the head!"},{"c":"regular-or-irregular","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out! Three of these verbs make their past with -ed. Which one doesn’t?","o":["swim","jump","splash","float"],"a":0,"hint":"Try each one in ‘Yesterday I ___.’ Which one sounds wrong with -ed?","why":"Swim is irregular: swim → swam. The others just add -ed: jumped, splashed, floated. Irregular verbs must be learnt by heart, like a secret code!"},{"c":"regular-or-irregular","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Three of these past forms are irregular. Which one is REGULAR?","o":["crawled","caught","bit","flew"],"a":0,"hint":"Find the base form of each: caught → catch… Which past form is just its base form + -ed?","why":"Crawl + -ed = crawled, so it is regular. Caught (catch), bit (bite) and flew (fly) change their spelling, so they are irregular. Baby turtles crawl to the sea!"},{"c":"regular-or-irregular","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Last week, the shark ___ a tooth, and a new one grew in its place. Pick the word AND the reason.","o":["losed — ‘lose’ is a regular verb","lost — ‘lose’ is an irregular verb","lost — ‘lose’ is a regular verb","losed — ‘lose’ is an irregular verb"],"a":1,"hint":"Think: ‘I ___ my pencil yesterday.’ Which past form do people really say? Does it end in -ed?","why":"Lose → lost, with no -ed, so ‘lose’ is irregular. Sharks lose teeth all the time, and new ones move forward to take their place. Some sharks go through thousands!"},{"c":"simple-present","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A hammerhead shark often ___ stingrays.","o":["eat","eating","eates","eats"],"a":3,"hint":"‘Often’ tells us it’s a habit. One shark = he, she or it. What does the verb need?","why":"For habits, use the simple present. With he, she or it (one shark), add -s: eats. ‘Eates’ is wrong: ‘eat’ ends in t, so just add -s. Wash and go take -es: washes, goes."},{"c":"simple-present","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A sea otter ___ its thick fur many times a day.","o":["washs","washes","wash","washing"],"a":1,"hint":"One otter = it. Say each choice aloud. Which ending can your mouth actually say?","why":"Verbs ending in -sh, -ch, -s, -x or -o add -es: wash → washes. Sea otters clean their fur for hours, because clean fur keeps them warm in cold water."},{"c":"simple-present","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Oops! Which animal fact has a verb mistake?","o":["A crab has ten legs.","Dolphins breathe air.","An octopus has three hearts.","A seahorse swim slowly."],"a":3,"hint":"Who does the action in each sentence: one animal or many? Does each verb match?","why":"One seahorse = it, so the verb needs -s: A seahorse swims slowly. ‘Dolphins breathe’ is right, because there are many dolphins. Seahorses are some of the slowest fish!"},{"c":"simple-present","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{R} tells his friend: “Don’t worry! A whale shark ___ people. It eats tiny plankton.”","o":["do not eat","does not eats","does not eat","not eats"],"a":2,"hint":"One whale shark = it. In a ‘not’ sentence, the -s jumps onto the helper word.","why":"With he, she or it, use ‘does not’ + the base verb: does not eat. ‘Does not eats’ adds the -s twice! Whale sharks are huge but harmless to people."},{"c":"present-continuous","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Look! The dolphins ___ next to our boat.","o":["is swimming","swims","are swimming","swam"],"a":2,"hint":"‘Look!’ means it is happening right now. How many dolphins are there?","why":"Right now = present continuous: am/is/are + -ing. Many dolphins = they, so ‘are swimming’. For one dolphin we say ‘The dolphin is swimming’."},{"c":"present-continuous","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which -ing word is spelt wrong?","o":["The seal is swimming.","The penguin is diving.","The crab is running.","The octopus is hideing."],"a":3,"hint":"Some verbs double a letter before -ing; others drop a silent ‘e’. Check each one.","why":"‘Hide’ ends in a silent e, so drop the e: hiding. ‘Dive’ → diving works the same way. Swim → swimming and run → running double their last letter."},{"c":"present-continuous","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"I ___ drawing a sawfish right now.","o":["is","am","are","was"],"a":1,"hint":"The doer is ‘I’. Which helper always goes with ‘I’ in the present?","why":"With ‘I’ we use ‘am’: I am drawing. ‘Is’ goes with he, she or it, and ‘are’ with you, we or they. ‘Was’ is for the past, but ‘right now’ means now!"},{"c":"present-continuous","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Shh! The sea lion pup ___ on the rock. Pick the verb AND the reason.","o":["sleeps — it is happening now","is sleeping — it is happening now","is sleeping — it happens every day","slept — it happened yesterday"],"a":1,"hint":"Why would someone say ‘Shh!’? Think about WHEN the pup is sleeping.","why":"‘Shh!’ means the pup is asleep at this very moment, so we use the present continuous: is sleeping. ‘Sleeps’ is for habits, like ‘It sleeps every afternoon.’"},{"c":"simple-past","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The baby turtle did not ___ back to its nest. It crawled to the sea!","o":["went","goes","go","going"],"a":2,"hint":"‘Did’ already shows the past. Does the next verb need to show it again?","why":"‘Did’ already carries the past, so the verb stays in its base form: did not go. ‘Did not went’ shows the past twice! Baby turtles race to the sea once they leave the nest."},{"c":"simple-past","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which question is WRONG?","o":["Did the crab ran away?","Did the shark find food?","Did you see the whale?","Did they catch a fish?"],"a":0,"hint":"Each question starts with ‘Did’. What form should the next verb be in?","why":"After ‘Did’, use the base form: Did the crab run away? ‘Ran’ is already past, and ‘did’ has done that job. Find, see and catch are base forms, so the others are right."},{"c":"simple-past","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Yesterday, {R} ___ his shark book to school and showed it to his class.","o":["bringed","brought","brang","brings"],"a":1,"hint":"‘Yesterday’ = past. ‘Bring’ is irregular. Think of ‘buy → bought’ and ‘think → thought’.","why":"Bring → brought. ‘Bringed’ isn’t a word, and ‘brang’ copies sing → sang, which doesn’t work here. Buy → bought and think → thought end the same way."},{"c":"simple-past","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Millions of years ago, a giant shark called megalodon ___ in the oceans. Pick the verb AND the reason.","o":["lives — megalodon is still alive","lived — ‘ago’ means right now","lived — ‘ago’ tells us it is past","is living — it is happening now"],"a":2,"hint":"Megalodon is extinct: there are none today. Which word in the sentence tells you WHEN?","why":"‘Ago’ points to the past, so: megalodon lived. It was one of the biggest sharks ever, but it died out millions of years ago. Today we mostly find its giant fossil teeth."},{"c":"past-continuous","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"At 5 o’clock yesterday, the seals ___ on the beach.","o":["was resting","are resting","rest","were resting"],"a":3,"hint":"‘At 5 o’clock yesterday’ = the action was going on at that past time. One seal or many?","why":"An action going on at a past time = past continuous: was/were + -ing. Many seals = they, so ‘were resting’. One seal would be ‘was resting’."},{"c":"past-continuous","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{R} ___ a shark video when the doorbell rang.","o":["were watching","is watching","was watching","watches"],"a":2,"hint":"The doorbell rang in the middle of something. Which choice shows an action already going on in the past?","why":"A longer past action cut short by a quick one takes the past continuous: {R} was watching… when the doorbell rang. ‘Were’ is for you, we and they."},{"c":"past-continuous","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Spot the sentence with the wrong helper: was or were?","o":["The crabs were digging in the sand.","I was reading about sawfish.","We were watching the turtles.","The whale were singing a song."],"a":3,"hint":"Check who does the action in each sentence. Does the helper match: one or many?","why":"One whale = it, so it takes ‘was’: The whale was singing. ‘Were’ goes with many (the crabs) and with we, you and they. Humpback whales really do sing!"},{"c":"past-continuous","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"The diver ___ photos of the coral when a turtle swam past her. Pick the verb AND the reason.","o":["was taking — her action was already going on","took — her action was already going on","was taking — it is happening right now","takes — it happens every day"],"a":0,"hint":"Which action started first and was still going on? Which one happened suddenly, in a moment?","why":"Taking photos was already going on when the turtle swam past (a quick action). The longer action takes the past continuous: was taking. ‘Took’ doesn’t show it was going on."},{"c":"which-tense","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"“The sawfish is digging in the mud for food.” Which tense is this?","o":["Simple present","Simple past","Past continuous","Present continuous"],"a":3,"hint":"Spot the helper word before the -ing verb. Does it point to now or to the past?","why":"‘Is’ + ‘digging’ = is + -ing, the present continuous: it’s happening now. Sawfish use their saw to stir up the mud and find hidden fish."},{"c":"which-tense","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"“Sea turtles laid their eggs on this beach last night.” Which tense is this?","o":["Simple past","Simple present","Present continuous","Past continuous"],"a":0,"hint":"Find the verb and the time words. Is there a helper like ‘is’ or ‘was’ with an -ing verb?","why":"‘Laid’ is the past form of ‘lay’, and ‘last night’ is over, so it’s the simple past. No helper and no -ing needed!"},{"c":"which-tense","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"“Clownfish live in sea anemones.” Which tense is this?","o":["Present continuous","Simple present","Simple past","Past continuous"],"a":1,"hint":"Is this happening only right now, or is it always true?","why":"‘Live’ with no helper is the simple present. We use it for facts that are always true: clownfish make their homes in sea anemones."},{"c":"which-tense","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"“The penguins were sliding on the ice.” Which tense is this? Pick the tense AND the clue.","o":["Present continuous — clue: the -ing","Past continuous — clue: ‘were’ + -ing","Simple past — clue: ‘were’ is past","Past continuous — clue: ‘the penguins’"],"a":1,"hint":"Look at the helper AND the verb together. Which tense needs both of them?","why":"‘Were’ (a past helper) + ‘sliding’ (-ing) = past continuous: it was going on in the past. The present continuous would be ‘are sliding’. Penguins love belly-sliding!"},{"c":"tense-clues","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The crab hid under a rock ___.","o":["yesterday","now","tomorrow","at the moment"],"a":0,"hint":"‘Hid’ is a past form. Which time word fits something that is already over?","why":"‘Hid’ is past, so it needs a past time word: yesterday. ‘Now’ and ‘at the moment’ go with the present continuous, and ‘tomorrow’ is for the future."},{"c":"tense-clues","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Every morning, the sea lions ___ fish from the zookeeper. Pick the verb AND the reason.","o":["are getting — it happens every morning","get — it is happening right now","get — it happens every morning","got — it happened only yesterday"],"a":2,"hint":"‘Every morning’ tells you how OFTEN it happens. Is it a habit, or is it happening just now?","why":"Habits and routines use the simple present: Every morning, the sea lions get fish. The present continuous (are getting) is for things happening right now."},{"c":"tense-clues","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Usually the octopus ___ in its den, but right now it ___ across the sand.","o":["is hiding / crawls","hides / is crawling","hide / is crawling","hides / crawled"],"a":1,"hint":"Two clues: ‘usually’ (a habit) and ‘right now’ (this moment). Match each blank to its clue.","why":"‘Usually’ = habit → simple present: hides. ‘Right now’ = this moment → present continuous: is crawling. One sentence, two tenses! Octopuses really can crawl on their arms."},{"c":"tense-clues","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{R} writes in his diary: “Last Sunday, we ___ to the beach and ___ ten crabs!”","o":["went / found","go / find","went / find","goes / found"],"a":0,"hint":"‘Last Sunday’ is over. Do BOTH blanks need to show the past?","why":"‘Last Sunday’ is finished time, so both verbs are in the simple past: we went (go) and found (find) ten crabs. ‘Find’ would jump back to the present."},{"c":"simple-present","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (simple present): My grandmother ___ (take) care of her health.","o":["taked","taking","takes","take"],"a":2,"hint":"My grandmother = she. What do we add to the verb after he, she or it?","why":"One person (she) in the simple present: add -s. My grandmother takes care of her health."},{"c":"s-es-ies","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Tenses handout: The baby ___ (cry) when it is hungry.","o":["crys","cryes","cry","cries"],"a":3,"hint":"‘cry’ ends in a consonant + y. What happens to the y?","why":"Consonant + y: change y to i and add -es. cry → cries."},{"c":"s-es-ies","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: Kabir ___ (go) for yoga every day.","o":["going","gos","goes","go"],"a":2,"hint":"Verbs ending in -o take -es after he or she.","why":"go → goes, just like do → does. ‘Every day’ tells us it is the simple present."},{"c":"s-es-ies","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: She ___ (carry) her books to school in a huge bag.","o":["carry","carrys","carryes","carries"],"a":3,"hint":"Look at the letter just before the y.","why":"r is a consonant, so the y changes to i and we add -es: carries."},{"c":"s-es-ies","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: Our parents ___ (love) us dearly.","o":["love","loving","lovies","loves"],"a":0,"hint":"Is ‘our parents’ one person or more than one?","why":"Parents = they. With we, they or a plural noun we do NOT add s: Our parents love us dearly."},{"c":"s-es-ies","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: He ___ (wash) his clothes on his own.","o":["washies","washs","washes","wash"],"a":2,"hint":"Verbs ending in -sh, -ch, -ss, -x or -o take -es.","why":"wash ends in -sh, so it takes -es: He washes his clothes."},{"c":"s-es-ies-rule","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class notes: when do we NOT add s, es or ies to the verb?","o":["Never: every present verb needs an s","When the subject is I, you, we, they or a plural noun","When the subject is he, she or it","When the subject is one boy or one bird"],"a":1,"hint":"‘I play tennis.’ ‘We eat vegetarian food.’ Do these verbs have an s?","why":"We add s/es/ies only after he, she, it or one person or thing. After I, you, we, they or a plural noun the verb stays plain: I play, they like."},{"c":"present-continuous","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (present continuous): John ___ (drive) the car very fast.","o":["was driving","is driveing","are driving","is driving"],"a":3,"hint":"Present continuous = am / is / are + -ing. Which helper goes with John?","why":"John = he → is. Drive ends in a silent e, so drop the e before -ing: is driving."},{"c":"present-continuous","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (present continuous): I ___ (solve) a puzzle.","o":["am solving","are solving","am solveing","is solving"],"a":0,"hint":"Which helper always goes with I?","why":"I → am. Drop the e of solve before adding -ing: I am solving a puzzle."},{"c":"present-continuous","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (present continuous): Somebody ___ (knock) at the door.","o":["is knocking","knocking","is knoking","are knocking"],"a":0,"hint":"‘Somebody’ is one person. And don't forget the helper!","why":"Somebody = one person → is. Without the helper, ‘Somebody knocking’ is not a full sentence."},{"c":"past-irregular","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (simple past): Mary ___ (speak) French very fluently.","o":["speaked","spoke","spoken","speaks"],"a":1,"hint":"‘speak’ is irregular: it doesn't take -ed.","why":"speak → spoke → spoken. The simple past is spoke; spoken needs has or have."},{"c":"past-irregular","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (simple past): I ___ (hurt) myself while playing in the park.","o":["hurt","hurting","hurts","hurted"],"a":0,"hint":"Some verbs look the same in the past: cut → cut, hit → hit…","why":"hurt → hurt → hurt. There is no word ‘hurted’."},{"c":"past-irregular","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (simple past): She ___ (begin) to fall down the steep slope.","o":["began","begun","begins","beginned"],"a":0,"hint":"begin – ___ – begun. Which form goes in the middle?","why":"begin → began → begun. The simple past is began; begun needs has or have."},{"c":"past-irregular","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (simple past): Rama ___ (pay) the bills of her phone yesterday.","o":["paid","payed","pays","is paying"],"a":0,"hint":"‘pay’ is irregular, like say → said.","why":"pay → paid (not payed), just like say → said. ‘Yesterday’ tells us it is the past."},{"c":"tense-clues","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet (simple past): I was cooking food in the kitchen when the doorbell ___ (ring).","o":["rang","rings","ringed","rung"],"a":0,"hint":"The cooking was going on; the doorbell interrupted it at one moment in the past.","why":"ring → rang → rung. A short action that interrupts a longer one takes the simple past: when the doorbell rang."},{"c":"past-regular","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Tenses handout (simple past): Meera ___ (travel) with her family last month.","o":["travelled","travelling","travels","travel"],"a":0,"hint":"‘Last month’ tells you the tense.","why":"‘Last month’ is the past, so travel takes -ed. In Indian and British spelling the l doubles: travelled."},{"c":"past-regular","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Tenses handout: how do we make the simple past of ‘try’ and ‘cry’?","o":["Keep the y and add -ed: tryed, cryed","Change y to i and add -ed: tried, cried","Keep the y and add -d: tryd, cryd","Change y to i and add -es: tries, cries"],"a":1,"hint":"It's the same y-change as in ‘cries’, but with -ed.","why":"Consonant + y: change y to i, then add -ed. try → tried, cry → cried. (tries and cries are present forms.)"},{"c":"past-continuous","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (past continuous): Mona ___ (write) a letter to her father.","o":["were writing","was writing","was writeing","is writing"],"a":1,"hint":"Past continuous = was / were + -ing. Mona is one person.","why":"One person → was. Drop the e of write before -ing: Mona was writing a letter."},{"c":"past-continuous","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (past continuous): The girls ___ (hurry) back home after school.","o":["were hurrying","were hurring","are hurrying","was hurrying"],"a":0,"hint":"More than one girl… and keep the y before -ing!","why":"Girls = they → were. Keep the y and add -ing: were hurrying."},{"c":"past-continuous","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet (past continuous): My father ___ (read) a novel while I ___ (watch) TV.","o":["is reading / am watching","were reading / were watching","was reading / was watching","was reading / were watching"],"a":2,"hint":"Check each helper: my father → ?   I → ?","why":"My father is one person → was. I → was. Both actions were going on at the same time: was reading, was watching."},{"c":"was-or-were","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Tenses handout: in the past continuous, which helper goes with you, we and they?","o":["is","were","was","am"],"a":1,"hint":"Try it out: ‘They ___ playing.’","why":"Use was with I, he, she, it and one person. Use were with you, we, they and more than one."},{"c":"verb-three-forms","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (past form of teach): Who ___ you English?","o":["taughted","taught","teach","teached"],"a":1,"hint":"‘teach’ is irregular, like catch → caught.","why":"teach → taught → taught. There is no word ‘teached’."},{"c":"verb-three-forms","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (past form of sing): She ___ a lovely song.","o":["sanged","sang","singed","sung"],"a":1,"hint":"sing – ___ – sung","why":"sing → sang → sung. The simple past is sang; sung needs has or have (She has sung)."},{"c":"past-participle","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (past participle): I have ___ that movie before.","o":["see","seen","saw","seed"],"a":1,"hint":"After have or has we use the third form.","why":"see → saw → seen. After ‘have’, use the past participle: I have seen that movie."},{"c":"past-participle","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (past participle): Rahul has ___ his ankle.","o":["breaks","broked","broke","broken"],"a":3,"hint":"break – broke – ___","why":"break → broke → broken. After ‘has’, use broken."},{"c":"base-form","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet (base form of build): The masons ___ the house.","o":["build","builded","building","builds"],"a":0,"hint":"The base form is the verb just as it is in the dictionary.","why":"The base form is build, and ‘masons’ is plural, so there is no s. (Its past form is built, never ‘builded’.)"},{"c":"verb-three-forms","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Verbs handout: which set of three forms is correct?","o":["choose – chose – chosen","choose – choosed – choosen","choose – chose – chose","choose – chosen – chose"],"a":0,"hint":"The order is base – past – past participle (with has/have).","why":"choose → chose → chosen: ‘Yesterday I chose…’, ‘I have chosen…’."},{"c":"verb-three-forms","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Verbs handout: wear – wore – ___","o":["wore","weared","worn","woren"],"a":2,"hint":"Think of tear – tore – torn.","why":"wear → wore → worn, just like tear → tore → torn. ‘I have worn this shirt.’"},{"c":"regular-or-irregular","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Verbs handout: which verb has the SAME form in all three columns, like cut – cut – cut?","o":["hit","fight","hold","bite"],"a":0,"hint":"Say each one: today I ___, yesterday I ___, I have ___.","why":"hit → hit → hit. The others change: hold → held, bite → bit → bitten, fight → fought."},{"c":"tense-clues","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: Who ___ the match yesterday?","o":["win","wins","winned","won"],"a":3,"hint":"Look for the time word in the sentence.","why":"‘Yesterday’ means the past. win → won → won."},{"c":"tense-clues","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Tenses handout: which time clue tells you to use the SIMPLE PRESENT?","o":["yesterday","last night","every day","now"],"a":2,"hint":"Habits and routines…","why":"‘Every day’ shows a habit → simple present. ‘Now’ → present continuous. ‘Yesterday’ and ‘last night’ → past."}],"z-arith":[{"c":"expression-value","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Three of these expressions have the same value. Which one is the odd one out?","o":["12 + 6","3 × 6","36 ÷ 2","25 − 8"],"a":3,"hint":"Find the value of each expression — the number it works out to.","why":"12 + 6, 3 × 6 and 36 ÷ 2 all have the value 18, but 25 − 8 = 17. Different expressions can share one value: 10 + 2, 15 − 3 and 3 × 4 are all 12!"},{"c":"expression-value","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Fill the blank so both sides have the same value: 17 + 5 = ___ + 8","o":["22","14","30","9"],"a":1,"hint":"Work out the left side first. What do you add to 8 to reach that same number?","why":"17 + 5 = 22, so the right side must be 22 too: 14 + 8 = 22. The ‘=’ sign means both sides have the SAME value — it doesn’t mean ‘write the answer next’."},{"c":"expression-value","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"Arrange in increasing order of value: P = 120 ÷ 4, Q = 46 − 17, R = 4 × 8, S = 19 + 12","o":["P, Q, R, S","Q, P, S, R","Q, P, R, S","R, S, P, Q"],"a":1,"hint":"Find the value of each expression first, then line them up from smallest to biggest.","why":"Q = 29, P = 30, S = 31, R = 32. Smallest to biggest: Q, P, S, R. Comparing expressions always means comparing their values."},{"c":"compare-by-reasoning","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Without adding, compare 2047 + 318 and 2045 + 321. Pick the answer with the right reason.","o":["Second — it gains 3 but loses only 2","First — 2047 is more than 2045","Equal — the small changes cancel out","Equal — both are roughly 2360"],"a":0,"hint":"2047 drops to 2045 — by how much? And 318 grows to 321 — by how much?","why":"From the first sum to the second, one number drops by 2 but the other grows by 3. Net change: 1 more! So 2047 + 318 < 2045 + 321 (2365 vs 2366)."},{"c":"compare-by-reasoning","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Raja had 254 marbles and lost 38. Joy had 253 marbles and lost 37. Who has more marbles left?","o":["Raja — he started with more","Joy — he lost fewer marbles","Can’t tell without subtracting","Both have the same number"],"a":3,"hint":"Raja started with 1 more marble. Did he also lose more? By how many?","why":"Raja started with 1 extra marble but also lost 1 extra, so they end up level: 254 − 38 = 253 − 37 = 216. No long subtraction needed!"},{"c":"compare-by-reasoning","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Both start from 615. Which leaves MORE: 615 − 87 or 615 − 78?","o":["615 − 87 — 87 is a bigger number","Same — they use the same digits","Can’t tell until you subtract","615 − 78 — less is taken away"],"a":3,"hint":"Same starting amount. Does taking away more leave you with more, or with less?","why":"Both start at 615. Taking away 78 removes less than taking away 87, so 615 − 78 leaves more (537 vs 528). Swapped digits don’t make two numbers equal!"},{"c":"why-brackets","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Tara has 12 stickers and gets 3 packs of 6 more. She writes 12 + 3 × 6 and gets 90. What went wrong?","o":["She did 12 + 3 before 3 × 6","Nothing — 90 is correct","She should write 12 × 3 + 6","She should subtract, not add"],"a":0,"hint":"How many stickers are in 3 packs of 6? Now add that to 12.","why":"3 × 6 is one term: 3 packs of 6 = 18 stickers, so 12 + 3 × 6 = 12 + 18 = 30. Doing 12 + 3 first would mean 15 packs of 6 — she never had those!"},{"c":"why-brackets","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"You buy a pen for ₹18 and a notebook for ₹45, and pay with a ₹100 note. Which expression gives your change, and why?","o":["100 − 18 + 45 — take away 18, then add 45","100 − (18 + 45) — take away the whole bill","18 + 45 − 100 — the bill minus the note","100 − 45 + 18 — notebook first, then the pen"],"a":1,"hint":"First find the whole bill. The change is 100 minus ALL of it.","why":"The bill is 18 + 45 = 63, so the change is 100 − (18 + 45) = ₹37. Without brackets, 100 − 18 + 45 = ₹127 — more than you paid! Brackets show what to take away."},{"c":"why-brackets","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"When you evaluate an expression that has brackets, what do you do first?","o":["Always work left to right","Do every addition first","Find the value inside the brackets","Ignore the brackets"],"a":2,"hint":"Brackets wrap up one part of an expression to treat it as a single package.","why":"Brackets come first. In 30 + (5 × 4) you work out 5 × 4 = 20, then 30 + 20 = 50 — just like Mallesh counting Arun’s 5 bags of 4 marbles."},{"c":"identify-terms","t":"Two-tier","p":50,"q":"What are the terms of 47 − 19?","o":["47 and 19 — terms are always positive","−47 and 19 — the sign goes on the first","47 and −19 — the minus stays with 19","Only 28 — the value is the term"],"a":2,"hint":"Subtracting is the same as adding the inverse. Rewrite it as 47 + (…).","why":"Subtracting 19 is the same as adding its inverse, −19. So 47 − 19 = 47 + (−19), and its terms are 47 and −19. A term keeps the sign in front of it!"},{"c":"identify-terms","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"How many terms does 8 × 3 + 15 − 6 × 2 have?","o":["3","2","4","5"],"a":0,"hint":"Change the − into ‘+ −’, then count the parts separated by + signs.","why":"8 × 3 + 15 + (−6 × 2) has 3 terms: 8 × 3, 15 and −6 × 2. A product like 8 × 3 has no + inside it, so it stays ONE term."},{"c":"identify-terms","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Kabir says the terms of 50 − 7 × 3 + 9 are 50, 7, 3 and 9. What did he get wrong?","o":["Nothing — every number is a term","7 × 3 is one term, and it is −7 × 3","He forgot the term −50","The 9 should be −9"],"a":1,"hint":"Terms are separated only by + signs. Is there a + between the 7 and the 3?","why":"As a sum: 50 + (−7 × 3) + 9. So the terms are 50, −7 × 3 and 9. Numbers joined by × stick together as one term, and the minus belongs to it."},{"c":"evaluate-by-terms","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What is the value of 45 − 3 × 7 + 10?","o":["34","304","14","56"],"a":0,"hint":"The terms are 45, −3 × 7 and 10. Work out each term, then add them.","why":"Terms: 45, −21 and 10, which add to 34. Going left to right (45 − 3 = 42, then × 7…) gives 304 — but 3 × 7 is one term and must be found first."},{"c":"evaluate-by-terms","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Find the value of 50 − 6 × 4 + 12 ÷ 2.","o":["32","94","20","2"],"a":0,"hint":"Three terms: 50, −6 × 4 and 12 ÷ 2. Evaluate each one before adding.","why":"50 + (−24) + 6 = 32. Each × or ÷ part is a single term. Going left to right gives 94, and 20 comes from wrongly making the last term −6."},{"c":"evaluate-by-terms","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Sana gets 324 for 5 × 4 − 2 × 3 × 6. What should the value be?","o":["324 — Sana is right","16 — values can’t be negative","56","−16"],"a":3,"hint":"Split it into terms first: 5 × 4 and −2 × 3 × 6. Work out each term, then add them.","why":"Terms: 5 × 4 = 20 and −2 × 3 × 6 = −36, and 20 + (−36) = −16. Sana went left to right (20 − 2 = 18, then × 3 × 6). Negative values are perfectly fine!"},{"c":"swap-and-group","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"“Swapping the terms does not change the sum.” What is this property called?","o":["Associative property of addition","Distributive property","Inverse property","Commutative property of addition"],"a":3,"hint":"‘Commute’ means to travel back and forth — like two terms trading places.","why":"Swapping → commutative: 6 + (−4) = (−4) + 6. Grouping differently → associative. Both work for negative terms too!"},{"c":"swap-and-group","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Arjun is adding a list of 6 numbers. He gets 5,280, then sees he skipped the 3rd one, 745. What should he do?","o":["Just add 745 to 5,280","Start the whole sum again","Subtract 745 from 5,280","Add 745 three times — it was 3rd"],"a":0,"hint":"Does the order in which you add numbers change the total?","why":"Terms can be added in any order and grouped any way, so 745 can join at the end: 5,280 + 745 = 6,025. No need to start over!"},{"c":"swap-and-group","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"(−9) + 14 + (−6): Ravi adds −9 and −6 first; Meera goes left to right. What do they get?","o":["Different answers — order matters","Both get 11","Both get −1","Both get −29"],"a":2,"hint":"Try both. Ravi: (−9) + (−6) = ?, then add 14. Meera: (−9) + 14 = ?, then add −6.","why":"Meera: −9 + 14 = 5, then 5 + (−6) = −1. Ravi: −9 + (−6) = −15, then −15 + 14 = −1. Terms can be added in ANY order — negative ones too!"},{"c":"swap-and-group","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Which expression has the same value as 64 − 29? Pick the one with the right reason.","o":["29 − 64 — swapping never changes it","−64 + 29 — flip both signs","29 + 64 — just drop the minus","−29 + 64 — same terms, swapped"],"a":3,"hint":"Write 64 − 29 as a sum of terms first: 64 + (…). Then swap the terms.","why":"64 − 29 = 64 + (−29). Swapping the terms gives (−29) + 64 = 35. But 29 − 64 has the terms 29 and −64, so its value is −35 — not the same!"},{"c":"expression-from-story","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Your YouTube edit has a 10-second intro and then 5 clips of 60 seconds each. Which expression gives its length?","o":["10 + 5 × 60","(60 + 10) × 5","5 + 60 + 10","5 × 10 + 60"],"a":0,"hint":"How many 60-second clips are there? And how many times does the intro play?","why":"The intro plays once, and 5 clips take 5 × 60 = 300 s: 10 + 5 × 60 = 310 s. (60 + 10) × 5 would put an intro before EVERY clip (350 s)."},{"c":"expression-from-story","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"38 children play “Fire in the mountain”. The teacher calls “6!” Which expression shows how they end up?","o":["38 ÷ 6","6 × 6 + 2","6 + 2","6 × 2 + 6"],"a":1,"hint":"How many full groups of 6 can 38 children make? How many are left out?","why":"6 groups of 6 use 36 children and 2 are out: 6 × 6 + 2 = 38. Read it as ‘2 more than 6 × 6’ — two terms, 6 × 6 and 2."},{"c":"expression-from-story","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"A bus ticket is ₹30 for an adult and ₹15 for a child. Which expression gives the cost for 2 adults and 4 children?","o":["(2 + 4) × (30 + 15)","2 × 15 + 4 × 30","6 × 30","2 × 30 + 4 × 15"],"a":3,"hint":"Make one term for the adults and another term for the children.","why":"Adults: 2 × 30 = 60. Children: 4 × 15 = 60. Total = 2 × 30 + 4 × 15 = ₹120. (2 + 4) × (30 + 15) would charge every person both fares — ₹270!"},{"c":"expression-from-story","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"A snail climbs 4 cm up a 12 cm post each day and slips 3 cm each night. On which day does it reach the treat on top?","o":["Day 12","Day 3","Day 9","Day 8"],"a":2,"hint":"Each day-and-night it gains just 1 cm. But on the day it reaches the top, does it slip back?","why":"After 8 days and nights it is 8 × (4 − 3) = 8 cm up. On day 9 it climbs 4 cm: 8 + 4 = 12 cm — treat reached before night! Day 12 forgets that last big climb."},{"c":"remove-brackets-minus","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Remove the brackets: 90 − (25 + 8) = ?","o":["90 − 25 + 8 — just drop the brackets","90 − 25 − 8 — take away both parts","90 + 25 − 8 — only the 8 is taken away","90 + 25 + 8 — add all three numbers"],"a":1,"hint":"First find 25 + 8 and take it away from 90. Which option gives that same value?","why":"Taking away (25 + 8) means taking away 25 and then 8: 90 − 25 − 8 = 57. A minus in front of a bracket flips the signs inside: +25 → −25, +8 → −8."},{"c":"remove-brackets-minus","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Kiara writes: 40 − (15 − 6) = 40 − 15 − 6 = 19. What is her mistake?","o":["Nothing — 19 is correct","The 15 should become +15; answer 49","Brackets can never be removed","The −6 should become +6; answer 31"],"a":3,"hint":"Work out the bracket first: 15 − 6 = ? Then take that away from 40.","why":"40 − (15 − 6) = 40 − 9 = 31. A minus before the bracket flips BOTH signs inside: 15 → −15 and −6 → +6. So it’s 40 − 15 + 6 = 31."},{"c":"remove-brackets-minus","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Jasoda subtracts 9 by taking away 10 and adding 1. Using the same idea, 83 − 8 is the same as…","o":["83 − 10 − 2 — take away the 2 as well","83 − 10 + 8 — add the 8 back on","83 + 10 − 2 — add 10, then take 2","83 − 10 + 2 — took 2 too many, so add 2"],"a":3,"hint":"8 = 10 − 2, so 83 − 8 = 83 − (10 − 2). Now remove the brackets.","why":"83 − (10 − 2) = 83 − 10 + 2 = 75. Taking away 10 removes 2 too many, so add 2 back. Jasoda’s trick is really just removing brackets!"},{"c":"remove-brackets-minus","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Three of these are equal to 58 − 17 − 3. Which one is NOT?","o":["58 − (17 − 3)","58 − (3 + 17)","−17 + 58 − 3","−3 − 17 + 58"],"a":0,"hint":"Remove the brackets in each one carefully. Which one ends up with different terms?","why":"58 − (17 − 3) = 58 − 17 + 3 = 44, because the minus flips −3 into +3. The others all have the terms 58, −17 and −3 in some order, giving 38."},{"c":"remove-brackets-plus","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which of these bracket removals is done correctly?","o":["30 + (12 − 5) = 30 + 12 + 5","50 − (16 − 4) = 50 − 16 − 4","45 + (20 − 7) = 45 + 20 − 7","60 − (25 + 9) = 60 − 25 + 9"],"a":2,"hint":"Look at the sign just before each bracket. After a +, do the signs inside change?","why":"After a +, terms keep their signs: 45 + (20 − 7) = 45 + 20 − 7. After a −, EVERY sign inside flips: 50 − 16 + 4 and 60 − 25 − 9 would be right."},{"c":"remove-brackets-plus","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Meher has 42 shells in one jar and 26 in another, and gives away 9 from the second. She writes 42 + (26 − 9) = 42 − 26 + 9. Right?","o":["Yes — removing brackets always flips signs","Yes — the 9 comes back, so add it","No — after a +, signs stay: 42 + 26 − 9","No — it should be 42 − 26 − 9"],"a":2,"hint":"Is there a + or a − just before the bracket? Does that flip the signs inside?","why":"With a + before the bracket, terms keep their signs: 42 + (26 − 9) = 42 + 26 − 9 = 59 shells. Meher’s 42 − 26 + 9 = 25 flips signs that should never flip."},{"c":"remove-brackets-plus","t":"Predict","p":50,"q":"Remove the brackets and find the value: −25 + (18 − 6)","o":["−37","−1","−49","−13"],"a":3,"hint":"Look only at the sign just before the bracket. Is it + or −?","why":"There’s a + before the bracket, so 18 and −6 keep their signs: −25 + 18 − 6 = −13. The −25 outside doesn’t change anything inside the bracket."},{"c":"tinker-terms","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"71 + (−28) = 43. Using reasoning, not a fresh sum, what is 71 + (−27)?","o":["42","43","44","98"],"a":2,"hint":"Is −27 one more or one less than −28?","why":"−27 is ONE MORE than −28 (it’s closer to zero). When one term goes up by 1, the value goes up by 1: 43 + 1 = 44."},{"c":"tinker-terms","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Fill the blank by reasoning: 315 − 47 = 320 − ___","o":["42 — one goes up, so the other goes down","52 — both numbers go up by 5","47 — what’s taken away stays the same","57 — add 10 to make it round"],"a":1,"hint":"315 became 320, which is 5 more. To keep the same value, what must happen to 47?","why":"Starting 5 higher means you must take away 5 more to land on the same value: 320 − 52 = 268 = 315 − 47. Both moved up by 5, so the gap stays the same!"},{"c":"tinker-terms","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"−60 + (−25) = −85. What is −61 + (−24)?","o":["−86","−85","−84","−87"],"a":1,"hint":"−61 is one less than −60. Is −24 one more or one less than −25?","why":"−61 is 1 less than −60, but −24 is 1 more than −25. One term goes down by 1 and the other up by 1 — they balance, so the value stays −85."},{"c":"distributive-property","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which expression is equal to 6 × (9 + 4)?","o":["6 × 9 + 4","9 + 6 × 4","6 × 9 + 6 × 4","6 × 4 × 9"],"a":2,"hint":"Find the value of 6 × (9 + 4) first. Which option has that same value?","why":"6 × (9 + 4) is 6 nines plus 6 fours: 6 × 9 + 6 × 4 = 54 + 24 = 78. The multiple of a sum is the sum of the multiples — the distributive property!"},{"c":"distributive-property","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A garden has 6 rows of 8 marigolds and 4 rows of 8 roses. Which ONE expression gives the total number of plants?","o":["8 × (6 + 4)","6 × 4 × 8","6 × 8 + 4","4 × 8 + 6"],"a":0,"hint":"Count the total number of rows first. Every row has the same number of plants.","why":"There are 6 + 4 = 10 rows of 8, so 8 × (6 + 4) = 80. That equals 6 × 8 + 4 × 8 = 48 + 32 — like the scouts and guides marching in rows of 5!"},{"c":"distributive-property","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Riya practises piano every day except Sundays and Wednesdays. Which does NOT give her practice days in 6 weeks?","o":["7 × 6 − 2","(7 − 2) × 6","7 × 6 − 2 × 6","5 × 6"],"a":0,"hint":"She skips 2 days EVERY week. Over 6 weeks, how many days does she skip in all?","why":"She skips 2 days each week, so 2 × 6 = 12 days in all: 7 × 6 − 2 × 6 = (7 − 2) × 6 = 30. But 7 × 6 − 2 removes only 2 days in total, giving 40."},{"c":"distributive-property","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Without calculating, compare 12 + 7 × 15 and (12 + 7) × 15. Pick the answer with the right reason.","o":["Equal — same numbers, same signs","First is bigger — it has no brackets","First is smaller — only 7 is × 15","First is smaller — it has fewer terms"],"a":2,"hint":"In 12 + 7 × 15, which numbers get multiplied by 15? And in (12 + 7) × 15?","why":"In 12 + 7 × 15 only the 7 is multiplied by 15 (value 117). In (12 + 7) × 15 the 12 is multiplied too (value 285). Brackets change the meaning!"},{"c":"smart-multiplication","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Given 47 × 16 = 752, find 57 × 16 without multiplying from scratch.","o":["762","768","912","852"],"a":2,"hint":"57 × 16 = (47 + 10) × 16. How much do the extra 10 sixteens add?","why":"57 × 16 = 47 × 16 + 10 × 16 = 752 + 160 = 912. Adding just 10 (762) or one 16 (768) forgets that ten more sixteens were added."},{"c":"smart-multiplication","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which is a smart way to work out 98 × 15?","o":["100 × 15 − 2","100 × 15 − 2 × 15","100 × 15 − 15","98 × 10 + 5"],"a":1,"hint":"98 is 2 less than 100. So 98 fifteens is 100 fifteens minus how many fifteens?","why":"98 × 15 = (100 − 2) × 15 = 100 × 15 − 2 × 15 = 1500 − 30 = 1470. Rounding up to 100 is smart — then take away 2 fifteens, not 2 and not just one 15."},{"c":"smart-multiplication","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Rohan works out 99 × 12 as 100 × 12 − 1 = 1199. What went wrong?","o":["Nothing — 1199 is right","He must take away 1 × 12, not 1","He should add 12 to get 1212","He should do 90 × 12 + 9 instead"],"a":1,"hint":"99 × 12 means 99 twelves. Rohan started from 100 twelves — how many twelves too many is that?","why":"(100 − 1) × 12 = 100 × 12 − 1 × 12 = 1200 − 12 = 1188. Rohan took away a 1 instead of a 12. Every part inside the bracket gets multiplied!"},{"c":"ws-words-to-expression","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: write as an expression — “Multiply 9 by the sum of 13 and 7.”","o":["9 × 13 + 7","(9 × 13) + (9 × 7) + 7","9 × (13 + 7)","9 + 13 × 7"],"a":2,"hint":"“The sum of 13 and 7” must be worked out first. How do you show that?","why":"The sum goes in brackets: 9 × (13 + 7) = 9 × 20 = 180. Without brackets, 9 × 13 + 7 = 124 — a different value."},{"c":"ws-words-to-expression","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: “Subtract 18 from the product of 12 and 5.” Which expression?","o":["(18 − 12) × 5","18 − 12 × 5","12 × (5 − 18)","12 × 5 − 18"],"a":3,"hint":"“Subtract 18 FROM something” — 18 comes after the minus sign.","why":"The product of 12 and 5 is 60, and 18 is taken away from it: 12 × 5 − 18 = 42."},{"c":"ws-words-to-expression","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: “Divide the difference between 96 and 36 by 6.” Which expression?","o":["6 ÷ (96 − 36)","96 − 36 ÷ 6","96 ÷ 6 − 36","(96 − 36) ÷ 6"],"a":3,"hint":"The whole difference is divided — so it needs brackets.","why":"(96 − 36) ÷ 6 = 60 ÷ 6 = 10. Without brackets, 96 − 36 ÷ 6 = 96 − 6 = 90, because division is done first."},{"c":"ws-words-to-expression","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: “Add the product of 8 and 9 to the product of 5 and 12.”","o":["8 × 9 + 5 × 12","(8 + 9) × (5 + 12)","8 + 9 × 5 + 12","8 × (9 + 5) × 12"],"a":0,"hint":"Two products, then add them.","why":"8 × 9 = 72 and 5 × 12 = 60, so 8 × 9 + 5 × 12 = 132. Multiplication is done before addition, so no brackets are needed."},{"c":"ws-words-to-expression","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: “Five times the difference between 42 and 18.” What is its value?","o":["120","192","30","102"],"a":0,"hint":"Find the difference first, then multiply by 5.","why":"5 × (42 − 18) = 5 × 24 = 120. Writing 5 × 42 − 18 instead gives 192 — that's why the brackets matter."},{"c":"ws-words-to-expression","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: “Divide the product of 15 and 12 by the difference between 10 and 5.” Value?","o":["13","30","175","36"],"a":3,"hint":"Work out the product and the difference separately, then divide.","why":"(15 × 12) ÷ (10 − 5) = 180 ÷ 5 = 36. Without the brackets, 15 × 12 ÷ 10 − 5 = 18 − 5 = 13."},{"c":"ws-words-to-expression","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: “Four multiplied by the sum of nine and two.”","o":["4 + 9 × 2","4 × (9 + 2)","4 × 9 + 2","(4 × 9) + (4 × 9 + 2)"],"a":1,"hint":"The sum is one block — keep it together.","why":"4 × (9 + 2) = 4 × 11 = 44. 4 × 9 + 2 would only give 38."},{"c":"ws-words-to-expression","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: “Divide the difference of eighteen and six by four.”","o":["(18 − 6) ÷ 4","18 − 6 ÷ 4","18 ÷ 4 − 6","4 ÷ (18 − 6)"],"a":0,"hint":"The difference is divided as a whole.","why":"(18 − 6) ÷ 4 = 12 ÷ 4 = 3."},{"c":"ws-properties","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 35 + 48 = 48 + 35 shows which property?","o":["Distributive property","Additive identity","Commutative property of addition","Associative property of addition"],"a":2,"hint":"Nothing changes except the ORDER of the two numbers.","why":"Swapping the order of numbers in addition doesn't change the sum — that's the commutative property."},{"c":"ws-properties","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 8 × (12 + 5) = (8 × 12) + (8 × 5) shows which property?","o":["Distributive property","Multiplicative identity","Commutative property","Associative property"],"a":0,"hint":"The 8 is shared out to BOTH numbers inside the bracket.","why":"Multiplying a sum is the same as multiplying each part and adding: that is the distributive property. Both sides are 136."},{"c":"ws-properties","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: a student writes 25 × 16 = 25 × (10 + 6) = 250 + 150 = 400. What's true?","o":["Wrong: 25 × 16 is 390","It's correct: the distributive property, and 25 × 16 = 400","Wrong: it should be 250 + 6","Wrong: the property used is associative"],"a":1,"hint":"Check each step: is 25 × 6 really 150?","why":"16 = 10 + 6, so 25 × 16 = 25 × 10 + 25 × 6 = 250 + 150 = 400. Every step is right, using the distributive property."},{"c":"ws-properties","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: fill the blank — 17 × (8 + 2) = (17 × 8) + (17 × __)","o":["2","8","17","10"],"a":0,"hint":"17 multiplies each number inside the bracket.","why":"17 × (8 + 2) = 17 × 8 + 17 × 2 = 136 + 34 = 170 = 17 × 10."},{"c":"ws-properties","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 93 + 0 = 93 and 56 × 1 = 56. What do these show?","o":["That 0 and 1 are equal","Adding 0 and multiplying by 1 leave a number unchanged (identities)","The distributive property","The commutative property"],"a":1,"hint":"What did adding 0, or multiplying by 1, change?","why":"0 is the additive identity and 1 is the multiplicative identity: n + 0 = n and n × 1 = n."},{"c":"ws-order-of-operations","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 64 − 6 × 7 + 15 = ?","o":["421","49","37","7"],"a":2,"hint":"Multiply first, then go left to right.","why":"6 × 7 = 42 first. Then 64 − 42 = 22, and 22 + 15 = 37. Going left to right without multiplying first gives 421 — wrong."},{"c":"ws-order-of-operations","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 84 ÷ 7 × 4 + 18 − 10 = ?","o":["40","11","64","56"],"a":3,"hint":"÷ and × are done left to right, before + and −.","why":"84 ÷ 7 = 12, then 12 × 4 = 48. Then 48 + 18 − 10 = 56. Doing 7 × 4 first (84 ÷ 28 = 3) leads to 11 — wrong."},{"c":"ws-order-of-operations","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 125 − 45 ÷ 5 × 3 = ?","o":["122","120","98","48"],"a":2,"hint":"Do 45 ÷ 5 × 3 first (left to right), then subtract.","why":"45 ÷ 5 = 9, 9 × 3 = 27, and 125 − 27 = 98."},{"c":"ws-order-of-operations","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 48 + 72 ÷ 8 − 6 × 4 = ?","o":["36","33","9","57"],"a":1,"hint":"Two jobs come first: 72 ÷ 8 and 6 × 4.","why":"72 ÷ 8 = 9 and 6 × 4 = 24. So 48 + 9 − 24 = 33."},{"c":"ws-order-of-operations","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 150 ÷ 5 + 12 × 4 − 18 = ?","o":["42","150","60","66"],"a":2,"hint":"Work out 150 ÷ 5 and 12 × 4 first.","why":"150 ÷ 5 = 30 and 12 × 4 = 48, so 30 + 48 − 18 = 60."},{"c":"ws-order-of-operations","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 200 − 60 ÷ 6 × 5 + 15 = ?","o":["115","135","165","213"],"a":2,"hint":"60 ÷ 6 × 5 comes first, left to right.","why":"60 ÷ 6 = 10 and 10 × 5 = 50. Then 200 − 50 + 15 = 165."},{"c":"ws-order-of-operations","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: for 18 + 6 × 5 − 12, Student A does (18 + 6) × 5 − 12 and Student B does 18 + (6 × 5) − 12. Who's right?","o":["A, and the value is 36","B, and the value is 36","A, and the value is 108","B, and the value is 108"],"a":1,"hint":"Which operation comes first when there are no brackets?","why":"Multiplication comes before addition, so B is right: 18 + 30 − 12 = 36. A added first and got (24 × 5) − 12 = 108."},{"c":"ws-order-of-operations","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: what is the FIRST step in 6 + 3 × 4?","o":["Divide 6 by 3","Add 6 and 3","Multiply 3 and 4","Subtract 4 from 3"],"a":2,"hint":"× comes before +.","why":"3 × 4 = 12 first, then 6 + 12 = 18."},{"c":"ws-terms","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: how many terms are in 4 + 15 − 9?","o":["4","2","5","3"],"a":3,"hint":"Terms are the parts joined by + (a minus becomes + a negative).","why":"4 + 15 − 9 = 4 + 15 + (−9): the terms are 4, 15 and −9 — three terms."},{"c":"ws-terms","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: what are the terms of 83 − 14?","o":["83 and 14","83 − 14 is one term","14 and −83","83 and −14"],"a":3,"hint":"Rewrite the subtraction as adding a negative.","why":"83 − 14 = 83 + (−14), so the terms are 83 and −14. The sign belongs to the term."},{"c":"ws-terms","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet (Assertion–Reason): A: 2 × 3 + 3 × 5 + 8 × 7 has three terms. R: terms are the parts separated by the × sign.","o":["Both true, but R doesn't explain A","A is true, but R is false","Both A and R are false","Both true, and R explains A"],"a":1,"hint":"What separates terms — + or ×?","why":"The terms 2 × 3, 3 × 5 and 8 × 7 are separated by + signs, so A is true. R is false: × joins numbers INSIDE a term."},{"c":"ws-terms","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet (Assertion–Reason): A: 12 × 5 + (−7 + 4) has two terms. R: terms are the parts of an expression separated by the + sign.","o":["A is true, but R is false","Both true, and R explains A","Both true, but R doesn't explain A","Both A and R are false"],"a":1,"hint":"A bracket counts as one block.","why":"The two terms are 12 × 5 and (−7 + 4), joined by a + sign. R gives exactly that reason."},{"c":"ws-terms","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which of these is NOT an arithmetic expression?","o":["7 × 6","15 − 4","6 and 3","9 ÷ 3"],"a":2,"hint":"An arithmetic expression uses numbers joined by +, −, × or ÷.","why":"“6 and 3” has no operation sign, so it isn't an arithmetic expression. (20 < 4 isn't one either — it's a comparison.)"},{"c":"ws-brackets","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which shows the brackets removed correctly?","o":["Only 100 − 43","Only 100 − 40 − 3","Both: 100 − (40 + 3) = 100 − 40 − 3 and 100 − (40 + 3) = 100 − 43","Neither"],"a":2,"hint":"Check that both sides equal 57.","why":"A minus before a bracket subtracts EVERYTHING inside it: 100 − 40 − 3 = 57 and 100 − 43 = 57. Both are correct."},{"c":"ws-brackets","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which is NOT equal to 500 − (250 − 100)?","o":["500 − 250 − 100","350","500 − 150","500 − 250 + 100"],"a":0,"hint":"Removing a bracket after a minus flips the signs inside.","why":"500 − (250 − 100) = 500 − 150 = 350 = 500 − 250 + 100. But 500 − 250 − 100 = 150."},{"c":"ws-brackets","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 14 − (12 − 10) = ?","o":["14 − 22","14 − 12 + 10","14 − 12 − 10","14 + 12 − 10"],"a":1,"hint":"Minus outside the bracket: the − inside becomes +.","why":"14 − (12 − 10) = 14 − 2 = 12, and 14 − 12 + 10 = 12 too. 14 − 12 − 10 = −8 is wrong."},{"c":"ws-brackets","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 4 × 5 + 3 × 5 is the same as…","o":["None of these","Only (4 + 3) × 5","All of these: (4 + 3) × 5, 5 × 7 and 35","Only 35"],"a":2,"hint":"Take out the common 5.","why":"4 × 5 + 3 × 5 = (4 + 3) × 5 = 5 × 7 = 35 — the distributive property in reverse."},{"c":"ws-brackets","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which shows 5 more than double 23?","o":["Only 2 × 23 + 5","None — you need brackets","Only 5 + 2 × 23","All of these: 2 × 23 + 5, 23 × 2 + 5 and 5 + 2 × 23"],"a":3,"hint":"Double means × 2; order of terms doesn't matter for +.","why":"Each one doubles 23 (46) and adds 5, giving 51."},{"c":"ws-compare-reasoning","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: without working them out, compare 137 + 335 and 136 + 339.","o":["137 + 335 > 136 + 339","They are equal","137 + 335 < 136 + 339","Can't tell without adding"],"a":2,"hint":"137 is 1 more than 136, but 339 is 4 more than 335.","why":"The first sum gains 1 but the second gains 4, so the second is bigger: 472 < 475."},{"c":"ws-compare-reasoning","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: compare 563 − 234 and 562 − 233 without calculating.","o":["They are equal","563 − 234 is bigger","562 − 233 is bigger","Can't tell"],"a":0,"hint":"Both numbers went down by 1.","why":"Taking 1 off both numbers keeps the difference the same: both are 329."},{"c":"ws-compare-reasoning","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: compare 103 − 18 and 104 − 17.","o":["103 − 18 > 104 − 17","103 − 18 < 104 − 17","Can't tell","They are equal"],"a":1,"hint":"A bigger starting number AND a smaller amount taken away…","why":"104 − 17 starts 1 higher and takes away 1 less, so it is 2 more: 85 < 87."},{"c":"ws-compare-reasoning","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: compare 11 × (12 − 8) and 11 × 12 + 11 × 8.","o":["Can't tell","11 × (12 − 8) is smaller","11 × (12 − 8) is bigger","They are equal"],"a":1,"hint":"One side has 12 − 8 inside, the other adds 11 × 8.","why":"11 × (12 − 8) = 11 × 12 − 11 × 8 = 44, but 11 × 12 + 11 × 8 = 220. The minus vs plus makes all the difference."},{"c":"ws-compare-reasoning","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: compare 25 × (78 + 16) and 25 × (77 + 17).","o":["The first is bigger","They are equal","Can't tell","The second is bigger"],"a":1,"hint":"What is inside each bracket?","why":"78 + 16 = 94 and 77 + 17 = 94, so both are 25 × 94."},{"c":"ws-compare-reasoning","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: which is the greatest — 86 − 23, 714 ÷ 7, 6 × 19 or 35 + 25?","o":["714 ÷ 7","86 − 23","6 × 19","35 + 25"],"a":2,"hint":"Work out each one roughly.","why":"6 × 19 = 114, 714 ÷ 7 = 102, 86 − 23 = 63 and 35 + 25 = 60. So in descending order: 6 × 19, 714 ÷ 7, 86 − 23, 35 + 25."},{"c":"ws-smart-calculation","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 47 × 23 = 1081. So 37 × 23 = ?","o":["1071","861","1058","851"],"a":3,"hint":"37 is 10 less than 47 — so take away ten 23s.","why":"37 × 23 = 47 × 23 − 10 × 23 = 1081 − 230 = 851."},{"c":"ws-smart-calculation","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: an easy way to work out 102 × 35?","o":["100 × 35 + 2 × 35 = 3,570","100 × 35 + 2 = 3,502","102 × 30 + 5 = 3,065","100 × 35 × 2 = 7,000"],"a":0,"hint":"Split 102 into 100 + 2.","why":"102 × 35 = 100 × 35 + 2 × 35 = 3,500 + 70 = 3,570 (distributive property)."},{"c":"ws-smart-calculation","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: an easy way to work out 49 × 25?","o":["50 × 25 − 1 = 1,249","50 × 25 − 25 = 1,225","50 × 25 + 25 = 1,275","49 × 20 + 5 = 985"],"a":1,"hint":"49 is one less than 50.","why":"49 × 25 = 50 × 25 − 1 × 25 = 1,250 − 25 = 1,225."},{"c":"ws-smart-calculation","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 99 × 9 = ?","o":["881","801","909","891"],"a":3,"hint":"99 = 100 − 1.","why":"99 × 9 = 100 × 9 − 9 = 900 − 9 = 891."},{"c":"ws-smart-calculation","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: fill the blank — 21 + 7 = __ + 9","o":["16","28","19","12"],"a":2,"hint":"Both sides must have the same value.","why":"21 + 7 = 28, and 19 + 9 = 28."},{"c":"ws-smart-calculation","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: fill the blank — 25 × 4 = 34 + __","o":["76","66","100","56"],"a":1,"hint":"Find the value of 25 × 4 first.","why":"25 × 4 = 100, and 34 + 66 = 100."},{"c":"ws-word-problems","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: art exhibition — 8 chart papers at ₹12, 5 packets of colours at ₹45 and 6 paintbrushes at ₹20. Total spent?","o":["₹77","₹1,463","₹441","₹341"],"a":2,"hint":"Write it as 8 × 12 + 5 × 45 + 6 × 20.","why":"8 × 12 = 96, 5 × 45 = 225 and 6 × 20 = 120. 96 + 225 + 120 = ₹441."},{"c":"ws-word-problems","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 15 rows of 24 seats, and 38 seats stay empty. Which expression gives the occupied seats?","o":["15 − 24 × 38","(15 + 24) − 38","15 × (24 − 38)","15 × 24 − 38"],"a":3,"hint":"First find ALL the seats, then take away the empty ones.","why":"15 × 24 = 360 seats in all, so 360 − 38 = 322 are occupied."},{"c":"ws-word-problems","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 125 mangoes are packed equally into 5 baskets and 3 baskets are sold. How many mangoes are sold?","o":["25","75","110","50"],"a":1,"hint":"How many in ONE basket first?","why":"125 ÷ 5 = 25 in each basket, so 3 baskets hold 25 × 3 = 75 mangoes. 50 are left."},{"c":"ws-word-problems","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: Meera buys 6 notebooks and Seema 7, all at ₹10 each. Total paid?","o":["₹120","₹130","₹67","₹13"],"a":1,"hint":"6 × 10 + 7 × 10 = (6 + 7) × 10.","why":"6 × 10 + 7 × 10 = (6 + 7) × 10 = 13 × 10 = ₹130."},{"c":"ws-word-problems","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: 75,000 sheets; each sheet makes 8 pages; each notebook has 200 pages. How many notebooks?","o":["3,000","30,000","375","600,000"],"a":0,"hint":"Total pages = 75,000 × 8. Then divide by 200.","why":"75,000 × 8 = 600,000 pages, and 600,000 ÷ 200 = 3,000 notebooks."}],"z-constr":[{"c":"perp-bisector-construction","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The perpendicular bisector of a line segment XY is a line that…","o":["passes through X at 90° to XY","cuts XY into two equal parts at any angle","runs parallel to XY","cuts XY into two equal parts at 90°"],"a":3,"hint":"Split the name: ‘bisector’ cuts into two equal parts. What does ‘perpendicular’ add?","why":"‘Bisect’ means divide into two identical parts; ‘perpendicular’ means at 90°. So the perpendicular bisector passes through the midpoint of XY and makes a right angle with it."},{"c":"perp-bisector-construction","t":"Order","p":50,"q":"Order the steps: P) Join A and B. Q) Open the compass to more than half of XY. R) From X and Y, draw arcs meeting at A and B.","o":["R, Q, P","Q, P, R","Q, R, P","P, Q, R"],"a":2,"hint":"Which step needs A and B to exist already? That step must come last.","why":"First set the compass to more than half of XY (Q). Keeping that opening, draw arcs from X and Y that meet at A above and B below (R). Finally join AB (P)."},{"c":"perp-bisector-construction","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"The book says the compass method finds the midpoint of a segment better than a marked scale does. Why?","o":["It rounds the length to a whole cm","It’s exact — no reading a scale","It needs no ruler at all","It works only for even lengths"],"a":1,"hint":"Measuring 7.3 cm and halving it gives 3.65 cm. How easy is it to mark that exactly?","why":"Reading a scale and halving always adds small errors. The arcs find the midpoint by pure geometry — exact! An unmarked ruler is still needed to join A and B."},{"c":"perp-bisector-construction","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Dev draws the arcs above XY, but changes the compass opening between the arc from X and the arc from Y. What goes wrong?","o":["Nothing — any two arcs will do","The arcs can never cross","The crossing isn’t equally far from X and Y","The line comes out parallel to XY"],"a":2,"hint":"The crossing point A must have AX = AY. Does it, if the two radii are different?","why":"The crossing point is one radius from X and a different radius from Y, so AX ≠ AY and A is NOT on the perpendicular bisector. Within a pair, keep the same opening!"},{"c":"compass-opening","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"XY = 8 cm. Which compass opening works for the perpendicular bisector arcs, and why?","o":["5 cm — more than half, so the arcs cross","3 cm — smaller arcs are neater","4 cm — exactly half is perfect","Any opening works"],"a":0,"hint":"Picture arcs from X and from Y. Will they reach each other above and below XY?","why":"Two 5 cm arcs reach 5 + 5 = 10 cm, more than 8, so they cross above and below XY. 4 cm arcs only touch at the midpoint, and 3 cm arcs never meet at all."},{"c":"compass-opening","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if you set the compass to EXACTLY half of XY and draw arcs from X and Y?","o":["They cross above and below as usual","They don’t meet anywhere","You get the bisector faster","They only touch, at the midpoint of XY"],"a":3,"hint":"How far is the midpoint of XY from X? And from Y?","why":"The midpoint is exactly half of XY from both ends, so both arcs pass through it and just touch there — one point, lying ON XY. You need points above and below, so open wider!"},{"c":"compass-opening","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Ira keeps one compass opening for her arcs from X and Y, but they never meet. XY = 10 cm. Which opening could she have used?","o":["4 cm","6 cm","7 cm","9 cm"],"a":0,"hint":"Two equal arcs from X and Y can only cross if their radii add up to more than XY.","why":"Two 4 cm arcs reach only 4 + 4 = 8 cm — short of 10 cm, so they never meet. Any opening above 5 cm (half of 10) makes them cross."},{"c":"equidistant-points","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Point P is 6 cm from X and also 6 cm from Y. Where must P lie?","o":["On the perpendicular bisector of XY","At the midpoint of XY","On a line parallel to XY","Closer to X than to Y"],"a":0,"hint":"Every point that is equally far from X and Y lies on one special line. Which one?","why":"Any point the same distance from X and Y lies on the perpendicular bisector of XY. P is the midpoint only if XY happens to be exactly 12 cm!"},{"c":"equidistant-points","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"XY = 8 cm. Arcs above XY use a 5 cm opening and meet at A; arcs below use 7 cm and meet at B. Is AB still the perpendicular bisector?","o":["No — all four arcs must be the same","Yes — A and B are each equally far from X and Y","No — the line will lean towards Y","No — only point A will be on it"],"a":1,"hint":"Check A: is it the same distance from X and Y? Now check B the same way.","why":"A is 5 cm from both X and Y; B is 7 cm from both. So each lies on the perpendicular bisector, and two points fix a line. Radii may change between pairs — not within a pair."},{"c":"equidistant-points","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Can both pairs of arcs be drawn ABOVE XY and still give the perpendicular bisector?","o":["No — one point must be below XY","Yes, with the same radius for both pairs","No — arcs above XY cross only once","Yes, if the two pairs use different radii"],"a":3,"hint":"A line needs two different points. With one radius, how many crossing points do you get above XY?","why":"Arcs of, say, 5 cm and then 7 cm from X and Y cross at two different points above XY. Both are equally far from X and Y, so the line through them is the perpendicular bisector."},{"c":"equidistant-points","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Śulba-Sūtras: a rope tied to pegs X and Y is pulled tight by its midpoint to A above XY and B below. Why is AB the perpendicular bisector?","o":["The rope makes a right angle at A","A and B are each equally far from X and Y","A is directly above peg X","The rope stretches to fit XY"],"a":1,"hint":"The rope’s midpoint splits it into two equal halves. What does that tell you about AX and AY?","why":"The two halves of the rope are equal, so AX = AY, and likewise BX = BY. Points equally far from X and Y lie on the perpendicular bisector — the compass idea with a rope!"},{"c":"perp-bisector-proof","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The book proves ∆AOX ≅ ∆AOY using AX = AY, ∠XAO = ∠YAO and the common side AO. Which condition is this?","o":["SAS","SSS","ASA","RHS"],"a":0,"hint":"Two sides and one angle. Is that angle between those two sides?","why":"Two sides (AX, AO) and the angle between them (∠XAO) match the other triangle’s — that’s SAS. SSS would need OX = OY, which is what we’re trying to prove!"},{"c":"perp-bisector-proof","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"AB meets XY at O. Once ∆AOX ≅ ∆AOY, why must ∠AOX be 90°?","o":["Every angle at a midpoint is 90°","∠AOX = ∠AOY, and together they make 180°","AX = AY, so the angle must be right","∠AOX = ∠AXO, so it is 90°"],"a":1,"hint":"∠AOX and ∠AOY sit side by side on the straight line XY. What do they add up to?","why":"Congruent triangles give ∠AOX = ∠AOY. Side by side on line XY, they add to 180°. Two equal angles that make 180° must be 90° each!"},{"c":"perp-bisector-proof","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"In the proof that AB is the perpendicular bisector of XY, A and B are where the arcs cross and O is where AB meets XY. Why is ∠XAO = ∠YAO?","o":["O is the midpoint of XY","AB is perpendicular to XY","∆ABX ≅ ∆ABY by SSS","AO is half of AB"],"a":2,"hint":"You can’t use what you’re trying to prove! Which two triangles have all three sides known to be equal?","why":"AX = AY, BX = BY and AB is shared, so ∆ABX ≅ ∆ABY by SSS, giving ∠XAO = ∠YAO. ‘O is the midpoint’ is what we’re trying to PROVE, so it can’t be the reason."},{"c":"right-angle-at-point","t":"Order","p":50,"q":"To construct a 90° angle at point O on a line with ruler and compass, what is the FIRST step?","o":["Draw arcs from X and Y above the line","Place a protractor at O","Mark X and Y on the line, equally far from O","Join O to a point A above the line"],"a":2,"hint":"You want O to become the midpoint of some segment on the line.","why":"First mark X and Y with OX = OY, so O is the midpoint of XY. Only then can arcs from X and Y meet at a point A above the line. Join OA — that’s 90°!"},{"c":"right-angle-at-point","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"For a 90° angle at point O on a line, why is ONE pair of crossing arcs enough, instead of two?","o":["One pair always fixes a whole line","A second pair would cancel the first","The arcs are drawn from O itself","O is already on the perpendicular bisector"],"a":3,"hint":"A line needs two points. How many points of the perpendicular bisector do you already have?","why":"OX = OY, so O is the midpoint of XY — already one point on the perpendicular bisector. One pair of arcs gives a second point A. Join OA: two points fix the line!"},{"c":"right-angle-at-point","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"To draw a perpendicular from a point P (not on line l) to l, find a segment on l whose perpendicular bisector…","o":["passes through P","is parallel to line l","meets l at one end of the segment","is the line l itself"],"a":0,"hint":"The line you want goes through P AND is at 90° to l.","why":"Draw an arc from P cutting l at X and Y. Now PX = PY, so P lies on the perpendicular bisector of XY. Construct it — it passes through P at 90° to l!"},{"c":"angle-bisector-steps","t":"Order","p":50,"q":"Order the steps to bisect ∠XOY: P) Equal arcs from A and B meet at C. Q) Join O to C. R) An arc from O cuts the arms at A and B.","o":["R, P, Q","P, R, Q","R, Q, P","Q, P, R"],"a":0,"hint":"You can’t draw arcs from A and B until A and B have been marked!","why":"First an arc centred at O marks A and B with OA = OB (R). Then equal arcs from A and B cross at C (P). Finally join O to C (Q) — it splits the angle into two equal parts."},{"c":"angle-bisector-steps","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In angle bisection, what is the first arc, centred at the vertex O, used for?","o":["To measure the angle in degrees","To mark A and B with OA = OB","To find the midpoint of arm OX","To draw a circle through C"],"a":1,"hint":"The arc cuts both arms. What is the same about the two points where it cuts?","why":"An arc centred at O cuts both arms at the same distance, so OA = OB. That equal pair of sides starts the two congruent triangles that make the bisection work."},{"c":"angle-bisector-steps","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Neel bisects ∠AOB but uses a 4 cm arc from A and a 6 cm arc from B to find C. What’s the problem?","o":["Nothing — any two arcs will do","The arcs must be drawn from O","The angle turns into 90°","C isn’t equally far from A and B"],"a":3,"hint":"For ∆OAC and ∆OBC to be congruent, which two sides must match?","why":"AC = 4 cm but BC = 6 cm, so ∆OAC and ∆OBC aren’t congruent and OC won’t split the angle equally. Keep the SAME radius for the arcs from A and B."},{"c":"angle-bisector-steps","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"While bisecting ∠AOB, the points A and B are 5 cm apart. What if you draw the arcs from A and B with a 2 cm opening?","o":["C lands exactly on O","You get the bisector faster","The arcs won’t meet, so there’s no C","C lands on arm OA"],"a":2,"hint":"Two 2 cm arcs from points 5 cm apart — can they reach each other?","why":"2 + 2 = 4 cm is less than the 5 cm gap, so the arcs never meet and there is no C. That’s why the book says to choose a ‘sufficiently long’ radius."},{"c":"angle-bisector-why","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"OA = OB, AC = BC and OC is common. Which condition shows ∆OAC ≅ ∆OBC?","o":["SAS","SSS","ASA","RHS"],"a":1,"hint":"Look at what you know: is it sides, angles, or a mix?","why":"Three pairs of equal sides → SSS. So ∠AOC = ∠BOC, which means OC bisects the angle. Notice: no angle was measured at all!"},{"c":"angle-bisector-why","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Bisecting ∠AOB, the equal arcs from A and B meet at C on the OTHER side of O. Does line CO still bisect ∠AOB?","o":["No — C must be inside the angle","No — it only splits the reflex angle","Only if ∠AOB is 90°","Yes — O and C are both equally far from A and B"],"a":3,"hint":"OA = OB and CA = CB. Which special line of segment AB do O and C both sit on?","why":"O and C are each equally far from A and B, so both lie on the perpendicular bisector of AB. The line through them, extended past O, splits ∠AOB into two equal parts!"},{"c":"angle-bisector-why","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"In angle bisection, OA = OB and AC = BC. So line OC is ALSO the perpendicular bisector of which segment?","o":["AB","OA","OB","AC"],"a":0,"hint":"Which two points are O and C both equally far from?","why":"O is equally far from A and B, and so is C. Points equidistant from A and B lie on the perpendicular bisector of AB — so OC bisects the angle AND cuts AB in half at 90°!"},{"c":"angle-bisector-why","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Nina marks A 3 cm from O on one arm, B 5 cm from O on the other, then equal arcs from A and B meet at C. Can she trust OC as the bisector?","o":["Yes — only the arcs from A and B matter","Yes — any two points on the arms work","No — without OA = OB, no congruent triangles","Yes — if the arcs are long enough"],"a":2,"hint":"In angle bisection, what does the first arc from O make sure of? Is that true for Nina?","why":"With OA = 3 cm and OB = 5 cm, ∆OAC and ∆OBC aren’t congruent, so nothing makes the two angles equal — OC is usually off. The first arc from O is what makes OA = OB."},{"c":"angle-60-120","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"To make 60° at A on segment AX, an arc centred at A cuts AX at B. What is the next step?","o":["Bisect the segment AB","Draw an arc from X with any radius","Draw a perpendicular at B","Same radius: arc from B cuts the first arc at C"],"a":3,"hint":"You are secretly building an equilateral triangle with AB as one side.","why":"Keeping the SAME radius, an arc from B cuts the first arc at C. Now AB = AC = BC, so ∆ABC is equilateral and ∠CAX = 60°."},{"c":"angle-60-120","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"60° at A: an arc from A cuts AX at B, and the same radius from B cuts that arc at C. Why is ∠CAX exactly 60°?","o":["The arcs are quarter circles","AB = AC = BC, so ∆ABC is equilateral","The compass is opened to 60 mm","C is directly above B"],"a":1,"hint":"Which lengths in this figure were all made with the same compass opening?","why":"AB and AC are radii of the arc from A; BC is the same radius from B. All three sides are equal, so ∆ABC is equilateral — and each angle of an equilateral triangle is 60°."},{"c":"angle-60-120","t":"Predict","p":50,"q":"You construct ∠CAX = 60° at A on a straight line. Without any more arcs, what angle do you get on the other side of AC?","o":["60°","30°","300°","120°"],"a":3,"hint":"The two angles at A sit side by side on a straight line.","why":"Angles on a straight line add up to 180°, so the other angle is 180° − 60° = 120°. Construct 60° and you get 120° for free — handy for regular hexagons!"},{"c":"angle-60-120","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Why can exactly six equilateral triangles fit together around a point, with no gap and no overlap?","o":["Six 60° corners add up to exactly 360°","A hexagon has six sides, so six fit","Any six triangles fit around a point","Six 60° corners make a straight angle"],"a":0,"hint":"Each corner of an equilateral triangle is 60°. What do the angles around a point add up to?","why":"Angles around a point total 360°, and 6 × 60° = 360° exactly — no gap, no overlap. Each corner of the hexagon they form is 60° + 60° = 120°."},{"c":"angles-by-bisecting","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"How does the chapter construct a 45° angle with a ruler and compass?","o":["Construct 60°, then bisect it","Bisect a straight angle once","Construct 90°, then bisect it","Bisect a 120° angle once"],"a":2,"hint":"45° is exactly half of an angle you already know how to construct.","why":"45° is half of 90°, so construct a 90° angle at a point, then bisect it. Bisecting 60° gives 30°, and bisecting a straight angle gives 90°."},{"c":"angles-by-bisecting","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Starting from a 60° angle and bisecting again and again, which angle can you NOT get?","o":["30°","20°","15°","7.5°"],"a":1,"hint":"Keep halving: 60° → ? → ? → ?","why":"Halving 60° gives 30°, then 15°, then 7.5°… You never land on 20° — that needs splitting 60° into THREE equal parts, and bisecting only ever splits into two."},{"c":"angles-by-bisecting","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"You’re designing an 8-petal flower logo. Its 8 rays from the centre are equally spaced. What angle sits between neighbouring rays?","o":["22.5°","90°","60°","45°"],"a":3,"hint":"All the way around the centre is 360°. Share it equally among the gaps.","why":"8 rays make 8 equal gaps around the centre: 360° ÷ 8 = 45°. And 45° is easy to construct — bisect a 90° angle! (22.5° would be 360° ÷ 16.)"},{"c":"angles-by-bisecting","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Which plan gives a 15° angle using only a ruler and compass?","o":["Construct 60°, then bisect it twice","Construct 90°, then bisect it twice","Construct 45°, then bisect it once","Bisect a straight angle three times"],"a":0,"hint":"Work out what each plan gives. Each bisection halves the angle.","why":"60° → bisect → 30° → bisect → 15°. Bisecting 90° twice, 45° once, or 180° three times all give 22.5° — not 15°!"},{"c":"copy-an-angle","t":"Order","p":50,"q":"Copying ∠A at X: equal arcs from A (cutting the arms at B and C) and from X (cutting the ray at Z) are drawn. What next?","o":["Join X to Z","Measure BC; cut the arc from Z at Y","Bisect the angle at A","Measure AB; mark it from X"],"a":1,"hint":"The two triangles need a third pair of equal sides. Which length in ∆ABC is still uncopied?","why":"Set the compass to BC and cut the arc from Z to get Y, so YZ = BC. Join XY. Now ∆ABC ≅ ∆XYZ by SSS, so ∠X = ∠A."},{"c":"copy-an-angle","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"After copying ∠A to make ∠X with ruler and compass, why are the two angles exactly equal?","o":["The arcs look the same size","XY is parallel to AB","∆ABC ≅ ∆XYZ by SSS","Copied angles are always close"],"a":2,"hint":"The method builds a triangle at A and a copy of it at X. What do you know about their sides?","why":"AB = XY and AC = XZ (same radius), and BC = YZ (copied length). Three equal sides → ∆ABC ≅ ∆XYZ by SSS, so the angles at A and X match exactly."},{"c":"copy-an-angle","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Line l crosses line m at A, and B is on l. To draw a line through B parallel to m, which angle do you copy, and where?","o":["A 90° angle, made at B","A 60° angle, made at A","Any angle at B, then bisected","The angle between l and m at A, copied at B"],"a":3,"hint":"Parallel lines make equal corresponding angles with a line that crosses them.","why":"Copy the angle that l makes with m at A onto point B, in the same (corresponding) position. The new line meets l at the same angle as m does, so it is parallel to m."},{"c":"tiling","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What does it mean to tile a region?","o":["Cover it using only squares","Cover it with shapes, no gaps or overlaps","Cover most of it; small gaps are OK","Draw a grid over it"],"a":1,"hint":"Think of floor tiles. What must never happen between them?","why":"Tiling means covering a region with shapes leaving NO gaps and NO overlaps. Squares are just one choice — triangles, hexagons and even Escher’s animals can tile!"},{"c":"tiling","t":"Two-tier","p":50,"q":"Can a 5 × 7 grid be tiled with 2 × 1 tiles? Pick the answer with the right reason.","o":["No — 35 squares is odd; each tile covers 2","Yes — just rotate some of the tiles","No — 5 and 7 are both prime","Yes — any grid can be tiled"],"a":0,"hint":"Count the unit squares. How many squares does every tile cover?","why":"A 5 × 7 grid has 35 squares. Each tile covers exactly 2, so tiles always cover an EVEN number of squares — never 35. Rotating can’t fix that!"},{"c":"tiling","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"A region is coloured like a chessboard: 8 white squares and 6 black. Can it be tiled with 2 × 1 tiles?","o":["Yes — 14 is an even number","Yes — if all tiles stand upright","No — each tile covers 1 black and 1 white","Only if the region is a rectangle"],"a":2,"hint":"A 2 × 1 tile covers two neighbouring squares. What colours are neighbours?","why":"Neighbouring squares always have different colours, so every tile covers one black and one white. A tiled region needs EQUAL black and white squares — 8 ≠ 6, so no!"},{"c":"tiling","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Honeycomb cells are regular hexagons that tile with no gaps. How many hexagons meet at each corner point?","o":["2","4","3","6"],"a":2,"hint":"Each corner of a regular hexagon is 120°. The angles around a point add up to 360°.","why":"3 × 120° = 360°, so exactly three hexagons meet at each corner — no gap, no overlap, no wasted space for the bees’ eggs and honey!"},{"c":"ws-ribbon-midpoint","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: to find the exact centre of a 11.2 cm ribbon with compass and ruler, you construct its…","o":["60° angle — each half is 11.2 cm","Angle bisector — each half is 5.6 cm","Perpendicular bisector — each half is 5.6 cm","Perpendicular bisector — each half is 5.1 cm"],"a":2,"hint":"The line that cuts a segment into two equal halves at 90°.","why":"The perpendicular bisector passes through the midpoint, so each half is 11.2 ÷ 2 = 5.6 cm."},{"c":"ws-bisect-115","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: you draw 115° with a protractor and bisect it. Each new angle is…","o":["57.5°","57°","58.5°","65°"],"a":0,"hint":"Bisect = cut into two equal parts.","why":"115 ÷ 2 = 57.5°. Measure both to check they're equal."},{"c":"ws-90-at-point","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: PQ = 7.6 cm. How do you make a 90° angle at P with compass and ruler?","o":["Draw 120°, then stop","Measure 7.6 cm from P","Extend the line beyond P and bisect the straight angle (180°) at P","Bisect a 60° angle at P"],"a":2,"hint":"A straight angle is 180°. Half of it is…","why":"Mark equal arcs on both sides of P on the line, then draw the perpendicular through P: half of 180° is 90°."},{"c":"ws-45-steps","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: the steps to construct 45° with compass and ruler are…","o":["Construct 120°, then bisect it","Construct 60°, then bisect it","Construct 30°, then add 15°","Construct 90°, then bisect it"],"a":3,"hint":"45 is half of what?","why":"Make a 90° angle (a perpendicular), then bisect it: 90 ÷ 2 = 45°."},{"c":"ws-60-same-radius","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: when constructing 60°, what do you NOT change between the two arcs?","o":["The position of the ruler","The length of the arm","The pencil","The compass opening (radius)"],"a":3,"hint":"The two arcs make an equilateral triangle.","why":"The same radius from A and then from B makes AB = AC = BC — an equilateral triangle, so the angle is 60°."},{"c":"hd-perp-radius","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class handout, perpendicular bisector of AB, step 2: how wide do you open the compass?","o":["A different width for each arc","Exactly half the length of AB","Less than half the length of AB","More than half the length of AB"],"a":3,"hint":"The arcs from A and from B have to cross each other.","why":"With more than half of AB, the arcs from A and B cross at P and Q. Exactly half only touches at the midpoint; less than half never meets."},{"c":"hd-perp-result","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class handout: PQ is the perpendicular bisector of AB and meets it at M. What is the result?","o":["AM = 2 × MB, and PQ meets AB at 90°","AM = MB, and PQ meets AB at 60°","AM = MB, and PQ meets AB at 90°","PQ = AB, and PQ is parallel to AB"],"a":2,"hint":"Bisector → two equal parts. Perpendicular → ?","why":"It bisects AB (AM = MB, so M is the midpoint) and it is perpendicular to AB (they meet at 90°)."},{"c":"hd-90-steps","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class handout, 90° at point P on AB: an arc centred at P cuts AB at C and D. What comes next?","o":["Equal arcs from C and D (more than half of CD) meet at Q; join P to Q","Draw a bigger arc from P, then join P to where it cuts AB","Join C to D with the ruler, then find the middle with a protractor","Equal arcs from A and B meet at Q; join A to Q and extend it"],"a":0,"hint":"P is now the midpoint of CD.","why":"Equal arcs from C and D meet at Q, so Q is as far from C as from D. PQ is then the perpendicular bisector of CD, so ∠QPB = 90°."},{"c":"hd-90-why","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Class handout: why is ∠QPB exactly 90° in the construction at point P?","o":["Q is directly above A, so the line PQ leans at exactly 90°","The first arc from P was large, so the angle at P is 90°","PC = PD and QC = QD, so PQ is the perpendicular bisector of CD","PQ is longer than CD, so it must stand straight up from AB"],"a":2,"hint":"Which two points are each equally far from C and D?","why":"P (PC = PD, from the first arc) and Q (QC = QD, from equal arcs) are both equally far from C and D, so line PQ is CD's perpendicular bisector."},{"c":"hd-bisector-steps","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class handout, bisecting ∠ABC: an arc from B cuts the arms at P and Q. What comes next?","o":["Draw a second arc from B with double the radius","Equal arcs from P and Q meet at R inside the angle; join B to R","Measure ∠ABC with a protractor and stop","Join P to Q and stop"],"a":1,"hint":"The two new arcs use the same compass width.","why":"Equal arcs from P and Q meet at R. Ray BR then splits ∠ABC into two equal angles: ∠ABR = ∠RBC."},{"c":"hd-bisector-result","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class handout: ray BR bisects ∠ABC = 84°. What is ∠ABR?","o":["24°","84°","168°","42°"],"a":3,"hint":"Bisect = cut into two equal parts.","why":"∠ABR = ∠RBC = 84 ÷ 2 = 42°."},{"c":"hd-60-order","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class handout, 60° at A: P) Join A and D.  Q) An arc from A cuts ray AB at C.  R) Same radius: an arc from C cuts the first arc at D. The right order is…","o":["Q, R, P","Q, P, R","R, Q, P","P, Q, R"],"a":0,"hint":"You can't join A to D until D exists.","why":"First the arc from A gives C, then the arc from C gives D, then join AD: ∠DAB = 60°."},{"c":"hd-15","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class handout: how many bisections turn a 60° angle into 15°?","o":["4","3","1","2"],"a":3,"hint":"60° → ? → 15°","why":"Bisect 60° to get 30°, then bisect 30° to get 15°: two bisections."},{"c":"hd-30","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Class handout: to construct 30°, you first construct…","o":["90°, then bisect it","120°, then bisect it","60°, then bisect it","45°, then bisect it"],"a":2,"hint":"Half of what is 30°?","why":"60° ÷ 2 = 30°. (Bisecting 90° gives 45°, and bisecting 120° gives 60°.)"},{"c":"hd-same-width","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"The handout keeps saying ‘without changing the compass width’. Why does it matter?","o":["So the crossing point is equally far from both centres","So the pencil tip doesn't break while the arcs are drawn","So the arcs look neat and tidy in your notebook","Because a compass can't be changed once it is opened"],"a":0,"hint":"Equal radius → equal distance.","why":"Arcs with the same radius from two centres meet at a point that is equally far from both. That is what makes the construction exact."}],"z-eng":[{"c":"finite-verbs","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A finite verb changes with its subject or tense. Which is the finite verb in ‘{Z} likes to edit videos using his laptop’?","o":["to edit","using","likes","laptop"],"a":2,"hint":"Swap ‘{Z}’ for ‘The boys’. Which word has to change?","why":"‘Likes’ is finite: it changes with the subject (the boys like) and the tense (liked). ‘To edit’ and ‘using’ never change, so they are non-finite."},{"c":"finite-verbs","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Which of these is NOT a complete sentence, because it has no finite verb?","o":["We won the match.","Winning feels great.","To win the match.","They want to win."],"a":2,"hint":"A complete sentence needs a verb that shows tense. Which one has only a ‘to’ verb?","why":"‘To win the match’ has only an infinitive and no finite verb, so it isn’t a full sentence. The others have finite verbs: won, feels, want."},{"c":"finite-verbs","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"In ‘She wanted to sing’, is ‘wanted’ finite or non-finite? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Non-finite — it ends in -ed","Finite — it shows past tense","Non-finite — ‘to sing’ follows it","Finite — all verbs are finite"],"a":1,"hint":"Ask: does this verb tell you WHEN the action happened?","why":"‘Wanted’ shows past tense (wants → wanted), so it’s finite. An -ed ending doesn’t make a verb non-finite. Here, ‘to sing’ is the non-finite verb."},{"c":"finite-verbs","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which verb form can NEVER be the finite verb of a sentence on its own?","o":["runs","ran","run","to run"],"a":3,"hint":"Try each one after ‘I’ or ‘He’. Which one can’t make a sentence?","why":"‘I run’, ‘He runs’ and ‘He ran’ are full sentences, so those are finite. ‘I to run’ isn’t! An infinitive (‘to’ + verb) is always non-finite."},{"c":"infinitives","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which of these is an infinitive?","o":["to the market","into the box","to finish","too tired"],"a":2,"hint":"An infinitive is ‘to’ + the base form of a VERB — not ‘to’ + a place or thing.","why":"‘To finish’ = to + verb, so it’s an infinitive. In ‘to the market’, ‘to’ is a preposition before a noun — a sneaky trap!"},{"c":"infinitives","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which sentence uses the infinitive WRONGLY?","o":["I want to learn coding.","We decided to leave early.","He forgot to bring his bat.","She hopes to wins the prize."],"a":3,"hint":"After ‘to’, an infinitive always takes the plain base form of the verb.","why":"An infinitive is ‘to’ + base form: ‘to win’, never ‘to wins’. The -s ending belongs to finite verbs, as in ‘she wins’."},{"c":"infinitives","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{Z} says, ‘I opened my laptop ___ my new video.’ Which choice shows WHY he opened it?","o":["to edit","edited","edits","editing"],"a":0,"hint":"To show the purpose of an action, we often use ‘to’ + verb.","why":"‘To edit’ is an infinitive of purpose — it tells WHY he opened the laptop. ‘Edits’, ‘edited’ and ‘editing’ don’t fit the gap or show purpose."},{"c":"infinitives","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"In ‘Dad made us plant ten saplings’, is ‘plant’ a finite verb? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — it shows the present tense","No — it’s an infinitive without ‘to’","Yes — it agrees with ‘us’","No — it is a gerund"],"a":1,"hint":"Change ‘made’ to ‘makes’. Does ‘plant’ change too?","why":"‘Made’ is the finite verb — it carries the tense. ‘Plant’ never changes (makes us plant, made us plant): it’s a bare infinitive, an infinitive without ‘to’."},{"c":"gerunds","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In ‘Swimming keeps you fit’, the word ‘swimming’ is a…","o":["Gerund","Finite verb","Present participle","Infinitive"],"a":0,"hint":"Swap it for a noun like ‘Football’. Does the sentence still make sense?","why":"‘Swimming’ is the subject — it names an activity, just like a noun. An -ing verb doing a noun’s job is a gerund. ‘Keeps’ is the finite verb here."},{"c":"gerunds","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"One of these sentences uses the wrong verb form after a preposition. Which one?","o":["She is fond of reading.","Thanks for helping me.","He is good at draw.","I’m tired of waiting."],"a":2,"hint":"After a preposition like ‘at’, ‘of’ or ‘for’, a verb takes its -ing form.","why":"After a preposition, use a gerund: ‘good at drawing’. The others already do: fond of reading, thanks for helping, tired of waiting."},{"c":"gerunds","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{Z} says, ‘I really enjoy ___ YouTube edits.’ Pick the correct form.","o":["make","to make","made","making"],"a":3,"hint":"Some verbs, like enjoy, finish and avoid, are followed by an -ing form.","why":"‘Enjoy’ takes a gerund: ‘I enjoy making edits’. ‘I enjoy to make’ is a common slip. The same goes for ‘finish making’ and ‘avoid making’."},{"c":"participles","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In ‘{Z} fixed the broken chair’, the word ‘broken’ is a…","o":["Past participle used as an adjective","Finite verb in the past tense","Gerund used as a noun","Infinitive without ‘to’"],"a":0,"hint":"Does ‘broken’ tell us what {Z} did, or what kind of chair it was?","why":"‘Broken’ is the past participle of ‘break’, describing the chair like an adjective. ‘Fixed’ is the finite verb — it shows past tense and goes with ‘{Z}’."},{"c":"participles","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which word is a participle in ‘Hearing the bell, the students rushed out’?","o":["rushed","Hearing","bell","out"],"a":1,"hint":"One verb tells what the students did, with a tense. The other describes them, with no tense.","why":"‘Hearing’ is a present participle — it describes the students and shows no tense. ‘Rushed’ is the finite verb: past tense, matching ‘the students’."},{"c":"participles","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"In ‘The tired players rested’, is ‘tired’ a participle or a finite verb? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Finite — it ends in -ed like a past verb","Finite — it tells what the players did","Participle — it comes before ‘rested’","Participle — it describes the players"],"a":3,"hint":"What did the players DO? That’s the finite verb. What KIND of players were they?","why":"‘Tired’ describes the players, like an adjective, so it’s a past participle. ‘Rested’ is the finite verb — it tells what they did and shows past tense."},{"c":"participles","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which phrase uses the wrong verb form as a describing word?","o":["a stolen bike","a frozen lake","a wrote letter","a fallen leaf"],"a":2,"hint":"Say each verb’s three forms, like speak – spoke – spoken. Which phrase uses the past form?","why":"The past participle of ‘write’ is ‘written’, so it’s ‘a written letter’. ‘Wrote’ is the simple past — a finite form. Stolen, frozen and fallen are all correct."},{"c":"gerund-vs-participle","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"In which sentence is the -ing word NOT a gerund?","o":["Reading helps you think.","I love dancing.","She is good at singing.","The crying baby woke up."],"a":3,"hint":"A gerund names an activity, like a noun. Which -ing word describes a noun instead?","why":"‘Crying’ describes the baby, like an adjective, so it’s a present participle. Reading, dancing and singing name activities, so they are gerunds."},{"c":"gerund-vs-participle","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"‘{Z} loves editing.’ Is ‘editing’ a gerund or a participle? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Participle — it ends in -ing","Gerund — it names what he loves","Participle — it describes {Z}","Gerund — it shows present tense"],"a":1,"hint":"Is the -ing word naming the thing he loves, or describing someone?","why":"‘Editing’ names what {Z} loves — it works like a noun (the object of ‘loves’), so it’s a gerund. An -ing ending alone doesn’t decide it; the job the word does decides it."},{"c":"gerund-vs-participle","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In ‘The dancing girl loves singing’, which word is the GERUND?","o":["dancing","loves","girl","singing"],"a":3,"hint":"One -ing word describes the girl. The other names something she loves.","why":"‘Singing’ names what she loves, like a noun, so it’s the gerund. ‘Dancing’ describes the girl, so it’s a present participle. ‘Loves’ is the finite verb."},{"c":"sva-basics","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Pick the correct verb: ‘{Z} and {R} ___ to the same school.’","o":["go","goes","going","gone"],"a":0,"hint":"Two people joined by ‘and’ make a plural subject.","why":"‘{Z} and {R}’ is a plural subject, so the verb is ‘go’. ‘Goes’ is for one person (‘{Z} goes’). ‘Going’ and ‘gone’ can’t be the verb on their own."},{"c":"sva-basics","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{R} writes in his shark notebook: ‘Sharks has rows of sharp teeth.’ What is the correct fix?","o":["Sharks → Shark’s","has → have","rows → row","sharp → sharper"],"a":1,"hint":"Is ‘Sharks’ one shark or many? Which verb matches?","why":"‘Sharks’ is plural, so the verb is ‘have’: ‘Sharks have rows of sharp teeth.’ ‘Has’ goes with one: ‘A shark has rows of sharp teeth.’"},{"c":"sva-basics","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which sentence has a subject–verb agreement error?","o":["She doesn’t like spinach.","He doesn’t eat fish.","They doesn’t play chess.","It doesn’t look easy."],"a":2,"hint":"‘Does’ goes with he, she and it. Which subject is plural?","why":"‘They’ is plural, so it takes ‘don’t’: ‘They don’t play chess.’ ‘Doesn’t’ fits singular subjects like he, she and it."},{"c":"sva-tricky","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Pick the correct verb: ‘The box of chocolates ___ on the table.’","o":["are","is","lie","were"],"a":1,"hint":"Find the real subject. Is it the box, or the chocolates?","why":"The subject is ‘box’ — just one — so the verb is ‘is’. ‘Of chocolates’ only describes the box. Don’t let the nearest noun fool you!"},{"c":"sva-tricky","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Pick the correct verb: ‘Each of the players ___ a new jersey.’","o":["have","has","are having","were having"],"a":1,"hint":"‘Each’ means every single one, taken one at a time.","why":"‘Each’ is singular, so it takes ‘has’. ‘Of the players’ is plural, but it isn’t the subject — ‘each’ is!"},{"c":"sva-tricky","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"‘Neither {Z} nor his friends ___ late.’ Pick the verb AND the rule.","o":["was — ‘neither’ is always singular","was — it agrees with ‘{Z}’","were — ‘nor’ always makes it plural","were — it agrees with ‘friends’"],"a":3,"hint":"With either…or and neither…nor, look at the subject closest to the verb.","why":"With neither…nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject. ‘Friends’ is closest and plural, so ‘were’. Flip it: ‘Neither his friends nor {Z} was late.’"},{"c":"sva-tricky","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Pick the correct verb: ‘Everyone in {R}’s class ___ his shark facts.’","o":["loves","love","are loving","have loved"],"a":0,"hint":"Words like everyone, somebody and nobody are singular, even when they mean many people.","why":"‘Everyone’ is singular, so it takes ‘loves’ — just like ‘he loves’. It feels like many people, but grammar counts it as one."},{"c":"sva-tricky","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which sentence has an agreement error hidden behind an extra phrase?","o":["One of my best friends lives in Pune.","The keys to the old cupboard are lost.","Arjun, along with his sisters, are coming.","Our cricket team is practising today."],"a":2,"hint":"‘Along with’ and ‘as well as’ don’t join subjects the way ‘and’ does.","why":"The subject is ‘Arjun’ — one person. ‘Along with his sisters’ is just extra information, so: ‘Arjun, along with his sisters, is coming.’"},{"c":"editing","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Editing — one word is wrong. Pick the fix: ‘Yesterday, {R} go to the aquarium with Dad.’","o":["go → went","to → at","the → a","with → by"],"a":0,"hint":"The word ‘Yesterday’ tells you when this happened.","why":"‘Yesterday’ puts the action in the past, so ‘go’ becomes ‘went’: ‘Yesterday, {R} went to the aquarium with Dad.’"},{"c":"editing","t":"Spot the mistake","p":50,"q":"Editing — pick the correct fix: ‘{Z} bought a umbrella before the monsoon.’","o":["bought → buyed","a → an","before → after","the → a"],"a":1,"hint":"Say ‘umbrella’ aloud. Does it begin with a vowel sound?","why":"‘Umbrella’ begins with a vowel sound, so it takes ‘an’: ‘an umbrella’. ‘Bought’ is already the right past tense of ‘buy’ — ‘buyed’ isn’t a word!"},{"c":"editing","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{Z}’s video post says: ‘New video out on 6 pm today!’ Which fix makes it correct?","o":["on → at","out → in","today → tomorrow","New → A new"],"a":0,"hint":"Which little word goes with an exact clock time?","why":"We use ‘at’ for clock times (at 6 pm, at noon) and ‘on’ for days and dates (on Monday, on 25 November). So: ‘New video out at 6 pm today!’"},{"c":"editing","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Editing — pick the correct fix: ‘She is interested in learn coding.’","o":["is → are","in → on","learn → to learn","learn → learning"],"a":3,"hint":"After a preposition like ‘in’, which form does a verb take?","why":"After the preposition ‘in’, we need a gerund: ‘interested in learning coding’. ‘In to learn’ doesn’t work — the ‘in’ would have to go."},{"c":"editing","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Editing — pick the correct fix: ‘{R} and me went to the beach on Sunday.’","o":["me → I","went → gone","the → a","on → in"],"a":0,"hint":"Drop ‘{R} and’. Would you say ‘me went’ or ‘I went’?","why":"‘{R} and I’ is the subject, so use ‘I’. Test it: drop ‘{R} and’ — ‘I went to the beach’ works, but ‘me went’ doesn’t!"},{"c":"editing-verb-forms","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Editing — pick the correct fix: ‘The list of winners were put up on the notice board.’","o":["winners → winner","put → putted","were → was","on → in"],"a":2,"hint":"Find the real subject. Is it the list, or the winners?","why":"The subject is ‘list’ — just one — so use ‘was’: ‘The list of winners was put up on the notice board.’ ‘Of winners’ only tells us which list."},{"c":"editing-verb-forms","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Editing — pick the correct fix: ‘The coach let us to play one more over.’","o":["let → lets","us → we","over → overs","to play → play"],"a":3,"hint":"Verbs like ‘let’ and ‘make’ are followed by a special kind of infinitive. Which kind?","why":"After ‘let’ and ‘make’, use the infinitive without ‘to’: ‘The coach let us play one more over.’ Just like ‘Dad made us plant ten saplings’."},{"c":"sva-tricky","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Pick the correct verb: ‘Either the boys or Dad ___ the balcony plants every evening.’","o":["water","waters","are watering","have watered"],"a":1,"hint":"With either…or, find the subject closest to the gap. Is it one person or many?","why":"With either…or, the verb agrees with the nearer subject. ‘Dad’ is nearer and singular, so ‘waters’. Flip it: ‘Either Dad or the boys water the plants.’"},{"c":"editing-verb-forms","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Editing — pick the correct fix: ‘{Z} finished to edit his video before dinner.’","o":["to edit → editing","finished → finish","his → him","before → since"],"a":0,"hint":"Which comes after ‘finish’: ‘to’ + verb, or a verb ending in -ing?","why":"‘Finish’ is followed by a gerund, not ‘to’ + verb: ‘{Z} finished editing his video before dinner.’ Same with ‘enjoy making’ and ‘avoid wasting’."}],"z-farm":[{"c":"agriculture-basics","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The word ‘agriculture’ comes from Latin. What do ‘agri’ and ‘culture’ mean?","o":["Farmer + to harvest","Field + to cultivate","Soil + to water","Seed + to grow"],"a":1,"hint":"‘Agri’ is the place where crops grow; ‘culture’ is what a farmer does there.","why":"In Latin, agri means field and culture means to cultivate. Broadly, agriculture covers farming, animal husbandry (raising livestock), forestry and horticulture."},{"c":"agriculture-basics","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A farmer keeps bees for honey and also rears fish in a pond. Which two words name these activities, in that order?","o":["Horticulture and apiculture","Pisciculture and horticulture","Apiculture and pisciculture","Animal husbandry and forestry"],"a":2,"hint":"One word comes from the Latin for bee, the other from the Latin for fish (think of Pisces).","why":"Apiculture is beekeeping and pisciculture is fish rearing. Both count as ‘agriculture and allied activities’, along with livestock, silkworm rearing and fibre crops like cotton."},{"c":"agriculture-basics","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Kabir always draws a ‘farmer’ as a man. Which fact from the chapter shows his picture is incomplete?","o":["Women only sell crops at the market","Farm work is now done only by machines","Women help only at harvest time","Over 75% of rural farm workers are women"],"a":3,"hint":"Think about who does most of the sowing, harvesting and threshing in rural India.","why":"In rural India, more than 75% of people working in agriculture are women (2025). They do most of the farming work — from sowing to harvesting and threshing."},{"c":"farming-history","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What were the staple crops of the Harappans?","o":["Barley and wheat","Rice and maize","Tea and coffee","Sugarcane and cotton"],"a":0,"hint":"In the Vedas, these two are called ‘yava’ and ‘godhūma’.","why":"Barley and wheat were the Harappans' staple crops. Several Harappan sites also show rice by the 3rd millennium BCE in some regions, plus millets and vegetables."},{"c":"farming-history","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"At Kalibangan (c. 2800 BCE), archaeologists found two sets of furrows crossing at right angles. What does this show?","o":["Crop rotation — one crop each season","Terrace farming on a hill slope","Drip irrigation using clay pipes","Intercropping — two crops grown together"],"a":3,"hint":"Furrows are lines cut for sowing seeds. Why would one field need two sets of them, crossing each other?","why":"The perpendicular furrows show intercropping — growing two or more crops at the same time. The same practice was still seen in the 1960s, around 4,800 years later!"},{"c":"farming-history","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Asha: “Rice grains from the 7th–8th millennium BCE in the Ganga Plain prove rice farming was well organised then.” What's the catch?","o":["No rice was ever found in the Ganga Plain","Rice reached India only in Mughal times","Grains alone don't prove systematic farming","Those grains were really wheat"],"a":2,"hint":"Finding a few grains is one thing; mastering a whole farming method is another.","why":"Experts point out that the early rice grains need not mean rice was already grown systematically — mastering it may have taken a few more millennia."},{"c":"monsoon-climate","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which monsoon brings rain from June to September and is crucial for kharif crops in northern and central India?","o":["Northeast monsoon","Southwest monsoon","Both monsoons equally","Neither — kharif needs no rain"],"a":1,"hint":"It's the one that arrives first, at the start of the summer rains.","why":"The southwest monsoon (June–September) is crucial for kharif crops in northern and central India. The northeast monsoon (October–December) brings rain to the east and south."},{"c":"monsoon-climate","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Tamil Nadu, West Bengal and Andhra Pradesh grow rice all year round. What makes this possible?","o":["Water from the monsoon plus irrigation","Rice there needs no water at all","Greenhouses cover every rice field","Only melting Himalayan snow"],"a":0,"hint":"The chapter names two sources of water working together.","why":"These states get water from the monsoon as well as from irrigation, so rice can grow all year. The Coromandel plains of Tamil Nadu even get rain from both monsoons!"},{"c":"monsoon-climate","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if the monsoon arrived very late this year? Which crops would be in the most trouble?","o":["Rabi — they need the heaviest rain","Zaid — they are sown in the monsoon","Kharif — they depend on a timely monsoon","None — monsoon timing never matters"],"a":2,"hint":"Which season does the chapter's crop calendar call the ‘monsoon crops’?","why":"Kharif crops like rice, maize and cotton depend on the timely arrival of the monsoon. Rabi crops grow in the cool season with less water, and zaid crops in summer."},{"c":"crop-seasons","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Three of these are rabi (winter) crops. Which one is the odd one out?","o":["Wheat","Mustard","Rice","Gram"],"a":2,"hint":"Rabi crops like it cool with less water. One of these loves hot, heavy rain.","why":"Wheat, mustard and gram are rabi crops of the cool season. Rice is a kharif crop that grows in the hot, rainy monsoon season."},{"c":"crop-seasons","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Watermelon, cucumber and muskmelon, grown in the summer season, are called…","o":["Kharif crops","Rabi crops","Kedāra crops","Zaid crops"],"a":3,"hint":"Kharif means monsoon crops and rabi means winter crops. What's the summer one?","why":"Zaid crops like watermelon, cucumber, muskmelon and pumpkin grow in summer. Kedāra was an old name for wet crops, used before the Arabic terms (from Mughal times) spread."},{"c":"crop-seasons","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"A farmer plans to sow wheat in June, just as the monsoon starts. What's the mistake?","o":["Nothing — wheat is a monsoon crop","Wheat is a summer (zaid) crop","Wheat can't grow in India at all","Wheat is a winter (rabi) crop"],"a":3,"hint":"Check the crop calendar: is wheat grown in the monsoon or in the cool season?","why":"Wheat is a rabi crop: sown around October–November, grown in the cool season with less water, and harvested in spring. June, when the monsoon begins, is kharif sowing time."},{"c":"crop-seasons","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Kerala grows lots of rice; Punjab grows mostly wheat. What if they swapped crops? Pick the likely result AND the reason.","o":["Same harvests — climate doesn't matter","Poorer harvests — climate wouldn't suit them","Bigger harvests — every crop grows anywhere","Both states would grow only millets"],"a":1,"hint":"Rice is a ‘hot, heavy rainfall’ crop; wheat likes it ‘cool, less water’. Which fits which state?","why":"Crops depend on climate, soil and water. Rice suits rainy Kerala, which gets both monsoons; wheat, a rabi crop, suits Punjab's cool winters. Swapped, each would be a poor fit."},{"c":"soils","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which soil forms from silt carried down by rivers and is rich in nutrients?","o":["Laterite soil","Alpine soil","Desert soil","Alluvial soil"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of the wide plains of the Ganga, where rivers drop mud and fine particles.","why":"Alluvial soil forms from silt deposits (mud, sand and other fine particles) that rivers bring from the mountains and plateaus. It is rich in nutrients."},{"c":"soils","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Is laterite soil fertile? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["No — rain washes most minerals away","Yes — it is rich river silt","Yes — it holds lots of moisture","No — it is made of ice crystals"],"a":0,"hint":"Laterite is also called ‘brick earth’. Think about what heavy rain does to the rock it forms from.","why":"Laterite forms when rain weathers rock and washes most minerals away, leaving mostly iron and aluminium — so it's not fertile. River silt makes alluvial soil; black soil holds moisture."},{"c":"soils","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Riya: “Soil is just crushed rock — nothing else.” What is she leaving out?","o":["Decayed plant and animal matter","Melted ice from glaciers","Salt from the sea","Nothing — soil is only rock"],"a":0,"hint":"Look at the top layers in the soil-formation diagram: humus and topsoil. Where does humus come from?","why":"Soil forms from the breakdown of rocks AND the decay of organic matter. Humus — dark matter from decomposed plants and animals — is rich in nutrients and holds moisture."},{"c":"soils","t":"Connect","p":50,"q":"Your family's farm grows areca nut. In the chapter's soil–crop map, areca, coffee and cashew are listed for which soil area?","o":["Alluvial soil","Laterite soil","Black soil","Red soil"],"a":1,"hint":"It's the soil nicknamed ‘brick earth’.","why":"Fig. 1.10 lists areca nut, coconut, coffee, rubber, cashew and tapioca in the laterite soil area. Laterite, or brick earth, forms when rain weathers rock."},{"c":"soil-care","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Ploughing along the natural curves of a hill slope, so rainwater doesn't wash soil away, is called…","o":["Crop rotation","Mulching","Terracing","Contour ploughing"],"a":3,"hint":"Map lines that join points of equal height on a hill are called contours.","why":"Contour ploughing follows the natural curves of a slope, so rainwater can't rush straight down and carry soil away. Terracing instead cuts flat steps into the hillside."},{"c":"soil-care","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Why do farmers grow different crops in the same field in different seasons? Pick the method AND its purpose.","o":["Crop rotation — so soil doesn't lose nutrients","Contour ploughing — to stop soil erosion","Intercropping — two crops at the same time","Crop rotation — so crops grow taller at once"],"a":0,"hint":"Each crop uses up particular nutrients. What if the same crop grew there every season?","why":"Crop rotation means growing different crops in the same field in different seasons, so the soil doesn't keep losing the same specific nutrients."},{"c":"soil-care","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"On your family's farm, pepper vines climb the areca palms in the same plot, both growing at once. Which practice is this?","o":["Crop rotation","Contour ploughing","Intercropping","Hydroponics"],"a":2,"hint":"Is it about changing crops season by season, or growing them side by side?","why":"Intercropping means growing two or more different crops at the same time — like pepper on areca palms, or sugarcane with pumpkin in the chapter. Crop rotation swaps crops by season."},{"c":"soil-care","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if pests wiped out one crop in a field where several crops grow together? Why might the farmer still be okay?","o":["Pests never attack mixed fields","At least one other crop would survive","Crop rotation brings it back to life","Mixed fields need no harvest at all"],"a":1,"hint":"Our ancestors didn't put all their eggs in one basket.","why":"Multiple cropping — growing several crops in one field — reduced the risk of pests and ensured at least one crop, with harvests coming at different times."},{"c":"irrigation","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which irrigation method sprays water into the air so it falls on crops like artificial rain?","o":["Drip irrigation","Phad system","Bamboo drip irrigation","Sprinkler irrigation"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of the spinning water-sprayers that keep a cricket ground green.","why":"Sprinkler irrigation sprays water into the air to fall like artificial rain. Drip irrigation instead delivers water slowly and directly to the plants' roots."},{"c":"irrigation","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Groundwater is falling fast in many parts of India. For your family's areca palms, which method waters them with the least waste?","o":["Flooding the whole plantation daily","Spraying the leaves with a hose at noon","Letting water run along open channels","Drip pipes at each palm's roots"],"a":3,"hint":"Which method sends water only where the palm actually drinks it?","why":"Drip irrigation delivers water slowly and directly to the roots through tubes and emitters, so very little is wasted — important as groundwater reserves fall fast."},{"c":"irrigation","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Phad systems, found especially in Maharashtra, are…","o":["Community canals that divert river water","Bamboo pipes carrying spring water","Sprinklers that spray artificial rain","Pipes that drip water at the roots"],"a":0,"hint":"Two of these are modern methods, and one belongs to northeast India.","why":"Phad systems are community-based irrigation systems, mainly in Maharashtra, using small canals to divert river water to fields. Bamboo pipes from springs are used in northeast India."},{"c":"irrigation","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A farmer in a dry area with an unreliable monsoon has no canal water. Which adaptation does the chapter describe?","o":["Grow more water-hungry crops","Choose drought-resistant crops","Sow only when the river floods","Spray extra chemical fertiliser"],"a":1,"hint":"Think about which kind of plant could survive a dry year.","why":"Rain-fed farmers face droughts and unpredictable monsoons. They adapt by choosing drought-resistant crops or using traditional water-harvesting techniques."},{"c":"seeds","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"ICAR promotes ‘beejamrit’, a paste of cow dung, urine and other materials. What is it used for?","o":["Coating seeds before planting","Killing pests on fruit trees","Feeding cattle in winter","Sealing leaks in irrigation pipes"],"a":0,"hint":"Split the name into two Hindi words: ‘beej’ (बीज) + ‘amrit’. What does ‘beej’ mean?","why":"Beejamrit (beej = seed) coats seeds before planting: it protects them from being spoilt and reduces germination time. The Arthaśhāstra also advised smearing cotton seeds with cow dung!"},{"c":"seeds","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Ravi: “Company seeds gave me a big harvest, so I'll save some of its seeds to plant next season.” What's the catch?","o":["Company seeds never germinate at all","Saved seeds always give a bigger crop","Their new seeds often aren't fit to replant","Company seeds can only be sown in winter"],"a":2,"hint":"The chapter says this creates a ‘dependency’ of farmers on seed companies. Why?","why":"Company seeds give higher yields and resist pests, but the seeds their plants produce are often unsuitable for the next season. So farmers must buy again — a dependency critics worry about."},{"c":"seeds","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The ancient Indian seed drill did three jobs in one go. Which three?","o":["Plough, water and harvest the crop","Sow, spray pesticide and thresh","Prepare soil, plant seeds, close furrow","Dig, fence and weed the field"],"a":2,"hint":"A seed drill works at sowing time, so all three of its jobs happen before the crop has grown.","why":"The Indian seed drill combined soil preparation, seed planting and closing the furrow in one operation — cutting the labour of a three-step job. Clever ancient engineering!"},{"c":"traditional-farming","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Kulāgar is a homestead farming system common in Goa's Konkan plains. Its Konkani name means…","o":["Cow + plough","Field + water","Seed + season","Family + storehouse"],"a":3,"hint":"It's a homestead system, with many kinds of crops grown all around the house.","why":"In Konkani, ‘kula’ means family and ‘āgar’ means storehouse. Families grow food crops, cash crops, fruits, vegetables, spices and medicinal plants around their homes."},{"c":"traditional-farming","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"In traditional farming, how do plants and tiny soil organisms help each other?","o":["Organisms eat the roots and harm the plant","Bacteria make nutrients usable; roots feed them","Plants and organisms fight over water","Organisms only live in chemical fertiliser"],"a":1,"hint":"It's a two-way deal: one side readies food for the plant, the other sends energy back.","why":"Bacteria, fungi and other soil organisms turn soil nutrients into forms plants can easily take up. In return, plants supply them energy through their roots — a real partnership!"},{"c":"traditional-farming","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if farmers on a steep Uttarakhand hillside skipped terraces and farmed the bare slope? What's the biggest risk?","o":["Rain would wash the soil downhill","The crops would grow twice as fast","The soil would turn into alluvium","Nothing — slopes never matter"],"a":0,"hint":"Terraces are flat steps. What do flat steps do to rainwater rushing down?","why":"Terraces create flat land on hillsides, which slows water runoff, prevents soil erosion and conserves water. On a bare slope, rain would carry the soil away."},{"c":"green-revolution","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Which of these did the Green Revolution of the 1960s–70s NOT bring?","o":["High-yielding variety (HYV) seeds","Chemical fertilisers and pesticides","A ban on chemical pesticides","Mechanised farm equipment"],"a":2,"hint":"One of these is the opposite of what the Green Revolution did. Sikkim did it much later.","why":"The Green Revolution brought HYV seeds, more irrigation, chemical fertilisers and pesticides, and machines. Banning chemicals is what Sikkim did in 2014 to go fully organic."},{"c":"green-revolution","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Did the Green Revolution change India's food supply? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — India became self-sufficient in food","No — food grain output stayed the same","Yes — it made every farm fully organic","No — it only produced cotton and jute"],"a":0,"hint":"Think of the huge jump in wheat and rice production.","why":"HYV seeds, irrigation, fertilisers and machines hugely raised food grain output, especially wheat and rice, and India became self-sufficient in food. M.S. Swaminathan was its architect."},{"c":"green-revolution","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Neha: “The Green Revolution had only good effects.” Which long-term problem proves her wrong?","o":["Too few tractors on farms","Farmers grew too little wheat","Groundwater depletion and polluted soil","Seeds became far too cheap"],"a":2,"hint":"Over decades, heavy use of chemicals and water began to catch up with the land.","why":"Over time its methods led to poorer soil, depleted groundwater, soil and water contaminated by pesticides and fertilisers, and harm to human and animal health."},{"c":"sustainable-farming","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which became the world's first 100% organic state, banning chemical fertilisers and pesticides in 2014?","o":["Punjab","Kerala","Goa","Sikkim"],"a":3,"hint":"This Himalayan state was visited in Part 1 of your textbook.","why":"Sikkim is the world's first 100% organic state. In 2014 it banned chemical fertilisers and pesticides and focused on natural farming practices."},{"c":"sustainable-farming","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A farmer switching to organic farming stops buying chemical fertiliser. What should she feed the soil with instead?","o":["Extra chemical pesticides","Compost, seed cake and animal manure","Nothing — organic soil needs no food","Only more water"],"a":1,"hint":"Think of what's left after oil is squeezed from seeds, and what cows leave behind.","why":"Organic farming relies on natural fertilisers: compost, seed cake (what's left after oil is extracted from seeds) and animal manure. It also avoids chemical pesticides."},{"c":"sustainable-farming","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Comparing traditional and modern farming, which statement matches the chapter's ‘trade-off’?","o":["Modern: more food; traditional: more sustainable","Traditional: more food; modern: more sustainable","Both give the same food and the same harm","Modern: no harm at all; traditional: no food"],"a":0,"hint":"One side wins on quantity, the other on caring for Nature over the long run.","why":"Modern methods produce lots of food quickly but have long-term harmful effects; traditional ones work with Nature and are more resilient but may yield less. The aim: balance both."},{"c":"challenges-and-support","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Are India's farms getting bigger or smaller over time? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Bigger — machines need large fields","Smaller — land is split among family","Bigger — the government adds land","Smaller — rivers wash farmland away"],"a":1,"hint":"What happens to a farm when it's passed down to several children, again and again?","why":"Land gets divided among family members generation after generation. Today the average landholding is about three-fourths of a hectare — roughly an average football field."},{"c":"challenges-and-support","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Why does the chapter call climate change one of the biggest challenges for farmers?","o":["It makes every winter cooler for rabi crops","It stops the monsoon from ever coming","Untimely rains and extreme weather ruin crops","It turns all soil into alluvial soil"],"a":2,"hint":"Think of rain arriving at the wrong time, or a severe drought.","why":"Weather is getting more unpredictable (like untimely rains), with more extreme events such as severe droughts or heavy rainfall — so the risk of crops being destroyed rises."},{"c":"challenges-and-support","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana helps farmers mainly through…","o":["Free tractors for every farmer","Crop insurance","Free land for new farmers","Training on seed drills"],"a":1,"hint":"Translate the Hindi words in the name: what do ‘fasal’ (फ़सल) and ‘bima’ (बीमा) mean?","why":"It's a crop insurance scheme (fasal = crop, bima = insurance) giving farmers financial help. The government also buys important crops so farmers get fair prices."}],"z-hindi":[{"c":"bimar-kahani","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"“नहीं होना बीमार” में बच्चे ने बीमारी का बहाना असल में क्यों बनाया?","o":["उसे सचमुच तेज़ बुखार था","होमवर्क नहीं किया था, सज़ा का डर था","नानीजी ने स्कूल जाने से मना किया","उस दिन स्कूल में छुट्टी थी"],"a":1,"hint":"What had he NOT finished for school that day?","why":"उसने होमवर्क नहीं किया था, स्कूल जाता तो सज़ा मिलती। He wasn’t really ill — Nanaji checked and found no fever!"},{"c":"bimar-kahani","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"अस्पताल में बच्चे ने सोचा, “क्या ठाठ हैं बीमारों के भी!” उसे ऐसा क्यों लगा?","o":["बीमार साफ़ बिस्तर पर लेटकर खीर खाते हैं","बीमारों को ढेर सारे खिलौने मिलते हैं","बीमारों को कड़वी दवा पीनी पड़ती है","बीमार लोग दिन भर टीवी देखते हैं"],"a":0,"hint":"Remember the hospital ward, and what Naniji fed Sudhakar Kaka.","why":"ठाठ = luxury. अस्पताल में काका साफ़-सुथरे बिस्तर पर लेटे थे और खीर खा रहे थे — so the boy thought being sick was a treat!"},{"c":"bimar-kahani","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"नानाजी ने बच्चे को दिन भर खाना क्यों नहीं दिया?","o":["वे बच्चे से बहुत नाराज़ थे","घर में उस दिन खाना नहीं बना","उनके हिसाब से भूखे रहने से विकार निकलते हैं","डॉक्टर ने खाना मना किया था"],"a":2,"hint":"Nanaji believed in an old home remedy for anyone feeling unwell.","why":"नानाजी मानते थे — तबियत ढीली हो तो भूखे रहना सबसे अच्छा उपाय है, इससे विकार (the body’s bad stuff) निकलते हैं। Not anger — just his old home remedy for feeling unwell!"},{"c":"bimar-kahani","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"“लेटे-लेटे पीठ दुखने लगी।” — इससे बच्चे के बारे में क्या पता चलता है?","o":["उसे सचमुच कोई बीमारी थी","वह आराम का बहुत मज़ा ले रहा था","नानाजी ने उसे चोट पहुँचाई थी","वह बिस्तर पर पड़े-पड़े ऊब गया था"],"a":3,"hint":"Was he really sick? So why would lying in bed all day make his back hurt?","why":"बच्चा बीमार नहीं था — दिन भर बिस्तर पर पड़े-पड़े वह ऊब गया (got bored) और पीठ दुखने लगी। The fake illness turned into real boredom!"},{"c":"bimar-kahani","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"कहानी के अंत में बच्चे ने फिर कभी बीमारी का बहाना नहीं बनाया। क्यों?","o":["बहाने से वह दिन भर अकेला और भूखा रहा","नानाजी ने उसे बहुत डाँटा था","स्कूल में उसे कड़ी सज़ा मिली","अगले दिन उसे सच में बुखार हो गया"],"a":0,"hint":"Think about how his ‘sick day’ really went — food? friends? fun?","why":"बहाने का नतीजा: न खाना, न खेल, बस अकेलापन और भूख! Nobody even scolded him — the fake sick day itself taught him the lesson."},{"c":"bimar-shabd","t":"Hindi","p":100,"q":"बच्चा “दबे पाँव” दरवाज़े तक गया। इसका मतलब क्या है?","o":["लंगड़ाते हुए, धीरे-धीरे","तेज़ी से दौड़ते हुए","पाँव पटकते हुए, गुस्से में","बिना आवाज़ किए, चुपके से"],"a":3,"hint":"He didn’t want anyone to know he had got out of bed.","why":"दबे पाँव = tiptoeing, without a sound. Careful: पाँव पटकना (stamping feet) is the opposite — he did THAT on his way back, angry!"},{"c":"bimar-shabd","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"जड़ी-बूटियाँ उबालकर बना पेय, जो सर्दी-खाँसी में पीते हैं — उसे क्या कहते हैं?","o":["शरबत","लस्सी","काढ़ा","खीर"],"a":2,"hint":"Nanaji gave the boy a tea that tasted like this bitter herbal drink.","why":"काढ़ा = a drink made by boiling herbs. कहानी में बच्चे को “काढ़े जैसी चाय” पीनी पड़ी — not tasty at all!"},{"c":"bimar-shabd","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"मुन्नू को आम चूसते देखकर बच्चे के मन में कौन-सा भाव आया?","o":["करुणा (pity)","आभार (thanks)","डर (fear)","जलन (jealousy)"],"a":3,"hint":"He stamped back to bed. How do you feel when someone gets a mango and you get nothing?","why":"कहानी में: “मैं जलन, गुस्से और कुढ़न में पाँव पटकता वापस बिस्तर में आ गया।” जलन = jealousy."},{"c":"chidiya-bhav","t":"Hindi","p":100,"q":"“सब मिल-जुलकर रहते हैं वे, सब मिल-जुलकर खाते हैं” — यह पंक्ति किस भाव की ओर इशारा करती है?","o":["प्रतिस्पर्धा और संघर्ष","समानता और एकता","स्वार्थ और ईर्ष्या","डर और अकेलापन"],"a":1,"hint":"मिल-जुलकर = together. What do you get when everyone lives and eats together?","why":"पक्षी साथ रहते और साथ खाते हैं — यह समानता और एकता (equality and unity) है। Competition and selfishness are the opposite of this."},{"c":"chidiya-bhav","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"“तुम देखो हमको, फिर अपनी सोने की कड़ियाँ तोड़ो” — “सोने की कड़ियाँ” किसका प्रतीक हैं?","o":["सोने के असली गहने","श्रम और संतोष","प्रेम और स्वतंत्रता","बंधन और लालच"],"a":3,"hint":"कड़ियाँ = links of a chain. A chain made of gold is pretty — but still a chain.","why":"सोने की कड़ियाँ = golden chains: धन का लालच जो इंसान को बाँध देता है। The bird says: break these chains (बंधन) and live free!"},{"c":"chidiya-bhav","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"पिकनिक में बहुत खाना बच गया। कविता की चिड़िया क्या करती?","o":["ज़रूरत भर लेती, बाकी औरों के लिए छोड़ती","सारा खाना अपने घर ले जाती","बचा हुआ खाना फेंक देती","अगले हफ़्ते के लिए छिपा लेती"],"a":0,"hint":"Birds take only what they need. What do they do with the rest?","why":"कविता: “बच जाता जो, औरों के हित, उसे छोड़ वे देते हैं।” Birds take what they need and leave the rest for others — no hoarding, no waste!"},{"c":"chidiya-bhav","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"कविता के अनुसार इनमें से कौन-सी बात पक्षियों में नहीं पाई जाती?","o":["प्रेम-प्रीति","लोभ और पाप","मिल-जुलकर रहना","निर्भय विचरण"],"a":1,"hint":"One of these is a bad quality. The poet says birds’ hearts are free of it.","why":"कवि कहते हैं — “उनके मन में लोभ नहीं है, पाप नहीं।” लोभ = greed, पाप = wrongdoing. Love, togetherness and fearless flying they DO have."},{"c":"chidiya-shabd","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"कविता में पक्षियों की सूची है। उसमें “कपोत” किसे कहा गया है?","o":["कौआ (crow)","तोता (parrot)","कबूतर (pigeon)","मोर (peacock)"],"a":2,"hint":"This bird says गुटर-गूँ and was once used to carry messages.","why":"कपोत = कबूतर (pigeon). कविता के पक्षी: खंजन, कपोत, चातक, कोकिल, काक, हंस, शुक — old Sanskrit-style bird names!"},{"c":"chidiya-shabd","t":"Hindi","p":100,"q":"पहेली: “कूहू कूहू मधुर आवाज़ सुनाती… काली हूँ पर काक नहीं।” — मैं कौन हूँ?","o":["कोयल","कौआ","मैना","कबूतर"],"a":0,"hint":"काक = crow. Which other black bird sings a sweet कूहू कूहू?","why":"कोयल (कविता में “कोकिल”)! Black like a crow, but sings sweetly. Fun fact: the koel doesn’t build a nest — it lays its eggs in a crow’s nest!"},{"c":"chidiya-shabd","t":"Hindi","p":100,"q":"“व्यापारी ने किसान से 10 मन अनाज खरीदा।” — यहाँ “मन” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["दिल या चित्त (mind)","इच्छा (wish)","वज़न की एक इकाई","मनुष्य (man)"],"a":2,"hint":"You can’t buy 10 ‘minds’ of grain! What do traders measure grain in?","why":"यहाँ मन = an old unit of weight (about 37 kg). In “उनके मन में लोभ नहीं” it means mind. शब्द एक, अर्थ अनेक — one word, many meanings!"},{"c":"vilom","t":"विलोम","p":50,"q":"“निर्भय” का विलोम क्या है?","o":["निडर","भयभीत","साहसी","बहादुर"],"a":1,"hint":"निर्भय = without fear. Its opposite is FULL of fear.","why":"निर्भय (fearless) ↔ भयभीत (frightened). निडर, साहसी, बहादुर mean almost the SAME as निर्भय — they are पर्यायवाची, not विलोम!"},{"c":"vilom","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"किस जोड़ी में विलोम शब्द गलत है?","o":["आशा – निराशा","मान – अपमान","उन्नति – उन्नत","सुख – दुख"],"a":2,"hint":"One pair only changes the word ending — the meaning doesn’t flip.","why":"उन्नति (progress) का विलोम अवनति (decline) है। “उन्नत” means ‘advanced’ — same idea, not the opposite."},{"c":"vilom","t":"विलोम","p":50,"q":"“जो मिलता है अपने श्रम से” — “श्रम” का विलोम क्या है?","o":["मेहनत","परिश्रम","उद्यम","विश्राम"],"a":3,"hint":"श्रम = hard work. What do you need after hard work?","why":"श्रम (labour) ↔ विश्राम (rest). मेहनत, परिश्रम, उद्यम all mean hard work — synonyms, the classic trap!"},{"c":"vilom","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"“मीठा” का विलोम “कड़वा” है। कहानी में बच्चे को कौन-सी कड़वी चीज़ खानी पड़ी?","o":["नानाजी की दी हुई पुड़िया","साबूदाने की खीर","मावे की बर्फी","बेसन की चिक्की"],"a":0,"hint":"Not the food he dreamed of — the ‘medicine’ Nanaji gave him.","why":"“न चाहते हुए भी मुझे कड़वी पुड़िया खानी पड़ी।” पुड़िया = medicine in a paper packet. Kheer, barfi and chikki were only in his hungry dreams!"},{"c":"paryayvachi","t":"पर्यायवाची","p":50,"q":"“सीमा-हीन गगन में उड़ते” — “गगन” का पर्यायवाची कौन-सा है?","o":["धरती","आकाश","पवन","सागर"],"a":1,"hint":"Where do birds fly? Look up!","why":"गगन = आकाश = आसमान = नभ (sky). धरती is the ground below, पवन is wind, सागर is the sea."},{"c":"paryayvachi","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"इनमें से कौन-सा शब्द “पक्षी” का पर्यायवाची नहीं है?","o":["खग","विहग","पवन","पंछी"],"a":2,"hint":"One of these is what birds fly THROUGH, not a bird.","why":"खग, विहग, पंछी = पक्षी (bird). पवन = हवा (wind). Fun: खग means ‘one who moves in the sky’ (ख = sky)!"},{"c":"paryayvachi","t":"पर्यायवाची","p":50,"q":"“जल, नीर, वारि” — ये तीनों किस शब्द के पर्यायवाची हैं?","o":["आग","हवा","बादल","पानी"],"a":3,"hint":"जलपरी is a mermaid. So what does जल mean?","why":"जल, नीर, वारि, अंबु — सब पानी (water) के नाम हैं। Mix-up alert: अनल = आग, अनिल = हवा."},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“मुँह में पानी आना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["बहुत प्यास लगना","स्वादिष्ट चीज़ देख खाने का मन होना","डर से चुप हो जाना","दाँत में दर्द होना"],"a":1,"hint":"The hungry boy smelled dal-chawal and fried green chillies…","why":"मुँह में पानी आना = mouth-watering, craving tasty food. It’s not about thirst! The hungry boy felt this all day."},{"c":"muhavare","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“आँख ज़रा लगती भी तो खाने की चीज़ें दिखाई देतीं।” — “आँख लगना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["आँख में चोट लगना","किसी को घूरना","नींद आ जाना","रोने लगना"],"a":2,"hint":"He was so hungry he saw kachori and barfi. When do you see things with your eyes closed?","why":"आँख लगना = to doze off. मुहावरे का अर्थ शब्दों से अलग होता है — no eye injury here! (Idioms don’t mean the literal words.)"},{"c":"muhavare","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{ZH} ने हफ़्ते भर लगातार मेहनत करके AcadaBee का लोगो बनाया। कौन-सा मुहावरा उस पर सही बैठता है?","o":["दिन-रात एक करना","हाथ पर हाथ धरे बैठना","नौ दो ग्यारह होना","आँखें चुराना"],"a":0,"hint":"Which idiom means working extremely hard, without rest?","why":"दिन-रात एक करना = to work very hard. हाथ पर हाथ धरे बैठना = do nothing; नौ दो ग्यारह होना = run away; आँखें चुराना = avoid someone."},{"c":"muhavare","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"किस वाक्य में मुहावरे का गलत प्रयोग हुआ है?","o":["झूठ पकड़ा गया तो वह पानी-पानी हो गया","प्यास लगी तो वह पानी-पानी हो गया","पुलिस को देख चोर नौ दो ग्यारह हो गया","शाबाशी सुनकर वह फूला न समाया"],"a":1,"hint":"पानी-पानी होना has nothing to do with drinking water. When does your face go red?","why":"पानी-पानी होना = to feel very ashamed (like when a lie is caught). Using it for thirst is the literal-meaning trap!"},{"c":"vakya-bhed","t":"वाक्य भेद","p":100,"q":"“शायद कोई माँगकर ले गया था।” — अर्थ के आधार पर यह कौन-सा वाक्य है?","o":["इच्छावाचक","प्रश्नवाचक","निषेधवाचक","संदेहवाचक"],"a":3,"hint":"Look at the first word — is the speaker sure or unsure?","why":"“शायद” (maybe) shows doubt → संदेहवाचक वाक्य. The family wasn’t sure who had borrowed the thermometer!"},{"c":"vakya-bhed","t":"वाक्य भेद","p":50,"q":"“काश! सुधाकर काका की जगह मैं होता!” — यह कौन-सा वाक्य है?","o":["आज्ञावाचक","निषेधवाचक","इच्छावाचक","प्रश्नवाचक"],"a":2,"hint":"काश = ‘If only…’ or ‘I wish…’","why":"काश से इच्छा प्रकट होती है → इच्छावाचक वाक्य (sentence of wish). The boy WISHED he were in the hospital bed eating kheer!"},{"c":"vakya-bhed","t":"वाक्य भेद","p":100,"q":"“मैं आज स्कूल जाऊँगा।” को निषेधवाचक वाक्य में बदलो।","o":["मैं आज स्कूल नहीं जाऊँगा।","क्या मैं आज स्कूल जाऊँगा?","शायद मैं आज स्कूल जाऊँगा।","आज स्कूल जाओ।"],"a":0,"hint":"निषेध = saying NO. Which small word makes a sentence negative?","why":"“नहीं” जोड़ने से वाक्य निषेधवाचक बनता है। क्या → प्रश्नवाचक, शायद → संदेहवाचक, जाओ → आज्ञावाचक."},{"c":"shabd-bhed","t":"Hindi","p":100,"q":"“वे कहते हैं, मानव! सीखो तुम हमसे” — इस पंक्ति के सर्वनाम कौन-से हैं?","o":["कहते, सीखो","मानव","मानव, हमसे","वे, तुम, हमसे"],"a":3,"hint":"सर्वनाम (pronoun) stands in place of a noun: I, you, they, us…","why":"वे (they), तुम (you), हमसे (from us) सर्वनाम हैं। मानव is a संज्ञा (noun); कहते, सीखो are क्रिया (verbs)."},{"c":"shabd-bhed","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"“पीपल की ऊँची डाली पर बैठी चिड़िया गाती है।” — इस वाक्य में क्रिया कौन-सी है?","o":["पीपल","ऊँची","डाली","गाती है"],"a":3,"hint":"क्रिया (verb) tells what someone is doing.","why":"चिड़िया क्या कर रही है? गा रही है → “गाती है” क्रिया है (गाती = मुख्य, है = सहायक)। ऊँची = विशेषण; पीपल, डाली = संज्ञा."},{"c":"shabd-bhed","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"इनमें से कौन-सा शब्द विशेषण नहीं है?","o":["सुंदर","कड़वा","सुंदरता","पाँच"],"a":2,"hint":"One word is the NAME of a quality, not a word that describes something.","why":"सुंदरता (beauty) भाववाचक संज्ञा है — a noun. सुंदर, कड़वा describe things; पाँच is a संख्यावाचक विशेषण (number adjective)."},{"c":"shabd-bhed","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"“ताजमहल आगरा में यमुना के किनारे है।” — “ताजमहल” कौन-सी संज्ञा है, और क्यों?","o":["व्यक्तिवाचक — एक खास इमारत का नाम","जातिवाचक — सब इमारतों का नाम","भाववाचक — एक भावना का नाम","समूहवाचक — इमारतों का समूह"],"a":0,"hint":"Is there only ONE Taj Mahal in the world, or many?","why":"ताजमहल आगरा की एक खास इमारत है → व्यक्तिवाचक संज्ञा (proper noun). The word “इमारत” on its own would be जातिवाचक."},{"c":"ling","t":"लिंग","p":50,"q":"“चिड़िया” का पुल्लिंग शब्द क्या है?","o":["चिड़ियाँ","चिड़ा","चिड़ियाघर","चिड़ियों"],"a":1,"hint":"Think of a similar pair: बुढ़िया (old woman) → बूढ़ा (old man).","why":"चिड़िया (female bird) ↔ चिड़ा (male bird). चिड़ियाँ and चिड़ियों are plurals — and चिड़ियाघर is a zoo!"},{"c":"ling","t":"लिंग","p":100,"q":"इनमें से कौन-सा वाक्य सही है?","o":["दही खट्टी हो गई।","दही खट्टे हो गए।","दही खट्टा हो गई।","दही खट्टा हो गया।"],"a":3,"hint":"Not every word ending in -ी is feminine. Do we say ‘मेरा दही’ or ‘मेरी दही’?","why":"दही पुल्लिंग है — so both the describing word and the verb are masculine: “दही खट्टा हो गया”।"},{"c":"ling","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"इनमें से कौन-सा शब्द स्त्रीलिंग है?","o":["पानी","घी","पुड़िया","मोती"],"a":2,"hint":"Three end in -ी but are masculine. Try saying ‘मेरा ___’ or ‘मेरी ___’.","why":"पुड़िया स्त्रीलिंग है — “कड़वी पुड़िया”। पानी, घी, मोती end in -ी but are पुल्लिंग: ठंडा पानी, महँगा घी, चमकता मोती."},{"c":"vachan","t":"वचन","p":50,"q":"“चिड़िया” का बहुवचन क्या है?","o":["चिड़ियाँ","चिड़ियें","चिड़ीयाँ","चिड़िये"],"a":0,"hint":"गुड़िया (doll) becomes गुड़ियाँ in plural. Follow the same pattern.","why":"-इया वाले शब्द बहुवचन में -इयाँ बनते हैं: चिड़िया → चिड़ियाँ, गुड़िया → गुड़ियाँ। Don’t forget the chandrabindu (ँ)!"},{"c":"vachan","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"किस वाक्य में वचन की गलती है?","o":["पेड़ों पर चिड़ियाँ बैठी हैं।","मेरे पास दो किताब है।","नानाजी पुड़िया लेकर आए।","बच्चे आँगन में खेल रहे हैं।"],"a":1,"hint":"Count the books! Do the noun and the verb match the number?","why":"दो = plural, so “मेरे पास दो किताबें हैं”। “नानाजी आए” is correct — elders get a plural verb out of respect."},{"c":"vachan","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"“तभी नानाजी आए।” — नानाजी एक ही हैं, फिर क्रिया “आए” (बहुवचन) क्यों है?","o":["नानाजी के साथ कई लोग थे","आदर दिखाने के लिए","भूतकाल में क्रिया हमेशा बहुवचन होती है","लेखक से गलती हुई है"],"a":1,"hint":"How do we talk about elders — ‘वह आया’ or ‘वे आए’?","why":"बड़ों के लिए आदर से बहुवचन क्रिया लगाते हैं: नानाजी आए, पापा बोले। This is the आदरसूचक बहुवचन (plural of respect)."},{"c":"karak","t":"कारक","p":100,"q":"“नानीजी ने चम्मच से खीर खिलाई।” — यहाँ “से” कौन-सा कारक है?","o":["अपादान","करण","कर्म","संबंध"],"a":1,"hint":"चम्मच is the TOOL used. Which कारक is about the means or tool?","why":"करण कारक = tool/means (with, by): चम्मच से खिलाई। अपादान also uses “से” but means separation: पेड़ से गिरा."},{"c":"karak","t":"कारक","p":100,"q":"“पेड़ से पत्ते गिरे।” — यहाँ “से” कौन-सा कारक है?","o":["करण","अधिकरण","अपादान","संप्रदान"],"a":2,"hint":"The leaves moved AWAY from the tree. Is the tree a tool, or what they separated from?","why":"अपादान कारक = separation (from): पत्ते पेड़ से अलग हुए। Same “से”, different job — compare करण: चम्मच से खाया (with a spoon)."},{"c":"karak","t":"कारक","p":50,"q":"“पीपल की डाली पर बैठी चिड़िया” — “पर” किस कारक का चिह्न है?","o":["अधिकरण","संबंध","संप्रदान","कर्म"],"a":0,"hint":"पर and में tell you WHERE something is.","why":"में, पर = अधिकरण कारक (place: on, in). And “की” in “पीपल की डाली” is संबंध कारक (of)!"},{"c":"vakya-shuddhi","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"किस वाक्य में गलती है?","o":["मैंने आम खाया।","नानीजी ने खीर बनाई।","मुन्नू ने दाल खाई।","मैंने खीर खाया।"],"a":3,"hint":"With “ने”, the verb matches the THING eaten, not the eater. Is खीर masculine or feminine?","why":"“ने” वाले वाक्य में क्रिया कर्म के अनुसार बदलती है: खीर (स्त्रीलिंग) → मैंने खीर खाई; आम (पुल्लिंग) → मैंने आम खाया."},{"c":"vakya-shuddhi","t":"Hindi","p":100,"q":"बच्चा नानीजी को बताता है कि सिर में दर्द है। सही वाक्य कौन-सा है?","o":["मेरे सिर में दर्द हो रहा है।","मेरा सिर में दर्द हो रहा है।","मेरी सिर में दर्द हो रहा है।","मेरा सिर में दर्द हो रही है।"],"a":0,"hint":"When “में” comes after सिर, does मेरा change its form?","why":"“में” आने पर मेरा → मेरे: मेरे सिर में, मेरे घर में। दर्द पुल्लिंग है, so “हो रहा है” — just as the boy said in the story!"},{"c":"vakya-shuddhi","t":"Hindi","p":50,"q":"किस शब्द की वर्तनी (spelling) सही है?","o":["आशिर्वाद","आर्शीवाद","आशीर्वाद","आशीरवाद"],"a":2,"hint":"Say it slowly: आ-शी-र-वा-द. Where does the half र go?","why":"सही: आशीर्वाद (blessing) — शी में बड़ी ई, और आधा र (रेफ) वा के ऊपर: र्वा। The common mistake “आर्शीवाद” puts the र in the wrong place."}],"z-home":[{"c":"home-to-many","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The chapter opens with ‘vasudhaiva kutumbakam’ from the Hitopadeśha. What does it mean?","o":["The whole world is family","This is mine, that is not mine","Charity begins at home","Each kingdom must care for itself"],"a":0,"hint":"The verse compares narrow-minded and noble-minded people. What do the noble-minded believe?","why":"Vasudhaiva kutumbakam means ‘the whole world is family’. ‘This is mine, that is not mine’ is how the verse says narrow-minded people think — the very opposite!"},{"c":"home-to-many","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"In Grade 6, Indians living away from their birthplace were called migrants. Which other type of migrant is this chapter about?","o":["Villagers who moved to Indian cities","Indians who went to live abroad","People from other lands who settled here","Tourists on a short holiday in India"],"a":2,"hint":"This time the journey crosses India’s borders — but in which direction?","why":"This chapter is about people who came to India from other parts of the world and settled here, adding another dimension to India’s diversity. Moving abroad is the reverse journey!"},{"c":"home-to-many","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A stranger knocks at midnight in heavy rain, asking for shelter. The chapter stretches this scene to which bigger question?","o":["How to keep your house safe at night","Why strangers can never be trusted","How to fix a broken-down car","Whether India should shelter refugees"],"a":3,"hint":"Zoom out from one house to a whole country. Who might knock on a country’s door?","why":"The midnight-stranger debate is a mini version of a big one: when refugees seek shelter in India, do similar pros and cons apply? The chapter asks you to weigh both sides."},{"c":"bene-israel","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Where did the Bene Israel, a Jewish community, live in India?","o":["Konkan coast, just south of Mumbai","Malabar coast, in Kerala","Sanjān, in coastal Gujarat","Parts of Bengal, in the east"],"a":0,"hint":"Kerala and Gujarat belong to other stories in this chapter. Of the two places left, which is theirs?","why":"The Bene Israel lived on the Konkan coast, just south of Mumbai. The Malabar coast (Kerala) welcomed Syriac Christians, and Sanjān in Gujarat welcomed the Parsis."},{"c":"bene-israel","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The Bene Israel lost their holy books in a shipwreck. How did they keep their faith alive?","o":["The Raja of Kochi gave them new books","They joined a local religion instead","They remembered their prayer about one God","They sailed back to Israel for new books"],"a":2,"hint":"Books can sink in a storm, but some things travel safely in your memory.","why":"The survivors remembered their prayer, which says there is only one God. Memory kept their faith alive — and over time they grew into India’s largest Jewish community!"},{"c":"bene-israel","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"From a handful of shipwreck survivors, the Bene Israel grew to over 25,000 soon after independence. What does this show?","o":["Most of them had left India by then","India was a safe home where they thrived","They were the newest group to arrive","They had given up their Jewish faith"],"a":1,"hint":"A small group can only grow that big over centuries if it is safe and free.","why":"The Bene Israel became India’s largest Jewish community — over 25,000 shortly after independence. India proved a safe haven where Jews could practise their faith without fear."},{"c":"kochi-jews","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Some Jewish groups who came between the 12th and 19th centuries settled near which city?","o":["Sanjān, in coastal Gujarat","Just south of Mumbai","Parts of Bengal","Cochin (present-day Kochi)"],"a":3,"hint":"Picture the two synagogue photos in the chapter. Which city were they taken in?","why":"These later groups, again fleeing persecution, settled near Cochin (Kochi). Just south of Mumbai was home to the far older Bene Israel; Sanjān welcomed the Parsis."},{"c":"kochi-jews","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Did the Jews of Kochi have to pay for their land? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — they paid the Raja rent every year","No — the Raja granted it free, for all time","Yes — they paid for it with spices","No — they took the land by force"],"a":1,"hint":"Think about how the Raja of Kochi welcomed these Jews, who were fleeing persecution.","why":"The Raja of Kochi granted them land free of cost ‘as long as the world, sun, and moon endure’ — a poetic way of saying forever. What a welcome for people fleeing persecution!"},{"c":"kochi-jews","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"The Jews of Kochi built a synagogue. Why did that matter so much?","o":["It kept them apart from Indian society","The Raja ordered every group to build one","They could keep their faith AND belong","It doubled as a market for spices"],"a":2,"hint":"A synagogue is where Jews worship and hold weddings. It helped with TWO things at once.","why":"The synagogue let them practise their faith ‘even as they became part of the Indian society’. Keeping your faith while belonging — that’s integration, not isolation!"},{"c":"vivekananda-1893","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Where did Swami Vivekananda give his famous 1893 speech about India sheltering refugees?","o":["The Raja’s court in Kochi","World Parliament of Religions, Chicago","A synagogue on the Konkan coast","The fire temple at Udvada"],"a":1,"hint":"It wasn’t a temple or a synagogue. Of the other two, where did he give this speech?","why":"At the World Parliament of Religions in Chicago (1893), he said he was proud of a nation that ‘sheltered the persecuted and the refugees of all religions and all nations’."},{"c":"vivekananda-1893","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Besides the Israelites (Jews), which community did Vivekananda name in his 1893 speech?","o":["The Siddis","The Arab merchants","The Syriac Christians","The Zoroastrians (Parsis)"],"a":3,"hint":"He called this community a ‘grand’ nation whose remnant was still being fostered.","why":"Besides the Israelites who took refuge in Southern India, he named ‘the grand Zoroastrian nation’ — the Parsis — whose remnant had been sheltered and was ‘still’ being fostered."},{"c":"vivekananda-1893","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"In his 1893 speech, what made Vivekananda proud of India?","o":["It sheltered the persecuted of all faiths","It had conquered many foreign nations","It grew rich from the spice trade","It had only one religion for everyone"],"a":0,"hint":"Think of the Jews and Parsis in this chapter. Why did they come, and how were they treated?","why":"He was proud of a nation that ‘sheltered the persecuted and the refugees of all religions and all nations of the Earth’ — like the Jews and the Zoroastrians in this very chapter."},{"c":"syriac-christians","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"In the Roman Empire, were Syriac Christians ever treated as heretics? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — their beliefs about Christ differed","Yes — they were spying for Persia","No — Rome treated them as close allies","No — only Persia ever troubled them"],"a":0,"hint":"A heretic holds beliefs against the dominant view. What did the official Church disagree with?","why":"Some of their beliefs about Christ differed from those of the official Church, so Rome sometimes treated them as heretics — people holding beliefs against the dominant view."},{"c":"syriac-christians","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Why did the Persian Empire persecute Syriac Christians?","o":["Their beliefs about Christ were different","They refused to pay the jizya tax","They were suspected of helping the Romans","They tried to take over Persia’s trade"],"a":2,"hint":"Rome and Persia were enemies. Whose side might Persia think these Christians were on?","why":"Rome and Persia were enemies, and Persia suspected them of secretly helping the Romans. Heretics to Rome, suspects to Persia — so some fled east to India."},{"c":"syriac-christians","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Fleeing persecution, some Syriac Christians travelled east along trade routes. Where in India did they arrive?","o":["Konkan coast, south of Mumbai","Sanjān, in coastal Gujarat","Parts of Bengal","Malabar coast (present-day Kerala)"],"a":3,"hint":"It’s the same state where Arab merchants helped build India’s oldest mosque.","why":"They reached the Malabar coast — present-day Kerala — where they could live and worship freely. Konkan was the Bene Israel’s coast; Sanjān welcomed the Parsis."},{"c":"syriac-christians","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which statement about Syriac Christians in India is WRONG?","o":["They are also called ‘Syrian Christians’","They form one single sect","They could worship freely in Kerala","They used the Syriac language in worship"],"a":1,"hint":"Three match the chapter. Recall the last line of the Syriac Christian section.","why":"Syriac (Syrian) Christians in India are divided into several sects — not one. The other three are true: the ‘Syrian’ name, free worship in Kerala, and the ancient Syriac language."},{"c":"parsis-flee-persia","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Zoroastrianism, the faith of the Parsis, is based on the worship of…","o":["One God, Ahurā Mazdā","Many gods called daevas","The prophet Zarathushtra","The Sun and the Moon"],"a":0,"hint":"Zarathushtra founded the faith — but he is its prophet, not its God.","why":"Founded by the prophet Zarathushtra in Central Asia, Zoroastrianism worships one God, Ahurā Mazdā, and values choosing good over evil. In the Avesta, daevas are evil gods!"},{"c":"parsis-flee-persia","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Which was NOT a way Zoroastrians were persecuted in Persia after the Sassanid Empire fell?","o":["Forced conversions to Islam","Religious taxes (jizya)","Being sold as slaves by traders","Their fire temples destroyed"],"a":2,"hint":"Three happened in Persia. One belongs to a different community’s story in this chapter.","why":"Zoroastrians faced forced conversions, the jizya tax, destroyed fire temples, and social and legal marginalisation. Being enslaved by traders is part of the Siddis’ story."},{"c":"parsis-flee-persia","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Sailing across the Arabian Sea, when did groups of Zoroastrians reach India’s western coast?","o":["Around 175 BCE","Between the 8th and 10th centuries","Between the 12th and 19th centuries","In the 18th century"],"a":1,"hint":"It was after Persia fell in the 7th century — but before the later Jewish groups came.","why":"They reached present-day Gujarat between the 8th and 10th centuries, after Persia fell in the 7th. 175 BCE is the Bene Israel; the 12th–19th centuries are the later Jewish groups."},{"c":"milk-and-sugar","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Why did Raja Jadi Rāṇā show the Parsis a full jug of milk instead of just telling them?","o":["They didn’t know each other’s languages","To offer them a welcome drink","Milk was holy in the Parsi faith","To test whether they were hungry"],"a":0,"hint":"Think about what the two sides could NOT do when they first met.","why":"They were unfamiliar with each other’s languages, so the Raja ‘spoke’ with a jug filled to the brim: my kingdom is full and cannot take in more people."},{"c":"milk-and-sugar","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"The wise man dissolved a spoon of sugar in the full jug without spilling a drop. What was he saying?","o":["We will stay separate from your people","We will quietly take over your kingdom","We have brought sugar to sell to you","We will blend in and sweeten, not overflow"],"a":3,"hint":"Think about what sugar does to milk — and what did NOT happen to the jug.","why":"Like sugar in milk, the Parsis would mix into society and make it richer without overflowing it. Jadi Rāṇā was pleased and gave them a secure place to settle."},{"c":"milk-and-sugar","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if the milk had spilled over when the wise man added the sugar?","o":["It would prove the Raja’s worry right","It would show the Parsis were very sweet","It would make no difference at all","The Raja would have been even more pleased"],"a":0,"hint":"Remember why the Raja showed them a FULL jug in the first place.","why":"The trick worked BECAUSE nothing spilled: newcomers who fit in without overflowing. A spill would have shown just what the Raja feared — no room for more people."},{"c":"milk-and-sugar","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A new student from another country joins {Z}’s class. Which behaviour is most like ‘sugar in the milk’?","o":["Stays silent and sits alone every day","Joins in and shares new ideas with all","Wants the class to follow only her ways","Plans to change schools next term"],"a":1,"hint":"Sugar does two things: it blends in AND makes the milk sweeter. Which choice does both?","why":"Sugar mixes in and makes things sweeter without pushing anything out. A newcomer who fits in AND shares something new is ‘sugar in the milk’ — just like the Parsis."},{"c":"parsis-today","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Where does the Parsis’ ancient sacred fire still burn today?","o":["Raja Jadi Rāṇā’s court in Sanjān","The Kochi synagogue","Udvada, in south Gujarat","A temple on the Malabar coast"],"a":2,"hint":"Look for an 18th-century fire temple close to where the Parsis first reached India.","why":"The ancient sacred fire still burns in Udvada (south Gujarat), a short distance from where the Parsis first arrived. A fire carried across the sea, still alight today!"},{"c":"parsis-today","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Which fact about India TODAY shows that the Parsis truly found a home here?","o":["Most Parsis have gone back to Persia","Parsis have dropped all their old customs","It has the most Zoroastrians of any country","Zoroastrianism is India’s state religion"],"a":2,"hint":"Three options are false. Recall the Parsi section’s line that begins ‘Today India has…’.","why":"Today India has the largest population of Zoroastrians in the world. Parsis kept their traditions, blended them with local culture, and continue to enrich Indian society."},{"c":"parsis-today","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Raja Ravi Varma’s ‘Decking the Bride’ shows a Parsi bride with many aspects of Indian culture. What does it suggest?","o":["Parsis lived cut off from Indian customs","Parsi culture blended with Indian culture","Parsis had given up their own faith","Parsi weddings were not allowed in India"],"a":1,"hint":"Indian touches at a Parsi wedding… are the two cultures kept apart or mixed?","why":"A Parsi bride shown with many aspects of Indian culture is a clue to integration: Parsis combined their traditions with local culture — while keeping their own faith and sacred fire."},{"c":"iran-india-ties","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The Vedic word ‘soma’ (a divine drink) matches which word in Old Avestan?","o":["yasna","haotar","daeva","haoma"],"a":3,"hint":"Look at a known pair: hotar (a priest) = haotar. Which option is soma’s ‘cousin’ in the same way?","why":"soma = haoma, a divine drink. Likewise hotar = haotar (a priest) and yajña = yasna (sacrifice). Vedic Sanskrit and Old Avestan share many word roots!"},{"c":"iran-india-ties","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which statement about the Vedas and the Avesta is WRONG?","o":["Vedic devas are beneficial gods","Avestan daevas are evil gods","Ahurā is the Avesta’s chief god","In later India, asuras are chief gods"],"a":3,"hint":"Some meanings flipped between the two. Who became the ‘bad guys’ in India after the Vedic period?","why":"After the Vedic period, asuras are seen in India as harmful powers or demons — yet Ahurā is the Avesta’s chief god! Devas and daevas flip too: good in the Vedas, evil in the Avesta."},{"c":"iran-india-ties","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Which is NOT something Zoroastrian and Vedic traditions share, according to the chapter?","o":["A cosmic order holding the universe","Great importance given to fire worship","Many word roots and grammar features","Seeing devas as good, helpful gods"],"a":3,"hint":"Two words can share a root and still mean opposite things.","why":"Both share the idea of a cosmic order, give importance to fire worship, and share word roots and grammar. But Vedic devas are good gods, while Avestan daevas are evil — a difference!"},{"c":"arab-merchants","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Did the early Arab settlers come to India as conquerors? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — they captured the west coast","No — they were brought as slaves","No — they came as peaceful traders","Yes — they built a mosque to rule from"],"a":2,"hint":"What filled their ships — armies or spices?","why":"These early Arab settlers came as peaceful traders, not conquerors. They traded spices, married local women and formed new communities. ‘Brought as slaves’ is the Siddis’ story."},{"c":"arab-merchants","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Kerala’s Cheraman Juma Masjid, which Arab merchants helped build, is special because it is…","o":["India’s oldest mosque","India’s largest mosque","Built by Arab conquerors after a war","Built by Parsis for Arab traders"],"a":0,"hint":"Arab merchants arrived from the 7th century onward — very early in India’s story.","why":"Arab merchants who became part of Kerala’s Mappila Muslim community helped build the Cheraman Juma Masjid — India’s oldest mosque. They came as peaceful traders, not conquerors."},{"c":"arab-merchants","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Dad’s farm in Karnataka grows pepper, a spice. Which community in this chapter traded spices and settled in Karnataka?","o":["The Parsis","Arab merchants","The Bene Israel","Syriac Christians"],"a":1,"hint":"This group came to trade, not to escape danger, and settled all along the west coast.","why":"From the 7th century onward, Arab merchants traded spices and other goods and settled along the west coast — in Kerala, Gujarat and Karnataka. Spices drew traders from far away!"},{"c":"siddis","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Were the Siddis refugees who chose India for safety? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — they fled persecution in Persia","No — they came as spice traders","Yes — their ship was wrecked nearby","No — they were brought here as slaves"],"a":3,"hint":"The chapter calls theirs ‘a different kind of story’. Did they choose to come?","why":"The Siddis, of African origin, were enslaved and brought to India by Arab, Portuguese and British traders between the 7th and 19th centuries. They did not choose to come."},{"c":"siddis","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Some Siddis rose high in Muslim rulers’ armies. The chapter says some of them even briefly controlled…","o":["The Malabar coast","Parts of Bengal","Sanjān in Gujarat","Parts of Punjab"],"a":1,"hint":"Not on the west coast at all. Recall the far-off region the chapter names.","why":"Some Siddis gained prominence in Muslim rulers’ armies and briefly controlled parts of Bengal — rising to power though their community first arrived enslaved."},{"c":"siddis","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Which is NOT part of the Siddis’ blend of African and Indian traditions?","o":["A dance with African-style drumming","Language adapted to regional influences","Keeping a sacred fire alight in Udvada","African practices blended with adopted faiths"],"a":2,"hint":"Three belong to the Siddis. One belongs to the Parsis’ story.","why":"Siddis dance to African-style drumming, adapted their language to regional influences, and blended African practices with Hinduism, Islam or Christianity. The Udvada fire is a Parsi treasure."},{"c":"siddis","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"The Siddis are a scheduled tribe. Why are efforts being made today to improve their education and livelihoods?","o":["Their economic condition is still poor","They arrived in India only recently","They refuse to learn local languages","They are mostly rich spice traders"],"a":0,"hint":"Right after ‘scheduled tribe’, the chapter says ‘However…’. What comes next?","why":"Though designated a scheduled tribe, the Siddis’ overall economic condition remains poor — so efforts aim to give them better access to education and livelihood options."},{"c":"siddis","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Why was Hirabai Lobi, from the Siddi community of Gujarat, awarded the Padma Shri in 2023?","o":["She led a Siddi army in Bengal","She uplifted Siddi women’s livelihoods","She painted ‘Decking the Bride’","She brought the sacred fire to Udvada"],"a":1,"hint":"The Padma Shri honours service that changes lives. What did she do for her own community?","why":"Hirabai Lobi worked for the upliftment of Siddi women — connecting them with banking services and teaching organic farming and other livelihoods. A Padma Shri for selfless service!"},{"c":"compare-communities","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Three of these came to India escaping persecution at home. Which one did NOT?","o":["Jews","Syriac Christians","Parsis","Arab merchants"],"a":3,"hint":"One group came to buy and sell goods, not to find safety.","why":"Jews, Syriac Christians and Parsis all fled persecution and found a safe haven in India. Arab merchants came as peaceful traders, looking for trade, not refuge."},{"c":"compare-communities","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Jews, Syriac Christians and Parsis all faced hostility at home, often with harassment or violence. What is this called?","o":["Persecution","Migration","Integration","Slavery"],"a":0,"hint":"It’s the chapter’s word for ill-treatment for religious, ethnic, social or political reasons.","why":"Persecution = hostility for religious, ethnic, social or political reasons, often with harassment or violence. Migration is moving away; integration is blending in after you arrive."},{"c":"compare-communities","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"Put these arrivals in order, earliest first: Parsis reach Gujarat; Bene Israel arrive; later Jewish groups reach Kochi.","o":["Parsis → Bene Israel → Kochi Jews","Bene Israel → Kochi Jews → Parsis","Bene Israel → Parsis → Kochi Jews","Kochi Jews → Parsis → Bene Israel"],"a":2,"hint":"One group came BCE, one in the 8th–10th centuries CE, and one in the 12th–19th centuries.","why":"Bene Israel: around 175 BCE (say some scholars). Parsis: 8th–10th centuries. Later Jewish groups near Kochi: 12th–19th centuries. Two Jewish arrivals over a thousand years apart!"}],"z-life":[{"c":"mouth-and-saliva","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Your teeth crush and chew food into fine pieces. What is this first breakdown of food called?","o":["Absorption","Mechanical digestion","Egestion","Rumination"],"a":1,"hint":"No digestive juice is needed for this step, just the grinding work of your teeth.","why":"Crushing and chewing food into fine pieces is mechanical digestion. Absorption happens in the intestine, egestion at the anus, and rumination is a cow's re-chewing trick!"},{"c":"mouth-and-saliva","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if your mouth made no saliva at all? Apart from digesting starch, which job would get much harder?","o":["Killing germs in the stomach","Breaking fats into tiny droplets","Absorbing water from waste","Swallowing — food would stay dry and hard"],"a":3,"hint":"Imagine eating a dry biscuit with a completely dry mouth.","why":"Saliva moistens chewed food, making it soft and easy to swallow. Killing germs is the stomach acid's job, fat droplets are bile's, and absorbing water is the large intestine's."},{"c":"mouth-and-saliva","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Iodine is added to (A) boiled rice and (B) well-chewed boiled rice. Which turns blue-black? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["B — chewing adds extra starch","A — its starch is still unchanged","Both — iodine colours any food","Neither — boiling destroys starch"],"a":1,"hint":"Iodine turns blue-black only where starch is present. What did saliva do to the starch in B?","why":"Blue-black means starch. In A the starch is untouched. In B, saliva broke most of it into sugar — which is also why chapati tastes sweet when you chew it for long!"},{"c":"mouth-and-saliva","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"In the rice–iodine test, what if you chewed the rice for 2 minutes instead of 30 seconds? Test tube B would most likely show…","o":["Even less blue-black colour","A deeper blue-black colour","Exactly the same colour as A","A bright green colour"],"a":0,"hint":"Longer chewing gives saliva more time to work. More time to do what?","why":"The longer saliva acts, the more starch it breaks into sugar. Less starch left means less blue-black colour — just what the chapter asks you to test by chewing for longer."},{"c":"mouth-and-saliva","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Yadu puts rice flour and water in tubes A and B, adds saliva to B only, waits 40 min, then adds iodine. What is he testing?","o":["Whether iodine dissolves in water","Whether saliva breaks down starch","Whether rice flour contains protein","Whether water alone digests starch"],"a":1,"hint":"Only one thing is different between tube A and tube B. That's what he is testing.","why":"Saliva is the only difference, so he's testing whether saliva breaks down starch. A turns blue-black (starch left); B doesn't, because saliva changed its starch into sugar."},{"c":"food-journey","t":"Order","p":50,"q":"After you swallow, in which order does food travel through the alimentary canal?","o":["Stomach → food pipe → small → large intestine","Food pipe → stomach → large → small intestine","Food pipe → small intestine → stomach → anus","Food pipe → stomach → small → large intestine"],"a":3,"hint":"The stomach comes straight after the swallowing tube, and the WIDER intestine comes last.","why":"Mouth → food pipe (oesophagus) → stomach → small intestine → large intestine → rectum → anus. Food finishes in the long small intestine before entering the wider large one."},{"c":"food-journey","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Which of these is NOT part of the tube that food travels through?","o":["Stomach","Food pipe","Liver","Small intestine"],"a":2,"hint":"Some organs form the tube; others sit beside it and send juices in.","why":"The liver is an associated part, not part of the alimentary canal. It sends bile into the small intestine, but food never passes through it. The pancreas works the same way."},{"c":"food-journey","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Astronauts float in space with no ‘up’ or ‘down’, yet swallowed food still reaches their stomach. What pushes it along?","o":["Gravity pulling it down","Wave-like squeezes of the food pipe walls","Air swallowed along with the food","The tongue pushing it all the way"],"a":1,"hint":"Without gravity, the push must come from your own body — and not just at the start of the swallow.","why":"The food pipe walls contract and relax in a wave-like motion that pushes food along — no gravity needed! The same movement moves food forward through the whole canal."},{"c":"stomach","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The inner lining of the stomach releases a secretion. What does it contain?","o":["Saliva, bile and acid","Bile, mucus and water","Digestive juice, acid and mucus","Pancreatic juice and bile"],"a":2,"hint":"One part digests protein, one kills germs, and one protects the stomach wall.","why":"The stomach's secretion has digestive juice, acid and mucus. Saliva is made in the mouth; bile (liver) and pancreatic juice (pancreas) act later, in the small intestine."},{"c":"stomach","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if the stomach suddenly stopped making mucus? What would most likely happen?","o":["Acid could damage the stomach lining","Proteins could no longer be digested at all","Food would stop being churned","Starch would turn into sugar there"],"a":0,"hint":"Mucus works like a protective coating. What is it protecting the lining from?","why":"Mucus protects the stomach lining from the acid. Without it, the acid could damage the stomach's own wall. Churning and the protein-digesting juice don't depend on mucus."},{"c":"stomach","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"How does your stomach protect you from many harmful bacteria in food? Pick the part AND what it does.","o":["Mucus — it traps and digests bacteria","Churning — it squeezes bacteria out","Digestive juice — it turns them to sugar","Acid — it kills many harmful bacteria"],"a":3,"hint":"Of the three things in the stomach's secretion, one is famously harsh on germs.","why":"Stomach acid helps break down proteins AND kills many harmful bacteria. Mucus has a different job: it protects the stomach lining from that strong acid."},{"c":"small-intestine","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Kavya: “The small intestine is called small because it is the shortest part of the gut.” What's her mistake?","o":["Nothing — it is the shortest part","It's about 6 m long — the longest part","It's small because it digests little food","It's small because it makes no juices"],"a":1,"hint":"The chapter says it's almost twice the height of your classroom!","why":"The small intestine is almost 6 metres long — the longest part of the alimentary canal! It's called ‘small’ because the large intestine (about 1.5 m) is wider than it."},{"c":"small-intestine","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Food enters the small intestine mixed with stomach acid. Bile and pancreatic juice neutralise it. Why can they do that?","o":["Both are basic, and bases neutralise acids","Both are acidic, so they cancel the acid","Both contain mucus that soaks up acid","Both are plain water that washes acid away"],"a":0,"hint":"Remember the neutralisation reaction from the chapter on acidic, basic and neutral substances.","why":"Juices reach the small intestine from its own lining, the liver and the pancreas. Bile is mildly basic and pancreatic juice is basic — and a base neutralises an acid!"},{"c":"small-intestine","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if a person's liver stopped sending bile to the small intestine? Digesting which food would become harder?","o":["Starch — bile turns starch into sugar","Proteins — only bile can digest them","Fats — bile breaks them into tiny droplets","Fibre — bile breaks fibre into nutrients"],"a":2,"hint":"Bile does two jobs: it neutralises acid, and it does something to one food component.","why":"Bile breaks fats into tiny droplets, making their digestion easier. Starch is broken down by saliva and pancreatic juice, and fibre by helpful bacteria in the large intestine."},{"c":"absorption","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Digested nutrients pass from the small intestine into the blood in its walls. What is this process called?","o":["Digestion","Egestion","Inhalation","Absorption"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of a sponge soaking up water.","why":"This is absorption: digested nutrients pass from the small intestine into blood vessels in its walls. The blood then carries them to every part of the body."},{"c":"absorption","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Would a smooth, flat inner lining absorb nutrients as well as the small intestine's real one? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — a smooth tube lets food move faster","No — finger-like projections add surface area","No — the ‘fingers’ chew the food again","Yes — absorption happens in the stomach"],"a":1,"hint":"Compare drying off with a smooth plastic sheet versus a fluffy towel.","why":"Thousands of finger-like projections increase the surface area for absorption. A smooth lining would have far less area, so fewer nutrients would pass into the blood."},{"c":"absorption","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"Mira has celiac disease: gluten damages the lining of her small intestine. Which of these is safe for her to eat?","o":["Wheat chapati","Barley porridge","Rye bread","Bajra roti"],"a":3,"hint":"The chapter's box on celiac disease names one group of grains that is naturally gluten-free.","why":"Millets like jowar, bajra and ragi are naturally gluten-free. In celiac disease, gluten (in wheat, barley and rye) damages the lining where nutrients are absorbed."},{"c":"large-intestine","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Most nutrients are absorbed before food reaches the large intestine. What does the large intestine absorb?","o":["Water and some salts","Most of the proteins","Oxygen from the air","Bile from the liver"],"a":0,"hint":"After this step, the waste becomes semi-solid instead of runny.","why":"The large intestine absorbs water and some salts, making the waste semi-solid (stool). It's stored in the rectum and expelled through the anus — that's egestion."},{"c":"large-intestine","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if your large intestine stopped absorbing water? What would the waste leaving your body be like?","o":["Harder and drier than usual","Exactly the same as usual","Much more watery than usual","Gone — there'd be no waste at all"],"a":2,"hint":"The large intestine's job is to take water OUT of the leftover waste.","why":"The large intestine absorbs water and some salts to make the waste semi-solid. If it stopped, the waste would stay watery. Fibre-rich food helps it work properly."},{"c":"large-intestine","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Tara: “All bacteria in our body are harmful, so a healthy gut should have none at all.” What does the chapter say?","o":["She's right — all gut bacteria are germs","Bacteria in the large intestine help digestion","Bacteria live only in the stomach acid","Bacteria live only inside fermented foods"],"a":1,"hint":"Curd is full of tiny living things, yet it's good for your tummy.","why":"Helpful bacteria in the large intestine break down undigested food, especially fibre, and make essential nutrients. Fermented foods like curd, buttermilk and kanji support a healthy gut."},{"c":"ruminants-birds","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A cow keeps chewing long after it stops grazing, bringing swallowed food back up. What is this process called?","o":["Rumination","Respiration","Egestion","Excretion"],"a":0,"hint":"Animals that do this get a special group name in the chapter, and the process has a matching name.","why":"This is rumination. Cows partly chew grass, swallow it, then bring the partly digested food back to the mouth to chew slowly. A cow spends about 8 hours a day chewing!"},{"c":"ruminants-birds","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Birds have no teeth. How does a pigeon break down hard grains? Pick the part AND how it works.","o":["Gizzard — walls squeeze it, helped by grit","Beak — it chews grain like teeth","Rumen — food comes back up to chew","Tongue — it grinds grain on the palate"],"a":0,"hint":"Birds swallow tiny stones on purpose. Where do those stones end up working?","why":"Birds have a chamber called a gizzard. Its walls contract and relax to break food down, often helped by grit (small stones) the bird swallows. Re-chewing is the cow's method!"},{"c":"ruminants-birds","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Cows re-chew their food; birds grind theirs in a gizzard. What big idea does this show?","o":["All animals digest exactly like humans","Only animals with teeth can digest food","Digestive canals vary to suit the food eaten","Birds and cows can't digest grass or grain"],"a":2,"hint":"Different foods, different tools. Is there one ‘right’ design for a digestive system?","why":"Animals show variations in the structure and function of the alimentary canal, adapted to digesting different kinds of food — like a cow's re-chewing and a bird's gizzard."},{"c":"airway-path","t":"Order","p":50,"q":"Trace the path of the air you breathe in, from outside to where gases are exchanged.","o":["Nostrils → windpipe → nasal passages → alveoli","Mouth → food pipe → lungs → alveoli","Nostrils → nasal passages → windpipe → alveoli","Nostrils → alveoli → windpipe → lungs"],"a":2,"hint":"Air must be cleaned first, then go down one tube that branches inside the lungs.","why":"Nostrils → nasal passages (hair and mucus trap dust) → windpipe → branches in the lungs → finer branches ending in tiny balloon-like sacs called alveoli."},{"c":"airway-path","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Should you breathe through your nose rather than your mouth? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["No — the mouth takes in cleaner air","Yes — the nose turns air into oxygen","No — both paths are exactly the same","Yes — nasal hair and mucus trap dust"],"a":3,"hint":"Look inside your nostrils in a mirror. What could catch dust there?","why":"Tiny hair and mucus in the nasal passages trap dust and dirt from the air you breathe in. That's why the chapter says to breathe through the nose, not the mouth."},{"c":"airway-path","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Match-up check! Which body part is paired with the WRONG job?","o":["Rib cage — protects the lungs","Windpipe — traps dust with tiny hair","Alveoli — exchange of gases","Nostrils — fresh air enters here"],"a":1,"hint":"Think about exactly where in your airway the tiny hair that traps dust is found.","why":"The hair and mucus that trap dust are in the nasal passages. The windpipe just carries air to the lungs. The other three pairs are all correct."},{"c":"breathing-mechanism","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"When you breathe IN, what do your diaphragm and ribs do?","o":["Diaphragm down; ribs up and out","Diaphragm up; ribs down and in","Diaphragm down; ribs down and in","Diaphragm up; ribs up and out"],"a":0,"hint":"Breathing in needs MORE room inside your chest.","why":"When you inhale, your ribs move up and outwards and the dome-shaped diaphragm moves down. The space inside the chest increases, so air enters the lungs."},{"c":"breathing-mechanism","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"In the bottle model of breathing, you pull the rubber sheet DOWN. What happens to the balloons, and what does the sheet stand for?","o":["They deflate — the sheet is the diaphragm","They inflate — the sheet is the rib cage","They deflate — the sheet is the windpipe","They inflate — the sheet is the diaphragm"],"a":3,"hint":"Pulling the sheet down makes more room inside the bottle — just like breathing in.","why":"Pulling the sheet down increases the space inside, so the balloons (lungs) inflate — just as your diaphragm moves down when you inhale. Let go, and they deflate."},{"c":"breathing-mechanism","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Kunal: “When I breathe out, my diaphragm moves DOWN to push the air out.” What's his mistake?","o":["It moves UP, making the chest space smaller","Nothing — it moves down when breathing out","The diaphragm doesn't move at all","Only the nose pushes air out"],"a":0,"hint":"Breathing out needs LESS space in the chest. Which way must the dome move?","why":"When you exhale, the ribs move down and inwards and the diaphragm moves UP, reducing space and pushing air out of the lungs. It moves down only when you breathe in."},{"c":"exhaled-air","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"You blow your breath through a straw into lime water, and pump room air into another tube of lime water. What happens?","o":["Only the breath tube turns milky","Only the room-air tube turns milky","Both turn milky equally","Neither one changes"],"a":0,"hint":"Lime water turns milky with one particular gas. Which air has much more of it?","why":"Lime water turns milky with carbon dioxide. The air you breathe out has much more carbon dioxide than the air you breathe in, so only the breath tube turns milky."},{"c":"exhaled-air","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Air we breathe in has nearly 0.04% carbon dioxide. About how much does the air we breathe out have?","o":["Nearly 4–5%","Nearly 21%","Nearly 0.04% — no change","Nearly 16–17%"],"a":0,"hint":"The milky lime water shows a BIG jump in this gas.","why":"Exhaled air has nearly 4–5% carbon dioxide — about a hundred times more than inhaled air. The 21% and 16–17% figures are for oxygen, breathed in and out."},{"c":"exhaled-air","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Jiya: “We breathe in oxygen and breathe out only carbon dioxide.” What's wrong with her idea?","o":["Nothing — that's exactly right","We breathe out oxygen and breathe in CO₂","Breathed-out air has no carbon dioxide","Breathed-out air still has 16–17% oxygen"],"a":3,"hint":"Check the chapter's lung diagram: how much oxygen is in the air we breathe OUT?","why":"We breathe in air and breathe out air — just with less oxygen and more carbon dioxide. Not all the oxygen is used up: exhaled air still has nearly 16–17% oxygen."},{"c":"respiration-gas-exchange","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Where does oxygen pass from the lungs into the blood? Pick the place AND what makes it work.","o":["Windpipe — it is the widest tube","Diaphragm — it squeezes oxygen in","Alveoli — thin walls with blood tubes around","Nostrils — their hair pushes oxygen in"],"a":2,"hint":"Look for the tiny balloon-like sacs at the very end of the finest branches.","why":"Alveoli have thin walls surrounded by fine tubes containing blood. Oxygen passes from the alveoli into the blood, while carbon dioxide passes from the blood into the alveoli."},{"c":"respiration-gas-exchange","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Complete the word equation for respiration: Glucose + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide + Water + ___","o":["Starch","Oxygen","Energy","Sunlight"],"a":2,"hint":"This is why we eat AND breathe — to get the power to run, play and think.","why":"Glucose + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide + Water + Energy. Respiration releases the energy that helps us walk, run, play and even think!"},{"c":"respiration-gas-exchange","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Anil: “Breathing and respiration are the same process.” What key difference is he missing?","o":["Breathing is chemical; respiration is physical","Breathing moves air; respiration releases energy","Respiration happens only in the lungs","Breathing happens only while exercising"],"a":1,"hint":"One process you can see in your moving chest; the other is chemical, happening inside the body.","why":"Breathing is a physical process: moving air in and out. Respiration is a chemical process in which oxygen breaks down glucose to release energy. Both are essential!"},{"c":"respiration-gas-exchange","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if you ate plenty of food but your body got no oxygen? Why couldn't your body get energy from that food?","o":["Food turns into oxygen in the stomach","Energy comes from air alone, not food","Food is digested only inside the lungs","Oxygen is needed to break down glucose"],"a":3,"hint":"Look at the left side of the respiration equation: two things go in.","why":"Energy is released when oxygen breaks down glucose — that's respiration. Food alone isn't enough, which is why we can't survive more than a few minutes without breathing."},{"c":"respiration-gas-exchange","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which body system carries nutrients and oxygen to every part of your body?","o":["Digestive system","Respiratory system","Excretory system","Circulatory system"],"a":3,"hint":"This system includes the heart, blood and blood vessels.","why":"The circulatory system — heart, blood and blood vessels — transports nutrients, oxygen and other substances to all parts of the body, and carries waste products away."},{"c":"animal-breathing","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Three of these animals breathe through lungs. Which one does NOT?","o":["Lizard","Elephant","Fish","Snake"],"a":2,"hint":"One of these takes oxygen from gases dissolved in water.","why":"Fish breathe through gills, exchanging gases with the water. Lizards, elephants and snakes all breathe through lungs — though their lungs differ in structure."},{"c":"animal-breathing","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if an adult frog stays underwater in a pond for a while? How does it exchange gases there?","o":["Through gills, just like a tadpole","Through its skin","It stops needing oxygen underwater","Through its lungs only"],"a":1,"hint":"The chapter says adult frogs use one body part on land and a different one in water.","why":"Tadpoles breathe through gills. Adult frogs use lungs on land and their skin for gas exchange when they stay in water — a neat adaptation for life in both places!"},{"c":"animal-breathing","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if an earthworm's skin dried out completely? Why would it be in serious trouble?","o":["Its lungs are under the skin","Its gills need wet skin to work","It exchanges gases through moist skin","Dry skin can't feel the soil"],"a":2,"hint":"Which part of an earthworm is always damp and touching the soil all around it?","why":"Earthworms use their moist skin to exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide. If the skin dried out, this gas exchange couldn't happen properly."}],"z-lit":[{"c":"rain-city-scene","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"‘How beautiful is the rain!’ Why does the poet feel this in the first stanza? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["It cools — the street was baked by summer sun","It sparkles — the drops shine like jewels","It cleans — the street had caught fire","It is rare — it never rains in cities"],"a":0,"hint":"Read the first stanza again. What came BEFORE the rain on that ‘fiery’ street?","why":"The rain comes ‘after the dust and heat’ on a ‘fiery’ street baked by the summer sun. Nothing was really burning — the rain is beautiful because it brings cool relief."},{"c":"rain-city-scene","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"Put the rain’s journey through the city in the order the poem describes it.","o":["Roofs → window-pane → spout → gutter","Gutter → spout → roofs → window-pane","Roofs → spout → window-pane → gutter","Spout → gutter → window-pane → roofs"],"a":2,"hint":"Rain lands on the top of a house first. Where does all that water end up?","why":"It clatters on the roofs, gushes out of the overflowing spout, pours across the window-pane, then roars down the gutter like a river. Top of the house down to the street!"},{"c":"rain-similes","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In the poem, the sound of rain clattering along the roofs is compared to…","o":["a river roaring down a gutter","the tramp of hoofs","the beating of drums","a leopard’s spotted hide"],"a":1,"hint":"The river roars somewhere else in the poem. On the roofs, which of these makes a heavy, clattering sound?","why":"‘How it clatters along the roofs, / Like the tramp of hoofs’ — rain on roofs sounds like horses’ heavy steps. The river simile belongs to the gutter, not the roofs."},{"c":"rain-similes","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"A classmate listed the poem’s similes. Which one did they get WRONG?","o":["Rain on the roofs — like the tramp of hoofs","Rain in the gutter — roars like a river","The plain — like a leopard’s spotted hide","The sick man’s brain — like a calm pool"],"a":3,"hint":"A simile uses ‘like’ or ‘as’. Which comparison does the poem never actually make?","why":"The poem says his fevered brain ‘grows calm again’ — no ‘like’, so no simile. Hoofs, river and leopard’s hide are Longfellow’s three real similes."},{"c":"rain-similes","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Longfellow says the country plain stretches ‘like a leopard’s tawny and spotted hide’. What is he picturing?","o":["Leopards hunting across the plain","Dry golden-brown fields with darker patches","Rain making the grass shine like fur","A dangerous plain, fierce like a leopard"],"a":1,"hint":"Picture a leopard’s coat. Now picture the dry plain from far away. What do the two have in common?","why":"‘Tawny’ means golden-brown. Seen far and wide, the sun-dried plain is golden-brown with darker patches, just like a leopard’s coat! It’s about colour and pattern — no real leopards here."},{"c":"rain-who-welcomes","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"How does the rain help the sick man who looks out from his chamber?","o":["It washes his dusty room clean","It wakes him from a long sleep","His fevered brain grows calm again","It brings a doctor to his door"],"a":2,"hint":"He can feel the cool ‘breath’ of each little pool. What does coolness do for a fever?","why":"Watching the rain and feeling its coolness, the sick man’s ‘fevered brain / Grows calm again’, and he blesses the rain. Nature’s own medicine on a hot day!"},{"c":"rain-who-welcomes","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"After a downpour, kids float paper boats in the puddles outside school. Which part of the poem does this match?","o":["The oxen in the furrowed land","The sick man’s ‘twisted brooks’","The ‘overflowing spout’","The schoolboys’ ‘mimic fleets’"],"a":3,"hint":"A ‘fleet’ is a group of ships. To ‘mimic’ something is to copy it.","why":"The boys rush out of school, noisier than usual, and sail their ‘mimic fleets’ (toy boats) down the wet streets — until the ‘treacherous pool’ swallows them up!"},{"c":"rain-who-welcomes","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In the furrowed fields, what do the patient oxen seem thankful for?","o":["A rest after their hard work","Extra food from the farmer","A muddy bath to play in","Freedom from the yoke for ever"],"a":0,"hint":"Look for the word ‘toil’ in that stanza. What do the oxen get after it?","why":"‘For this rest in the furrow after toil’ — the tired oxen breathe the clover-scented air and their large, shining eyes seem to thank God. They still wear the yoke, so not freedom!"},{"c":"rain-word-meanings","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The schoolboys come out ‘with more than their wonted noise’. Here ‘wonted’ means…","o":["wanted","usual","unwanted","frightened"],"a":1,"hint":"Careful — it only LOOKS like another word! Try each meaning in the line: ‘more than their ___ noise’.","why":"‘Wonted’ means usual or normal. The rain makes the boys even noisier than usual! ‘Wanted’ looks alike but is a totally different word."},{"c":"rain-word-meanings","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Which word from the poem is matched with its meaning AS USED IN THE POEM?","o":["tramp — a homeless wanderer","fevered — cool and calm","chamber — a room, a bedroom","treacherous — full of treasure"],"a":2,"hint":"In this poem, ‘tramp’ is a sound you hear, not a person you meet.","why":"A chamber is a room — the sick man looks out from his bedroom. In the poem ‘tramp’ is the sound of heavy steps, ‘fevered’ means unwell and ‘treacherous’ means not to be trusted."},{"c":"rain-poetic-devices","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"‘Clatters’ and ‘roars’ sound like the noises they describe. This poetic device is called…","o":["simile","personification","alliteration","onomatopoeia"],"a":3,"hint":"It’s the long name for sound words like buzz, splash and bang.","why":"Onomatopoeia = words that imitate sounds. You can almost HEAR the rain clatter on the roofs and roar down the gutter! A simile would need ‘like’ or ‘as’."},{"c":"rain-poetic-devices","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if Longfellow had written ‘the end of the overflowing spout’ instead of ‘the throat’? What would the line lose?","o":["Personification — the spout seems alive","Its rhyme between ‘out’ and ‘spout’","Its simile about the tramp of hoofs","Nothing at all — it means the same"],"a":0,"hint":"Swap ‘throat’ for ‘end’ and read the line aloud. The rhyme stays — so what goes missing?","why":"‘Throat’ makes the spout seem alive, with rain gushing and struggling out of it. That’s personification! ‘Spout’ still ends the line, so the rhyme with ‘out’ would stay."},{"c":"harold-nest-fall","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In ‘Harold: Our Hornbill’, what did Harold’s father use to seal up the nest in the hollow tree trunk?","o":["Leaves, feathers and silk","Earth, sticks and dung","Stones and wet sand","Twigs and spider webs"],"a":1,"hint":"Harold’s father needed things that would stick together and dry hard, like a wall.","why":"Harold’s father walled up the hollow with earth, sticks and dung, making a closed room where the egg could be laid and Harold hatched. Smelly, but strong!"},{"c":"harold-nest-fall","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if the civet cat had never broken into the nest? Which of these would most likely NOT have happened?","o":["Harold being born","The egg being laid in the nest","Harold’s father sealing the nest","Harold joining the narrator’s family"],"a":3,"hint":"Which event only happened BECAUSE Harold fell out during the fight?","why":"The sealed nest, the egg and Harold’s birth all came BEFORE the civet. Its attack made Harold fall out — and that’s how he came to the narrator and Grandfather."},{"c":"harold-nest-fall","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"Of these four events, which happens LAST in Harold’s story?","o":["Harold falls out of the tree hollow","A civet cat thumps on the nest wall","The verandah enclosure is no longer needed","Grandfather and the narrator take over"],"a":2,"hint":"The civet cat’s attack started it all. What did it cause, and what came after that?","why":"Civet thumps on the wall → Harold falls out → Grandfather and the narrator raise him → he grows up, so his verandah enclosure isn’t needed any more."},{"c":"harold-new-family","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"How did young Harold feel about the narrator and Grandfather, and how did that change?","o":["Nervous at first, then took them as parents","Friendly at first, then flew off","Angry at first, then pecked them daily","Calm at first, then scared of them"],"a":0,"hint":"Harold had just tumbled from his nest and been hurt. How would a baby feel among strangers at first?","why":"Like a baby far from home, Harold was nervous at first. But Grandfather and the narrator gave him food and shelter, and he soon accepted them as his parents."},{"c":"harold-new-family","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"How did Harold eat a rice ball?","o":["He pecked it into tiny crumbs","Caught it, tossed it up, let it drop in","He soaked it in water first","He hid it away to eat later"],"a":1,"hint":"Hornbills have huge beaks. How could Harold get a rice ball from the tip right into his mouth?","why":"Harold would catch a rice ball, flip it up into the air and gulp it down as it fell — a juggler at mealtimes!"},{"c":"harold-new-family","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Harold offered the narrator beetles and insects. What does this show? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Teasing — he knew boys hate beetles","Fear — he paid in food to be left alone","Love — he shared like family does","Fullness — beetles tasted bad to him"],"a":2,"hint":"Would you share your favourite snack with someone you didn’t care about?","why":"Harold liked insects, so offering them was a real gift. Sharing his food shows he treated the narrator as family — a hornbill’s way of saying ‘I like you’!"},{"c":"harold-rain-habits","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What did Harold start doing about an hour before it rained?","o":["Hiding inside his enclosure","Flapping round in circles","Going completely silent","Chuckling to himself"],"a":3,"hint":"Bond makes Harold seem almost like a person here — and one family member found it irritating.","why":"About an hour before rain, Harold began chuckling to himself — a feathered weather forecaster! Bond describes him almost like a person who laughs."},{"c":"harold-rain-habits","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Who in the story found Harold’s chuckling before the rain irritating?","o":["Aunt Ruby","Grandfather","The narrator","The civet cat"],"a":0,"hint":"This person often got caught out in the rain.","why":"Aunt Ruby often got caught in the rain, so a bird chuckling before every shower was the last thing she wanted to hear!"},{"c":"harold-rain-habits","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"As storm clouds gather and the wind blows hard, Harold’s chuckle turns into an eerie whistle. Why? Pick answer AND reason.","o":["Scared — he was afraid of thunder","Excited — he loved the rain","Sick — the cold wind hurt his throat","Angry — rain spoiled his feathers"],"a":1,"hint":"Remember what the narrator wishes Harold could have ‘all’ of at the very end.","why":"The whistle shows excitement — Harold loved rain! ‘Eerie’ means strange and spooky-sounding, but here it’s a happy sound, not a frightened one."},{"c":"harold-looks-love","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What did Harold get from his ‘rouge pot’ to make himself look good?","o":["Red berry juice for his beak","Mud to clean his wings","A rich yellow fluid","Oil to keep his nest dry"],"a":2,"hint":"‘Rouge’ is make-up — but Harold’s wasn’t red! He rubbed it on his feathers and neck.","why":"Harold’s ‘rouge pot’ gave him a rich yellow fluid, which he spread over his feathers and neck. Bond writes as if Harold cared about his looks like a person!"},{"c":"harold-looks-love","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"As a baby, Harold was no beauty. Did his looks matter to the narrator’s family? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — they only kept good-looking birds","Yes — they waited till he grew feathers","No — they planned to free him at once","No — they cared for him as their own"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of how parents feel about a newborn who isn’t exactly cute.","why":"As a featherless baby Harold was no beauty, but that didn’t matter. Grandfather and the narrator cared for the weak little bird just like parents."},{"c":"harold-looks-love","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Years later, the narrator hopes that in heaven Harold has tennis balls to catch and plenty of rain. What does this show?","o":["He still remembers Harold with love","He hopes Harold will learn tennis","He is sorry he ever kept Harold","He thinks Harold still lives on the verandah"],"a":0,"hint":"He’s grown up now — yet he still thinks of Harold’s favourite things.","why":"Catching things and rain were Harold’s great joys. Wishing him these, years later, shows the narrator’s love for Harold never faded."},{"c":"george-watch-mixup","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"When George woke it was dark, yet he was sure it was morning. Why didn’t the darkness give him a clue?","o":["He never opened his curtains","He was still half dreaming","Winter fog made mornings dark too","London had no sunrise in winter"],"a":2,"hint":"Think about the season and the weather that week.","why":"It was winter, very near the shortest day, after a week of fog — so even eight in the morning would have been dark. The darkness gave George no clue at all!"},{"c":"george-watch-mixup","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if George had wound up his watch before going to bed? What would most likely have happened?","o":["He’d have been late for work in the City","He’d have seen it was night and slept on","He’d still have dashed off to Holborn","His watch would have stopped at nine"],"a":1,"hint":"A working watch would have shown the REAL time when he woke.","why":"The adventure began because the stopped watch said a quarter past eight. A wound watch would have shown the middle of the night, and George could have gone back to sleep!"},{"c":"george-watch-mixup","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which sentence about George’s adventure is WRONG?","o":["His watch stopped at a quarter past eight","He thought it was morning, not night","He found no fire and no breakfast ready","He was actually very late for work"],"a":3,"hint":"Check each sentence against the time the clock at Holborn struck.","why":"George wasn’t late at all — he was nearly six hours EARLY! That’s the joke: he skipped breakfast and raced through London for nothing."},{"c":"george-empty-streets","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Why were the London streets almost empty when George rushed out? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Night — it had only just gone 3 a.m.","A holiday — the shops were shut for it","Fog — people chose to stay in bed","Rain — everyone was sheltering indoors"],"a":0,"hint":"Remember what the policeman told George to listen to.","why":"It was only just after three in the morning! George thought the fog had kept people lazily in bed, but they were simply asleep — as he should have been."},{"c":"george-empty-streets","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What did George find when he reached Holborn?","o":["Crowds of office workers","Shops just opening their shutters","Shops shut, no buses, and a policeman","Mrs. G. buying his breakfast"],"a":2,"hint":"Holborn is busy by day. Was anything open yet?","why":"‘Not a shutter was down! not a bus was about!’ Every shop was still closed. Just three men (one a policeman), a cart of cabbages and a shabby cab."},{"c":"george-empty-streets","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A clock strikes, and George says in an injured tone, ‘But it’s only gone three!’ How many strikes was he expecting?","o":["Eight","Nine","Twelve","Seven"],"a":1,"hint":"Look at what his watch said when he reached Holborn.","why":"His watch showed five to nine, so George wanted nine. ‘How many did you want it to go?’ asks the policeman — as if clocks strike to order! It was really just after three."},{"c":"george-back-home","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"Put George’s night in the right order.","o":["Cold bath → chess → Holborn → policeman","Policeman → cold bath → chess → Holborn","Chess → Holborn → cold bath → policeman","Cold bath → Holborn → policeman → chess"],"a":3,"hint":"He got ready for work before he left home. Was the chess game before or after he learned the real time?","why":"Cold bath and a cold-water shave → out with no breakfast → Holborn and the policeman → home again, where he lit the lamp and played chess against himself."},{"c":"george-back-home","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Back home, George tried to make breakfast to pass the time. Why did he give up?","o":["He feared Mrs. G. would think he was a burglar","There was no food left in the house","The policeman had told him not to","He had to leave for work at once"],"a":0,"hint":"Two plain-clothes constables were watching the house from across the street…","why":"He kept dropping things and making a racket. He feared Mrs. G. would think burglars had got in and call ‘Police!’ — and the watching detectives would rush in!"},{"c":"george-back-home","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"On his second walk, George hid in dark doorways when policemen came near. Did it help? Pick the answer AND the reason.","o":["Yes — the police lost sight of him","Yes — it kept him warm in the fog","No — it made the police trust him less","No — Mrs. G. saw him and called the police"],"a":2,"hint":"How would YOU feel about someone ducking into doorways at four in the morning?","why":"Hiding made him look guiltier! The police grew more distrustful, and two plain-clothes constables even walked him home to check he really lived there."},{"c":"george-character-language","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Odd one out: which word does NOT describe George in this episode?","o":["Hasty","Patient","Nervous","Quick to blame others"],"a":1,"hint":"Did George ever stop and calmly check his watch before rushing off?","why":"George rushed out without checking, called Mrs. G. a ‘lazy old woman’, and grew so nervous he pictured himself on trial! Patient is the one word that doesn’t fit."},{"c":"george-character-language","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Most people set alarms so they don’t oversleep. Why is George’s last line — he’s ‘never got up too early since’ — funny?","o":["He was given an official police warning","He was late for work every day after","He never wore a watch again","His lesson is the opposite of the usual one"],"a":3,"hint":"After a fright, do people usually promise not to get up late — or not to get up early?","why":"Most of us vow never to oversleep. George’s ‘warning’ was the reverse: never get up too EARLY! Jerome flips a normal lesson upside down for a laugh."},{"c":"george-character-language","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"At Holborn, George saw ‘a dilapidated looking cab’. What does ‘dilapidated’ mean?","o":["Old and falling apart","Shiny and brand-new","Very fast","Packed with passengers"],"a":0,"hint":"It’s the kind of cab nobody would pick if a better one were waiting.","why":"‘Dilapidated’ means old, shabby and in poor repair. At three in the morning, even the only cab in Holborn looked worn out!"}],"z-maths":[{"c":"compass-construction","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Equilateral triangle on base AB = 4 cm: what radius do you set the compass to for the arcs from A and B?","o":["2 cm — half the base","4 cm","8 cm — double the base","Any radius works"],"a":1,"hint":"In an equilateral triangle, how do the other two sides compare with the base?","why":"All sides must be 4 cm. Arcs of 4 cm from A and from B cross at a point 4 cm from both — that's C! With 2 cm arcs they'd only touch on the base."},{"c":"compass-construction","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Base AB = 4 cm. An arc of 5 cm from A and an arc of 6 cm from B cross at C. So AC and BC are…","o":["Both 5.5 cm — C is in the middle","Both 4 cm, same as the base","Unknown until you measure them","5 cm and 6 cm — C is on both arcs"],"a":3,"hint":"Every point on an arc is the same distance from the arc's centre.","why":"C sits on the 5 cm arc around A, so AC = 5 cm, and on the 6 cm arc around B, so BC = 6 cm. That's why the crossing point is the third vertex — no guessing!"},{"c":"compass-construction","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"For a 4, 5, 6 cm triangle, Riya draws AB = 4 cm, then an arc of 4 cm from A and 6 cm from B. What went wrong?","o":["The arc from A should be 5 cm","The base must be the longest side","Both arcs must be the same radius","Nothing — she gets a 4, 5, 6 triangle"],"a":0,"hint":"Write down the three side lengths her construction actually gives.","why":"Her arcs make AC = 4 cm and BC = 6 cm, so she gets a 4, 4, 6 triangle. The arc from A must be 5 cm. Any side can be the base — the textbook itself used 4 cm."},{"c":"triangle-inequality","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Only ONE of these sets of lengths can make a triangle. Which one?","o":["2, 3, 6","4, 4, 9","3, 5, 8","5, 7, 11"],"a":3,"hint":"Add the two smaller numbers. Is the sum MORE than the biggest?","why":"5 + 7 = 12 beats 11. The others fail: 2 + 3 < 6, 4 + 4 < 9, and 3 + 5 = 8 exactly — that lies flat as a straight line, not a triangle."},{"c":"triangle-inequality","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Tent to tree is 30 m. Tent to pole is 12 m. Could the pole be just 15 m from the tree?","o":["Yes — any distance works on open land","Yes — if the pole is between them","Only if the pole is really tall","No — the detour would beat the direct path"],"a":3,"hint":"Which is always the shortest way from the tent to the tree?","why":"Tent → pole → tree would be 12 + 15 = 27 m, shorter than the straight 30 m path. But the straight path is ALWAYS the shortest! So 15 m is impossible."},{"c":"triangle-inequality","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Arjun checks 3, 5, 10: “3 is less than 5 + 10, so it IS a triangle!” Spot his mistake.","o":["Nothing — one check is always enough","He should check 3 + 5 + 10 = 18","He skipped the longest: 10 > 3 + 5","A triangle can't have an odd side"],"a":2,"hint":"Small sides pass easily. Which side is the real test?","why":"The shortest side ALWAYS passes, so checking it proves nothing. The real test is the longest: 10 is more than 3 + 5 = 8, so the two short sides can never meet. No triangle!"},{"c":"triangle-inequality","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"To check if three lengths make a triangle, which ONE comparison is enough?","o":["Shortest < sum of the other two","All three must be equal","Longest < sum of the other two","The total must be over 10"],"a":2,"hint":"Which side has the hardest job of being shorter than the other two put together?","why":"If even the longest side is less than the other two added, the smaller sides pass for sure. For 4, 5, 8 the textbook only checks 8 < 4 + 5 = 9."},{"c":"triangle-inequality","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Can 1 cm, 100 cm and 100 cm be the sides of a triangle? Pick the answer WITH the right reason.","o":["No — 1 cm is far too tiny","Yes — 100 is less than 1 + 100","No — two sides can't be equal","Yes — the total is over 200 cm"],"a":1,"hint":"Test the longest side against the sum of the other two.","why":"The longest side is 100, and 1 + 100 = 101 is bigger. So yes — a tall, skinny isosceles triangle! A tiny side is fine as long as the inequality holds."},{"c":"third-side-range","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Two sides of a triangle are 4 cm and 9 cm. Which could be the third side?","o":["8 cm","5 cm","13 cm","3 cm"],"a":0,"hint":"Try each one: is the longest side still less than the other two added?","why":"The third side must be between 5 and 13 cm. 8 cm works! At exactly 5 cm or 13 cm the shape goes flat, and 3 cm can't reach (3 + 4 < 9)."},{"c":"third-side-range","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Two sides of a triangle are 1 cm and 100 cm. The third side must be…","o":["Anything above 100 cm","Exactly 100 cm","Anything between 1 and 100 cm","Strictly between 99 and 101 cm"],"a":3,"hint":"Test the edges: would 99 cm work? Would 101 cm?","why":"At 101 cm, 1 + 100 only equals it — flat. At 99 cm, 1 + 99 only equals 100 — flat too. So it must be strictly between: 99.5, 100 or 100.7 all work!"},{"c":"third-side-range","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Two sides of a triangle are both 5 cm. What is the BIGGEST whole-number length the third side can be?","o":["10 cm","5 cm","25 cm","9 cm"],"a":3,"hint":"What happens to the triangle when the third side equals 5 + 5?","why":"The third side must be less than 5 + 5 = 10 cm. At exactly 10 cm the two sides lie flat along it. So 9 cm is the biggest whole number that still works."},{"c":"circles-meeting","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Base = longest side. Circles with the two smaller lengths as radii are drawn from its ends. A triangle forms when they…","o":["Cross each other at two points","Just touch at one point","Don't meet at all","Have the same centre"],"a":0,"hint":"The third vertex must lie on BOTH circles, off the base line.","why":"When the circles cross, each crossing point is the right distance from both ends — a vertex! Just touching gives a point ON the base (flat); not meeting gives nothing."},{"c":"circles-meeting","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Base AB = 9 cm. A circle of radius 4 cm is drawn from A and one of 5 cm from B. What happens?","o":["They touch at one point — no triangle","They cross — a thin triangle forms","They don't meet at all","They cross at four points"],"a":0,"hint":"Compare 4 + 5 with the base length.","why":"4 + 5 = 9 exactly, so the circles just kiss at one point on AB. All three ‘corners’ sit on one straight line — a squashed, flat shape, not a triangle."},{"c":"circles-meeting","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if A, B and C lie on one straight line, with B between them, AB = 3 cm and BC = 5 cm?","o":["AC = 8 cm — a very thin triangle","AC = 2 cm — a flat triangle","AC = 8 cm, and it is not a triangle","AC can be any length"],"a":2,"hint":"Walk from A to B, then keep going to C. How far did you go?","why":"AC = 3 + 5 = 8 cm, so the direct path equals the detour. The sides enclose no space and no corners open up — it's just a line segment, not a triangle."},{"c":"circles-meeting","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Circles of radius 6 cm and 4 cm, centred at A and B, just touch at a point ON the base AB. How long is AB?","o":["24 cm","5 cm","10 cm","6 cm"],"a":2,"hint":"The touching point splits AB into two pieces. How long is each piece?","why":"From A to the touching point is one radius (6 cm); from there to B is the other (4 cm). AB = 6 + 4 = 10 cm — the ‘two smaller = longest’ case, so no triangle."},{"c":"included-angle","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In “two sides and the included angle”, the included angle is…","o":["The biggest angle of the triangle","The angle facing the longest side","The angle between those two sides","Any one angle you choose"],"a":2,"hint":"In the textbook example, sides AB and AC were given with ∠A. Where does ∠A sit?","why":"The included angle is formed where the two given sides meet — it sits between them. For sides AB and AC, it's ∠A."},{"c":"included-angle","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"To build ∆ABC with AB = 5 cm, ∠A = 45°, AC = 4 cm, you first draw AB. What comes next?","o":["Draw a 4 cm arc from B","Join B to C","Make a 45° angle at A","Make a 45° angle at B"],"a":2,"hint":"Which vertex does the 45° belong to? Which side starts from there?","why":"Next make ∠A = 45° at A, mark C on that new arm so AC = 4 cm, then join BC. A 4 cm arc from B would make BC = 4 cm — the wrong side!"},{"c":"included-angle","t":"What if","p":50,"q":"What if two sides of 4 cm and 6 cm have an included angle of 180°?","o":["A very wide, flat triangle","A right-angled triangle","An equilateral triangle","They form a straight line — no triangle"],"a":3,"hint":"What does a 180° angle look like?","why":"180° is a straight angle, so the two sides line up into one 10 cm straight line. There's no third corner! Any included angle less than 180° works fine."},{"c":"two-angles-rule","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Two angles and the side between them are given. When does a triangle exist?","o":["When they add to exactly 180°","Only when both are less than 90°","Only when the two angles are equal","When the angles add to less than 180°"],"a":3,"hint":"Think of 120° and 30° — would those arms meet?","why":"If the two angles total less than 180°, the arms lean towards each other and meet. 120° + 30° = 150° works, so both needn't be under 90°. At 180° or more they never meet."},{"c":"two-angles-rule","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Base AB with ∠A = 40°. Which ∠B makes the arms from A and B NEVER meet?","o":["140°","130°","100°","90°"],"a":0,"hint":"Arms that never meet are parallel. Which angles on the same side of a transversal add to 180°?","why":"At ∠B = 140°, 40° + 140° = 180°, so the arm from B is parallel to the arm from A — parallel lines never meet! At 130°, 100° or 90° they still meet at a C."},{"c":"two-angles-rule","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"A triangle has angles 60° and 70° on a 5 cm base. What if you redraw it on a 7 cm base with the same two angles?","o":["The third angle gets bigger","The third angle stays 50°","The third angle gets smaller","The third angle becomes 70°"],"a":1,"hint":"Does the base length appear anywhere in the angle sum rule?","why":"The third angle is 180° − 60° − 70° = 50°, whatever the base. A longer base gives a bigger triangle of the same shape — like zooming in on a video frame."},{"c":"angle-sum","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Two angles of a triangle are 48° and 67°. What is the third angle?","o":["65°","115°","75°","245°"],"a":0,"hint":"Add the two angles first, then think about what all three must total.","why":"48° + 67° = 115°, and 180° − 115° = 65°. 115° is just the sum so far, and 245° comes from wrongly using 360°."},{"c":"angle-sum","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{Z} designs a triangle logo with one 90° corner and one 35° corner. The third corner must be…","o":["65°","125°","45°","55°"],"a":3,"hint":"Every triangle, even a logo, has the same angle total.","why":"90° + 35° = 125°, and 180° − 125° = 55°. A designer can change size and colour freely, but a triangle's angles always add to 180°!"},{"c":"angle-sum","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Meera draws a triangle and labels its angles 50°, 60° and 80°. What's wrong?","o":["Nothing — all three are acute","A triangle needs a 90° angle","They add to 190°, not 180°","Two angles must always be equal"],"a":2,"hint":"Add up all three labels.","why":"50 + 60 + 80 = 190°. Every triangle's angles total exactly 180°, so a label is off by 10°. Triangles don't need a 90° angle or equal angles."},{"c":"angle-sum-proof","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"To prove the angle sum is 180°, the textbook draws a line through the top vertex that is…","o":["Perpendicular to the base","Parallel to the base","Through the midpoint of the base","As long as the base"],"a":1,"hint":"It's the same idea used to show two angles must add to less than 180°.","why":"A line XY through A, parallel to BC, makes copies of ∠B and ∠C right at A. This clever idea is in Euclid's ‘The Elements’, from about 300 BCE!"},{"c":"angle-sum-proof","t":"Connect","p":50,"q":"XY is drawn through A parallel to BC. Why is ∠XAB equal to ∠B?","o":["They are corresponding angles","They are alternate angles","They are vertically opposite","They are both exterior angles"],"a":1,"hint":"AB cuts across the two parallel lines. The two angles sit on opposite sides of AB.","why":"AB is a transversal of the parallel lines XY and BC. ∠XAB and ∠ABC lie between the lines on opposite sides of AB — alternate angles, so they're equal."},{"c":"angle-sum-proof","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Fold the three corners of a paper triangle so their tips meet at one point on the base. Together they form…","o":["A full circle","A right angle","A straight line","Three-quarters of a turn"],"a":2,"hint":"What do the three angles of a triangle always add up to? Which option is that size?","why":"The three corners fit side by side into a straight angle — 180°. It's a quick paper check of the angle sum property, no protractor needed!"},{"c":"equal-angles","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"In ∆ABC, ∠B = ∠C and ∠A = 40°. What is ∠B?","o":["40°","70°","140°","50°"],"a":1,"hint":"Take ∠A away from 180°, then share what's left equally.","why":"180° − 40° = 140° is shared equally by ∠B and ∠C, so each is 70°. 140° is the total for both — don't forget to split it!"},{"c":"equal-angles","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"If all three angles of a triangle are equal, each angle must be…","o":["90°","60°","45°","120°"],"a":1,"hint":"Split the angle total into three equal parts.","why":"180° ÷ 3 = 60°. That's why an equilateral triangle has three 60° angles. Three 90° angles would need 270° — impossible!"},{"c":"equal-angles","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Sam wants a triangle with all three angles equal to 70°. Can he make it?","o":["No — 3 × 70° = 210°","Yes — it would be equilateral","Yes — all its angles are acute","Only if its sides are 70 cm"],"a":0,"hint":"Multiply, then compare with the angle total.","why":"Three 70° angles total 210°, more than 180°, so no such triangle exists. If two angles are 70°, the third must be 180° − 140° = 40°."},{"c":"exterior-angle","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"An exterior angle of a triangle is formed between…","o":["Two sides, inside the triangle","A side and the extension of another side","A side and an altitude","Two altitudes of the triangle"],"a":1,"hint":"‘Exterior’ means outside. Stretch one side beyond a corner.","why":"Extend side BC past C to D. The angle between CD and side CA — ∠ACD — lies outside the triangle. That's an exterior angle."},{"c":"exterior-angle","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"In ∆ABC, ∠A = 45° and ∠B = 75°. BC is extended to D. Find the exterior angle ∠ACD.","o":["120°","60°","135°","105°"],"a":0,"hint":"Find ∠ACB first. ∠ACB and ∠ACD sit together on a straight line.","why":"∠ACB = 180° − 45° − 75° = 60°, so ∠ACD = 180° − 60° = 120°. Shortcut: exterior angle = ∠A + ∠B = 45° + 75°. 60° is the inside angle, not the outside one."},{"c":"exterior-angle","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"In ∆ABC, BC is extended to D. The exterior angle ∠ACD is 130° and ∠A = 70°. What is ∠B?","o":["50°","110°","70°","60°"],"a":3,"hint":"Find ∠ACB from the straight line first.","why":"∠ACB = 180° − 130° = 50°, so ∠B = 180° − 70° − 50° = 60°. Shortcut: exterior angle = ∠A + ∠B, so 130° = 70° + 60°. 50° is ∠ACB, not ∠B."},{"c":"altitude","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"An altitude of a triangle is a line segment from a vertex that is…","o":["Through the midpoint of the opposite side","Parallel to the opposite side","Perpendicular to the opposite side","Equal to the longest side"],"a":2,"hint":"Think of how you measure a person's height — straight up from the floor.","why":"An altitude drops at 90° from a vertex to the opposite side (or its extension). A line to the midpoint is different — it only matches in special triangles."},{"c":"altitude","t":"What if","p":50,"q":"∆ABC has a right angle at B. Which side is ALSO the altitude from A to BC?","o":["AC","BC","AB","No side can be an altitude"],"a":2,"hint":"Which side already meets BC at 90°?","why":"Since ∠B = 90°, AB is already perpendicular to BC — so AB is the altitude from A! In right triangles, a side can double as an altitude."},{"c":"altitude","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"∆ABC has an obtuse angle at B. Where does the altitude from A to BC land?","o":["Outside, on BC extended past B","Inside, between B and C","Exactly at the midpoint of BC","Exactly on vertex C"],"a":0,"hint":"B's wide angle pushes A out sideways. Where is the spot directly ‘below’ A?","why":"The wide angle at B leans A out past B, so the foot of the perpendicular is beyond B. We extend CB and drop the altitude there — outside the triangle."},{"c":"triangle-types","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A triangle with sides 6 cm, 6 cm and 9 cm is…","o":["Equilateral","Scalene","Impossible to draw","Isosceles"],"a":3,"hint":"Count how many sides are equal. Then check it can exist at all.","why":"Two equal sides make it isosceles. It can exist, because 9 < 6 + 6 = 12. Scalene needs three different lengths; equilateral needs all three equal."},{"c":"triangle-types","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Tia says: “An acute-angled triangle is one that has an acute angle.” What's the mistake?","o":["It needs exactly two acute angles","All three angles must be acute","Acute means exactly 90°","Nothing — Tia is right"],"a":1,"hint":"Does a right-angled triangle also have acute angles?","why":"Every triangle has at least two acute angles — even right and obtuse ones! So ‘acute-angled’ needs ALL THREE angles less than 90°."},{"c":"triangle-types","t":"What if","p":50,"q":"Can an equilateral triangle ever be right-angled?","o":["Yes — if it is big enough","No — every angle is always 60°","Yes — just turn it on its side","Only if one side is a bit longer"],"a":1,"hint":"What are the angles of an equilateral triangle?","why":"Equal sides mean equal angles: 180° ÷ 3 = 60° each, at any size or position. So it can never have a 90° (or obtuse) angle."},{"c":"triangle-types","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"Which of these triangles is IMPOSSIBLE?","o":["One with two obtuse angles","A right-angled isosceles triangle","An obtuse-angled isosceles triangle","An acute-angled scalene triangle"],"a":0,"hint":"Try giving real angles to each one. Do they add to 180°?","why":"Two obtuse angles already add to more than 180°. The rest exist: 90°, 45°, 45° is right isosceles; 120°, 30°, 30° is obtuse isosceles; 50°, 60°, 70° is acute scalene."},{"c":"ws-angle-ratio","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: a triangle's angles are in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4. What are they, and what kind of triangle is it?","o":["40°, 60°, 80° — acute-angled","20°, 30°, 40° — acute-angled","45°, 60°, 75° — acute-angled","40°, 60°, 80° — obtuse-angled"],"a":0,"hint":"2 + 3 + 4 = 9 parts make 180°.","why":"180° ÷ 9 = 20°, so the angles are 40°, 60° and 80°. All are less than 90°, so it is acute-angled."},{"c":"ws-angle-ratio","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: an exterior angle is 135°, and the two opposite interior angles are in the ratio 2 : 3. Find them.","o":["18° and 27°","54° and 81°","45° and 90°","72° and 108°"],"a":1,"hint":"The two opposite interior angles add up to the exterior angle.","why":"135° is split 2 : 3 → 5 parts of 27°. So 54° and 81° (and 54 + 81 = 135)."},{"c":"ws-isosceles","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: a triangle has two angles of 65°. The third angle, and its type by sides?","o":["65° — equilateral","50° — isosceles","50° — equilateral","115° — scalene"],"a":1,"hint":"Angle sum is 180°. Equal angles sit opposite equal sides.","why":"180 − 65 − 65 = 50°. Two equal angles mean two equal sides, so it is isosceles."},{"c":"ws-isosceles","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: in △PQR, PQ = PR and ∠P = 44°. Find ∠Q and ∠R.","o":["44° and 92°","68° and 68°","46° and 90°","72° and 64°"],"a":1,"hint":"Angles opposite equal sides are equal.","why":"∠Q = ∠R because PQ = PR. So 2 × ∠Q = 180 − 44 = 136, and ∠Q = ∠R = 68°."},{"c":"ws-isosceles","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: a roof has two equal sides with 80° between them. The other two angles?","o":["40° and 60° — scalene","50° and 50° — equilateral","80° and 20° — isosceles","50° and 50° — an isosceles triangle"],"a":3,"hint":"The base angles of an isosceles triangle are equal.","why":"180 − 80 = 100, shared equally: 50° each."},{"c":"ws-isosceles","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: in that roof (80°, 50°, 50°), what is the exterior angle next to a 50° angle?","o":["100°","50°","80°","130°"],"a":3,"hint":"An exterior angle and its interior angle make a straight line.","why":"180 − 50 = 130°. Check: it equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles, 80 + 50."},{"c":"ws-isosceles","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: a student says a triangle with angles 90°, 45°, 45° is both right-angled and isosceles. Is that right?","o":["Yes — one 90° angle and two equal angles","No — those angles are impossible","No — it is equilateral","No — a triangle can't be two types"],"a":0,"hint":"Look at the angles one way, then the other.","why":"It has a right angle, and two equal 45° angles mean two equal sides. So it is a right isosceles triangle."},{"c":"ws-exterior-angle","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: an exterior angle is 125° and one opposite interior angle is 55°. The other one?","o":["55°","110°","125°","70°"],"a":3,"hint":"Exterior angle = sum of the two opposite interior angles.","why":"125 − 55 = 70°."},{"c":"ws-exterior-angle","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: in △ABC the exterior angle at C is 120° and ∠A = 50°. Find ∠B and the interior ∠C.","o":["∠B = 10°, ∠C = 60°","∠B = 70°, ∠C = 60°","∠B = 70°, ∠C = 120°","∠B = 60°, ∠C = 70°"],"a":1,"hint":"Exterior angle property for ∠B; a straight line for ∠C.","why":"∠B = 120 − 50 = 70°, and the interior ∠C = 180 − 120 = 60°. Check: 50 + 70 + 60 = 180."},{"c":"ws-angle-sum","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: can a triangle have angles 75°, 65° and 50°?","o":["No — 75° is too big","Yes — any three angles work","No — they add up to 190°","Yes — all are less than 90°"],"a":2,"hint":"Add them up.","why":"75 + 65 + 50 = 190°, but a triangle's angles must add up to exactly 180°."},{"c":"ws-angle-sum","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: a road sign has angles x°, (x + 20)° and 2x°. Find x and the angles.","o":["x = 50: 50°, 70°, 100°","x = 40: 40°, 60°, 80°","x = 45: 45°, 65°, 90°","x = 40: 40°, 20°, 80°"],"a":1,"hint":"x + (x + 20) + 2x = 180.","why":"4x + 20 = 180, so 4x = 160 and x = 40. The angles are 40°, 60° and 80°."},{"c":"ws-angle-sum","t":"School sheet","p":50,"q":"Teacher's sheet: in that road sign (40°, 60°, 80°), the exterior angle next to the largest angle is…","o":["100°","120°","80°","140°"],"a":0,"hint":"Straight line: 180° minus the interior angle.","why":"180 − 80 = 100°, which also equals 40 + 60."},{"c":"ws-triangle-inequality","t":"School sheet","p":100,"q":"Teacher's sheet: two sides are 5 cm and 8 cm. Can the third side be 14 cm?","o":["Yes — any length works","No — 5 + 8 = 13, which is less than 14","No — it must be exactly 13 cm","Yes — 14 is more than 8"],"a":1,"hint":"Any two sides must add up to MORE than the third.","why":"5 + 8 = 13 < 14, so the two short sides can't reach. The third side must be more than 3 cm and less than 13 cm."}],"z-muha":[{"c":"muha-dikhna-nazar","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“ईद का चाँद होना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["बहुत सुंदर दिखना","बहुत दिनों बाद दिखाई देना","रात को देर तक जागना","त्योहार की ख़ुशी मनाना"],"a":1,"hint":"People search the sky for the thin Eid moon. Is it easy to spot, or rare?","why":"ईद का चाँद होना = to be seen very rarely. People wait and wait to spot the thin Eid moon — just like a friend who hardly ever shows up!"},{"c":"muha-dikhna-nazar","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"चाचू पूरे एक साल बाद घर आए। उनके इतने कम आने पर दादाजी ने कौन-सी बात कही होगी?","o":["अरे, तुम तो ईद का चाँद हो गए!","अरे, तुम तो आँखों के तारे हो!","अरे, तुम तो टेढ़ी खीर हो!","अरे, तुम तो पानी-पानी हो गए!"],"a":0,"hint":"Dadaji is teasing Chachu about his rare visits, not praising him.","why":"ईद का चाँद होना = to be seen very rarely. आँखों का तारा = very dear (sweet, but not about rare visits); पानी-पानी होना = to feel ashamed."},{"c":"muha-pyaar-khushi","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“बहुत प्यारा होना” — इसके लिए सही मुहावरा कौन-सा है?","o":["आँखें चुराना","ईद का चाँद होना","आँखों का तारा होना","टेढ़ी खीर"],"a":2,"hint":"प्यारा = dear, loved. Two options are about eyes — only one is about love.","why":"आँखों का तारा = the apple of one’s eye, very dear. आँखें चुराना = to avoid someone’s gaze; ईद का चाँद = seen rarely; टेढ़ी खीर = a tough task."},{"c":"muha-pyaar-khushi","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"किस वाक्य में “आँखों का तारा” का सही प्रयोग हुआ है?","o":["नन्हा पोता दादी की आँखों का तारा है।","रात को छत से आँखों का तारा दिखा।","धूल पड़ी तो आँखों का तारा चमका।","डर के मारे वह आँखों का तारा बन गया।"],"a":0,"hint":"A मुहावरा never means its literal words. Which sentence is about love, not real stars?","why":"आँखों का तारा = very dear, the apple of one’s eye. A grandson and his Dadi — perfect fit! The others treat it as a real star or tie it to fear — both wrong."},{"c":"muha-bhaagna-bachna","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"गेंद से पड़ोसी का शीशा टूटा, तो सारे बच्चे फ़ौरन ___।","o":["नौ दो ग्यारह हो गए","फूले न समाए","आग बबूला हो गए","आँखों के तारे बन गए"],"a":0,"hint":"What do naughty kids do the moment a window breaks? Think speed!","why":"नौ दो ग्यारह होना = to run away fast. 9 + 2 = 11, and the kids vanished just as quickly! (आग बबूला = furious — that’s the neighbour’s job.)"},{"c":"muha-bhaagna-bachna","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“नौ दो ग्यारह होना” — इसका सबसे नज़दीकी English अर्थ कौन-सा है?","o":["To add numbers quickly","To run away fast","To be at sixes and sevens","To be on cloud nine"],"a":1,"hint":"Not maths! Read this: “चोर पुलिस को देखकर नौ दो ग्यारह हो गया।” What did the thief do?","why":"नौ दो ग्यारह होना = to run away, vanish fast. “At sixes and sevens” = confused; “on cloud nine” = super happy. Numbers fool you in both languages!"},{"c":"muha-dar-sharm-hairani","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“बहुत घबरा जाना” के लिए सही मुहावरा कौन-सा है?","o":["हाथ पर हाथ धरे बैठना","फूला न समाना","पेट में चूहे दौड़ना","हाथ-पैर फूलना"],"a":3,"hint":"घबराना = to panic. फूलना appears twice — one is about panic, the other about joy.","why":"हाथ-पैर फूलना = to panic, get nervous. पेट में चूहे दौड़ना is NOT butterflies in the tummy — it means very hungry! हाथ पर हाथ धरे बैठना = to sit idle."},{"c":"muha-dar-sharm-hairani","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"हाथ-पैर फूलना, फूला न समाना, बाल-बाल बचना — किस वाक्य में मुहावरे का गलत प्रयोग है?","o":["जीत की ख़ुशी में उसके हाथ-पैर फूल गए।","शेर देखकर उसके हाथ-पैर फूल गए।","नया बैट पाकर वह फूला न समाया।","तेज़ बाउंसर से वह बाल-बाल बचा।"],"a":0,"hint":"हाथ-पैर फूलना and फूला न समाना sound alike. Which is for fear, which for joy?","why":"हाथ-पैर फूलना = to panic. Nobody panics from the joy of winning — that needs फूला न समाना (overjoyed). बाल-बाल बचना = a narrow escape."},{"c":"muha-mehnat-jeet","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"हमारे गेंदबाज़ों ने विरोधी टीम को सिर्फ़ 50 रन पर आउट कर दिया। उन्होंने विरोधियों के…","o":["कान भर दिए","आँसू पोंछ दिए","दाँतों तले उँगली दबा दी","दाँत खट्टे कर दिए"],"a":3,"hint":"All out for just 50 — the bowlers didn’t just win, they crushed them. Which idiom shows that?","why":"दाँत खट्टे करना = to defeat badly, to thrash. दाँतों तले उँगली दबाना = to be amazed; कान भरना = to tell tales. Same दाँत, very different meanings!"},{"c":"muha-mehnat-jeet","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"दाँत खट्टे करना, कमर कसना, ख़ून-पसीना एक करना — किस वाक्य में मुहावरे का गलत प्रयोग है?","o":["फ़ाइनल के लिए टीम ने कमर कस ली।","फ़ाइनल हारकर हमने उनके दाँत खट्टे किए।","कोच ने ख़ून-पसीना एक कर टीम बनाई।","हमने फ़ाइनल में उनके दाँत खट्टे किए।"],"a":1,"hint":"Read the story inside each sentence. Does one of them contradict itself?","why":"दाँत खट्टे करना = to defeat someone badly. If WE lost the final, we didn’t thrash them — they thrashed us! कमर कसना = to gear up; ख़ून-पसीना एक करना = to toil hard."},{"c":"muha-gussa-shor","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"खिलौना टूटा तो दो साल के चिंटू ने रो-रोकर ___।","o":["आसमान सिर पर उठा लिया","आसमान छू लिया","आँखें चुरा लीं","घी के दीये जलाए"],"a":0,"hint":"Two options have आसमान. One is about reaching great heights; the other is about noise.","why":"आसमान सिर पर उठाना = to raise the roof, make a huge racket. आसमान छूना = to reach great heights — Chintu’s crying was loud, not high-flying!"},{"c":"muha-gussa-shor","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“बहुत ज़्यादा शोर मचाना” — इसके लिए मुहावरा चुनो।","o":["ज़मीन-आसमान एक करना","नाक में दम करना","आसमान छूना","आसमान सिर पर उठाना"],"a":3,"hint":"शोर = noise. Only one sky idiom is about noise; the others are about effort and success.","why":"आसमान सिर पर उठाना = to make a huge racket. ज़मीन-आसमान एक करना = to try your hardest; आसमान छूना = to reach great heights; नाक में दम करना = to pester."},{"c":"muha-chugli-dhokha","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“कान भरना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["ध्यान से बात सुनना","कान में रुई डालना","किसी के ख़िलाफ़ चुगली करना","ज़ोर से चिल्लाना"],"a":2,"hint":"Not about listening well or shouting! Is it something kind, or something sneaky?","why":"कान भरना = to poison someone’s ears, telling tales against another person. Listening carefully is “कान लगाकर सुनना” — a different phrase."},{"c":"muha-chugli-dhokha","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"कान भरना, नाक में दम करना, आँखें चुराना — किस वाक्य में मुहावरे का गलत प्रयोग है?","o":["उसने टीचर के कान भरे कि मैंने धक्का दिया।","गाना ध्यान से सुनने के लिए उसने कान भरे।","भाई ने सवाल पूछ-पूछकर नाक में दम कर दिया।","झूठ पकड़ा गया तो वह आँखें चुराने लगा।"],"a":1,"hint":"Check each idiom’s real meaning, not its literal words. Which sentence would puzzle your Hindi teacher?","why":"कान भरना = to tell tales against someone, never to listen carefully. नाक में दम करना = to pester; आँखें चुराना = to avoid someone’s eyes out of guilt."},{"c":"muha-gussa-shor","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"मच्छरों ने रात भर ___, हम सो ही नहीं पाए।","o":["कान भर दिए","आँखें चुरा लीं","पेट में चूहे दौड़ाए","नाक में दम कर दिया"],"a":3,"hint":"Buzzing in your ears is a trap! Which idiom means making someone’s life miserable?","why":"नाक में दम करना = to pester, make life miserable. कान भरना sounds buzzy, but it means telling tales against someone!"},{"c":"muha-gussa-shor","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“बहुत परेशान करना” — इसके लिए सही मुहावरा कौन-सा है?","o":["नाक में दम करना","नाक कटना","आँखें चुराना","हाथ-पैर फूलना"],"a":0,"hint":"परेशान करना = to trouble, pester. Two options have नाक — only one is about pestering.","why":"नाक में दम करना = to pester, harass. नाक कटना = to lose face (be disgraced); हाथ-पैर फूलना = to panic; आँखें चुराना = to avoid someone."},{"c":"muha-pyaar-khushi","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“घी के दीये जलाना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["घी बर्बाद करना","मंदिर में पूजा करना","बहुत ख़ुशियाँ मनाना","घर में रोशनी करना"],"a":2,"hint":"Long ago, ghee was precious. When would people happily burn it in lamps?","why":"घी के दीये जलाना = to celebrate with great joy. Ghee was costly, so lighting lamps with it meant a really BIG celebration!"},{"c":"muha-pyaar-khushi","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"घी के दीये जलाना — किस वाक्य में इसका प्रयोग गलत है?","o":["भारत जीता तो पूरे देश ने घी के दीये जलाए।","भैया की नौकरी लगी, तो घर में घी के दीये जले।","अपनी टीम हारी, तो हमने घी के दीये जलाए।","दादाजी ठीक होकर लौटे, तो घी के दीये जले।"],"a":2,"hint":"These lamps are lit only for happy news. Read each reason for lighting them.","why":"घी के दीये जलाना = to celebrate joyfully. Nobody celebrates their own team losing! India winning, a new job, Dadaji getting well — all reasons to rejoice."},{"c":"muha-nasamjhi","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“अक्ल पर पत्थर पड़ना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["सिर पर पत्थर से चोट लगना","बुद्धि काम न करना, मूर्खता करना","बहुत ज़्यादा पढ़ाई करना","बहुत ज़िद्दी हो जाना"],"a":1,"hint":"अक्ल = good sense. No real stones fall! What happens when good sense gets blocked?","why":"अक्ल पर पत्थर पड़ना = to lose one’s good sense, to act foolishly. It’s said when someone makes a silly, harmful choice — no real stone involved!"},{"c":"muha-nasamjhi","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"अक्ल पर पत्थर पड़ना — किस वाक्य में इसका प्रयोग गलत है?","o":["चोर की अक्ल पर पत्थर पड़े, वह थाने में घुसा।","सही जवाब देकर उसकी अक्ल पर पत्थर पड़ गए।","आँधी में छत पर चढ़ा? अक्ल पर पत्थर पड़े हैं!","बिना देखे सड़क पार की? अक्ल पर पत्थर पड़े हैं!"],"a":1,"hint":"Ask of each sentence: was the person being foolish or being wise?","why":"अक्ल पर पत्थर पड़ना = to lose one’s good sense. Giving the right answer is smart, not foolish! A thief walking into a police station — now that fits."},{"c":"muha-chugli-dhokha","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"पिंटू ने मैच के लिए अपना बैट देने का वादा किया, पर ऐन वक़्त पर साफ़ मना कर दिया। पिंटू ने क्या किया?","o":["कमर कस ली","कान भर दिए","अंगूठा दिखा दिया","दाँत खट्टे कर दिए"],"a":2,"hint":"A promise broken at the very last moment. Which idiom means flatly refusing just when you’re needed?","why":"अंगूठा दिखाना = to flatly refuse, often at the last moment. Careful: it is NOT a thumbs-up “yes” — in this idiom the thumb means a cheeky “no!”"},{"c":"muha-ulta-badhana","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"जो अपनी तारीफ़ ख़ुद ही करता रहे, उसके लिए कौन-सा मुहावरा है?","o":["मुँह में पानी आना","मुँह की खाना","आँखों का तारा होना","अपने मुँह मियाँ मिट्ठू बनना"],"a":3,"hint":"तारीफ़ = praise. Three options have मुँह — only one is about praising yourself.","why":"अपने मुँह मियाँ मिट्ठू बनना = to blow your own trumpet, praise yourself. मुँह में पानी आना = mouth-watering; मुँह की खाना = to suffer a defeat."},{"c":"muha-dikhna-nazar","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"होमवर्क न करने के कारण मोनू पूरे पीरियड टीचर से नज़र नहीं मिला पाया। कौन-सा मुहावरा ठीक है?","o":["आँखें बिछाना","आँखें चुराना","आँखों का तारा होना","दाँतों तले उँगली दबाना"],"a":1,"hint":"He felt guilty and kept looking away. Three options have आँखें — which one fits guilt?","why":"आँखें चुराना = to avoid someone’s eyes, usually out of guilt. आँखें बिछाना = to welcome someone warmly; आँखों का तारा = very dear."},{"c":"muha-gussa-shor","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"टूटा शीशा देखकर पड़ोसी अंकल ___ और चिल्लाने लगे।","o":["फूले न समाए","पानी-पानी हो गए","आग बबूला हो गए","ईद का चाँद हो गए"],"a":2,"hint":"He started shouting. Was he happy, ashamed or angry?","why":"आग बबूला होना = to be furious, boiling with anger. फूला न समाना = overjoyed; पानी-पानी होना = deeply ashamed. Broken glass + shouting = angry!"},{"c":"muha-ulta-badhana","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"चेला गुरुजी को पढ़ाने बैठ गया! इसके लिए कौन-सा मुहावरा ठीक है?","o":["उल्टी गंगा बहाना","गिरगिट की तरह रंग बदलना","तिल का ताड़ बनाना","हाथ पर हाथ धरे बैठना"],"a":0,"hint":"Normally the guru teaches the chela. Here it is the reverse of the usual way.","why":"उल्टी गंगा बहाना = to do things the opposite of the normal way — like the Ganga flowing back up the mountain! गिरगिट की तरह रंग बदलना = to keep switching sides."},{"c":"muha-khana-peena","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"दिन भर क्रिकेट खेलकर आए भूखे {ZH} को सिर्फ़ दो बिस्किट मिले। यह तो ___ है!","o":["टेढ़ी खीर","ऊँट के मुँह में जीरा","दाल में काला","ईद का चाँद"],"a":1,"hint":"Two biscuits after a whole day of cricket — is that plenty, or far too little?","why":"ऊँट के मुँह में जीरा = far too little for the need — like one cumin seed for a camel! टेढ़ी खीर = a tough task; दाल में काला = something fishy."},{"c":"muha-mehnat-jeet","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“कमर कसना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["कमर पर बेल्ट बाँधना","हिम्मत हार जाना","कमर में दर्द होना","किसी काम के लिए तैयार होना"],"a":3,"hint":"Long ago, people tied their waistcloth tight before a big job. Why would they do that?","why":"कमर कसना = to gear up, get fully ready for a task. It’s not about a real belt — and losing courage (हिम्मत हारना) is the opposite!"},{"c":"muha-mehnat-jeet","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"पापा ने तेज़ धूप में ख़ूब मेहनत करके खेत में सुपारी के पौधे लगाए। कौन-सा मुहावरा ठीक बैठता है?","o":["हाथ पर हाथ धरे बैठना","उल्टी गंगा बहाना","ख़ून-पसीना एक करना","ऊँट के मुँह में जीरा"],"a":2,"hint":"Hard work under a hot sun on the farm. Which idiom is about the hardest possible effort?","why":"ख़ून-पसीना एक करना = to toil very hard, to sweat blood. हाथ पर हाथ धरे बैठना is the opposite — sitting idle. Farm work is tough work!"},{"c":"muha-chugli-dhokha","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"मैच में जो टीम आगे होती, गोलू उसी की तरफ़ हो जाता। गोलू के लिए कौन-सा मुहावरा ठीक है?","o":["अपने मुँह मियाँ मिट्ठू बनना","आँखों का तारा होना","चिकना घड़ा होना","गिरगिट की तरह रंग बदलना"],"a":3,"hint":"Golu keeps switching sides to suit himself. Which idiom is about changing again and again?","why":"गिरगिट की तरह रंग बदलना = to keep changing sides or opinions, like a chameleon changing colour. चिकना घड़ा = someone nothing gets through to."},{"c":"muha-nasamjhi","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"मैच हारकर रोहन पहले ही उदास था, ऊपर से भैया ने उसका मज़ाक उड़ाया। भैया ने क्या किया?","o":["घाव पर नमक छिड़का","कान भरे","आँखें बिछाईं","कमर कसी"],"a":0,"hint":"Rohan was already hurting. Did Bhaiya make it better or worse?","why":"घाव पर नमक छिड़कना = to rub salt in someone’s wounds, hurting a sad person even more. Next time, Bhaiya, try cheering him up!"},{"c":"muha-nasamjhi","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"किस वाक्य में “चिकना घड़ा” का सही प्रयोग हुआ है?","o":["बिट्टू सबका लाडला है, वह चिकना घड़ा है।","बिट्टू पर समझाने का असर नहीं, वह चिकना घड़ा है।","बिट्टू हर बात झट मानता है, वह चिकना घड़ा है।","बिट्टू बहुत दिनों बाद आया, वह चिकना घड़ा है।"],"a":1,"hint":"Pour water on a smooth, oily pot. Does any of it stay?","why":"चिकना घड़ा = a thick-skinned person nothing gets through to — advice slides off like water off an oily pot. Someone who listens at once is the opposite!"},{"c":"muha-khana-peena","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"{ZH} ने कई दिन कोशिश की, तब जाकर वीडियो का वह इफ़ेक्ट बना। यह काम सच में ___ था।","o":["दाल में काला","आँखों का तारा","टेढ़ी खीर","ऊँट के मुँह में जीरा"],"a":2,"hint":"It took him days and days of trying. Which idiom names a really tough job?","why":"टेढ़ी खीर = a hard nut to crack, a very tough task. Well done finishing it, {Z}! (दाल में काला = something fishy.)"},{"c":"muha-ulta-badhana","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“तिल का ताड़ बनाना” का अर्थ क्या है?","o":["छोटी बात को बहुत बड़ा बना देना","तिल के लड्डू बनाना","बड़ी बात को छिपा लेना","ऊँचे पेड़ पर चढ़ना"],"a":0,"hint":"तिल = a tiny sesame seed. ताड़ = a very tall palm tree. What happens to the small thing?","why":"तिल का ताड़ बनाना = to make a mountain out of a molehill, turning a tiny matter into a huge fuss. Hiding a big matter is the opposite."},{"c":"muha-dar-sharm-hairani","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"जादूगर ने ख़ाली टोपी से खरगोश निकाला, तो सब बच्चों ने ___।","o":["दाँत खट्टे कर दिए","आँखें चुरा लीं","दाँतों तले उँगली दबा ली","कान भर दिए"],"a":2,"hint":"A rabbit out of an empty hat! How do you react to something unbelievable?","why":"दाँतों तले उँगली दबाना = to be amazed, astonished. दाँत खट्टे करना also has दाँत, but it means to beat someone badly — the magician didn’t fight anyone!"},{"c":"muha-khana-peena","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"छोटा भाई अचानक बहुत मीठा बोलकर मेरा कमरा साफ़ करने लगा। मुझे लगा कि…","o":["वह ईद का चाँद है","दाल में कुछ काला है","यह टेढ़ी खीर है","वह चिकना घड़ा है"],"a":1,"hint":"Suddenly so sweet and helpful? Maybe he wants a favour, or broke something!","why":"दाल में कुछ काला होना = something fishy is going on. A suddenly super-helpful brother usually means a secret… or a favour coming!"},{"c":"muha-dar-sharm-hairani","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"“बहुत शर्मिंदा होना” — इसके लिए सही मुहावरा कौन-सा है?","o":["मुँह में पानी आना","पानी फेरना","फूला न समाना","पानी-पानी होना"],"a":3,"hint":"शर्मिंदा = ashamed. Three options have पानी — only one is about shame.","why":"पानी-पानी होना = to feel deeply ashamed. मुँह में पानी आना = mouth-watering; पानी फेरना = to ruin someone’s efforts; फूला न समाना = overjoyed."},{"c":"muha-khana-peena","t":"मुहावरा","p":50,"q":"स्कूल से लौटते ही {ZH} बोला, “दादी, जल्दी खाना दीजिए, मेरे पेट में ___!”","o":["दाल में काला है","पानी आ रहा है","तारे नाच रहे हैं","चूहे दौड़ रहे हैं"],"a":3,"hint":"He’s starving! Careful — मुँह में पानी आना belongs to the mouth, not the tummy.","why":"पेट में चूहे दौड़ना = to be very hungry, as if mice are running around an empty tummy! मुँह में पानी आना = craving tasty food, a different idiom."},{"c":"muha-pyaar-khushi","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"{ZH} की बनाई पेंटिंग स्कूल के गेट पर लगाई गई। यह देखकर {ZH}…","o":["पानी-पानी हो गया","आग बबूला हो गया","नौ दो ग्यारह हो गया","फूला न समाया"],"a":3,"hint":"How would you feel seeing your own artwork shown to the whole school?","why":"फूला न समाना = to be overjoyed, bursting with happiness. पानी-पानी होना = deeply ashamed; आग बबूला होना = furious. Proud artist, happy heart!"},{"c":"muha-bhaagna-bachna","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"इनमें से कौन-सा मुहावरा “भाग जाना” के अर्थ वाला नहीं है?","o":["नौ दो ग्यारह होना","रफ़ूचक्कर होना","बाल-बाल बचना","सिर पर पाँव रखकर भागना"],"a":2,"hint":"Escaping isn’t always running away. Which one is about a lucky escape from danger?","why":"बाल-बाल बचना = to have a narrow escape, by a hair’s breadth. नौ दो ग्यारह होना, रफ़ूचक्कर होना and सिर पर पाँव रखकर भागना all mean to run away fast."},{"c":"muha-mehnat-jeet","t":"मुहावरा","p":100,"q":"पूरी टीम मैदान साफ़ कर रही थी, पर सनी कोने में बस बैठा देखता रहा। सनी…","o":["हाथ पर हाथ धरे बैठा रहा","कमर कसे खड़ा रहा","ख़ून-पसीना एक करता रहा","आँखों का तारा बना रहा"],"a":0,"hint":"Everyone else worked; Sunny just watched. Which idiom is about doing nothing at all?","why":"हाथ पर हाथ धरे बैठना = to sit idle, doing nothing. ख़ून-पसीना एक करना is the opposite — toiling hard. Come on, Sunny, grab a broom!"}],"z-sci":[{"c":"luminous-objects","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Which of these gives out its OWN light?","o":["The Moon","Venus","The Pole Star","A mirror"],"a":2,"hint":"Which of these would still shine if the Sun vanished?","why":"The Pole Star is a star, so it makes its own light — it's luminous. The Moon, Venus and a mirror only send back light that falls on them."},{"c":"luminous-objects","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if the Sun stopped shining forever? Would the Moon still glow in the night sky?","o":["Yes — the Moon makes its own light","Yes, but only on full-moon nights","Yes — it would glow even brighter","No — moonlight is only reflected sunlight"],"a":3,"hint":"Is the Moon luminous or non-luminous?","why":"The Moon is non-luminous: it only reflects the Sun's light. No sunlight means nothing for it to reflect — the Moon would go dark."},{"c":"luminous-objects","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Kabir: “Venus is so bright in the evening sky, it must make its own light.” What's his mistake?","o":["None — planets glow on their own","Venus is a star, so he's right","Venus can only be seen at noon","Bright isn't luminous — Venus reflects light"],"a":3,"hint":"In our solar system, which object shines with its own light?","why":"Only the Sun makes light in our solar system. Venus looks bright because it is close to us and reflects a lot of sunlight — but it's non-luminous."},{"c":"luminous-objects","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"India is pushing everyone to switch to LED lamps. Why?","o":["Less power, brighter, longer life","They make no shadows","They work without electricity","They can go in any dustbin"],"a":0,"hint":"Think about the electricity bill and how often you'd change the lamp.","why":"LEDs use much less power, are brighter and last longer — good for bills and the planet. But at their end of life they must be recycled, not thrown in the garbage!"},{"c":"light-straight-line","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"Torch light passes through holes in 3 matchboxes in a line, making a spot on a screen. What if you slide the middle box sideways?","o":["Light bends round to the spot","The spot gets brighter","The spot disappears","Nothing changes at all"],"a":2,"hint":"Can light take a detour to find the moved hole?","why":"Light travels in a straight line, so it can only get through when all three holes line up. Move one box and the solid card blocks the path — no spot."},{"c":"light-straight-line","t":"Predict","p":50,"q":"Four friends look at a candle flame through flexible pipes. Only one pipe is held straight. Who sees the flame?","o":["The one with the longest pipe","The one with the straight pipe","The one with the widest pipe","All four — light finds its way"],"a":1,"hint":"What shape is the path light travels in?","why":"Light goes in straight lines, so it can't follow the curve of a bent pipe — it hits the inside wall instead. Only the straight pipe gives light a clear path."},{"c":"light-straight-line","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if the Sun suddenly stopped giving out light? When would we on Earth first notice?","o":["That very same second","About one day later","About 8 minutes 20 seconds later","After one full year"],"a":2,"hint":"Light is super fast, but the Sun is very, very far away.","why":"Sunlight takes about 8 min 20 s to reach Earth. Light already on its way would keep arriving, so we'd only notice after that. Sunlight you see left the Sun 8 minutes ago!"},{"c":"light-straight-line","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Sunlight enters a dusty room through a small gap in the roof. What does the beam look like?","o":["A wavy, zigzag ribbon","A round ball of light","A straight shaft with clear edges","Glow spread evenly over the room"],"a":2,"hint":"The dust just helps you SEE the path light is taking.","why":"Dust in the air lights up the path, and that path is a straight line from the gap to the floor. It's the same idea as the laser beam in milky water."},{"c":"see-through-materials","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Tracing paper lets light pass…","o":["Partly — it is translucent","Fully — it is transparent","Not at all — it is opaque","Only when it is wet"],"a":0,"hint":"Can you see a lamp through it clearly, blurrily, or not at all?","why":"Tracing paper is translucent: some light passes, so you see a blurry glow but no clear image. That's why it works as the screen in a pinhole camera."},{"c":"see-through-materials","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Which material is OPAQUE?","o":["Clear window glass","Tracing paper","Thick cardboard","Frosted glass"],"a":2,"hint":"Which one would leave a torch spot completely dark?","why":"Thick cardboard blocks light fully — opaque. Clear glass is transparent, while tracing paper and frosted glass are translucent: they let some light through."},{"c":"see-through-materials","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"A torch makes a spot on the wall. You hold up clear glass, then tracing paper, then cardboard. The spot goes…","o":["Gone → dim → bright","Bright → dim → gone","Dim → bright → gone","Bright → bright → bright"],"a":1,"hint":"Transparent, translucent, opaque — how much light gets through each?","why":"Clear glass lets almost all light through (bright), tracing paper lets some through (dim) and cardboard blocks it all (gone). Shadow and spot tell the same story!"},{"c":"shadow-needs","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"To see a shadow, you need a light source, an opaque object and…","o":["A mirror behind the object","A completely dark room","A coloured object","A screen, like a wall or the ground"],"a":3,"hint":"Where does the shadow actually show up?","why":"The shadow has to fall on something — a wall, the floor, the ground. You don't need a dark room: you see shadows outdoors in bright sunlight all the time!"},{"c":"shadow-needs","t":"What if","p":50,"q":"A torch, a ball and a screen make a nice shadow. What if you switch the torch off?","o":["The shadow gets even darker","The shadow is gone — no light to block","The shadow stays a few seconds","Only the ball's outline glows"],"a":1,"hint":"A shadow is the place where light is blocked. Is there any light now?","why":"A shadow is just the patch where light can't reach. With no light, there's nothing to block, so there's no shadow — the whole screen is equally dark."},{"c":"shadow-needs","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Ria says: “A shadow forms because the object gives out darkness.” Which idea is correct?","o":["The object blocks light from reaching there","She's right — objects give out darkness","A shadow is a reflection of the object","The screen makes the shadow by itself"],"a":0,"hint":"Light travels in straight lines. What happens when something gets in the way?","why":"Nothing ‘gives out’ darkness! Light travels in straight lines, and an opaque object stops it. The dark patch behind is simply where light doesn't arrive."},{"c":"shadow-size-shape","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"The torch and wall stay fixed. You move a ball closer to the TORCH. Its shadow on the wall…","o":["Gets smaller","Gets bigger","Stays the same size","Disappears"],"a":1,"hint":"Near the torch, the ball blocks a wider cone of light.","why":"Close to the torch, the ball blocks a big part of the spreading beam, so a large patch of wall gets no light. Try it with your hand and a phone torch!"},{"c":"shadow-size-shape","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"The torch and wall stay fixed. You move your hand closer to the WALL. Its shadow…","o":["Gets smaller and sharper","Gets bigger and blurrier","Stays exactly the same","Becomes lighter in colour"],"a":0,"hint":"When your hand nearly touches the wall, how does its shadow compare with your hand?","why":"Near the wall, the shadow shrinks to about your hand's real size and its edges get crisp. Far from the wall it grows and the edges blur."},{"c":"shadow-size-shape","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if TWO torches shine on a ball from two different spots on its left side?","o":["One extra-dark single shadow","No shadow at all","One shadow, twice as big","Two shadows appear on the screen"],"a":3,"hint":"Each torch's light is blocked by the ball separately.","why":"Each torch sends straight light that the ball blocks, so each makes its own shadow, in a slightly different place. Football players under stadium lights show many shadows!"},{"c":"shadow-size-shape","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"In Togalu Gombeyaata (Karnataka shadow puppetry), how can a puppeteer make a puppet's shadow look HUGE?","o":["Move it closer to the light","Move it closer to the screen","Paint the puppet a darker colour","Make the screen whiter"],"a":0,"hint":"Think about where the puppet blocks the widest part of the beam.","why":"Near the light, the puppet blocks a wide spread of the beam, so its shadow on the screen is huge. Colour doesn't change an opaque puppet's shadow."},{"c":"shadow-darkness-colour","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"A red ball and a blue ball (both opaque) stand in torch light. Their shadows will be…","o":["Red and blue","Pink and light blue","Both dark — same colour","Only the blue one dark"],"a":2,"hint":"A shadow is where light does NOT reach. Can the ball's colour get there?","why":"An opaque ball blocks the light, and colour can't travel into the shadow. Changing an opaque object's colour doesn't change its shadow — both are dark."},{"c":"shadow-darkness-colour","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which of these makes the DARKEST shadow?","o":["A sheet of tracing paper","A wooden block","A clear glass","A piece of frosted glass"],"a":1,"hint":"The less light gets through, the darker the shadow.","why":"Wood is opaque — it blocks all the light, so the shadow is darkest. Translucent things like tracing paper or frosted glass make lighter shadows."},{"c":"shadow-darkness-colour","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Neel: “Clear glass is transparent, so it can NEVER make any shadow.” Is he right?","o":["Yes — all light passes, so no shadow","No — glass casts the darkest shadows","Yes — only dark objects make shadows","No — some transparent things cast faint shadows"],"a":3,"hint":"Look closely at the shadow of a glass of water on a sunny table.","why":"Light passes ALMOST completely through transparent things — not 100%. So some, like a glass tumbler, still make faint shadows. Opaque ones make the darkest."},{"c":"sun-shadows","t":"Predict","p":50,"q":"You measure your shadow at 9 AM, 12 PM and 4 PM. When is it the SHORTEST?","o":["9 AM — the Sun is nearest then","12 PM — the Sun is highest","4 PM — the light is weaker","It's the same at all three times"],"a":1,"hint":"Where is the Sun in the sky around noon?","why":"Around noon the Sun is high overhead, so light hits you from above and your shadow is short. Morning and evening Sun is low, so it stretches your shadow long."},{"c":"sun-shadows","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"In the evening the Sun is low in the west. Which way does your shadow point?","o":["Towards the west","Straight down, under you","Towards the east","Towards the north"],"a":2,"hint":"The shadow always forms on the side away from the light.","why":"Light comes from the west in a straight line and you block it, so your shadow falls on the opposite side — the east. It's long, too, because the Sun is low."},{"c":"sun-shadows","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"We don't see the shadow of a bird flying very high, but we do when it swoops low. Why?","o":["Sharp when low; blurs away when high","High-flying birds become see-through","Sunlight can't reach high birds","Birds only make shadows when landing"],"a":0,"hint":"Shadow sharpness depends on how far the object is from the screen.","why":"The ground is the screen. Near it, the bird's shadow is sharp and dark. Far above, the shadow spreads and fades so much that we can't make it out."},{"c":"reflection","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"When a mirror changes the direction of light falling on it, we call this…","o":["A shadow","Reflection of light","Lateral inversion","Absorption of light"],"a":1,"hint":"Lateral inversion is the left–right swap; absorption means soaking light up. Neither turns light.","why":"A mirror bounces light into a new direction — reflection. Lateral inversion is something different: the left-right swap you see in a mirror image."},{"c":"reflection","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A polished steel plate in a torch beam casts a shadow behind it AND a bright spot on the wall behind the torch. Why?","o":["Light passes through the steel","The plate gives out its own light","The shiny plate reflects the light","The shadow turns into light"],"a":2,"hint":"Steel is opaque. So where can the blocked light go?","why":"The plate is opaque, so it casts a shadow. But it's also shiny, so it reflects the light it blocks onto the opposite wall — that's the bright spot!"},{"c":"reflection","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"You bounce sunlight off a mirror to make a bright spot on a shady wall. What if you tilt the mirror a little?","o":["The spot stays in the same place","The spot vanishes for good","The spot changes colour","The spot moves to a new place"],"a":3,"hint":"Tilting the mirror changes the direction of the reflected light.","why":"The mirror changes light's direction, so tilting it sends the straight reflected beam somewhere else — and the spot slides along the wall. Great for signalling!"},{"c":"plane-mirror-image","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The image of a pen in a plane mirror is…","o":["Smaller and upside down","Same size and upright (erect)","Bigger and upright","Same size but upside down"],"a":1,"hint":"Move the pen around: does the tip stay on top? Does it shrink?","why":"A plane mirror image is always the same size as the object and erect — the pen's tip stays on top wherever you hold it."},{"c":"plane-mirror-image","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if you place a screen behind a plane mirror, right where your image seems to be?","o":["A clear image forms on it","No image forms on the screen","A blurry image forms on it","An upside-down image forms"],"a":1,"hint":"Is any light really reaching the space behind the mirror?","why":"The image only SEEMS to be behind the mirror — no light goes there. So it can't be caught on a screen, in front of or behind the mirror."},{"c":"plane-mirror-image","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"You stand 2 m from a plane mirror, then step 1 m closer. Now your image appears…","o":["2 m behind the mirror","3 m behind the mirror","Right on the mirror's surface","1 m behind the mirror"],"a":3,"hint":"When you move closer to the mirror, what does your image do?","why":"Your image appears as far behind the mirror as you are in front of it. Step closer and it comes closer too: you're now 1 m away, so it's 1 m behind."},{"c":"plane-mirror-image","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Ishaan: “When I step back from a plane mirror, my image actually shrinks.” What's the mistake?","o":["It stays the same size as him","None — the image really shrinks","The image actually grows","The image vanishes beyond 2 m"],"a":0,"hint":"Move a pen near and far from a mirror and compare the sizes.","why":"A plane mirror image is always the same size as the object. It only LOOKS smaller from far — just like a friend looks smaller as they walk away."},{"c":"lateral-inversion","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Why is the word AMBULANCE painted back-to-front on the front of an ambulance?","o":["So the patients inside can read it","It's a secret code for hospitals","Drivers ahead read it right in their mirrors","Just to look cool"],"a":2,"hint":"Who needs to read it, and through what?","why":"A mirror swaps left and right (lateral inversion). Painted reversed, the word reads ‘AMBULANCE’ in the rear-view mirror of the car ahead, so the driver makes way."},{"c":"lateral-inversion","t":"Predict","p":50,"q":"You raise your RIGHT hand in front of a plane mirror. Your image seems to raise its…","o":["Right hand","Both hands","Left hand","No hand — images don't move"],"a":2,"hint":"Mirrors swap one pair of sides. Which pair?","why":"Your right appears as the image's left — that's lateral inversion. The image isn't upside down, just swapped left to right."},{"c":"lateral-inversion","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"A: A plane mirror image is laterally inverted. B: The letters T and O look the same in a mirror. Which is right?","o":["A is true, B is false","A is false, B is true","Both are false","Both true — T and O are symmetric"],"a":3,"hint":"Fold a T and an O down the middle. Do the two halves match?","why":"Mirrors do swap left and right — but T and O have matching left and right halves, so the swap changes nothing! Try R or Z: those look reversed."},{"c":"pinhole-camera","t":"Predict","p":50,"q":"You look at a tall tree through a pinhole camera. On the tracing-paper screen, the tree appears…","o":["Upside down","Upright, as in a mirror","Turned sideways","As a plain dark shadow"],"a":0,"hint":"Remember the candle flame on the screen in the pinhole activity.","why":"A pinhole camera makes an inverted image: the treetop appears at the bottom and the trunk at the top. Its colours show too!"},{"c":"pinhole-camera","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Why does a pinhole camera's image come out upside down?","o":["Straight rays from top and bottom cross","The tracing paper flips the picture","The box is held upside down","Light curls around inside the box"],"a":0,"hint":"Draw a straight line from the treetop through the hole. Where does it land?","why":"Light travels in straight lines. A ray from the treetop goes down through the hole and lands low; a ray from the roots goes up. They cross at the pinhole, flipping the image."},{"c":"pinhole-camera","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Which sentence about a pinhole image and a plane-mirror image is TRUE?","o":["Both are upside down","Both can be caught on a screen","Mirror flips upside down; pinhole doesn't","Pinhole flips upside down; mirror swaps sides"],"a":3,"hint":"One forms on tracing paper; the other just ‘appears’ behind glass.","why":"A pinhole camera gives an upside-down image on a screen. A mirror image is erect but laterally inverted, and it can't be caught on a screen."},{"c":"mirror-devices","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"A simple periscope is made using…","o":["One mirror and a pinhole","Two plane mirrors in a Z-shaped box","Three mirrors joined in a triangle","A tracing-paper screen"],"a":1,"hint":"Light has to turn twice to get from above the wall to your eye.","why":"Two plane mirrors in a Z-shaped tube each reflect the light once, so you see things you can't see directly. Three mirrors in a triangle make a kaleidoscope."},{"c":"mirror-devices","t":"Real life","p":50,"q":"You're stuck behind taller friends at a parade. Which gadget from this chapter helps you see over them?","o":["A kaleidoscope","A pinhole camera","A torch","A periscope"],"a":3,"hint":"Submarines and soldiers in bunkers use it for the same reason.","why":"A periscope's two mirrors bring light from above the crowd down to your eye. Kaleidoscopes make patterns, and a pinhole camera can't see around anything."},{"c":"mirror-devices","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"A kaleidoscope shows lots of copies of a few bangle pieces, and a new pattern each turn. Why?","o":["Its 3 mirrors reflect the reflections","The beads give out their own light","The tracing paper copies them","The tube has many tiny pinholes"],"a":0,"hint":"How many mirrors are inside, and what can a mirror reflect?","why":"With 3 mirrors, each one reflects the beads AND the images in the other mirrors — reflections of reflections. Turn it, the pieces shift, and a new pattern appears."}],"z-spa":[{"c":"donde-relativo","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Paraguay es el país ___ mucha gente habla guaraní.","o":["dónde","cuando","donde","que"],"a":2,"hint":"It means ‘Paraguay is the country ___ many people speak Guaraní.’ Is this sentence a question?","why":"‘Paraguay is the country where many people speak Guaraní.’ ‘Donde’ links back to ‘el país’, so it has NO accent. ‘Dónde’ asks where: ¿Dónde está? and No sé dónde está (I don’t know where it is)."},{"c":"donde-relativo","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Busca el error: ¿qué frase usa mal “donde”?","o":["Esta es la escuela dónde estudio.","¿Dónde está Montevideo?","El parque donde juego es grande.","La ciudad donde vivo tiene un río."],"a":0,"hint":"‘Which sentence misuses donde?’ Dónde (where?) asks a question; donde (where) adds info in a statement.","why":"‘Esta es la escuela donde estudio’ = This is the school where I study. It isn’t a question, so no accent. ‘¿Dónde está Montevideo?’ (Where is Montevideo?) is a question, so it keeps one."},{"c":"donde-relativo","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Lucía es de Montevideo. Montevideo es la ciudad ___ viene Lucía.","o":["en donde","cuando","que","de donde"],"a":3,"hint":"‘Montevideo is the city Lucía comes ___.’ Which little word goes with ‘venir’ to show where you start?","why":"‘De donde’ = from where: Montevideo is the city Lucía comes from. Spanish says ‘venir de’ (to come from), so ‘de’ goes in front of ‘donde’. ‘En donde’ means ‘in which’."},{"c":"ir-a-infinitivo","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Mañana {R} y {Z} ___ nadar en el mar.","o":["vamos a","van a","va a","van"],"a":1,"hint":"‘Tomorrow {R} and {Z} are going to swim in the sea.’ Who is going: I, he, we or they?","why":"Ir + a + infinitive = ‘going to’. For ‘ellos’ (they), ir becomes ‘van’: van a nadar = they are going to swim. ‘Vamos’ is for ‘we’, and ‘van nadar’ is missing the ‘a’."},{"c":"ir-a-infinitivo","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Busca el error: ¿qué plan está mal escrito?","o":["Voy a comer chipa.","¿Vas a ver la película?","Nosotros vamos visitar Asunción.","Ella va a estudiar español."],"a":2,"hint":"‘Which plan is written wrongly?’ Each says someone is ‘going to’ do something. Check ir + a + infinitive.","why":"It should be ‘Nosotros vamos a visitar Asunción’ = We are going to visit Asunción. This future always needs the little ‘a’ between ir and the infinitive (visitar = to visit)."},{"c":"ir-a-infinitivo","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"Ordena las palabras: visitar · voy · Uruguay · a","o":["Voy visitar a Uruguay.","A Uruguay voy visitar.","Voy a visitar Uruguay.","Visitar voy a Uruguay."],"a":2,"hint":"It means ‘I am going to visit Uruguay.’ Copy the pattern of ‘Voy a comer’ (I am going to eat).","why":"‘Voy a visitar Uruguay’ = I am going to visit Uruguay. The order is always ir (voy) + a + infinitive (visitar). The ‘a’ is the glue between the two verbs."},{"c":"prep-ir-venir-llegar","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"{Z} va a la escuela ___ autobús.","o":["a","en","de","con"],"a":1,"hint":"‘{Z} goes to school by bus.’ Which little word goes with transport, as in ‘___ coche’ (by car)?","why":"Ir en + transport = to go by: en autobús (by bus), en coche (by car), en tren (by train). ‘A’ shows where you go: a la escuela = to school."},{"c":"prep-ir-venir-llegar","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"El avión llega ___ Montevideo a las diez.","o":["a","en","con","para"],"a":0,"hint":"‘The plane arrives in Montevideo at ten.’ Use the same little word as in ‘Voy ___ Montevideo’ (I go to).","why":"Spanish says ‘llegar a’ + place: llega a Montevideo = it arrives in Montevideo. ‘Llega en’ copies the English ‘in’, a common slip. ‘En’ is for transport: llega en avión (by plane)."},{"c":"prep-ir-venir-llegar","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"¿Qué frase con ir, venir o llegar tiene un error?","o":["Voy al parque en bicicleta.","Vengo del mercado.","Mañana voy a el zoo.","Llego a casa a las cuatro."],"a":2,"hint":"‘Which sentence with go/come/arrive has a mistake?’ Remember: de + el (from the) always joins to make del.","why":"‘A + el’ must join into ‘al’: Mañana voy al zoo = Tomorrow I’m going to the zoo. In the same way, de + el = del: Vengo del mercado = I’m coming from the market."},{"c":"prep-ir-venir-llegar","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Hoy no hay autobús. {R} va a la escuela ___ pie.","o":["en","con","por","a"],"a":3,"hint":"‘No bus today. {R} goes to school on foot.’ Feet aren’t a vehicle like ‘en coche’ (by car).","why":"Spanish says ‘a pie’ = on foot. ‘En’ is only for vehicles, like en coche (by car) or en autobús (by bus). So: {R} va a la escuela a pie = {R} walks to school."},{"c":"muy-mucho","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"En Uruguay hay ___ playas bonitas.","o":["muchos","muchas","mucha","muy"],"a":1,"hint":"‘In Uruguay there are many pretty beaches.’ ‘Mucho’ must match ‘playas’ (beaches): gender AND number.","why":"Before a noun, ‘mucho’ matches it: ‘playas’ is feminine plural, so ‘muchas playas’ = many beaches. ‘Muy’ (very) never goes straight before a noun."},{"c":"muy-mucho","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"En Asunción, en enero, hace ___ calor. Elige la palabra y la razón.","o":["muy — “calor” es un adjetivo","mucha — “calor” es femenino","mucho — “calor” es un sustantivo","muchos — “calor” es plural"],"a":2,"hint":"Word for word: ‘it makes much heat’ (= it’s very hot). Is ‘calor’ (heat) a thing or a describing word?","why":"‘Calor’ (heat) is a masculine noun, so it takes ‘mucho’: hace mucho calor = it is very hot. ‘Muy’ goes with adjectives and adverbs: muy bonito (very pretty), muy bien (very well)."},{"c":"muy-mucho","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"¿Dónde está el error con “muy” o “mucho”?","o":["Tengo mucha hambre.","Me gusta mucho el mate.","El río Paraná es mucho largo.","Hay muchos ríos en Paraguay."],"a":2,"hint":"‘Where is the mistake?’ Muy (very) goes with describing words; mucho (a lot) with things and actions.","why":"‘Largo’ (long) is an adjective, so it needs ‘muy’: El río Paraná es muy largo = The Paraná River is very long. ‘Hambre’ (hunger) is a noun, so ‘mucha hambre’ (very hungry) is right."},{"c":"muy-mucho","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"{R} ve tiburones en el acuario y dice: “¡Me gustan ___!”","o":["mucho","muy","muy mucho","mucha"],"a":0,"hint":"{R} sees sharks and says ‘I like them very much!’ Careful: Spanish doesn’t say ‘very much’ word for word.","why":"After a verb, ‘mucho’ (a lot) never changes: Me gustan mucho = I like them a lot. ‘Muy mucho’ copies the English ‘very much’, but Spanish never puts ‘muy’ in front of ‘mucho’."},{"c":"ordinales","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Lección 5 y Lección 6: la ___ y la ___ lección.","o":["quinto / sexto","cinco / seis","sexta / séptima","quinta / sexta"],"a":3,"hint":"‘The fifth and the sixth lesson.’ Ordinals match their noun, and ‘lección’ (lesson) is feminine.","why":"Quinto = 5th and sexto = 6th, and they change to match the noun. ‘Lección’ (lesson) is feminine, so: la quinta y la sexta lección = the fifth and the sixth lesson."},{"c":"ordinales","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Hoy es el ___ día de clase (1.º). Elige la palabra y la razón.","o":["primera — “día” es femenino","primero — “día” es masculino","primer — “día” es masculino","primer — “día” es femenino"],"a":2,"hint":"‘Today is the first day of class.’ ‘Día’ ends in -a, but look closely: is it ‘el día’ or ‘la día’?","why":"‘Día’ (day) is masculine (el día), even though it ends in -a. Before a masculine singular noun, primero drops its -o: el primer día = the first day. Same with tercero: el tercer día."},{"c":"ordinales","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"Ordena de menor a mayor: noveno, segundo, séptimo, cuarto","o":["segundo → cuarto → séptimo → noveno","segundo → cuarto → noveno → séptimo","cuarto → segundo → séptimo → noveno","segundo → séptimo → cuarto → noveno"],"a":0,"hint":"‘Put them in order, smallest first.’ Each ordinal sounds a bit like its number: séptimo ≈ siete (seven).","why":"Segundo = 2nd, cuarto = 4th, séptimo = 7th ({Z}’s grade!) and noveno = 9th. Noveno comes from nueve (nine), so it goes last."},{"c":"conjunciones","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"{Z} estudia español ___ inglés en la escuela.","o":["y","e","u","ni"],"a":1,"hint":"‘{Z} studies Spanish and English at school.’ Listen to the very first sound of ‘inglés’.","why":"‘Y’ (and) turns into ‘e’ before a word starting with an ‘i’ sound: español e inglés = Spanish and English. Say ‘y inglés’ aloud and the two ‘i’ sounds crash together!"},{"c":"conjunciones","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"¿Quieres siete ___ ocho chipas? Elige la palabra y la razón.","o":["o — “ocho” empieza por o","u — “ocho” es un número","u — “ocho” empieza por o","e — “ocho” empieza por vocal"],"a":2,"hint":"‘Do you want seven or eight chipas?’ Say ‘siete o ocho’ aloud. What clashes?","why":"‘O’ (or) turns into ‘u’ before a word starting with an ‘o’ sound: siete u ocho = seven or eight. It’s the sound that matters, not the number. Chipa is Paraguay’s cheese bread!"},{"c":"conjunciones","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Una frase usa mal la conjunción. ¿Cuál?","o":["Ana e Isabel son amigas.","¿Vas a Paraguay u Uruguay?","¿Prefieres mate o tereré?","Hay diez u once alumnos."],"a":1,"hint":"‘One sentence uses the wrong conjunction.’ O (or) turns into u only before one sound. Say each one aloud.","why":"‘O’ becomes ‘u’ only before an ‘o’ sound, like once (eleven). ‘Uruguay’ starts with a ‘u’ sound, so keep ‘o’: ¿Vas a Paraguay o Uruguay? = Are you going to Paraguay or Uruguay?"},{"c":"conjunciones","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"—¿Por qué estudias español? —___ me gusta mucho.","o":["Por qué","Porque","Porqué","Cuando"],"a":1,"hint":"‘Why do you study Spanish? — Because I like it a lot.’ Is the gap a question word or an answer word?","why":"‘¿Por qué?’ (two words, accent) asks ‘why?’. ‘Porque’ (one word, no accent) answers ‘because’: Porque me gusta mucho = Because I like it a lot."},{"c":"futuro-regular","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"¿Qué forma está en futuro?","o":["comeré","como","comí","comía"],"a":0,"hint":"One of these means ‘I will eat’. The others mean I eat, I ate and I used to eat.","why":"Comeré = I will eat (comer + é). Como = I eat, comí = I ate, comía = I used to eat. The future is easy: keep the whole infinitive and add -é, -ás, -á, -emos, -éis, -án."},{"c":"futuro-regular","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Mañana nosotros ___ un museo en Montevideo.","o":["visitarémos","visitaréis","visitarán","visitaremos"],"a":3,"hint":"‘Tomorrow we will visit a museum in Montevideo.’ Which ending matches ‘nosotros’ (we)?","why":"Visitaremos = we will visit. The ‘nosotros’ ending is -emos, with NO accent. -éis is for ‘vosotros’ (you all) and -án is for ‘ellos’ (they)."},{"c":"futuro-regular","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"¿Qué frase en futuro tiene un error?","o":["Yo hablaré con Lucía.","Tú comerás chipa.","Ellos vivirán en Asunción.","Mañana ella viajarás a Uruguay."],"a":3,"hint":"‘Which future sentence is wrong?’ Match each ending to its person: yo (I), tú (you), ella (she), ellos (they).","why":"‘Ella’ (she) needs -á: Mañana ella viajará a Uruguay = Tomorrow she will travel to Uruguay. ‘Viajarás’ is the ‘tú’ (you) form."},{"c":"futuro-irregular","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"El sábado yo ___ tiempo para jugar. (tener)","o":["teneré","tenré","tendrá","tendré"],"a":3,"hint":"‘On Saturday I will have time to play.’ ‘Tener’ is irregular: it doesn’t keep its whole infinitive.","why":"Tener is irregular: tendr- + é = tendré (I will have). ‘Teneré’ is a very common mistake! Poner → pondré and salir → saldré change the same way."},{"c":"futuro-irregular","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"¿Qué futuro irregular está mal formado?","o":["Saldré de casa a las ocho.","Podremos ir al parque.","Ellos vendrán mañana.","Te deciré un secreto."],"a":3,"hint":"Meanings: I’ll tell you a secret, I’ll leave at 8, we’ll be able to go, they’ll come. One stem is wrong.","why":"Decir is irregular: dir- + é = diré. So: Te diré un secreto = I will tell you a secret. Saldré (salir), podremos (poder) and vendrán (venir) are all correct."},{"c":"futuro-irregular","t":"Odd one out","p":100,"q":"¿Qué forma del futuro es diferente de las otras tres?","o":["tendré","comeré","saldré","vendré"],"a":1,"hint":"These mean I’ll eat, I’ll have, I’ll leave, I’ll come. Look closely at the letters just before ‘-ré’.","why":"Comeré (I’ll eat) is regular: comer + é. The other three are irregular and slip in a ‘d’: tener → tendré, salir → saldré, venir → vendré."},{"c":"uruguay","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"¿Cuál es la capital de Uruguay?","o":["Montevideo","Asunción","Buenos Aires","Punta del Este"],"a":0,"hint":"‘What is the capital of Uruguay?’ Buenos Aires is just across the river, but it’s Argentina’s capital.","why":"Montevideo is Uruguay’s capital and biggest city. Asunción is Paraguay’s capital, and Punta del Este is a famous Uruguayan beach town."},{"c":"uruguay","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"En Uruguay se paga con…","o":["el peso uruguayo","el guaraní","el euro","el real"],"a":0,"hint":"‘In Uruguay, people pay with…’ Careful: the guaraní is Paraguay’s money, not Uruguay’s.","why":"Uruguay’s money is the peso uruguayo (Uruguayan peso). The guaraní is Paraguay’s money and the real is Brazil’s."},{"c":"uruguay","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"En enero hace frío en Delhi. En Montevideo, en enero, es…","o":["invierno","otoño","primavera","verano"],"a":3,"hint":"‘In January it’s cold in Delhi. In Montevideo, in January, it is…’ Montevideo is south of the equator.","why":"Uruguay is in the Southern Hemisphere, so the seasons are flipped: January is summer (verano) and beach time in Montevideo, while Delhi is in winter (invierno)."},{"c":"uruguay","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"En Uruguay mucha gente toma mate. ¿Qué es el mate?","o":["una bebida caliente de hojas","un baile típico","una fruta tropical","un instrumento musical"],"a":0,"hint":"‘In Uruguay many people have mate. What is mate?’ People carry a thermos around for it all day.","why":"Mate is a hot drink made from dried yerba mate leaves, sipped from a gourd through a metal straw called a bombilla. Many Uruguayans carry a thermos of hot water everywhere!"},{"c":"paraguay","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Paraguay tiene dos lenguas oficiales: el español y el…","o":["portugués","guaraní","quechua","inglés"],"a":1,"hint":"‘Paraguay has two official languages: Spanish and…’ The same name is used for Paraguay’s money.","why":"Guaraní is an Indigenous language, and most Paraguayans speak it, along with Spanish. Paraguay’s money is even called the guaraní! Quechua is spoken in Peru and Bolivia."},{"c":"paraguay","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Asunción es la capital de…","o":["Uruguay","Paraguay","Bolivia","Argentina"],"a":1,"hint":"‘Asunción is the capital of…’ This country’s second official language is Guaraní.","why":"Asunción, on the Paraguay River, is Paraguay’s capital. Don’t mix it up with Montevideo, the capital of Uruguay!"},{"c":"paraguay","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"¿Paraguay tiene playas de mar? Elige la respuesta y la razón.","o":["Sí — está junto al océano Atlántico","Sí — está junto al océano Pacífico","No — está rodeado de otros países","No — es una isla muy pequeña"],"a":2,"hint":"‘Does Paraguay have sea beaches? Pick the answer and the reason.’ Picture the map: what is all around it?","why":"Paraguay is landlocked: Argentina, Brazil and Bolivia surround it, so it has no sea coast. Bolivia is South America’s only other landlocked country. Uruguay has the Atlantic beaches!"},{"c":"paraguay","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Hace mucho calor en Asunción. Te ofrecen una bebida fría de yerba mate. ¿Cómo se llama?","o":["tereré","chipa","ñandutí","asado"],"a":0,"hint":"‘It’s very hot in Asunción. You’re offered a cold yerba mate drink. What is it called?’ Chipa is a bread.","why":"Tereré is mate served ice-cold, perfect for Paraguay’s heat. Chipa is a cheese bread, ñandutí is a lace that looks like a spider’s web, and asado is a barbecue."},{"c":"conjunciones","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Completa: Me gusta el fútbol, ___ no me gusta el tenis.","o":["e","u","pero","porque"],"a":2,"hint":"The second part goes against the first part. Which word means 'but'?","why":"pero = but. It joins two ideas that contrast: I like football, but I don't like tennis. e and u only replace y/o; porque = because."},{"c":"conjunciones","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Paraguay no tiene mar, ___ tiene ríos muy grandes. ¿Qué palabra va?","o":["porque","o","por qué","pero"],"a":3,"hint":"No sea, yet big rivers: the two ideas contrast.","why":"Paraguay has no sea, but (pero) it has very big rivers, like the Paraná and the Paraguay. porque = because, o = or, ¿por qué? = why?"}],"z-sst":[{"c":"iron-pillar","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The famous Iron Pillar at Mehrauli, Delhi, was put up during the reign of which king?","o":["Chandragupta Maurya","Chandragupta II","Ashoka","Samudragupta"],"a":1,"hint":"Its inscription names a king called ‘Chandra’ — but from which dynasty?","why":"The inscription’s ‘Chandra’ is identified as Gupta king Chandragupta II. Don’t mix him up with Chandragupta Maurya of the earlier Maurya dynasty!"},{"c":"iron-pillar","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A bicycle left in the rain rusts within a year. Why has the Iron Pillar stood in the open for 1,600+ years without rusting?","o":["It is repainted every monsoon","It has always stood under a roof","It is really made of stone","A thin protective layer forms on its surface"],"a":3,"hint":"Scientists studied the pillar’s surface. Rust starts when air and water reach the iron.","why":"Scientists believe the special iron and oxygen from the air form a unique thin layer that shields it from corrosion — proof of ancient India’s advanced metal skills!"},{"c":"iron-pillar","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which statement about the Iron Pillar of Delhi is WRONG?","o":["It was first set up where it stands now","It weighs about 6 tonnes","It was dedicated to Vishnu","Its inscription celebrates a king"],"a":0,"hint":"The pillar may have made a long journey before it reached Mehrauli.","why":"It was probably first erected in front of the Udayagiri caves (Madhya Pradesh) and brought to Delhi a few centuries later. The other three facts are true."},{"c":"rise-and-decline","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The Gupta Empire flourished from the 3rd to the 6th century CE. Where was its capital?","o":["Kanchipuram (Tamil Nadu)","Nandivardhana (near Nagpur)","Mehrauli (Delhi)","Pataliputra (present-day Patna)"],"a":3,"hint":"Today this city is the capital of Bihar.","why":"The Guptas ruled from Pataliputra, today’s Patna. Kanchipuram was the Pallava capital, Nandivardhana the Vakataka one, and Mehrauli is just where the Iron Pillar stands now."},{"c":"rise-and-decline","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"What change in the 3rd century CE opened the way for the Guptas to rise?","o":["The Hunas conquered north India","The Pallavas took over Pataliputra","Faxian arrived from China","The Kushana Empire began to weaken"],"a":3,"hint":"A big empire in the north and northwest was losing its grip.","why":"As the Kushana Empire weakened, new kingdoms emerged. The Guptas, regional rulers near today’s Uttar Pradesh, rose to build a powerful empire. The Hunas and Faxian came later!"},{"c":"rise-and-decline","t":"What if","p":100,"q":"What if the Hunas had never attacked? Would the Gupta Empire have faced no trouble at all?","o":["Yes — the Hunas were the only problem","No — the Romans would have invaded","No — strong regional rulers caused conflicts","No — the Kushanas were rising again"],"a":2,"hint":"The decline had trouble from OUTSIDE the empire and trouble from INSIDE it.","why":"Two causes: fierce Hunas from central Asia attacked again and again, weakening control of north India, AND powerful regional rulers rose, causing internal conflicts."},{"c":"gupta-kings","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which Gupta ruler was also known as ‘Vikramaditya’?","o":["Chandragupta I","Samudragupta","Chandragupta II","Chandragupta Maurya"],"a":2,"hint":"Art, literature and science flourished most in this king’s time.","why":"Chandragupta II, son of Samudragupta, was also called Vikramaditya. Art, architecture, literature and science flourished especially during his reign."},{"c":"gupta-kings","t":"Order","p":100,"q":"Put these Gupta kings in order, earliest first.","o":["Samudragupta → Chandragupta I → Chandragupta II","Chandragupta I → Samudragupta → Chandragupta II","Chandragupta II → Samudragupta → Chandragupta I","Chandragupta I → Chandragupta II → Samudragupta"],"a":1,"hint":"Think family: grandfather, father, son. Who was Chandragupta II’s father?","why":"Chandragupta I (the grandfather) laid the foundation, Samudragupta (the father) expanded it by war, and under Chandragupta II (the son) art and science flourished most."},{"c":"gupta-kings","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Why do historians write ‘II’ after Chandragupta II’s name?","o":["His grandfather was also a Chandragupta","He was the king’s second son","He ruled the empire twice","He had two capitals"],"a":0,"hint":"It’s about someone else in his family with the very same name.","why":"His grandfather was Chandragupta I, so historians added ‘II’. Naming the first son after his grandfather is a tradition some Indian families still follow today!"},{"c":"empire-building","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Court poet Harisena wrote the Prayaga prashasti, a pillar inscription. Whose achievements does it praise?","o":["Chandragupta II","Chandragupta I","Prabhavati Gupta","Samudragupta"],"a":3,"hint":"This warrior king’s dream was ‘dharani-bandha’ — to unify the Earth.","why":"The Prayaga prashasti in Prayagraj praises Samudragupta, Chandragupta II’s father, for his many wars and his support for art, learning and trade."},{"c":"empire-building","t":"Odd one out","p":50,"q":"Gupta kings took grand titles to claim supreme power. Which is an older, SIMPLER title they wanted to rise above?","o":["Maharajadhiraja","Samrat","Chakravartin","Rajan"],"a":3,"hint":"Three of these claim to be above all other kings; one does not.","why":"‘Maharajadhiraja’, ‘samrat’ and ‘chakravartin’ proclaimed supreme authority. Earlier rulers used simpler titles like ‘rajan’ and ‘maharaja’."},{"c":"empire-building","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Chandragupta II’s daughter Prabhavati married a Vakataka prince. What did this marriage alliance achieve?","o":["Strong ties with the Guptas’ southern neighbours","A war between Guptas and Vakatakas","The Vakatakas moved to Pataliputra","The end of the Vakataka kingdom"],"a":0,"hint":"Gupta rulers grew their empire by conquest, diplomacy — and marriages.","why":"When her husband died early, Prabhavati ruled the Vakataka kingdom as regent and kept its ties with the Guptas strong. One inscription calls her ‘mother of two kings’!"},{"c":"administration-revenue","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"What was the MAIN source of income for the Gupta rulers?","o":["Land tax","Fines","Taxes on mines","Taxes on crafts"],"a":0,"hint":"Most people in the Gupta age were farmers. What could the king collect from every village?","why":"Land tax was the primary source of revenue. Fines and taxes on mines, irrigation, trade and crafts brought in extra money."},{"c":"administration-revenue","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Gupta land grants were carefully inscribed on copper plates. Why?","o":["Copper was cheaper than paper","To decorate palace walls","To keep accurate, lasting records","So they could be melted into coins"],"a":2,"hint":"Think about proving who was given which land — and collecting the right tax.","why":"Copper plates kept accurate records of land grants, which helped proper tax collection. Archaeologists have discovered many of them in recent times!"},{"c":"administration-revenue","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Instead of controlling everything from the centre, Gupta kings granted land to local rulers. What did this achieve?","o":["Kings no longer needed any taxes","Local rulers became emperors","Efficient rule, with locals keeping some control","The capital moved every year"],"a":2,"hint":"Think of a head teacher who lets class monitors look after each class.","why":"Provinces and land grants to local rulers, priests and chieftains helped proper tax collection and efficient rule, while local leaders kept some control of their regions."},{"c":"indian-ocean-trade","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which goods did India export in the Gupta era?","o":["Silk, tea and porcelain","Horses, wine and olive oil","Textiles, spices, ivory and gemstones","Coffee, potatoes and rubber"],"a":2,"hint":"Silk and porcelain were China’s goods; coffee, potatoes and rubber reached India centuries later.","why":"India sent textiles, spices, ivory and gemstones to the Mediterranean world, Southeast Asia and China through the Indian Ocean trade network."},{"c":"indian-ocean-trade","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"How do we know Indian traders visited Socotra Island in the Arabian Sea over several centuries?","o":["Faxian’s travelogue describes them","A Gupta iron pillar stands there","Pottery, Brahmi inscriptions, a stupa design","Indian kings ruled the island"],"a":2,"hint":"Archaeologists dig up evidence — what could traders leave behind?","why":"Archaeologists found pottery, Brahmi-script inscriptions and Buddhist stupa designs. Traders from Egypt, Arabia, Rome and Greece came too — a real meeting point of cultures!"},{"c":"indian-ocean-trade","t":"Predict","p":100,"q":"Taxes on trade were one source of Gupta revenue. If trade boomed, what would most likely follow?","o":["Land tax would be abolished","Fewer temples would be built","Kings would stop paying their guards","More money for army, temples and scholars"],"a":3,"hint":"Follow the money: what was Gupta revenue spent on?","why":"More trade meant more revenue — which paid for administration, the army, temples, infrastructure, scholars and artists. That’s why the empire had to promote vibrant trade."},{"c":"faxian-and-sources","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Why did the Chinese traveller Faxian make his long journey to India in the early 5th century CE?","o":["To trade silk at Indian ports","To join the Gupta army","To map the empire for China’s emperor","To visit Buddhist sites and collect texts"],"a":3,"hint":"He was a pilgrim — and he wanted to carry something precious back home.","why":"Faxian came on a pilgrimage to sacred Buddhist sites, to learn from Indian scholars and to collect Buddhist manuscripts for China. His travelogue survives today!"},{"c":"faxian-and-sources","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Which is NOT something Faxian wrote about Gupta-era society?","o":["Everyone had to register with officials","Farmers of royal land paid part of their grain","The king’s guards were paid salaries","Merchants ran houses for charity and medicine"],"a":0,"hint":"Faxian said people were happy — partly because of one thing they did NOT have to do.","why":"Faxian wrote that people had ‘no need to register households or attend to officials’. The other three are all in his account."},{"c":"faxian-and-sources","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Faxian described Gupta cities as rich and kind. Why do historians still check other sources?","o":["Faxian wrote only about China","It shows one writer’s view at one time","All travellers make things up","His travelogue was lost long ago"],"a":1,"hint":"Faxian also described chandalas being treated harshly. Did the praise show everyone?","why":"A travelogue shows one writer’s view, at one time, of part of society. Faxian himself described the harsh treatment of chandalas, so historians corroborate with more sources."},{"c":"classical-age","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Why do some historians call the Gupta period India’s ‘classical age’?","o":["Long peace led to great achievements","Classical music was invented then","It was the first time India had kings","It had more wars than any other age"],"a":0,"hint":"The label is about what got achieved — and what made it possible.","why":"A prolonged period of peace and stability promoted notable achievements in literature, science, art and metallurgy — so some historians label it the ‘classical age’."},{"c":"classical-age","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Complete the Gupta chain: peace and stability → stronger economy → ??? → cultural and intellectual growth.","o":["Kings stopped collecting taxes","Huna attacks grew stronger","Trade with China came to an end","The state could support scholars and artists"],"a":3,"hint":"Once there’s more money, whom can the state afford to back?","why":"Stability strengthened the economy, so the state could support scholars, artists and scientists — which led to cultural and intellectual growth."},{"c":"classical-age","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Gupta kings were devoted to Vishnu, yet they supported Nalanda and Buddhist viharas. What does this show?","o":["They had secretly become Buddhists","Nalanda was really a Vishnu temple","Their approach was inclusive and open","They only supported their own faith"],"a":2,"hint":"Backing traditions other than your own shows a certain attitude.","why":"Though devout followers of Vishnu, Gupta rulers patronised Buddhist institutions like Nalanda university and many viharas (monasteries) — an inclusive, open approach."},{"c":"gupta-science","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Aryabhata proposed a reason for the alternation of day and night. What was it?","o":["The Sun circles the Earth daily","The Earth spins on its axis","The Moon hides the Sun each night","Clouds block sunlight at night"],"a":1,"hint":"Picture yourself turning round on the spot — what keeps changing in front of you?","why":"In his Aryabhatiya (around 500 CE), Aryabhata proposed that the Earth spins on its axis, which explains why day and night keep alternating."},{"c":"gupta-science","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"Aryabhata: a year is 365 days 6 h 12 min 30 s. Modern value: 365 days 5 h 48 min 45 s. How far off was he?","o":["About 1 hour","About 24 minutes","About 36 minutes","Exactly right"],"a":1,"hint":"Subtract carefully: 6 h 12 min 30 s minus 5 h 48 min 45 s. Borrow from the hour!","why":"6 h 12 min 30 s − 5 h 48 min 45 s = 23 min 45 s, so about 24 minutes. Only a few minutes off — about 1,500 years ago!"},{"c":"gupta-science","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A farmer today checks the weather forecast before sowing. Which Gupta-era book covered weather forecasting AND farming?","o":["Aryabhatiya","Meghadutam","Brihat Samhita","Charaka Samhita"],"a":2,"hint":"This encyclopedic book by an Ujjayini scholar also covered town planning.","why":"Varahamihira’s Brihat Samhita covered astronomy, astrology, weather forecasting, architecture, town planning and even farming. Meghadutam describes weather, but it’s a poem!"},{"c":"gupta-science","t":"Spot the mistake","p":100,"q":"Spot the WRONG statement about Aryabhata.","o":["He lived in Kusumapura, near Patna","He wrote the poem Meghadutam","He estimated the size of the Earth","He explained eclipses correctly"],"a":1,"hint":"One of these belongs to a famous poet, not a mathematician.","why":"Meghadutam is Kalidasa’s poem. Aryabhata lived in Kusumapura, wrote the Aryabhatiya, gave a fair estimate of Earth’s size and correctly explained solar and lunar eclipses."},{"c":"kalidasa","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"In Kalidasa’s Meghadutam, how does the banished yaksha reach his beloved?","o":["He sends a letter by pigeon","He sends a message through a passing cloud","He rides a cloud home himself","He sings to her across a river"],"a":1,"hint":"The title Meghadutam means ‘The Cloud Messenger’.","why":"A yaksha (minor deity), banished by his master, sends a message to his beloved through a passing cloud. On the way, the poem paints north India’s landscapes and weather."},{"c":"kalidasa","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"Kalidasa wrote that wisdom and knowledge — not luxuries — strengthen a kingdom. Which modern choice fits his advice best?","o":["Spending on schools and research","Building bigger palaces for leaders","Rewarding people who flatter leaders","Showing off power with army parades"],"a":0,"hint":"Which choice builds wisdom and knowledge in a country?","why":"In Raghuvansham, Kalidasa says force, mere diplomacy and flattery aren’t enough — wisdom and knowledge truly strengthen a kingdom. Schools and research build exactly that!"},{"c":"kalidasa","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Legend says Kalidasa was once ridiculed by others. How did he respond, according to the story?","o":["He gave up writing forever","He worked hard and transformed his life","He left India for China","He became a soldier instead"],"a":1,"hint":"The legend is told as an inspiring story.","why":"Little is known about his life, but legends say the ridicule motivated him to work hard and transform his life. He became renowned for refined Sanskrit poetry."},{"c":"ayurveda","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which two famous Ayurveda texts were given their final shape in the Gupta period?","o":["Charaka Samhita and Sushruta Samhita","Aryabhatiya and Brihat Samhita","Meghadutam and Raghuvansham","Vishnu Purana and Prayaga prashasti"],"a":0,"hint":"You want medicine books — not maths, poetry or royal praise.","why":"The Charaka Samhita and Sushruta Samhita were compiled and given their final shape in the Gupta period. They laid the foundation for Ayurvedic practices still used today."},{"c":"ayurveda","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Ayurveda was ‘codified’ in the Gupta period. What does that tell us?","o":["Ayurveda was invented from scratch then","It was written in a secret code","Gupta kings banned it","Older knowledge was arranged systematically"],"a":3,"hint":"Ayurveda’s roots go back several centuries BCE — so ‘codified’ can’t mean ‘invented’.","why":"Codified means arranged or written in an organised, systematic way. Ayurveda’s roots are centuries older; in the Gupta era, knowledge from earlier times was compiled into texts."},{"c":"ayurveda","t":"Real life","p":100,"q":"A coach says, “A calm mind helps a healthy body.” Which idea from Gupta-era Ayurveda texts does this echo?","o":["Only surgery can truly cure illness","Diet has nothing to do with health","Mind, body and nature are deeply linked","Treat each body part on its own"],"a":2,"hint":"Ayurveda is famous for ‘holistic’ healing — looking at the whole person.","why":"Ayurveda emphasises holistic healing and a deep link between mind, body and nature. Its texts also stressed diet for good health — so “diet doesn’t matter” is the opposite!"},{"c":"gupta-art","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"The Ajanta caves, famous for paintings of the Jataka tales, are in which present-day state?","o":["Madhya Pradesh","Uttar Pradesh","Maharashtra","Tamil Nadu"],"a":2,"hint":"Same state as Ramtek hill, where some of Prabhavati Gupta’s temples stand.","why":"The Ajanta caves are in Maharashtra and were carved with support from the Guptas and Vakatakas. Udayagiri is in Madhya Pradesh and Sarnath in Uttar Pradesh."},{"c":"gupta-art","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which Gupta-era art centre near Varanasi is known for its exquisite sculptures of the Buddha?","o":["Sarnath","Udayagiri","Kanchipuram","Ajanta"],"a":0,"hint":"It isn’t a cave site — and it’s in Uttar Pradesh.","why":"Sarnath, near Varanasi in Uttar Pradesh, is known for exquisite Buddha sculptures. Ajanta shows the Buddha too, but it’s in Maharashtra, far from Varanasi."},{"c":"gupta-art","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"In Gupta terracotta art, how can you tell the river goddesses Ganga and Yamuna apart?","o":["By the colour of their clothes","Ganga always holds a veena","Yamuna always rides a lion","By their vahanas: makara and tortoise"],"a":3,"hint":"Look at what each goddess is standing on.","why":"Ganga stands on a makara (a mythical creature like a crocodile) and Yamuna on a tortoise. Water flowing over their heads and the pot remind us they are rivers."},{"c":"neighbour-kingdoms","t":"Recall","p":50,"q":"Which city, the Pallava capital, is often called the ‘city of a thousand temples’?","o":["Pataliputra","Kanchipuram","Ujjayini","Nandivardhana"],"a":1,"hint":"It’s in present-day Tamil Nadu.","why":"Kanchipuram in Tamil Nadu was the Pallava capital and a major centre of learning in the south. Most Pallavas were devotees of Shiva and built magnificent temples."},{"c":"neighbour-kingdoms","t":"Two-tier","p":100,"q":"Samudragupta defeated the Pallava and Kamarupa kings but let them keep their thrones. Why did this work for him?","o":["He was afraid of their armies","They accepted his rule and paid tribute","Their lands had no wealth at all","They promised to become Buddhists"],"a":1,"hint":"Think about what the defeated kings had to give and accept in return.","why":"Kings kept their thrones as long as they accepted his rule and paid tribute. That kept peaceful relations — even with far-off Kamarupa (Assam), without direct control."},{"c":"neighbour-kingdoms","t":"Connect","p":100,"q":"The Guptas rose as the Kushanas weakened. In the south, the Pallavas gained power as which dynasty declined?","o":["Satavahanas","Vakatakas","Mauryas","Varmans"],"a":0,"hint":"The Pallavas seem to have once been a tributary power under this dynasty.","why":"The Pallavas appear to have been a tributary power under the Satavahanas and gained power as the Satavahanas declined — the same rise-after-a-fall pattern as the Guptas!"}]};
